INSERT INTO `Categories` VALUES (6,'Target Age Group : Toddlers',NULL),(7,'Target Age Group : Younger Children',NULL),(8,'Target Age Group : Older Children',NULL),(9,'Target Age Group : Teenagers',NULL),(10,'Target Age Group : Adults',NULL),(11,'Game System : Randomization Method : Dice','Random chance is a major part of many gamebooks, and the most common method for introducing random numbers is the use of dice.'),(12,'Game System : Randomization Method : Coins','While dice are the randomization method of choice in the gamebook world, flipping coins to make decisions and determine random outcomes is not unheard of.'),(13,'Complexity Level : Basic (No Game System)','This category contains books with  branching plots but without additional game mechanics.'),(14,'Complexity Level : Intermediate (Some Game Elements)','This category contains books that are quite similar to those in the <a href=\"/Category/13\">basic</a> category but which feature some more advanced features like <a href=\"/Category/17\">visual puzzles</a> to solve or <a href=\"/Category/11\">dice-rolling</a>.'),(15,'Complexity Level : Advanced (Full Game System)','This category contains books with branching plots and detailed game systems involving character creation, combat resolution or other role-playing-inspired mechanics.'),(16,'Game System : Inventory Management','Many gamebooks require the reader to keep track of what his or her character is holding onto.  Sometimes this serves the same purpose as <a href=\"/Category/21\">codewords</a>, with a found item indicating that the reader has visited a particular section of the book.  Other times, it adds strategy to the gameplay, forcing the reader to make optimal use of limited resources.  Some books allow characters to carry an unlimited number of items (often leading to rather absurd accumulations of junk) but others restrict the number, leading to an additional level of decision-making during play as some items must be abandoned in favor of others.'),(17,'Game System : Visual Puzzles','Standard puzzles found in activity books (such as mazes, connect-the-dots, word searches, etc.) have often found their way into gamebooks.  In some cases, these puzzles are the primary reason for a series\' existence; in others, only occasional puzzles are used to make the gameplay a little more challenging.  Sometimes, puzzles are completely superfluous to the plot but exist simply to add a little novelty.'),(18,'Game System : Scores','It is helpful for gamebook readers to be proficient at arithmetic.  Some very basic gamebooks feature a running score which determines the level of success at the end.  More sophisticated books tend to have a variety of figures for players to keep track of.  Often, characters have a variety of attributes measuring inherent properties like strength or speed.  Additionally, many books feature a measure of health (often called \"hit points\") which changes during play and can lead to death if it reaches zero.  Sometimes skills and spells are fueled by a pool of points, and there are also often inventory-related totals such as gold coins or ammunition involved in play.  In many books, character scores are initially determined randomly, though others give readers a pool of points and allow them to be distributed strategically.'),(19,'Game System : Skill Selection','Just as some books require that players keep track of a character\'s <a href=\"/Category/16\">inventory</a>, others also require the selection of less tangible characteristics like magic spells known or proficiencies in certain useful activities.  Most books that feature skills require the reader to pick a limited number of abilities from a list before beginning the game; some also allow more skills to be learned during play or as the result of successfully completing an adventure.  Sometimes certain skills are required in order to make certain choices, and other times skills allow other rules to be bent in some manner.'),(20,'Genre : Programmed Instruction',NULL),(21,'Game System : Codewords','In some gamebooks, your past actions will affect your future outcomes.  Since a book has no way of remembering what you have done, some gamebooks will require their readers to record codewords which indicate where they have been and what they have done.  Later branches of the plot will depend on which codewords have been written down.'),(22,'Game System : Combat','Many gamebooks, particularly the more complex variety, feature an emphasis on fighting.  For this reason, many contain special rules for resolving conflict.  Sometimes a special table is used to determine combat outcomes, while other times it is a matter of rolling dice, adding various attribute scores and skill- or item-based modifiers, and then comparing the results.  Usually, the fighter with the best score damages the fighter with the weaker score, though some systems also account for the possibility of mutual injury.  No matter what happens in the heat of combat, the outcome is usually the same: whichever fighter runs out of health points first is killed; if this is the reader’s character, the game is generally over.  If not, the reader proceeds, hoping to recover strength before having to fight again.'),(23,'Game System : Multiple Players Supported','Gamebooks are generally considered to be a solitary activity; however, there are a select few which use various clever means to allow multiple players to participate as either allies or adversaries.'),(24,'Genre : Adventure',NULL),(25,'Genre : Contemporary Fiction',NULL),(26,'Genre : Fantasy',''),(27,'Genre : Historical Fiction',NULL),(28,'Genre : Horror',NULL),(29,'Genre : Humor',NULL),(30,'Genre : Mystery',NULL),(31,'Genre : Religious Fiction',NULL),(32,'Genre : Romance',NULL),(33,'Genre : Science Fiction',NULL),(34,'Genre : Sports Fiction',NULL),(35,'Genre : Superhero Fiction',NULL),(36,'Genre : Western',NULL),(37,'Product Family : Fighting Fantasy',''),(38,'Product Family : Choose Your Own Adventure',''),(39,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Doctor Who',''),(40,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Star Trek',''),(41,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Star Wars',''),(42,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Movie / TV Tie-In',''),(43,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Video Game Tie-In',''),(44,'Game System : BASIC Computer Programs Included','In the early days of computing, it was not uncommon to find publications containing computer games that could be typed into a home computer and played.  In a few cases, this idea was combined with fiction to produce interactive computer adventures.'),(45,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Comic Book Tie-In',''),(46,'Game System : Character Advancement','Most gamebooks are one-time deals; you play them, and then it\'s all over.  In a few cases, though, it is possible to carry your character and equipment from book to book, gaining in power as you go.'),(47,'Game System : Magic','Since so many gamebooks take place in the <a href=\"/Category/26\">fantasy</a> genre, it is not surprising that a variety of systems for simulating spell-casting have materialized over the years.'),(48,'Complexity Level : Solitaire RPG (External Rules Required)','This category contains branching-plot stories designed to complement an existing set of role-playing rules.'),(49,'Format : Paperback','The vast majority of gamebooks were released in paperback format, which is a shame, as they tend to fall apart after a while.'),(50,'Format : Hardback','Although it is uncommon, a few gamebooks have been published in durable hardcover editions.  It is rare for an entire series to be published in this format; in most cases, only a few selected volumes get hardcover treatments.'),(51,'Format : eBook','This category contains gamebooks which are distributed in a downloadable electronic format regardless of whether they are intended to be printed out or played on an electronic device.  It does not list series which have been illegally distributed in this fashion.  For books designed to be played through a web browser, see the <a href=\"/Category/52\">Web Page</a> category.'),(52,'Format : Web Page','This category lists gamebooks which are designed to be played using a web browser.  For single-file electronic gamebooks, see the <a href=\"/Category/51\">eBook</a> category.'),(53,'Product Family : Lone Wolf',''),(54,'Product Family : World of Orb',''),(55,'Product Family : Librogame',''),(56,'Product Family : Find Your Way',''),(57,'Product Family : Choice Is Yours',''),(58,'Product Family : Plot-It-Yourself / Plot-Your-Own',''),(59,'Writing Style : First Person',NULL),(60,'Writing Style : Second Person',NULL),(61,'Writing Style : Third Person',NULL),(62,'Writing Style : Present Tense',NULL),(63,'Writing Style : Past Tense',NULL),(64,'Game System : Randomization Method : In Book','For the convenience of players lacking <a href=\"/Category/11\">dice</a> or </Category/12>coins</a>, some gamebooks include ways of creating random numbers.  One common method is printing \"dice rolls\" on the pages and having the player flip to a random page; another involves printing a table of random numbers and having the player point at it randomly with a pencil.'),(65,'Writing Style : Visual','Some gamebooks include little or no text, instead using graphics almost exclusively to convey information.'),(66,'Writing Style : Comic Book','Some gamebooks use the comic book format, complete with illustrated panels and speech bubbles, to tell interactive stories.'),(67,'Product Family : Dungeons & Dragons',''),(68,'Product Family : Twistaplot',''),(69,'Product Family : Which Way',''),(70,'Product Family : Pick a Path to Adventure',''),(71,'Product Family : Traveller',''),(72,'Amateur Publication','This category contains self-published and print-on-demand products.'),(73,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Asterix',''),(74,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Famous Five',''),(75,'Product Family : Car Wars',''),(76,'Product Family : What-Do-I-Do-Now?',''),(77,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Novel Tie-In',''),(78,'Product Family : Un livre dont vous êtes le héros',''),(79,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Indiana Jones',''),(80,'Product Family : Stormbringer',''),(81,'Product Family : Tunnels & Trolls',''),(82,'Product Family : RuneQuest',''),(83,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Disney',''),(84,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Conan',''),(85,'Licensed / Adapted Property : James Bond',''),(86,'Product Family : Find Your Fate',''),(87,'Product Family : d20 System',''),(88,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Toy Tie-In',''),(89,'Product Family : Alternity',''),(90,'Product Family : Lost Worlds',''),(91,'Genre : Abstract Strategy','These books contain no story at all, using the interactive format solely for playing a game (such as Tic-Tac-Toe).'),(92,'Format : Cards and Reader',''),(93,'Genre : Erotica',''),(94,'Product Family : Singular',''),(95,'Product Family : World of Legend',''),(97,'Format : Board Book','A book with thick cardboard pages capable of withstanding abuse by small children.'),(98,'Product Family : You Choose',''),(99,'Product Family : Aquelarre',''),(100,'Game System : Randomization Method : Cards',''),(101,'Product Family : 5th Edition OGL','These products are compatible with the open-licensed version of the rules behind <i>Dungeons &amp; Dragons</i> 5th edition.'),(102,'Genre : Political',''),(104,'Product Family : Choose Your Path',''),(105,'Product Family : La BD dont vous etes le heros',''),(106,'Product Family : Multipath Adventures',''),(107,'Product Family : Multipath Movies',''),(108,'Game System : Escape-Room-Inspired','In an escape-room-inspired gamebook, gameplay generally revolves a sequence of puzzles which gradually unlock the story or lead to a conclusion.'),(109,'Product Family : Comic Quests',''),(110,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Board/Card/Miniatures Game Tie-in',''),(111,'Licensed / Adapted Property : 2000 AD',''),(112,'Genre : Simulation / Management',''),(113,'Game System : Achievements',''),(114,'Product Family : GURPS',''),(115,'Product Family : libri NOSTRI',''),(116,'Product Family : Quasar',''),(117,'Product Family : Warhammer',''),(118,'Game System : Open World','In some gamebooks, you are able to return to previously visited sections and explore other options.'),(119,'Game System : Serial Narrative','In some gamebook series, the plot is directly continued between books.'),(120,'Game System : State Carries Over','In some gamebook series with a <a href=\"/Category/119\">Serial Narrative</a> the player\'s state is carried over between books. Carried over state may include <a href=\"/Category/18\">scores</a>, <a href=\"/Category/16\">inventory</a>, <a href=\"/Category/19\">skills</a>, <a href=\"/Category/21\">codewords</a>, etc.'),(121,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Cthulhu Mythos',''),(122,'Licensed / Adapted Property : Dracula','');
/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `Categories` ENABLE KEYS */;
UNLOCK TABLES;

