INSERT INTO `Series` VALUES (1,'Catch-a-Crook Adventure','This short gamebook was released to tie in with Disney\'s <i>Dick Tracy</i> movie. Its cover features two logos (\"You Choose the Way\" and \"A Catch-a-Crook Adventure\") that sound like series titles, but this is the only book of its type.',1),(2,'Life\'s Lottery','This book is no more complicated than a <a href=\"\"\"/show_series.php?name=Choose+Your+Own+Adventure\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> book in terms of gameplay, but its story is among the most intricate and unusual works of interactive fiction I\'ve encountered. Because of its complexity (and some of its subject matter), this is definitely a book meant for a mature audience.',1),(3,'Robin of Sherwood','These books, designed to tie in with the British television series of the same name, allow the reader to take on the role of Robin Hood.  The series uses a fairly complex game system which resembles that of <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> in a couple of ways: it is necessary to keep track of both backpack items (of which a limited number may be carried) and special items (which are kept in places other than the backpack), and combat is resolved by comparing abilities and referring to a special table.  However, in other respects, the system here is more advanced than that of <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a>.  Rather than randomly rolling up character attributes and abilities, the reader gets to determine which points are assigned to which abilities (though the basic pool of points is randomly determined).  Additionally, unlike most gamebooks of this sort, character wounds are applied to specific parts of the body rather than to a pool of points representing health in general.  Certain wound locations lead more quickly to defeat, and as wounds accumulate, combat ability diminishes.  Finally, there are magic points (called \"Power of Light and Darkness points\") which may be used throughout each adventure; however, they must be conserved, for they are also used as a measure of success at the end of the adventure, and using them too freely negatively impacts the final outcome.  There is no explicit continuity between the two books, nor is there any sort of character advancement system.  Both books were written by the team of Paul Mason and Graham Staplehurst, but for simplicity’s sake, each author chose to take credit for one full book rather than to share credit for both.  More titles were planned, but the series was cut short due to perceived lack of interest.',1),(4,'Robin Hood','',5),(5,'Aventuras de Robin Hood, As','',7),(6,'Be an Interplanetary Spy','These books, designed by Byron Preiss (also known for their work on <a href=\"/Series/143\">Explorer</a> and <a href=\"/Series/78\">Time Machine</a>), cast the reader as a spy who travels through space tracking down various alien criminals.  The books are very visually oriented, having a style that feels like a mix between comic books and early eighties video games.  Puzzles like mazes and visualization problems are common, and what text there is is very brief.  Although the books are not heavy on plot, there is continuity between them, with some foes recurring from book to book and the reader’s character gaining in rank as the series goes on.  Although not as influential as <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> or <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>, this series is generally held in high esteem by fans and was an important force in the early days of the American gamebook market Because the books encourage the reader to write in them, it\'s somewhat difficult to find \"clean\" copies.',1),(7,'Choose the Fate of Apollo 13','This book was released to tie in with Universal Pictures\' film, <i>Apollo 13</i>.',1),(8,'Forgotten Forest','These gamebooks, all set in the same fantasy world, were first released as one of Troll\'s four <a href=\"/Category/57\">The Choice is Yours</a> sets.  They were reissued almost twenty years after their original release with new covers and a slightly different ordering (in the new series, the first book was released last and vice versa).',1),(9,'Haz de Espia Interplanetario','',6),(10,'Elige tu aventura','This series includes translations from all four different gamebook series originally published in English by Troll.',6),(11,'Fighting Fantasy (1982-1995, Puffin)','Just as <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> popularized the basic style of gamebook in the United States, so this series established more advanced gamebooks in the United Kingdom.  The series was the first to include a complete set of rules to supplement the gameplay, and all of the books share the same basic system.  Characters have three main attributes (Skill, Stamina and Luck) which are randomly generated at the start of play, and items and treasures are collected and kept track of during the course of an adventure.  Enemies are frequently battled using a simple system in which the player rolls two dice for each combatant and adds Skill scores to each roll; the fighter with higher total does two Stamina points\' worth of damage to the loser.  This is repeated until someone dies from loss of Stamina.  Luck points add another mechanic to the game; from time to time, the reader has to roll two dice and compare the result to his or her Luck.  A roll equal to or less than Luck is a good thing; a higher roll is a bad thing.  Luck goes down by one point every time it is tested in this fashion, but there are also opportunities to regain it during the course of an adventure.  An element of strategy is added due to the fact that optional Luck rolls may be made to affect the outcome of combat.  In addition to these basic rules, different volumes tend to add different special features, ranging from spellcasting rules to instructions on controlling teams of characters rather than individuals.\n\n<p>Despite the word \"fantasy\" in the name of the series, this is actually a multi-genre system, with numerous science fiction adventures and a single book each in the horror and superhero genres.  Even the fantasy books in the series didn\'t all take place in the same world.  One early adventure, <a href=\"/Item/48\">Talisman of Death</a>, introduced the <a href=\"/Category/54\">world of Orb</a>, which was featured in several later gamebook series, most notably <a href=\"/Series/469\">The Way of the Tiger</a>.  As the series matured, though, increasing effort was made to set the books in a cohesive world, and the release of spin-off products including <a href=\"/Series/156\">Warlock</a> magazine, two different multiplayer role-playing games and a series of regular novels further helped to flesh out such settings as Allansia, Khul and The Old World.  The series never made great efforts at maintaining continuity, and there were never opportunities to carry character statistics or equipment from book to book, but the series did gradually grow a distinctive sense of place.\n\n<p>The series was first published by Puffin in the United Kingdom, and shortly thereafter, Dell began releasing American editions (some featuring original cover art) of the first twenty-one books.  The series lasted well over a decade and nearly sixty volumes before going out of print.  For several years, erroneous information about <a href=\"/Item/35\">Bloodbones</a>, the unpublished sixtieth title in the series, kept fans\' hopes up, but the books didn’t see the light of day again until their resurrection by Wizard Books in 2002.  The Wizard editions feature new cover art and a slightly revamped interior layout and are arranged in a different order than the original Puffin releases, also integrating the <a href=\"/Series/15\">Sorcery!</a> spin-off series into the main numbering.  Ibooks, an American publisher, began releasing American versions of the Wizard re-releases in late 2003.  To increase collectors\' confusion, Wizard relaunched the series a second time in 2009 with new changes to format and book order.  For more information, see the entries for <a href=\"/Series/12\">Fighting Fantasy Reissues (Series 1)</a> and <a href=\"/Series/1285\">Fighting Fantasy Reissues (Series 2)</a>.\n\n<p>Several boxed sets (or, more accurately, sets of books in card slipcases) were produced by Puffin during the publication life of the original series. All of these are very rare and highly prized by collectors. To make matters extra-confusing, several of the boxed sets have the same (or similar) names but have different slipcase art and contents.',1),(12,'Fighting Fantasy (2002-2007, Wizard Books Series 1)','This series combines classic volumes from the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series with the <a href=\"/Series/15\">Sorcery!</a> spin-off.  These new editions of the books are in a different order than the originals were; for more information on the original series, visit the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> entry.\n\nNine different boxed set editions of Wizard\'s Series 1 reissues were released: \"Type 1\" versions had box art based on Wizard\'s new book cover art, whilst \"Type 2\" versions came in single-colour boxes with large round FF shield logos. However, the contents of each of the respective Type 1 and 2 boxed sets were identical (ie Sets 1 contained books 1-4, Sets 2 contained books 5-8, Sets 3 contained books 1-8 and the Sorcery boxes each contained all four Sorcery books. A fourth \"Type 1\" box was also produced as an exclusive for The Book People containing books 1-10.\n\n<p>In 2009, the series was relaunched a second time with yet another new ordering.  See <a href=\"/Series/1285\">Fighting Fantasy Reissues (Series 2)</a> for more details.',1),(13,'Dungeons & Dragons','This, the original fantasy role-playing game, was an obvious influence on the development of more complex gamebooks like <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>.  Although Judges Guild put out the <a href=\"/Item/822\">Survival of the Fittest</a> module (playable by a group of characters without a Dungeon Master) in 1979, the game\'s publisher didn\'t produce an official solitaire adventure until 1983, when the red-boxed third edition <a href=\"/Item/823\">Basic Set</a> came out, complete with a brief, untitled solitaire adventure designed to introduce new players to mapping and fighting.  The same year, two complete stand-alone adventures, the \"M Series\" of modules, were released.  These adventures use an invisible ink pen to reveal their text one section at a time, and due to dried-up pens and the chemistry involved, are difficult to find in fully readable condition.  Following the invisible ink modules, several more adventures were released, one using the \"Magic Viewer\" system in which a sheet of red plastic reveals text hidden behind splotches of red ink, others simply printing text in the normal fashion.</a>  \n\n<p>The 1991 release of the game ( <a href=\"/Item/5169\">\"The New Easy to Master Dungeons & Dragons\"</a>) includes a set of cards that act as a tutorial, alternating between introducing the basic rules and concepts of the game and having the reader play out episodes of a dungeon crawl introductory solo adventure. This adventure requires a tabletop map and miniatures (both included with the game) to play. The last D&D solo adventure released by TSR, <a href=\"/Item/825\">Rage of the Rakasta</a>, is barely a solo adventure at all; rather than giving a narrative in which the reader makes choices and turns to different sections of text, it instead features a map with all of the area descriptions from the module.  Players read the description of a room, decide what to do, then turn to the appropriate section of the multiplayer adventure and act as their own Dungeon Masters.  Years after all of these products were out of print, several were revived by Wizards of the Coast as eBooks sold through <a href=\"http://www.rpgnow.com/\">RPGNow.com</a>.',1),(14,'Golden Dragon Fantasy Gamebooks','This series of gamebooks was designed as a direct competitor to <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>, and the similarities are obvious.  Characters start out by rolling values for three attributes (Vigour, Psi and Agility), combat is resolved using six-sided dice, and treasure and useful items are gathered during the course of each adventure.  The system is, if anything, slightly simpler than that of <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>, and each adventure is a bit shorter than the average <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> book.  There is no plot continuity between the books, although the sixth book in the series is actually an expanded and altered version of an adventure first serialized in <a href=\"/Series/538\">White Dwarf</a> magazine. All six volumes were reprinted in the United States with strange new cover art showing weapon-wielding children in heroic poses.  An additional mini-adventure using this series\' game system was published in <a href=\"/Series/1566\">Crunch</a> magazine. \n\n<p>In 2013, five of the six books in this series were re-issued by Fabled Lands Publishing with different updated cover art through Amazon\'s print-on-demand service. The remaining one was re-issued in 2016. In 2023, Book 2 was re-issued in hardback as a \"collector\'s edition\". In 2024, Book 1 was revised with a different title for a \"40th Anniversary Edition\", also simplifying the rules.',1),(15,'Sorcery!','This <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> spin-off was designed to appeal to a slightly more sophisticated audience, and it allows its readers to go on a lengthy mission to recover a valuable artifact known as the Crown of Kings.  In most ways, the books look like thicker, longer <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> adventures.  The critical difference is that players have the choice of playing as either a warrior or a wizard.  When playing as a warrior, the rules are the same as in <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>.  Playing as a wizard, though, adds an interesting new dimension to the game.  There is a list of forty-eight spells (first sold separately as <a href=\"/Item/104\">The Sorcery Spellbook</a> and later included within each gamebook); each spell is accompanied by a three-letter code.  Before starting the adventure, the reader is encouraged to study the spellbook and memorize which codes go with which spells; as an additional challenge, some spells require physical components which must also be remembered.  Once gameplay starts, the spellbook may not be looked at again, and whenever a spell is to be cast, the reader is presented simply with a list of three-letter codes.  Unsurprisingly, picking the wrong code can be disastrous.  This nicely simulates the fantasy convention of wizards having to memorize their spells before adventuring, making the reader\'s job intriguingly similar to that of the character he or she controls and thus making the adventures especially immersive.\r\n\r\n<p>Because of their complexity, the books were originally published by Penguin’s main imprint rather than by their juvenile Puffin imprint.  When Wizard Books began releasing the <a href=\"/Series/853\">Fighting Fantasy Reissues</a>, however, they simply renumbered the main series, integrating the <a href=\"/Series/15\">Sorcery!</a> titles in the process.',1),(16,'2300 AD','This science fiction role-playing game, a revision of the earlier <b>Traveller: 2300</b> system, features an introductory solitaire adventure in the 32-page <i>Play Aids</i> booklet included with the main boxed set.',1),(17,'Adventure Gamebooks','These gamebooks, which have a rather non-descriptive series title, allow the reader to explore past eras of history.  Only two have been published in physical formats in their original English, but translations have led to the publication of the remaining two volumes in other languages.  The remaining English titles were made available as eBooks many years after their original writing.',1),(18,'Ace of Aces','These books simulate aerial combat by using a cross-reference system; each player has a book full of illustrations, and by calling out numbers to one another and turning the pages, the participants are able to battle in the skies.  The first four titles are set during World War I, the fifth during World War II and the sixth in the present.  Rules vary from book to book, with the newer titles generally being more complex than the older ones.  The series was first published by Gameshop, Inc., a company which quickly changed its name to Nova Game Designs.  Over the years, most of the entries in the series have been published in a variety of different editions, some replacing illustrations with photographs and others adding additional rules or aircraft to the mix.  Only the first set was reprinted in the United Kingdom.',1),(19,'Advanced Dungeons & Dragons Third-Party Products','Interestingly enough, the first <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> solitaire adventures were not produced by the game\'s publisher.',1),(20,'Advanced Dungeons & Dragons','Despite being one of the most popular RPGs ever, only one solitaire module has been released for this game system, unless you count third-party products or magazine-published adventures. The single published solo module is designed for use with the first edition of the rules.  A couple of group adventures, also designed for the first edition, were later adapted into <a href=\"/Series/21\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons Adventure Gamebooks</a>.',1),(21,'Advanced Dungeons & Dragons Adventure Gamebooks','This series began life as the <b>Super Endless Quest</b> series, changing its name to <b>Advanced Dungeons & Dragons Super Endless Quest</b> with the third book and dropping the Super Endless Quest part entirely starting with book four.  As the original name suggests, these are more complex companions to the more simplistic <a href=\"/Series/79\">Endless Quest</a> books.  Each book features a pre-created character (printed on a bookmark) which the reader can customize by distributing points among various attributes.  These attribute scores are then added to dice rolls to determine success or failure in various actions during the book.  There are also hit points to keep track of, and some books feature experience points (which can be spent to modify dice rolls), spellcasting, or other special rules.  Most of the books take place in different <a href=\"/Series/20\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons</a> campaign settings, though the fifth volume is set in historic Japan.  Most volumes stand alone with no continuity between them, except for books seven, nine and eleven, which form the <i>Kingdom of Sorcery Trilogy</i>.  Although these three books feature a continuing storyline, no character attributes are carried over from one book to another.',1),(22,'Advanced Dungeons & Dragons Coloring Album','This is primarily a storybook with line drawings to color in, but it also features a game derived from <a href=\"/Series/20\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons</a> which can be played either solitaire or by up to four cooperating players.  A dungeon map with numbered rooms is printed in the center of the book, and numbered passages corresponding with the rooms are scattered throughout at the bottoms of the pages.  A party of four characters is moved through the map, battling monsters until a treasure is found or everyone is killed.  There’s no real plot and little in the way of interactivity (the only real choice to make is where to move next), but some variety is achieved thanks to the fact that different character classes behave differently during combat.',1),(23,'Adventure for You to Share with Rupert, An','This line of simple 32-page gamebooks is based on the long-running British children’s character of Rupert Bear, whose popularity dates back to the twenties.',1),(24,'Adventure Squad, The','This pair of gamebooks, inspired by Enid Blyton novels, allow the reader to make decisions on behalf of the Adventure Squad, a group of mystery-solving children reminiscent of the Famous Five.  While reading the books, the reader must occasionally make a mark on an adventure sheet; these marks indicate whether good or bad decisions were made, and they both affect the future flow of the story and act as a means of assessing performance.',1),(25,'Adventures of Goldhawk','These large-format, short, full-color books were designed to introduce young readers to the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> concept.  The books feature a simplified version of the game system in which there is only one attribute (Skill) which is used both for combat resolution and for keeping track of health.  Readers are also asked to keep track of their supply of gold.  Unlike the main <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> books, these adventures form a continuous storyline in which Prince (later King) Goldhawk goes on various fantasy adventures.  The first two books were published with realistic cover paintings in the United Kingdom and more cartoony cover illustrations in the United States; the last two titles never saw an American release.',1),(26,'1000 Gefahren','This series started out by translating entries from several English series: <a href=\"/Series/28\">The Adventures of You Series</a>, <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a>, <a href=\"/Series/74\">Choose Your Own Adventure for Younger Readers</a> and <a href=\"/Series/89\">Choose Your Own Nightmare</a>.  Ravensburger also published <a href=\"/Series/2843\">English editions of some titles</a>.  One title that appeared in two of the English series, <a href=\"/Item/163\">Journey Under the Sea</a>, ended up getting translated with different titles by two different translators.  Eventually, the series added original German titles; to make matters more confusing, one of these original adventures shares the title of the original <a href=\"/Item/163\">Journey Under the Sea</a> translation! A <a href=\"/Series/2844\">Junior spin-off series</a> was launched in 2021. Note that the numbering below is intended to show release order, but the books themselves are not numbered.',3),(27,'Sugarcane Island','This book, originally part of <a href=\"/Series/28\">The Adventures of You Series</a>, was published as a stand-alone volume on several occasions: as a British hardcover from W. H. Allen, as a German educational tool for English learners (and a companion to the <a href=\"/Series/26\">1000 Gefahren</a> translations), and later as an American paperback during the transition from Archway\'s <a href=\"/Series/28\">The Adventures of You Series</a> to their original <a href=\"/Series/29\">Which Way</a> adventures.',1),(28,'Adventures of You Series, The','Although better known for starting the <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> series, Edward Packard actually began publishing interactive stories with this series.  The books were first published in hardback by Vermont Crossroads Press.  The first title was picked up by Archway and reprinted in paperback, first under the banner of this series, then as a stand-alone title, and finally as part of the <a href=\"/Series/29\">Which Way Books</a>; years later, a revised edition of the story showed up as a <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a>.  The second title reached the <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> series sooner than the first, being the second paperback release in that series.  Both titles were released in 1979 in England by W. H. Allen, while just the first title was released in both German- and English-language editions as part of Ravensburger\'s <a href=\"/Series/26\">1000 Gefahren</a> series.  While not the first books of their type, these adventures are a significant piece of early gamebook history.',1),(29,'Which Way Books','This series is, in a way, an offshoot of the <a href=\"/Series/28\">Adventures of You Series</a> and thus a sibling to <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a>.  A few years after Archway released a paperback edition of the first <a href=\"/Series/28\">Adventures of You</a> book, they introduced this series, a collection of simple branching-plot books.  The books cover a variety of genres and are distinguished mainly by their often very brief text passages between choices.  A couple of titles further distinguished themselves by featuring puzzles that made them slightly more game-oriented than the rest of the books.  The series eventually ended up containing yet another reprint of that first <a href=\"/Series/28\">Adventures of You</a> title, and it was ultimately successful enough to acquire a license to include <a href=\"/Category/40\">Star Trek</a> adventures and to spawn several spin-offs (the <a href=\"/Series/170\">Follow Your Heart Romances</a>, the <a href=\"/Series/306\">Play-It-Your-Way Sports Books</a>, the <a href=\"/Series/310\">Super Powers Which Way Books</a> featuring DC superheroes, and the <a href=\"/Series/491\">Which Way Secret Door Books</a> for younger readers).  Some (but not all) volumes were reprinted in England by Carousel.',1),(30,'Choose Your Own Adventure (1979-1998)','With close to two hundred titles, <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> is by far the longest-running gamebook series.  It grew out of Edward Packard\'s <a href=\"/Series/28\">Adventures of You Series</a>, with its first manifestation being Lippincott\'s hardback release of <a href=\"/Item/333\">Deadwood City</a> and <a href=\"/Item/332\">The Third Planet from Altair</a> which, while not yet officially part of a series, featured the phrase \"Choose Your Own Adventure\" on the covers.  However, it was when Bantam acquired the rights to release the books in paperback starting in 1979 that the series\' full potential was realized, and the books almost single-handedly started the American gamebook boom of the eighties.  They made less of an impression in England, where <a href=\"/Item/332\">The Third Planet from Altair</a> was retitled <i>Exploration Infinity</i> and included in the single-volume <a href=\"/Series/71\">Plot-It-Yourself Adventures in Space</a> series and <a href=\"/Item/333\">Deadwood City</a> was released as a stand-alone hardback and paperback, but the majority of titles were available only as imports.\r\n\r\n<p>The books almost entirely avoid gimmickry, so there’s never any coin-flipping or dice-rolling to confuse the flow of reading and making choices.  The books cover an extremely broad array of subject matter, but there is some continuity between certain books in the series.  Some stories follow straight on from each other, while others feature recurring characters (like the adventurous Dr. Nera Vivaldi) or themes (as in a long sequence of titles revolving around martial arts).\r\n\r\n<p>There were several major spin-offs from the series.  The first was <a href=\"/Series/74\">Choose Your Own Adventure for Younger Readers</a>, which simply takes the format and tailors it for a younger audience.  Taking this idea to a further extreme inspired the <a href=\"/Series/561\">Your First Adventure</a> series and the <a href=\"/Series/87\">Choose Your Own Adventure – Walt Disney</a> books.  Going the other way and making the books longer led to <a href=\"/Series/86\">Choose Your Own Adventure Super Adventure</a>, while an experiment in more solid story arcs and recurring characters led to <a href=\"/Series/83\">Choose Your Own Adventure – Passport</a> and <a href=\"/Series/85\">Choose Your Own Adventure – Space Hawks</a>.  An effort to ride the success of R. L. Stine\'s Goosebumps books inspired <a href=\"/Series/89\">Choose Your Own Nightmare</a>, and lucrative license agreements made possible <a href=\"/Series/88\">Choose Your Own Adventure – The Young Indiana Jones Chronicles</a> and <a href=\"/Series/90\">Choose Your Own Star Wars Adventure</a>, the last series of books to bear the <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> name until the label was revived almost a decade later for the <a href=\"/Series/870\">Choose Your Own Adventure Reissues</a> and <a href=\"/Series/871\">Interactive Movies</a>.\r\n\r\n<p>The books in the series have been released in a variety of forms.  The most common are Bantam\'s editions, most of which are the standard size for small paperbacks, though the last nine titles in the series were released in a larger format more reminiscent of the <a href=\"/Series/74\">Choose Your Own Adventure for Younger Readers</a> books.  In addition to the previously mentioned Lippincott hardbacks, various titles can be found in large print hardback format thanks to the efforts of Grey Castle Press and Gareth Stevens Publishing.  The books are listed below in the order of their paperback publication, as this is the sequence by which they are numbered; however, in terms of chronological release, book eight came first, then book seven, then the rest.',1),(31,'Adventures on Tékumel','Although the original <b>Empire of the Petal Throne</b> role-playing game never featured any solitaire adventures, this follow-up game, featuring a new system but the same setting, made solitaire gaming a part of character creation.  The first book, <a href=\"/Item/164\">Growing Up on Tékumel</a>, guides the reader through the character creation process.  Once a character is created, it can be given some experience by working through a series of adventures in the following three books, which appear to have been originally intended to be one thick volume rather than three slim booklets.  Some sections are repeated between the three booklets in order to help them stand alone if the player doesn\'t own a complete set.',1),(32,'Alien Adventures','These mild science fiction gamebooks, linked together only by theme, were released as one of Troll’s four <a href=\"/Category/57\">The Choice is Yours</a> sets.',1),(33,'Alien Star','This science fiction gaming magazine contained the occasional solitaire adventure designed for use with the <a href=\"/Category/71\">Traveller</a> role-playing game.',1),(34,'Amazing Stories','Around the time that these gamebooks came out, TSR had acquired the rights to the classic science fiction magazine of the same name, and a television series based on the magazine was on television.  The gamebooks were designed to cash in on both of these things.  The books are as thick as some of TSR\'s more complex releases like the <a href=\"/Series/21\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons Adventure Gamebooks</a>, but they feature no game systems and have extremely long stretches of text (often dozens of pages) between choices.  The stories told in the series are along the lines of those published in the magazine: pulpish adventure, horror and sci-fi tales.  One title, the fourth in the series, is actually an interactive revision of a previously-published linear novel.  For the most part, storytelling takes precedence over gameplay in all of the books, though some offer more meaningful decisions than others.',1),(35,'American Girl, The','This is an amateur magazine.  The third issue is an interactive retelling of its author’s trip overseas.  It is published in a small format that is obviously hand-made with the help of a photocopier, a stapler, scissors and some sort of hand stamp.  Some pages fold out to reveal more information.  I don\'t know if any other issues feature interactivity.',1),(36,'Animorphs Alternamorphs','These adventures allow the reader to join the heroes of the popular <i>Animorphs</i> series of novels and television programs.  The books are somewhat unusual in format, having numbered chapters but choices that direct the reader to specific page numbers; sometimes several chapters go by between choices.',1),(37,'Arcade Explorers','This series of gamebooks distinguishes itself from the crowd in a rather unusual way: as each adventure unfolds, the reader is prompted at various times to type lines of code in the BASIC programming language into his or her computer (an Apple II, Commodore 64 or Radio Shack Color Computer).  By the end of the story, these lines of code make up a complete computer game which the reader can  play; depending on the choices made, different aspects of the game will vary.  A text-based equivalent to the game is also included for readers without access to a computer.',1),(38,'Asterix Adventure Games','These books use the format initiated in <a href=\"/Series/39\">Enid Blyton\'s Famous 5 Adventure Games</a> to adapt the adventures of R. Goscinny\'s comic book character Asterix the Gaul in his battles against the Romans.  The books are written in the third person and come in plastic sleeves full of extra equipment.  The equipment varies from book to book, but it includes a custom six-sided die, maps, a variety of cards (including translations of words used in the text and sketches of various characters, among other things), a \"password scroll\" plastic overlay used to reveal phrases hidden in jumbles of letters, and cardboard wheels used to keep track of potion measures and gold remaining.   As with other gamebooks that include such extras, these are a challenge to collect.',1),(39,'Enid Blyton\'s Famous 5 Adventure Games','This series, adapted from some of Enid Blyton\'s popular British children\'s books, allow the reader to make decisions while reading a third-person story about the Famous Five, a group of young detectives.  Like most Stephen Thraves books, these distinguish themselves by including a lot of flashy extras.  Each book comes in a plastic sleeve that holds a special six-sided die with the silhouette of a different character on each side (plus one side marked \"Mystery\"), a small map and a variety of cards.  Two slotted pieces of cardboard designed to hold the cards are also provided, one illustrated with a lunchbox, the other with a rucksack.  At the start of the game, the player takes the lunchbox and rucksack, fills the lunchbox with three picnic cards (which are used like hit points) and chooses a single item of equipment (a map, torch, code book or measure) to put in the rucksack.  Additional equipment can be discovered during play, and these items are used in different ways.  \r\n\r\n<p>Different books contain different assortments of equipment, and some of the items are identical between multiple books, while others (mainly the code book and the map) vary from title to title.  The map and the measure (marked in both paces and meters) are fairly self-explanatory.  The torch and the binoculars are actually clear pieces of plastic with opaque patterns on them; when placed upon certain jumbles of letters in the books, they reveals messages.  The compass is similar to the torch and binoculars, but it reveals a number from a ring of numbers rather than a message from a mass of letters.  The code books allow secret messages in the text to be decoded.  All of this equipment makes these books something of a challenge to collect, as they are difficult to find without dice or other components missing.  Additionally, they have been reprinted many times, so the true completist will be challenged to find every existing variation of front and back cover designs.  Even the dice changed over time, with some having more rounded corners than others.',1),(40,'Astérix - Alea jacta est!','These large-format, graphics-heavy gamebooks are based on the popular comic book series, casting the reader as a character named Justforkix.  Justforkix has three attributes (Fighting Fitness, Skill and Charm) whose initial values are determined by distribution of points, he gains secondary aptitudes as the game progresses, and he may carry up to five items and an unlimited amount of money.  He also has several doses of magic potion which may be consumed to automatically win fights or succeed at tests of skill.',2),(41,'Asterix - Alea Jacta Est','This is one of the very few gamebook series to be translated from another language into English, and only three of the four original titles ended up being released.',1),(42,'Australian Adventure Gamebooks','This series was published by the Australian arm of Penguin under their Puffin label.  The books were intended to stand alone but to all have the common theme of Australian settings.  Unfortunately, only one book was actually published before the series was discontinued; however, more than a decade after its publication, the author of one of the intended follow-up volumes released his work on the Internet as a free download (and later as a Kindle product).  The first book is a time travel adventure while the second is an interactive horror tale; both have relatively basic game systems.  The first book requires that the reader keep track of a limited inventory and pay attention for clues, but that’s about it; the second book also has a limited inventory but also adds the need to track wounds and select skills.',1),(43,'Autoduel Quarterly','Although originally intended solely as a board game, <a href=\"/Series/61\">Car Wars</a> eventually took on a role-playing element, and while its official magazine was in print, it occasionally included a solitaire adventure in its pages using the board game for combat resolution.\n\n<p>All of the issues of this magazine have been digitized and are available for sale online through the publisher.',1),(44,'AutoVentures','This series of adventures are designed to work with several different highway battle game systems: <a href=\"/Series/61\">Car Wars</a>, <b>Highway 2000</b> and <b>BattleCars</b>.  Each adventure includes rules for using the module with different game systems, the adventure itself and required accessories like car counters and maps.  Only solitaire adventures are listed below, but the series also included several modules requiring a game master for play.',1),(45,'Back to the Future','This series of tiny 16-page booklets was distributed in cereal boxes in England; each contains only a couple of inconsequential choices, and all together, they retell an abbreviated version of the plot of the first <i>Back to the Future</i> film.  The booklets are illustrated with photographs and their authors are not given credit.',1),(46,'Batman Role-Playing Game','This basic role-playing game is a spin-off from <a href=\"/Series/102\">DC Heroes</a>.',1),(47,'Battle Quest','These fantasy gamebooks follow the component-heavy format initiated by the <a href=\"/Series/39\">Enid Blyton\'s Famous 5 Adventure Games</a>.  Each book comes with a variety of components used during play.  These collections are fairly similar between the two volumes, with both including a pair of special dice used for combat, a treasure counter wheel, a strength rating counter wheel and a trance spell overlay used to reveal text in special parts of the book.  The first book also includes an infra-red power overlay, a \"book of wisdom\" card containing hints, and a scroll card containing passwords; the second book instead provides a foresight power overlay, a \"book of ciphers\" card and a scroll card decrypting various codes.  Players start the game with a strength of six and without any treasure or equipment; the various special items are brought into play as the story unfolds.',1),(48,'BattleBooks','These full-color comics actually form a combat game nearly identical to the <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds Fantasy Combat Books</a>.  The comic book format allows the artists to insert speech bubbles for flavor, and the character sheets are smaller, but otherwise the rules are the same as in <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds</a>.  The series was introduced with the release of limited-edition \"blue print\" (non-colored) versions of the first nine characters; full-color versions (the \"Streets of Fire\" series) with different covers, along with additional characters, soon followed.  After the main line of <a href=\"/Series/48\">BattleBooks</a> was discontinued, new versions of the <a href=\"/Item/243\">Shi</a> and <a href=\"/Item/244\">Wolverine</a> books were released as the \"Judgment Night\" series.  The original <a href=\"/Item/228\">Shi</a> book actually ended up having three versions: the \"blue print\" version plus two different color editions, one with a Ross cover, the other with a Tucci cover.  The <a href=\"/Item/240\">Darkchylde</a> book had two versions: one with a Queen cover and one with a Tucci cover.',1),(49,'Lost Worlds Fantasy Combat Books','These visual combat books, a successor to <a href=\"/Series/18\">Ace of Aces</a>, each represent a fantasy warrior.  The books are all compatible with one another, so any two fighters can face off; however, there is no guarantee that the fight will be fair, as some characters are much tougher than others.  To play, two people each choose a character, taking the character sheet for that character and giving the corresponding books to their opponents.  Each character sheet provides some basic statistics (height, body points and attacks) and lists all of the moves that a player\'s character may make.  Each turn, the players choose their moves and call them out to each other.  By cross-referencing these moves with their own in the books they are holding and then turning the page, players are then given a picture of their opponent\'s current position along with an indication of whether or not a wound has been scored and restrictions on the next turn’s moves.  Wounds are subtracted from body points, and when a character runs out of body points, it is defeated.  Experience points are awarded to the victor, and in campaign play, it is possible for characters to grow in strength over time.\r\n\r\n<p>Over the years, a variety of gimmicks have been added to make the game more interesting.  Some characters can cast spells or perform other special actions represented by cards.  The earliest cards were included with certain books or sold in separate packages.   Later books even include randomly inserted collectible cards representing spells, equipment and special moves which may be used to even up battles between poorly matched opponents or simply spice up battles.  Some of these cards were also sold in special packs.  There were also some kits released which, with the help of molded plastic dungeon floors, allowed the combat books to be used with miniatures for tactical combat and role-playing.\r\n\r\n<p>The series was first published by Nova Game Designs, then by Chessex and finally by Greysea.  Flying Buffalo, Nutmeg Games and 1% Inspiration Games also published some additional items under license.  Emithill reproduced some of the early Nova releases for English audiences; these British editions look exactly like the American ones (Nova logo and all), but the cover graphics are slightly less sharp and the back cover features a white box near the middle containing the text \"Printed under license by Emithill Ltd., Manchester, England.\"',1),(50,'Battletech Science Fiction Combat Books','These <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds</a> style books are based on the <b>Battletech</b> miniatures board game.  Each book represents a different giant robot (called a BattleMech), and any two of them may battle one another.  The rules are essentially the same as <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds</a> but considerably more complex.  As before, players simultaneously decide on their actions, then use cross-referencing and page-turning to find the results.  However, while doing this, it is also necessary to keep track of the \'Mech\'s heat level and ammunition, and there are additional rules for allowing the \'Mech\'s pilot to gain in experience and skill over the course of several battles.  Terrain cards can optionally be used to add even further detail to the game.',1),(51,'Beyond the Labyrinth','This young adult novel features a gamebook-playing protagonist and has two different endings.  A die roll at the end of the book determines which ending is read.',1),(52,'Biggles Adventure Games','These gamebooks use the format of the earlier <a href=\"/Series/39\">Enid Blyton’s Famous 5 Adventure Games</a> to create gamebook adaptations of Captain W. E. Johns’ Biggles novels.  Each book is written in the third person, with the reader influencing the heroic airman’s decisions from afar.  The books are accompanied by a variety of special items used during play.  Both books have custom dice, maps, binoculars, compass cards and fuel gauge cards.  The first book also includes a torch card while the second instead provides a codebook card.  Players start play with only one piece of equipment, and they must keep an eye on their fuel gauges as they go.  Many of the equipment cards are plastic overlays used to reveal messages in the text, and they are identical to the ones included in the <a href=\"/Series/39\">Enid Blyton’s Famous 5 Adventure Games</a>.',1),(53,'Blackstone\'s Magic Adventure','These books, featuring famous magician Harry Blackstone, are straightforward multiple-ending mysteries written by multiple authors all using the pen name of Milo Dennison.  Aside from this eccentricity, the only unusual feature of the books is the inclusion of instructions for performing magic tricks.  The books were followed by the very similar <a href=\"/Series/54\">Blackstone\'s Magical Adventure</a> books for younger readers.',1),(54,'Blackstone\'s Magical Adventure','This series is nearly identical to <a href=\"/Series/53\">Blackstone’s Magic Adventure</a>, but it is written at a more basic reading level and aimed at a slightly younger audience.',1),(55,'Dragon Warriors','Although initially this multi-player role-playing game did not feature any solitaire adventures, the original edition was published in a gamebook-like paperback format and eventually inspired the <a href=\"/Series/56\">Blood Sword</a> gamebooks, so it has often been viewed fondly by gamebook fans. In 2008, Magnum Opus released a revised and expanded second edition through Mongoose Publishing. In 2011, the rights were transferred to Serpent King Games, which reprinted the Magnum Opus editions in electronic, paperback and hardback format and also added new titles. In 2021, Red Ruin Publishing started to issue solitaire adventures in pdf-format, which are freely available through DriveThruRPG.com. These are labelled as belonging to a series called \"Tales of Red Ruin\".',1),(56,'Blood Sword','These thick gamebooks are set in the world of the authors\' <a href=\"/Series/55\">Dragon Warriors</a> role-playing game, which was published in a gamebook-like paperback format by Corgi, though they do not require knowledge of that game for play.  The series distinguishes itself from the masses in a couple of notable ways.  First of all, it supports up to four players; a single person may play with a powerful character, or a team of four people may play with a diversified group of less powerful characters (a Warrior, an Enchanter, a Sage and a Trickster).  When multiple players participate, a single person is designated as the reader, and he or she must read the book aloud to the group; however, at certain points, different characters have to read sections to themselves privately, giving different knowledge to different people.  Another innovation is the inclusion of a particularly tactical combat system.  Whenever there is a fight, a map is provided showing where the players and their foes are located, and players must resolve the fight using counters or miniatures. \n\n<p>On 20 August 2014, the first four books were re-issued by Fabled Lands Publishing with different updated cover art through Amazon\'s print-on-demand service. The fifth book was finally published on 10 June 2019 after a successful Kickstarter campaign.\n\n<p>Book 1 was also further re-issued in a deluxe hardcover edition by Megara Entertainment with new colour artwork alongside the original black-and-white illustrations.',1),(57,'Boot Hill','This was a Western role-playing game released by TSR, the original publisher of <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a>.  Over the years, three different (largely incompatible) editions of the game were released: a 1975 debut, a 1979 revision and a 1990 total rewrite.  Only the second edition spawned a solitaire adventure, which was sold separately as a supplement to the rules in the game\'s main boxed set.',1),(58,'Bounty Hunter','This is a one-off combat set similar to <a href=\"/Series/18\">Ace of Aces</a> but with a Western gunfighting theme.',1),(59,'Breakout!','This is an Australian gaming magazine.',1),(60,'Call of Cthulhu','This popular role-playing game has spawned multiple solitaire adventures, some released by third-party publishers.',1),(61,'Car Wars','This board game featured sufficient role-playing elements to lead to several solitaire adventures being published for it.  Most appeared in <a href=\"/Series/43\">Autoduel Quarterly</a> magazine, and one of these was later expanded into a boxed set.',1),(62,'Car Wars Adventure Gamebooks','Through an interesting collaboration with Steve Jackson Games, these books are based on the popular <a href=\"/Series/61\">Car Wars</a> board game, with at least one (the fifth book) being an expansion of a previously-released adventure from <a href=\"/Series/43\">Autoduel Quarterly</a> magazine.  The books, like the game, are set in a grim, violent future in which \"autoduelling\" in modified, heavily-armed vehicles is as good a way as any to make a living.  Although the books are fairly similar to the <a href=\"/Series/21\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons Adventure Gamebooks</a> in format since they feature point-based character creation, action resolution with six-sided dice and character sheet bookmarks, they are actually among TSR’s most complex gamebook releases due to a detailed vehicular combat system which features multiple damage locations, critical hits and the need to track ammunition.',1),(63,'Carmen Sandiego','These gamebooks follow the formula of the popular computer games that inspired them.  Each book contains four cases, and in each case, an object has been stolen, and the reader must retrieve it by using clues to travel from place to place (and in some cases, from time to time) while also trying to identify the criminal.  Each book contains tear-out cards representing criminals and locations to visit; these are useful for ruling out possibilities.  Additional cards are used as character sheets on which clues may be recorded and move points tallied.  Move points are added every time the reader moves from section to section, and the end score is higher if the case is completed in a smaller number of moves.',1),(64,'Carmen Sandiego - Mark & See','This simple series, aimed at young readers, requires players to use a special red marker to reveal hidden images on its pages; these then influence decision-making as several stolen treasures are tracked through time.  A set of pull-out cards are also included.  The series, which fails to give credit to its authors or illustrators, also includes another title, <i>Where in the World is Carmen Sandiego?</i>, which is a linear story featuring no branching-plot elements and thus is not listed here.  A series of <a href=\"/Series/63\">Carmen Sandiego</a> books for older readers was also released; these all featured interactive elements and pull-out cards, but none of them used invisible ink.',1),(65,'Endless Quest Books: Crimson Crystal Adventures','This spin-off of the <a href=\"/Series/79\">Endless Quest</a> series featured the same basic format but added one significant twist: each book included a clear sheet of red plastic.  This \"Crimson Crystal\" was used to reveal images hidden behind blobs of red ink in various sections of each adventure.  Unfortunately, this gimmick has little or no impact on plot and gameplay, though it does give this series a slightly more game-like feel than its completely straightforward parent.  Three of the books take place in the <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> setting (or something remarkably like it), though one adventure seems to be a generic science fiction tale unrelated to any of TSR\'s role-playing settings (though <b>Star Frontiers</b> would probably be the closest match).',1),(66,'Castle of Grom Trilogy, The','This trilogy of fantasy gamebooks was designed as an aid to reading instruction; it was followed by the science fiction series <a href=\"/Series/439\">Thrilling Adventures in Space</a>.',1),(67,'Castle Thrax','This is a \"group solo\" fantasy adventure designed for a group of role-players who have no game master.\r\n',1),(68,'Catacombs Solo Quest','These large-format gamebooks were one of TSR\'s last forays into the form.  Each book contains about 150 pages and numerous illustrations.  Three of the books are set in various <a href=\"/Series/20\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons</a> settings, while two (including the unpublished fifth volume) are inspired by the <b>Top Secret/S.I.</b> role-playing game.  Rules vary from book to book, but each volume includes some extra items used during play (multiple character bookmarks for the <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> adventures and a flap full of \"clues\" like computer printouts and maps for the <b>Top Secret</b> book) and a combat system.  The <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> adventures use twelve-sided dice while the <b>Top Secret</b> one uses ten-sided ones; in either case, dice rolls are printed on the bottoms of the pages so that players without dice may flip through the book at random to generate numbers.  The <b>Top Secret</b> book features the most complex rules, with the option of creating a new character (consisting of randomly-rolled attributes, skills whose values are derived from those attributes, and \"advantages\" chosen using a point distribution system) instead of using the pregenerated one.  The second book in the series was released as a free eBook by Wizards of the Coast years after its original publication.',1),(69,'Challenge Magazine','This role-playing magazine published three solitaire adventures, one for the original <a href=\"/Series/232\">Star Wars Role-Playing Game</a>, one based on <b>Warhammer 40,000</b> and one for <b>Space: 1889</b>.  It also included many non-interactive short stories, several of which were adapted into one of the <a href=\"/Series/506\">Shadowrun Novels</a>.',1),(70,'Choice Adventures','This series, written in the third person, follows the adventures of the Ringers, a group of Christian children.  Unlike the vast majority of books of this type, the stories begin without any explanatory material suggesting that reading the stories from cover to cover would be unwise.',1),(71,'Plot-It-Yourself Adventures in Space','This British series consisted only of a retitled paperback reprint of the original edition of <a href=\"/Item/332\">The Third Planet from Altair</a>, one of the two hardbacks that established the <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> series.  The book features the same interior artwork as the original hardback, but it has a new cover illustration.',1),(72,'Football Fantasy','The publisher of this series claims that these are \"the first, and only, interactive sports books in the world.\"  This is not exactly true; there have been sports gamebooks ever since 1977\'s <a href=\"/Item/2655\">Action Football</a>.  However, these do seem to be the first gamebooks to apply the graphical, multiplayer format of series like the <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds Fantasy Combat Books</a> to team sports.  \n\n<p>As with <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds</a>, each book consists mostly of illustrations, and gameplay consists of two players examining these pictures, choosing from several available options, calling out their choices nearly simultaneously, and then cross-referencing tables to see what happens.  Each player controls an eleven-person team; the books prevent this from becoming confusing by always focusing on the player with the ball.  Each illustrated page depicts the player with the ball or the nearest defending player(s) along with a diagram showing the ball\'s position on the playing field and icons for six possible actions such as running, tackling, passing, blocking or diving.  Actions are chosen from a larger pool of possibilities and the particular actions presented vary from page to page depending on positioning and other circumstances.\n\n<p>The rules offer a lot of options so that players can enjoy the game exactly the way they want to.  The end of a match can be determined by reaching a set number of goals or by playing for a set period of time.  For more complex play, there is an advanced game in which certain players are not allowed to make certain moves even when they are presented on the page; this adds more strategy and makes the differences between players and teams more clear than in the basic game, where all presented moves may always be chosen.  For solitaire play, a random letter table is included which can be used to automatically pick actions for an unpredictable opponent.  This works with either the basic or advanced rules.  Further game variants, such as tournament guidelines, can be found on the series\' official website.\n\n<p>Although these books are more about gameplay and graphics than text, they do have a stronger textual element than most visual gamebooks thanks to the inclusion of ten pages of fictional team histories and biographies following each volume\'s rules.\n\n<p>Wizard ran out of funding before the series could be completed meaning Derwent Town and Tame Wanderers were never released.',1),(73,'Tortured Souls','This British fanzine contained solitaire adventures for first-edition <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> in several issues along with more traditional multi-player modules and some one-on-one adventures for a single player and a single DM.  The solitaire adventures allowed a single player to run multiple characters.',1),(74,'Choose Your Own Adventure for Younger Readers','This <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> spin-off is aimed at ages seven through nine.  Apart from having larger print, fewer pages and simpler writing, the books follow the same basic format as their parent series, covering a wide variety of genres and avoiding any unusual gameplay gimmicks.   After this one, Bantam published a further spin-off series aimed at even younger readers, called <a href=\"/Series/561\">Your First Adventure</a>.  Although most books were released as paperbacks, some early titles were initially released in hardback by Bantam and a handful more were reissued in hardback by the Weekly Reader Book Club.',1),(75,'Laser Books','This series of science fiction novels contains one book which was later adapted into a gamebook in the <a href=\"/Series/34\">Amazing Stories</a> series.',1),(76,'Choose Your Own Adventure Software Series','',1),(77,'Choose Your Own Adventure Television Special','',1),(78,'Time Machine','This series, developed by Byron Preiss (the group also responsible for <a href=\"/Series/6\">Be an Interplanetary Spy</a> and <a href=\"/Series/143\">Explorer</a>), offers a balance of education and entertainment.  The reader is a time traveler, and each volume offers a different mystery that must be solved by exploring a particular historical period (or, in the case of one book, the future).  Each book begins with the rules of time travel and a mission briefing explaining the period to be explored, and some also require the reader to select one or more items to bring along.  Whether or not the reader\'s decision is involved, an equipment list is provided.  The adventures are each structured with only one ending, so the challenge is not in surviving but simply in reaching the conclusion as efficiently as possible; unfortunately, in some of the adventures requiring inventory selection, it is possible for an ill-equipped player to get stuck in an infinite loop and never be able to complete the book.  The series has an appealing visual design, with each set of choices looking like an instrument panel with buttons; as a nice touch, some choices have hints associated with them, and a special icon on the buttons indicates when the reader may check the hint page.\n\n<p>The first twenty-four volumes of the series were printed as numbered silver paperbacks and were released fairly regularly; the twenty-fifth book was released more than a year after the final silver book and was a slightly longer unnumbered \"special edition.\"  At least some of the books were given alternate printings for the British market which look very similar, but not identical, to the American versions -- most notably, volume 9, for which the British printing has a number on the spine but the American printing does not. The most obvious way to differentiate between the versions is to look at the verso of the title page, which says \"Printed and bound in Great Britain by Cox & Wyman Ltd., Reading\" in place of the more common \"Printed in the United States of America.\"\n\n<p>In the midst of its run, the series also spawned a spin-off for younger readers called <a href=\"/Series/335\">Time Traveler</a>.\n\n<p>After more than a decade out of print, all but two of the books (but not the <a href=\"/Series/335\">Time Traveler</a> spin-off) were re-released as downloadable eBooks in 2001.',1),(79,'Endless Quest','These books were one of the most successful American series to follow in the footsteps of <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> and were the first of many interactive book offerings to be published by TSR.  Each adventure is based on one of TSR\'s role-playing games or other licenses, with most taking place in one of the worlds of <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> but others using the settings of <b>Gamma World</b>, <b>Star Frontiers</b>, <b>Top Secret</b> or even the worlds of familiar pulp literary characters Conan and Tarzan.  Despite their role-playing origins, the books completely avoid game mechanics, with no dice-rolling or character statistics to be found.  There is, however, a certain touch of role-playing to be found in the fact that each book casts the reader as a specific character with a defined past rather than following the more common gamebook tradition of attempting to keep reader identity as generic as possible.  More complex gameplay was later introduced in the <a href=\"/Series/21\">Super Endless Quest</a> books (which were quickly renamed the <a href=\"/Series/21\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons Adventure Gamebooks</a>) while simplified stories for younger readers were released in the <a href=\"/Series/151\">Fantasy Forest</a> line.\n\n<p>The <a href=\"/Series/79\">Endless Quest</a> books were released in four distinct series; the first thirty-six books were released by TSR during the eighties and featured fairly large type and a format identical to that of the <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> books.  This format was carried over to two spin-offs (the gimmicky <a href=\"/Series/65\">Endless Quest Books: Crimson Crystal Adventures</a> and the romance-oriented <a href=\"/Series/196\">HeartQuest</a> books) as well as the <a href=\"/Series/221\">Lazer Tag Adventures</a> series.  \n\n<p>A second set of Endless Quest books was released in the nineties by Wizards of the Coast. These books differed from their eighties predecessors in several notable ways: their text was smaller and they featured text sections numbered independently from the actual pages (thus following the layout scheme used by <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> rather than <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> though still retaining the rules-free simplicity of the latter series). Moreover, the settings for this second series were based on a different set of games (mostly <a href=\"/Series/20\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons</a> campaign settings, but also the <b>Gamma World</b> and <b>Amazing Engine</b> RPGs, the <b>DragonStrike</b> board game, and the never-released <b>WildSpace</b> board game). Finally, the volumes themselves were not assigned official numbers, though they are numbered (starting with thirty-seven) in the list below for convenience.\n\n<p>The series was relaunched by Mirrorstone in the 2000s with limited success. This reissued series included one book from the eighties series (<a href=\"/Item/932\">Claw of the Dragon</a>), alongside a rebranded book from the <a href=\"/Series/65\">Crimson Crystal Adventures</a> series (see <a href=\"/Series/1225\">Endless Quest Reissues</a>). \n\n<p>The final release of the series by Wizards of the Coast consists of six original titles (not officially numbered, but listed as books 50-55 below). These books, which were printed in full color, are all based on the <a href=\"/Category/67\">Dungeons and Dragons</a> game and set in the <b>Forgotten Realms</b> and <b>Ravenloft</b> universes.\n\n<p>In addition to the above releases, two books from the eighties series (<a href=\"/Item/906\">Villains of Volturnus</a> and <a href=\"/Item/915\">Captive Planet</a>, both based on the <b>Star Frontiers</b> RPG) were made freely available over the Internet several years after their original publication. These versions (which can only be read in a web browser) were released by a fan website with permission from Wizards of the Coast.',1),(80,'Your AMAZING Adventures','These fantasy gamebooks feature a strange form of interactivity; rather than making decisions, the reader solves mazes.  Depending on where he or she comes out of each maze, a different path through the story is taken.  Even though the reader has no conscious influence on the storyline, the books do feature some continuity, all being set in the same world and containing some recurring characters.  At book five, the name of the series changed to <b>Your MAZE Adventures</b>, and all of the books are part of the <a href=\"/Category/76\">What-Do-I-Do-Now Books</a> collection that also includes the two <a href=\"/Series/53\">Blackstone</a> series and the <a href=\"/Series/311\">Zork</a> books.',1),(81,'Deviens conquérant de l\'impossible','These gamebooks are based on the adventure novels of Philippe Ebly.  The reader, as hero Serge,  is accompanied by three friends on each adventure and makes decisions with the help of their advice.  It is necessary to keep track of the Skill and Hit Points of all four characters and to collect an inventory of items along the way.',2),(82,'Give Yourself Goosebumps','After the monumental success of R. L. Stine\'s <b>Goosebumps</b> children\'s horror novels, this interactive spin-off was created.  Stine was no stranger to gamebooks, and many of these adventures are similar in feel to the earlier <a href=\"/Series/319\">Twistaplot</a> series, being relatively straightforward branching-plot novels occasionally enlivened by a puzzle or gimmicky choice.  Although all of the books are credited to R. L. Stine, it seems likely that many were ghostwritten by other authors, since style and quality varies wildly from book to book.  All of the plots are rather silly, but some adventures have surprisingly sophisticated game design while others are comparatively amateurish and directionless.  The first twenty-two adventures were released with colorful prismatic patterns on the front, but the remainder of the series had more traditional (and presumably less expensive) covers.  The series spawned one spin-off: <a href=\"/Series/184\">Give Yourself Goosebumps Special Edition</a>, which contained some books featuring more complex gameplay mechanisms.',1),(83,'Choose Your Own Adventure - Passport','This short-lived <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> spin-off casts the reader as part of a trio of young reporters traveling the world in search of interesting stories.  Apart from recurring main characters and a slightly educational flavor, there is little to set these books apart from the main series.',1),(84,'Choose Your Own Ending','This series of books, clearly modeled on <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> but aimed at an adult audience, was intended to feature a variety of interactive storylines in multiple genres.  Unfortunately, it appears that its small publisher stopped printing adventures after only one volume was released.',1),(85,'Choose Your Own Adventure - Space Hawks','This <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> spin-off casts the reader as a member of the Space Hawks, an elite group of space pilots.  It’s main distinguishing characteristic is the fact that there is a running story arc through the books; in the first volume, the reader qualifies to join the Space Hawks, then in the subsequent volumes, he or she helps to fend off various threats.  Although the other <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> series are not completely devoid of continuity between books, this series relies a lot more heavily on it.',1),(86,'Choose Your Own Adventure Super Adventure','This two-book <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> spin-off isn’t really very different from its parent series; the only differences are that these books are longer by about fifty pages and the first adventure is extra challenging, having only one successful path through.  The two books aren’t related to each other in terms of plot, nor do they have any obvious connections to any of the adventures in the main series.  Considering the short life of this series, it seems that it was not a particularly successful experiment.',1),(87,'Choose Your Own Adventure - Walt Disney','These thin, large-format hardbacks allow the reader to go on adventures with characters from various Disney films.  The books are illustrated throughout in full color, but the artists involved are not given credit.',1),(88,'Choose Your Own Adventure - Young Indiana Jones Chronicles','This series uses the <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> format to tell stories inspired by the <i>Young Indiana Jones Chronicles</i> television series, allowing the reader to control the actions of Indiana Jones.  The books are set between 1908 and 1920, with an even split between those set during Indiana Jones\' childhood and those during his teenage years.  Various well-known historic events are portrayed, and each volume includes a lengthy glossary and a list of suggestions for further reading, making these the most educational books in the <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> family.',1),(89,'Choose Your Own Nightmare (1995-1997)','This spin-off of <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> features larger format books like the last few from the main series.  It is likely that they were released to exploit the enormous success of juvenile horror novels at the time of their publication.  The text on the back of some of the earlier books in the series (\"Give yourself goosebumps...choose your own nightmare...\") seems a fairly obvious jab at <a href=\"/Series/82\">the competition</a>. All of the original titles were reprinted in hardcover by Gareth Stevens. More than a decade after the series ended, a <a href=\"/Series/1622\">second series</a> with the same name but different titles began.',1),(90,'Choose Your Own Star Wars Adventure','These simple gamebooks were published at about the same time as the original <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> series came to an end.  Each features a hologram on its cover and contains an interactive adaptation of one of the original <i>Star Wars</i> films.  Apart from the fancy covers and popular license, these adventures have no particularly unusual features.',1),(91,'Coach Your Own Football Team','This simple gamebook allows the reader to make critical decisions during a football game.  There is no game system, but there is some useful material prior to the start of play which provides player statistics and information on various play strategies and formations.',1),(92,'Combat Command','At the same time that Tor Books was publishing its <a href=\"/Series/97\">Crossroads</a> series of interactive novels based on popular fantasy series, Ace produced these books, each of which is set in the world of a well-known science fiction author (except for one title which is based on a famous fantasy novel).  Both the <a href=\"/Series/97\">Crossroads</a> and <a href=\"/Series/92\">Combat Command</a> series feature game systems designed by Bill Fawcett; while <a href=\"/Series/97\">Crossroads</a> was RPG-inspired, these books seem to have their roots more firmly in wargames.  At various points in the adventures, military units must perform special actions or battle one another; at these times, a system using six attributes (Manpower, Ordnance, Attack Strength, Melee Strength, Stealth and Morale), multiple tables, and a pair of six-sided dice comes into play.  Although the original authors of the works that inspired these books contributed to little of the actual writing of the series, they did in most cases at least provide a foreword or afterword.',1),(93,'Combat Heroes','These gamebooks by <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> creator Joe Dever take a highly visual approach, and the series actually consists of two distinct sets of two books each.  Each book features a brief solitaire game, but the majority of the space in each set is devoted to a two-player duel in which each player takes one of the books and battles the other by calling out numbers, cross-referencing on tables and turning pages.  The first two books, which are reminiscent of a more complex version of the <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds Fantasy Combat Books</a>, feature a solitaire game in which the reader wanders a maze searching for treasure and a two-player game in which a pair of warriors battle in a maze similar to that found in the solo game.  The second pair of books are more similar to <a href=\"/Series/18\">Ace of Aces</a>, allowing the reader to take control of a fantasy flying machine, either hunting down an adversary in the two-player game or exploring the world and collecting magical items in the solitaire game.  All of the books are played without the use of dice or random numbers.',1),(94,'Count Duckula Adventure Scratchgame','Four short Count Duckula gamebooks were distributed in Shreddies cereal boxes as part of a promotion related to the animated series of the same name. They were referred to as a \"scratchgame\" because the numbers associated with choices were hidden until rubbed with a coin. The books used a combination of comic panels and text to tell their story, and readers reaching the successful endings could participate in a prize draw.',1),(95,'Create Your Own Erotic Fantasy','These books allow the reader to participate in works of erotica.',1),(96,'Cretan Chronicles','This trilogy presents a continuing storyline set in the Ancient Greece of mythology.  The books are designed to visually resemble the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series, but they feature a completely different game system.  Characters have the attributes of Might, Protection, Honour and Shame; Might and Protection affect combat, Honour is gained or lost based on behavior and may be spent to gain divine favor, and Shame can potentially force the player to commit suicide if accumulated excessively.  There are several additional things to keep track of: health, of which there are four levels (healthy, wounded, seriously wounded and dead); weapons, armor and equipment; and the character\'s degree of favor or disfavor with various deities.  The books also feature a fairly interesting mechanism known as \"taking a hint.\"  Whenever the reader encounters a section of the story with its number in italics, if he or she feels that his or her character could perform an action not mentioned in the text of the current section, he or she may add twenty to the paragraph number and flip ahead.  If this is successful, a special action is performed; if not, the reader is punished with loss of Honour or increase of Shame.',1),(97,'Crossroads Adventures','These adventures, each based on a popular series of fantasy novels, use a game system (designed by <a href=\"/Series/333\">SwordQuest</a> author Bill Fawcett) that appears to be inspired by <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> based on the six attributes it uses: Strength, Intelligence, Wisdom, Constitution, Dexterity and Charisma.  Players also keep track of Hit Points and manage their inventory of collected items.  A simple combat system is included which consists simply of rolling three dice and comparing them to a \"to-hit\" value.  Compared to the average gamebook of this complexity level, the books have a smaller number of longer sections.',1),(98,'Crystal Maze, The','This book is based on the British TV game show of the same name; in it, the player controls a team of four players.  Each has three attributes (strength, dexterity and intelligence) and may carry one item.  The object of the adventure is to travel to four different time zones and solve puzzles there; time crystals are earned by solving puzzles, but if things go wrong, team members can get captured.  The reader loses if the entire team is captured, and if he or she wins, a final score is determined based on how many team members remain free and how many time crystals have been earned.  There\'s not much in the way of plot, but some of the puzzles are quite sophisticated.',1),(99,'Kies jou eie avontuur','',9),(100,'Danger International','This adventure/espionage role-playing game is designed to be compatible with the <b>Hero System</b> rules found in the <b>Champions</b> superhero game.  Its rulebook includes a brief (14 section) solitaire adventure intended to demonstrate the game\'s combat system.',1),(101,'Dangermouse','The British cartoon series spawned a department-store exclusive gamebook programmed (but not written) by <a href=\"/Series/214\">Storytrails</a> author Allen Sharp, and its format resembles that series, with each section of text presented as a two-page spread.  The book prominently features the phrase <i>Choose Your Own Adventure</i> on the cover, but it is not officially part of Edward Packard\'s empire.',1),(102,'DC Heroes','This role-playing game provided an introductory solitaire adventure in <a href=\"/Item/719\">A Titan, Nevermore!</a>, one of the booklets in its main first edition boxed set, and several full-fledged solo adventures were sold separately.  Later editions of the rules featured a solo adventure originally released in the <a href=\"/Series/46\">Batman Role-Playing Game</a> spin-off.',1),(103,'DC Universe Roleplaying Game','This RPG contains a solitaire adventure featuring Robin in its rulebook.',1),(104,'Decide Your Own Adventure','These religious gamebooks were clearly modeled on the <a href=\"/Series/78\">Time Machine</a> series.  In each adventure, the reader travels through time and space in a chariot of fire.  Before setting off on this journey, though, he or she is presented with rules of travel, a quest to complete and information on the time period being explored.  The quests, as in <a href=\"/Series/78\">Time Machine</a>, generally involve learning about something, the difference being that while <a href=\"/Series/78\">Time Machine</a>\'s missions were historical, the ones in this series are Biblical.',1),(105,'Decision is Yours, The','These gamebooks, released in paperback and library editions, allow the reader to make decisions about common childhood situations like bullying or underage drinking.',1),(106,'Derrière la porte','This work of erotica allows the reader to participate as either a male or female character while exploring a carnival in search of the right partner.  The book has been printed in several different formats.',2),(107,'Behind Closed Doors','',1),(108,'Diceman','Each issue of this interactive comic book (a spinoff of <a href=\"/Series/1468\">2000 AD</a>) contains two or three adventures.  Several of these adventures were later collected in Titan Books\' <a href=\"/Item/2196\">Slaine Gaming Book</a>, which features a \"Diceman\'s Fantasy Gaming Series\" logo on its cover.',1),(109,'Dicing with Dragons','This oft-reprinted reference guide to role-playing games contains a solo adventure called \"Eye of the Dragon\" (not to be confused with the <a href=\"/Series/14\">Golden Dragon Fantasy Gamebook</a> of the same title).  The adventure uses the \"Fantasy Quest\" system, which has a few randomly-generated attributes and the need to keep track of an inventory; it\'s very much in keeping with the style of the author\'s <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series, which debuted at about the same time this volume was published.',1),(110,'Different Worlds','This magazine published three solitaire adventures during its run, the first for <a href=\"/Series/427\">Stormbringer</a>, the second for <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> and the third for <a href=\"/Series/112\">RuneQuest</a>.',1),(111,'Tunnels and Trolls','One of the earliest competitors to <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a>, Ken St. Andre\'s <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> role-playing game carved out a niche by being somewhat less complex than the competition and by providing a long line of solitaire adventures.  Indeed, <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> was the first role-playing game to support solitaire play, and a number of its adventures still remain in print.  Many of the adventures have gone through multiple editions (and sometimes dramatic rewrites), evolving from amateurish efforts into professional-looking products.  Several adventures were released in England in 1986 in two-book combined volumes (except for <a href=\"/Item/2702\">City of Terrors</a>, which was released by itself).  These editions were edited to remove some mild sexual content and had simplified rules included so that they could be played as stand-alone gamebooks as well as solitaire role-playing adventures.  In more recent years, many titles have become available in electronic formats as well.',1),(112,'RuneQuest','One of the more popular early fantasy role-playing games, this line spawned a few solitaire modules during its early years, each unusual in its own way.  When the game was revived many years later by Mongoose Publishing, new solo adventures began to appear in electronic form courtesy of Auxo Limited.',1),(113,'Dinky Dungeons','This light-hearted fantasy role-playing game was published in an extremely small format; the books are three by four inches each, and the dice are astoundingly tiny.  A single solitaire adventure was released; it includes a folded map resembling a board game, and the reader must roll dice, move along the board, and look up the results in the accompanying booklet.',1),(114,'Disney Adventures','This magazine occasionally includes interactive comic strips based on Disney properties.',1),(115,'Do-It-Yourself Jewish Adventure Series','These books allow the reader to participate in notable events in Jewish history.  The exact format and length varies from book to book, but all are fairly large, high-quality trade paperbacks and include glossaries.',1),(116,'Doctor Who','This pair of television tie-in gamebooks was released alongside the publisher\'s <b>Doctor Who Role Playing Game</b>, though that game is not required for play.  In each book, the reader takes on the role of the Doctor, keeping track of a few attributes and skills and occasionally using a pair of six-sided dice to resolve actions.  Despite their American origins, the books are fairly true to the British show and feature some familiar adversaries.  Unfortunately, some continuity errors were introduced in the first book because it was originally written for the fourth Doctor’s character but had to be hastily revised when the artist painted the sixth Doctor on the cover illustration.',1),(117,'Double Game','These fantasy gamebooks feature a fairly typical game system.  Players have fifty points to distribute among five attributes (Strength, Agility, Luck, Magic and Swordsmanship), with certain characters being more inclined to use certain attributes than others.  Players also keep track of inventory items and select spells to cast.  Casting spells costs Strength points, which are a measure of health.  Combat can also reduce Strength; it is resolved by rolling two dice and comparing them to a character\'s Swordsmanship attribute; whenever a roll is less than or equal to a character’s Swordsmanship, Strength damage is dealt to his or her opponent; this proceeds until one combatant is killed.  The feature which makes the books unusual is that, although they can be played alone in the usual fashion, they can also be played with a friend.  When two players participate, at the end of each paragraph, players call out the section numbers they intend to turn to.  In some cases, cross-reference tables are provided which allow one player\'s actions to affect the other player\'s position in the book.  Although the multiplayer game is largely cooperative in nature, there are opportunities for players to betray one another.',1),(118,'Dragon Magazine','This is one of the original roleplaying magazines, having been around since the late seventies. During its long existence it has contained a few solo adventures for different games.',1),(119,'Dragonquest','This stand-alone gamebook casts the reader as an Assistant Dragon Keeper in the fantasy world of Rosslandia.  The adventure is highly puzzle-oriented (rather like a more advanced, text-oriented version of <a href=\"/Series/6\">Be an Interplanetary Spy</a>), with the reader keeping track of coins and equipment while solving a variety of brain teasers.  The book includes a cut-out tangram set in the back.',1),(120,'Dragonriders of Pern','This is a one-off combat set similar to <a href=\"/Series/18\">Ace of Aces</a>.',1),(121,'Dragontales','These fantasy adventures are a bit longer than average for basic-style gamebooks, being about two hundred pages in length.  There is little explicit continuity between the books, but they do take place in the same fantasy world.  An interesting characteristic of the series is that the odd-numbered books feature white covers and female protagonists while the even-numbered ones have black covers and male protagonists.  Many of the adventures feature a romantic element in addition to the usual fantasy heroics.\n\n<p>Some books from the series were later reissued first as the paperback <a href=\"/Series/1987\">Dragon Pathways</a> and then as the e-book <a href=\"/Series/1613\">Dragon Roads</a>.',1),(122,'Dream Palace, The','This gamebook, a light fantasy adventure aimed at a slightly older audience than most interactive books, is rather unique.  Released in conjunction with a contest to win $500 and a gold wizard statue, it is designed so that it can be read either interactively or as a linear novel.  This is accomplished by putting assorted numbered sections in the back of the book while arranging the bulk of the text in linear order.  Readers wishing to interact with the book can occasionally be directed to the numbered sections, where it is possible to get sent forward or backward in the text or even be killed; those who just want to enjoy the novel can ignore such instructions.  The degree of interactivity is obviously limited by this approach, but it’s an interesting concept.',1),(123,'Dream Your Own Romance','These interactive juvenile romance novels were first published by Wanderer using the same format as their various <a href=\"/Category/58\">Plot-Your-Own</a> books, then reprinted by Carousel in England about a year later.',1),(124,'Duel Master','Each title in this two-player series is actually a set of two books, one containing even-numbered sections, the other containing odd-numbered ones.  The first and last sets don\'t have distinguishing titles to tell the two volumes apart, while the middle two sets do.  The sets cover a wide variety of scenarios, from gladiatorial combat to international relations, and some books are playable solitaire while others really are not.  Because of the diversity of topics, the rules vary from book to book.  They are adaptable to various levels of complexity, and they make use of dice-rolling for randomization and codewords for tracking certain conditions.  Coordination between the two players is achieved by use of \"WAIT\" instructions.  When a player encounters a \"WAIT\" instruction, he or she must wait for the other player to also receive a \"WAIT\" instruction before they can both begin reading again – this keeps both players\' experiences more or less synchronized.',1),(125,'Dungeon','This magazine has included four solitaire adventures. The first of them was designed for the Mentzer edition of <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a>. The second and third ones were made for the first edition of <a href=\"/Series/20\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons</a> (though the third one is also compatible with second edition rules). The last of these adventures is designed for the Dungeons and Dragons Essentials ruleset.',1),(126,'It\'s Your Choice','This trio of simple gamebooks allows the reader to control the decisions of teens at Rosemont High as they encounter situations involving drugs and alcohol.  Several years later, the same characters faced different challenges in the <a href=\"/Series/211\">Sexuality Decision-Making Series for Teens</a>.',1),(127,'Dungeoneer','One issue of this magazine contained a solitaire adventure compatible with both <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> and <a href=\"/Series/154\">The Fantasy Trip</a>.',1),(128,'Dungeons & Dragons Third-Party Products','Not surprisingly, <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> inspired some third-party companies to produce \"compatible\" products, including at least one solitaire adventure.',1),(129,'Dungeons & Dragons Cartoon Show Books','These books, based on the <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> television program, follow the same basic format as the <a href=\"/Series/151\">Fantasy Forest</a> series, though some of them contain visual puzzles like connect-the-dots and mazes.  Each allows the reader to portray a different character from the series.',1),(130,'Dungeons & Dragons Annual','Two hardback annuals based on the <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> cartoon show were published in the United Kingdom in 1986 and 1987; only the first one contained interactive stories, both of which make use of a pair of six-sided dice.',1),(131,'Marks and Spencer Exclusives','These books were produced by a chain store (Marks and Spencer) in the United Kingdom.  Each features a brief interactive story which also includes scenery on which reusable stickers may be positioned.\n\nThe first adventure in the series was later remade into an interactive game included in a DVD boxed set which also contained all episodes from the <a href=\"/Series/129\">Dungeons and Dragons Cartoon Show</a>.',1),(132,'Earth Brain','This stand-alone science fiction gamebook features an emphasis on puzzle-solving.',1),(133,'épée de légende, L\'','',2),(134,'Earth Inspectors','This series was started in the late eighties by Edward Packard of <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> fame. In the books, the reader is an alien trying to learn about the Earth, with each volume offering a different lesson. The ultimate result is rather similar to (but weaker than) the <a href=\"/Series/78\">Time Machine</a> series; each book presents the reader with a mission and leads to only one ending.',1),(135,'Earth 2 Choose Your Own Journey','These fan-produced gamebooks were based on the short-lived <i>Earth 2</i> NBC science fiction series and were sold as convention fundraisers.  The first book retells some of the major events of the series, while the second picks up where the series cliffhanger left off.  Although their amateur origins are evident in their writing (which often feels like the fan fiction that it is) and production values (which don’t exceed the photocopy shop level), the books are not without merit and should appeal to fans of the series.',1),(136,'Find Your Fate Junior - The Transformers','This was the first of the <b>Find Your Fate Junior</b> series (the second being <a href=\"/Series/167\">Find Your Fate Junior – Golden Girl</a>).  Like the other series in the <a href=\"/Series/159\">Find Your Fate</a> family, the stories are based on a popular license (in this case, obviously, the Transformers line of toys) and there is little in the way of gameplay (though there\'s a small amount of puzzle-solving, number-guessing and coin-flipping in a few of the books).  Rather strangely, a British series of <a href=\"/Series/336\" The>Transformers</a> gamebooks was released in England at the same time as this series.  The only real differences between the British and American books are that the British books are written in the first-person while the American ones are third-person and that the American books seem more faithful to the animated films and television shows than the British adventures.  Even though the British <a href=\"/Series/160\">Make Your Own Adventure with Doctor Who</a> books were reprinted as part of the <a href=\"/Series/159\">Find Your Fate</a> line, the British <a href=\"/Series/336\" The>Transformers</a>Transformers</a> adventures were not similarly integrated and thus never made it to America, nor did this American series see British publication.',1),(137,'Eclipse Graphic Novels','One of the books in this series is an interactive comic.',1),(138,'ELT Adventure Gamebooks','These three gamebooks are written using simple language and include glossaries so that foreign students can use them to practice their English.  Fortunately, their educational origins aren’t really obvious and don’t intrude into gameplay.',1),(139,'Career Adventures','These books allow the reader to try out different kinds of jobs.',1),(140,'Escape from Tenopia','In these books, the reader is a young space traveler stranded on the dangerous planet of Tenopia after an accident involving a meteor.  As the name of the series suggests, the ultimate goal is to escape; each book takes the reader closer to freedom.  The cover of each volume proclaims it as being \"from Edward Packard, creator of <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a>.\"  However, the books are structured more like the <a href=\"/Series/78\">Time Machine</a> adventures – there is only one ending to each book, with no way to fail; the challenge simply comes from navigating through the choices in such a way as to find the conclusion.  A few puzzles are included for flavor, and victory also requires careful map-reading and a good memory (since it is necessary to remember which of Tenopia\'s often strangely-named locations have been previously visited).  The series was followed by <a href=\"/Series/141\">Escape from the Kingdom of Frome</a>, which uses the same basic style but tells a completely unrelated story.',1),(141,'Escape from the Kingdom of Frome','This follow-up to <a href=\"/Series/140\">Escape from Tenopia</a> uses the same basic format but features a more fantasy-oriented story in which the reader sets out to explore the dangerous land of Frome.  Of course, the real challenge eventually becomes to task of getting home.',1),(142,'Eternal Champions','This pair of gamebooks is based on a Sega fighting video game.  As one would expect, the books feature a combat-oriented game system.  Character attributes are Speed, Endurance, Power, Recovery, Defense and Equilibrium; the reader is given points to assign to these at the start of play, and their values can change over time.  There are also Life Points to keep track of and a variety of Special Moves which can be learned during play and executed by using Inner Strength points.  Every character has a specialized Fighting Style (Savate, Kung Fu, Karate, Aikido, Jujitsu, Kickboxing or Ninjutsu), and it is possible to collect inventory items during play.  Characters from the first book may be carried over to the second, which inexplicably shares the title of the second <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> adventure.',1),(143,'Explorer','These educational gamebooks, designed by Byron Preiss, the team also responsible for the <a href=\"/Series/6\">Be an Interplanetary Spy</a> and <a href=\"/Series/78\">Time Machine</a> series, cast the reader as an \"explorer\" who participates in scientific adventures like shrinking and traveling through a human body or investigating natural resources on the moons of Jupiter.  The books are a bit more whimsical than the <a href=\"/Series/78\">Time Machine</a> series and offer the possibility of failure, but they are otherwise fairly similar.',1),(144,'Eye of the Idol, The','This board game, released in British chain store Marks & Spencer, includes a gamebook as part of the package in order to introduce the plot.',1),(145,'Fabled Lands','Probably the most intricately designed gamebooks ever published, the <a href=\"/Series/145\">Fabled Lands</a> books are all interconnected; each book represents a different segment of a larger world, and it is possible to hop from book to book in a non-linear fashion, completing quests and gaining in experience.  Players have six attributes (Charisma, Combat, Magic, Sanctity, Scouting and Thievery) whose initial scores are determined by character profession.  There are also Stamina points to represent health and a limited inventory for storing items.  Codewords are used in each book to keep track of which areas have already been visited and which major events have already occurred, and there are sometimes places in the text where players may leave behind items to be retrieved later.  The unfortunate thing about this series, though, is that it was never completed; after six volumes, it went out of print, so the entire world is inaccessible to readers.  For many years, the situation was even worse in the United States, where only two books were published under the <b>Quest</b> label.  Fortunately, starting in 2010, the original six books were reissued. The first book was also further re-issued in a deluxe hardcover edition by Megara Entertainment with full-colour artwork.\n<p>In early 2018 the long-awaited seventh book finally appeared, funded by a Kickstarter campaign and published by Megara Entertainment.',1),(146,'Falcon','These books put the reader in control of Falcon, a time-traveling special agent, with each volume presenting a distinct mission to complete.  The books feature small print and detailed illustrations, making them feel as if they are aimed at slightly older readers than the average gamebook.  In spite of this, their game system isn’t especially complex.  The player must keep track of only four attributes, two of which represent psychic abilities: Attack Modifier, Evasion Modifier, Thinkstrike Modifier and Power of Will Modifier.  These attributes are added to dice rolls at various points in each adventure.  There are also Endurance points which measure the character\'s current physical condition, and special items that can be acquired during each mission.  Some of the books feature a special scoring mechanism which assigns letters to certain sections.  The reader must write down these letters as they are encountered, and at the end of the adventure, a chart is consulted and a score is tallied from them.  As an unusual bonus, the first volume in the series includes a pair of cut-and-assemble paper dice. \n\n<p>In 2015, this series started to be re-issued with slightly different updated cover art through Amazon\'s print-on-demand service. Only the first book has been re-issued to date and, as sales were poor, it seems unlikely that any further books in this series will be reprinted.',1),(147,'Famous Five and You, The','These books are based on Enid Blyton\'s popular British children\'s books.  They are designed so that there is a right and wrong answer at every choice point.  Making the wrong choice doesn\'t end the game, but it does cause the reader to accumulate red herring points; the goal of the game is to get through with as few of these points as possible.',1),(148,'Fantastic Adventures','These gamebooks, which cover a broad range of themes and are related only by tone, were released as one of Troll’s four <a href=\"/Category/57\">The Choice is Yours</a> sets.',1),(149,'Legends of Lone Wolf','This series of novels is adapted from the <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> gamebooks. For a short <a href=\"/Series/2044\">American run</a>, the numbering was slightly different because the third book was split into two parts. A second series, the <a href=\"/Series/1212\">Chronicles of Magnamund</a> novels, appeared more than a decade later.',1),(150,'Legends of Lone Wolf Audiobooks','Abridged audiobook adaptations of the Legends of Lone Wolf novelisations. Only the first was produced (as a double cassette set). A second title was advertised for release in \"late-1992/early-1993\" but was never released.\n<p>In 2014 the first cassette was streamed on YouTube (with Joe Dever\'s approval) in eight parts (ie each side of each cassette was split into two equal parts turning four sides into eight segments).',1),(151,'Fantasy Forest','This series is quite similar to the <a href=\"/Series/79\">Endless Quest</a> books, but it is aimed at a somewhat younger audience. Most of the books are set in the world of <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a>, but two adventures are based on the <b>Star Frontiers</b> role-playing game instead.',1),(152,'Fantasy Forum','This magazine contained a single solitaire adventure for <a href=\"/Series/154\">The Fantasy Trip</a>.',1),(153,'Fantasy Gamer','This magazine included a solitaire adventure for <a href=\"/Series/60\">Call of Cthulhu</a> in one issue.  It is available for sale in electronic format through the publisher.',1),(154,'Fantasy Trip (original release), The','In 2019, this role-playing game was re-released featuring both revised and new material; see <a href=\"/Series/2428\">The Fantasy Trip (re-release)</a>.',1),(155,'Fatemaster','These somewhat larger-than-average gamebooks are mainly distinguished by their mapping system; as the reader moves through each adventure, he or she is given small drawings which may be copied and assembled together to create a detailed map. When outdoors, these map segments are in hexagon format, while indoor maps are printed as if on graph paper. The rest of the system is fairly standard, though it does show a bit of creativity. Rather than using hit points, players lose points from their attributes, thus making their characters weaker in combat as they sustain damage. Spell-casting is also nice, with a lengthy list of possible spells to cast, each costing a certain number of points; it\'s not as flexible or impressive as the <a href=\"/Series/15\">Sorcery!</a> system, but it\'s workable. It appears that only two books were released, with a third never making it past the planning stage. Perhaps the series met its end as the direct result of its flexibility -- there\'s so much to do in each book, you almost don\'t need another one. Still, it\'s always a shame to see a series die prematurely, and while the end of <a href=\"/Series/155\">Fatemaster</a> isn\'t as tragic as the failure of <a href=\"/Series/145\">Fabled Lands</a>, it would have been nice to watch the series evolve further.',1),(156,'Warlock','This was the official magazine of <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>, featuring at least one complete adventure in each issue.  After a few issues containing abridged or altered versions of <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> gamebooks, the magazine began producing original storylines.  Issue ten and eleven\'s adventures featured rules so that it was compatible with <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> (although it still contained regular <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> rules for players who preferred them).  Publication passed from Penguin to Games Workshop with issue six.',1),(157,'Fighting Fantasy: Clash of the Princes','These books, released as a boxed set, can be played as standard <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> gamebooks or may be combined for a two-player experience.  In the two-player game, two scores (Action and Status) must be kept track of on a piece of paper in order to keep both players\' game experiences synchronized.',1),(158,'Fighting Fantasy 10th Anniversary Yearbook','This book was released in celebration of the tenth anniversary of the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series.  It\'s a 1993 appointment calendar with various <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>-themed articles interspersed throughout its pages.  At the bottoms of many pages are sections of a 200-paragraph adventure, a modified version of \"Rogue Mage,\" which was first published in <a href=\"/Series/156\">Warlock</a> magazine.',1),(159,'Find Your Fate','This series was designed to cash in on various licenses to create gamebooks based on familiar properties.  The main series started things off with several Indiana Jones adventures, and as time went by, it also added James Bond books and spawned a variety of spin-offs.  The series\' two Morgan Swift adventures are something of an oddity, though, being based on an obscure character who seems only to have appeared outside of these gamebooks in a handful of young adult novels; their inclusion seems to have been intended to lure more female readers to the series.  In any case, the books themselves are fairly straightforward gamebooks which occasionally introduce elements of randomness by asking the reader to guess a number or turn the page based on the current day of the week.  A handful of the Indiana Jones adventures were reprinted in 1994 with new covers by uncredited artists.',1),(160,'Find Your Fate - Doctor Who','This series was released nearly simultaneously in both the United States and the United Kingdom.  The British version of the series, published by Severn House, was called <b>Make Your Own Adventure with Doctor Who</b>, while the American editions were absorbed into Ballantine\'s <a href=\"/Category/86\">Find Your Fate</a> line.  Internally, both versions are identical.  The books lack a formal game system, but they do rely so heavily on dice rolls and puzzles that they feel more game-like than the other <a href=\"/Category/86\">Find Your Fate</a> offerings.  Since there are no character-related rules to supplement the dice-rolling, this means that there is a heavy reliance on pure luck and thus many frustrating endings.  In terms of story, each book casts the reader as a child who somehow becomes involved with characters from the TV series; unfortunately, even though most of these books were written by scriptwriters for the show, the tone of the TV program is lost in the books and little attention is paid to series continuity (although some familiar friends and foes make appearances here and there).  Rather surprisingly, the strictly American <a href=\"/Series/116\">Doctor Who</a> gamebooks published by FASA, which were written by a war game designer, capture the feel of the series more successfully.\r\n\r\n<p>The problem is likely that the scriptwriters hired to write these things had no idea what to do with the gamebook form. Either they failed to research the genre or they chose not to apply much of what they learned. Flaws of gameplay are compounded by the fact that the writers seem self-conscious about the fact that they are writing for children; the books have a condescending tone that, oddly enough, isn\'t shared by the episode novelizations released by Target Books (which were also aimed at children and some of which were even written by the same authors as these books). If these books had been given real interactivity and had shared more of the feel of the Target novelizations, this would have been a stronger series.',1),(161,'Find Your Fate - G. I. Joe','This <a href=\"/Series/159\">Find Your Fate</a> spin-off features sometimes bizarre military adventures inspired by the famous G. I. Joe action figures and cartoon.  Rather strangely, the author credits inside the third and fourth books have been accidentally reversed; the correct people are credited on the covers, however.',1),(162,'Find Your Fate - Jem','These books, based on the Jem cartoon series, allow the reader to control the actions of the supercomputer-using rock star. The books actually resemble the two <b>Find Your Fate Junior</b> series (<a href=\"/Series/136\">The Transformers</a> and <a href=\"/Series/167\">Golden Girl</a>) more than the regular <a href=\"/Series/159\">Find Your Fate</a> books, despite the lack of a \"Junior\" in their series title.  Surprisingly, although they feature colorful covers, the adventures lack interior illustrations.',1),(163,'Find Your Fate - Random House','Although these books are numbered as if they were a single series, they are in fact three different sets of books.  The first two and last two books are given the <b>Find Your Fate Mystery</b> label, and they are based on the popular children\'s mystery novels featuring the Three Investigators.  Two of the books are written in the second person, allowing the reader to actually work with the Investigators, and the other two are written in the third person, forcing the reader to make decisions from a more distant perspective.  The third and fourth books in the series are the <b>Find Your Fate Fantasy</b> series, based on the Thundercats cartoon and toys.  These are written in the third person and are thinner than the rest of the series.  Finally, the fifth and sixth books make up the <b>Find Your Fate Thriller</b> series.  They are written in the first person, are physically smaller than the rest of the series, and don\'t seem to be based on a well-known property of any sort.',1),(164,'Dealing with Feelings','This series allows young children to learn about and experiment with different intense feelings.  Both paperback and library-bound editions were produced.',1),(165,'Date with Destiny Adventures','This series of humorous gamebooks mimics the format of <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a>, but its content is aimed squarely at adults, with sex and dating being a major part of each story.  Each title includes a long list of \"future <b>Date with Destiny Adventures</b>,\" but most of these appear to be jokes and are not listed below.',1),(166,'Big Night Out','This thick book offers its readers a sleazily humorous night on the town in search of love... or something.',1),(167,'Find Your Fate Junior - Golden Girl','These books form a female counterpart to the slightly earlier <a href=\"/Series/136\">Find Your Fate Junior – The Transformers</a> series.  Like that series, they are based on a series of action figures (though a less famous one), and they also feature a similar layout and complexity level (though the text is at a slightly higher reading level).  The storyline revolves around Golden Girl, a fantasy heroine constantly searching for a set of elusive magical gemstones, as she battles numerous foes.',1),(168,'Find Your (Unfortunate) Fate','This book was the last gasp of the <a href=\"/Series/159\">Find Your Fate</a> line, a stand-alone adaptation of the cult favorite comic book and television series.  It tells the story of an unpleasant camping trip, and it features occasional interruptions by the Crypt Keeper (printed in bold-faced italics).',1),(169,'Find Your Way','This series of short movie tie-in novels shares its format with two other books not using the <b>Find Your Way</b> label: <a href=\"/Series/7\">Choose the Fate of Apollo 13</a> and <a href=\"/Series/250\">Make a Simple Wish of Your Very Own</a>.  The final two volumes in the series included temporary tattoos.',1),(170,'Follow Your Heart Romance','These interactive romance novels follow the format of the <a href=\"/Series/29\">Which Way Books</a>, though they are aimed at a somewhat older audience.',1),(171,'Food Chain, The','This gamebook is designed to teach its readers about the global food problem.',1),(172,'Football Adventure Game, The','This sports gamebook follows the format introduced by <a href=\"/Series/39\">Enid Blyton\'s Famous 5 Adventure Games</a>; the game comes in a plastic wallet stuffed with special components like cards and a custom die.  The reader\'s job is to manage a football (i.e. soccer) team through ten games.  The special components ensure that a different mix of teams is found in each playthrough, making replay value quite high.',1),(173,'Forbidden Gateway','These gamebooks tell horror stories in the vein of H. P. Lovecraft.  The game system is straightforward, with five attributes (Strength, Stamina, Mentality, Endurance and Dexterity) that can be tested with two six-sided dice in order to perform various actions.  A \"conflict table\" provides a way of pitting one individual\'s attributes against another\'s, thus making combat and other opposed actions possible.  Various weapons can be collected which inflict varying amounts of damage to enemies\' Stamina and/or Endurance, and other non-combat items may be collected during play.  As in the popular <a href=\"/Series/60\">Call of Cthulhu</a> role-playing game, it is possible to fail either by being physically killed or by being driven insane.  The two books feature a connected storyline, and characters may be carried from the first book into the second.',1),(174,'Freeway Warrior','This four-book series casts the reader as Cal Phoenix, one of the few survivors in a world recovering from nuclear holocaust.  Over the course of the books, Cal attempts to lead a band of survivors from Texas to California, where they plan to set up a permanent home.  Cal has two basic attributes: Close Combat Skill and Endurance.  These are generated randomly, but the reader has control in distributing points among Cal\'s five Survival Skills: Driving, Shooting, Field Craft, Stealth and Perception.  Further skill points are gained as a result of successfully completing books.  There are a limited number of items that may be taken along, with a large load reducing Stealth.  It is also necessary to keep track of water, first aid supplies and several different types of ammunition.  Close combat is handled similarly to battles in the author\'s better-known <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> series, but the inclusion of firearms makes things slightly more complex.  The books were first published in England by Beaver Books, then reissued in the United States by Berkley Pacer.  The American editions have black and white rather than color maps, new cover artwork (though the illustrators are not credited) and in the case of the second volume, a less violent-sounding title.\n\n<p>The series was reissued by Swedish publisher Åskfågeln between 2017 and 2021.',1),(175,'Game Boy','These books, based on games for the Game Boy Color and Game Boy Advance portable video game systems, are less complicated than the earlier <a href=\"/Series/286\">Nintendo Adventure Books</a> since they don\'t include any game rules, but they are more closely related to the games that inspired them, with some of the events in the plots designed to provide useful information for players of the video games.  Each book also includes a center section of color illustrations.  The first book features the phrase \"Choose Your Own Adventure\" in an inverted star on the front cover, but the later titles instead use the phrase \"You Decide on the Adventure,\" perhaps due to trademark issues.',1),(176,'Gamer, The','This magazine once printed a solitaire adventure for <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a>.',1),(177,'GAMES','This long-lived magazine usually contains games and puzzles of the most traditional variety -- card game variants, crossword puzzles and such. However, its editors occasionally spice things up by including gamebook-style solitaire adventures.',1),(178,'GangBusters','The original boxed set of this twenties crime drama role-playing game included a sixteen-page adventure booklet along with the main rulebook, map, counters and dice.  The adventure is designed for use by up to four people, but it requires no game master and can be played solitaire.',1),(179,'Ghost Adventure Games','These horror gamebooks use the format initiated by <a href=\"/Series/39\">Enid Blyton’s Famous 5 Adventure Games</a>; each book comes in a plastic sleeve complete with a special six-sided die and an assortment of components used during play: a ghost counter (a cardboard dial that tracks how many supernatural encounters the reader has had), a map, and a variety of cards (which vary from book to book, but include plastic key overlays used to reveal hidden phrases in the text and various reference materials containing clues).  Players start with just one item of equipment and must complete their adventures without having more than four ghostly encounters.',1),(180,'Ghostbusters International','This second edition of the <b>Ghostbusters Role-Playing Game</b> contains an introductory booklet which in turn contains a 49-paragraph solitaire adaptation of one of the multi-player scenarios included in the first edition.',1),(181,'Giochi del 2000, I','This series of small, inexpensive booklets contained several gamebooks along with other types of games.  Only the gamebooks are listed below.',5),(182,'Grampa Barmo Presents...','This label was created to offer inexpensive games and gamebooks; it ties in with <a href=\"/Series/183\">Grampa Barmo\'s Discount Game Magazine</a>.  The first two titles in the line are authorized translations of <a href=\"/Series/181\">I Giochi del 2000</a> games.  One of them, listed below, is a gamebook; the other is <i>Kalevala</i>, a role-playing game inspired by Finnish mythology.',1),(183,'Grampa Barmo\'s Discount Game Magazine','This magazine, devoted to coverage of games costing less than $10, was an attempt to illuminate an interesting corner of the gaming industry. Unfortunately, though, it didn\'t work terribly well, and it was abandoned after a year of quarterly issues.  It did at least provide an excuse to publish the first English-language translation of a brief Italian solitaire adventure.',1),(184,'Give Yourself Goosebumps Special Edition','This <a href=\"/Series/82\">Give Yourself Goosebumps</a> spin-off for the most part features more complex adventures than its parent series.  The first book is the most involved, featuring nearly as many sections as one of R. L. Stine\'s earlier <a href=\"/Series/193\">Hark</a> books and requiring the reader to roll dice and keep track of inventory.  All of the subsequent books returned to the more traditional format of one section per page, but several continued to rely on inventory selection.  The fifth book offers the reader the choice of being either a hunter or spellcaster and choosing spells or equipment from lists, much like the <a href=\"/Series/185\">Wizards, Warriors & You</a> series, while the final book is unusual in that it contains four unrelated stories, and a \"lucky number\" picked at the beginning determines which one unfolds.  Only the sixth book is completely without special features, making one wonder why it was included in this series rather than the main one.',1),(185,'Wizards, Warriors & You','These books were first published by Avon in the United States, but a few of them were reissued with new covers by Carousel in the United Kingdom.  They take place in the fantasy kingdom of King Henry, where a legendary pair of unnamed heroes, the Wizard and the Warrior, are frequently called upon to complete dangerous missions.  The central gimmick of the series is that the reader has the choice in each adventure of taking on the role of either the Wizard or the Warrior.  As the Warrior, the reader must select a limited number of weapons from a list in the back of the book before proceeding on a mission; the Wizard is given a full complement of spells but often has to rely more on luck than the Warrior does.  The spell and weapon lists changed twice during the course of the series, adding some variety to the adventures.  The books rely heavily on randomization, and most challenges are resolved by flipping coins; sometimes, though, other methods are used, like asking the player to pick a random number or check the time of day or day of the week.  All of this use of chance means that the books aren\'t particularly skill-based, but they have a unique flavor and a considerable number of fans.',1),(186,'Gladiators','These two gamebooks are based on <i>Gladiators</i>, the British version of the <i>American Gladiators</i> TV series, and each allows its reader to simulate the various competitive physical challenges portrayed in the series.  Released a couple of years apart, the two books are wildly different in style and format.  The first book uses a format that was later featured in the <a href=\"/Series/411\">Star Wars Missions</a> and <a href=\"/Series/328\">Star Wars Episode I Adventures</a>; in place of using numbered paragraphs or turning to specified pages, the reader simply reads the book straight through.  Whenever a choice is made, the reader scans through a list of outcomes and finds the one that corresponds with the choice he or she made.  A six-sided die is used to generate random numbers at certain points, and the whole book revolves around score, with different choices leading to different changes to a running total of points.  The second book reverts to a more standard format featuring numbered paragraphs.  The reader picks a gender and randomly determines five attributes (Stamina, Agility, Upper Body Strength, Lower Body Strength and Luck).  Different attributes are tested as a result of choosing different actions, so it\'s best for the reader to make choices that reflect his or her strengths and weaknesses.  Luck works just like the attribute of the same name in <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>.  Both books are rather brief but are more replayable than average due to their lack of plot.',1),(187,'GM','This magazine contained a <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a>-inspired adventure in one issue.',1),(188,'Grailquest','This series casts the reader as Pip, a youth chosen by Merlin to perform quests in the service of King Arthur.  The books are notable for a number of reasons: their sense of humor (brought out by Merlin\'s eccentricity and various strange foes), their bonus features (many include instructions on how to build things like noisemakers and paper boats), their use of \"dreamtime\" sections which are read whenever Pip sleeps, and various other quirks which set them apart from the run-of-the-mill gamebook.  For all their unusual features, the rules are pretty typical, with Pip having to keep track of Life Points, Experience Points (which can be used to gain permanent Life Points), inventory items and spells.  Eight books were published in England, with only the first six reprinted in the United States, some with different covers and all with inferior quality (smaller format, cardstock character and rule sheets reduced to regular paper, thinner paper, etc.). In 2025, the books started to be reprinted with new cover art by Two Yards publishers.',1),(189,'Kaos','During the course of its existence, this magazine printed several mini-gamebooks.',5),(190,'Gaming Frontiers','This magazine is devoted to the <a href=\"/Category/87\">d20 System</a>.  Each large, perfect-bound and colorful issue includes adventures, articles and previews submitted by a variety of different publishers taking advantage of Wizards of the Coast\'s \"open source\" game system.  Just one issue (the second) contained a solitaire adventure taking place in the modern-day espionage \"Shadowforce Archer\" setting.',1),(191,'GURPS','<a href=\"/Series/191\">GURPS</a>, short for Generic Universal RolePlaying System, is one of the most popular and successful attempts to create a generic system that can be used for role-playing in multiple genres.  It embraced one-player gaming from the beginning, including a fantasy solo adventure with the first three editions of its basic rules and then following this up with a line of adventures. The introductory adventure was not included with the fourth edition of the GURPS basic rules, though a version updating the adventure with fourth-edition rules was made available for free as a pdf over the Internet by the publisher. \n\n<p>The standalone adventures (all of which use third edition rules) tied in with popular properties licensed for use with the game (Conan, Horseclans and Humanx Commonwealth), but they only required the Basic Set for play, not any of the numerous world books released to supplement the rules.  While some are considerably thicker than others, the books are all large-format and feature numerous numbered sections along with character sheets, special rules and, in some cases, material that can be used in multiplayer <a href=\"/Series/191\">GURPS</a> campaigns.  One book, <a href=\"/Item/1275\">Bili the Axe: Up Harzburk!</a>, was recalled from the market due to several design flaws that rendered it unplayable.  Some copies still exist in the hands of collectors, however. An additional solo adventure for the <i>GURPS Prisoner</i> setting (based on the British science fiction television show <b>The Prisoner</b>) was completely or mostly written, but never published. \n\n<p>Years after the original batch of adventures was released, a solo adventure was included in a supplement for the <i>GURPS Prime Directive</i> science fiction setting, using third edition rules. While all the aforementioned solo adventures are currently out of print, the Third Edition Basic Set and the Conan-based solos (as well as the Module Prime Alpha supplement) are available in digital format from the Warehouse 23 store.\n\n<p>Two additional GURPS solo adventures have only been published in electronic format. The first of them, based on the <i>GURPS Horror</i> supplement, was written at about the same time as the first GURPS solos, but was never published in book form. Several years later, the game\'s publisher released the adventure for free over the Internet in a browser-only version. The second adventure (which apparently was written considerably later than the first) uses the setting from the <i>GURPS Fantasy</i> supplements. This latter adventure was also freely available in a browser version from the author\'s website (no longer online as of December 2023). Nowadays, readers can either access and play the adventure through the Internet Archive, or they can play a reworked version using the rules from <a href=\"/Series/321\">Risus: The Anything RPG</a></i>.',1),(192,'Hard-Boiled','This book contains three solitaire mystery adventures.  In each adventure, the player first creates a private investigator character by distributing points between three attributes (Muscle, Moxie and Magnetism), then attempts to solve a mystery by moving from location to location on a map of Los Angeles.  Locations on the map can only be visited when they have a star next to them, and only a few locations are marked at the start of an adventure.  Each story also has two goals: to survive, and to solve a mystery.  Survival relies on good decision-making and luck with attribute tests; mystery solving requires that, in addition to surviving, the reader finds all of the objectives of a given mystery and deduces the solutions to those objectives by using clues gathered during play.  Thus, each adventure works not just as a gamebook but also as a \"solve it yourself\" mystery novel in the vein of <b>Encyclopedia Brown</b> or <b>Two-Minute Mysteries</b>.',1),(193,'Hark','These two books, set on the strange and deadly planet of Hark, are fairly unique; they have an emphasis on gameplay over plot even though they don\'t have any sort of formal game system apart from a meaningless score total which accumulates during play.  The books end up feeling more complex than they actually are for two main reasons: the sections are short (and printed several to a page) and finding the victorious ending requires a great deal of trial and error (and more than a little mapping).',1),(194,'Have It Your Way','',1),(195,'Have Your Own Extra-Terrestrial Adventure','This stand-alone gamebook allows the reader to control the actions of Del Curb, a private investigator seeking Rimeq the Renegade, a dangerous alien bent on destroying the Earth.  It mixes a film noir tone with the usual science fiction trappings, and it is slightly unusual for containing no fatal endings despite having ten possible outcomes with varying levels of success.  Due to its first-person writing and British flavor, the overall feel is quite similar to the <a href=\"/Series/214\">Storytrails</a> series.',1),(196,'HeartQuest','These interactive romances, set in the world of <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a>, follow the same basic format as the first thirty-six <a href=\"/Series/79\">Endless Quest</a> adventures.  The books feature cut-out covers designed so that a round frame overlays a full-page color illustration; these cut-outs often get snagged on other books, making undamaged copies difficult to find.',1),(197,'Hercules and Xena','This boxed role-playing game includes an introductory solo adventure as a separate booklet.',1),(198,'Heroes','This magazine included solo adventures in its last two issues.',1),(199,'HeroQuest','This series of books is based on the board game of the same name.  Each volume includes a non-interactive novella and a brief solitaire adventure inspired by the board game.  The first book\'s adventure involves four characters; these may all be controlled by one player, or their roles may be taken on by multiple players who then read the book together and confer on decisions.  The second and third volumes\' adventures are for just one character each, respectively a wizard and a barbarian.  In all of the adventures, an adaptation of the board game\'s basic rules is used.  Characters have four attributes (Body, Mind, Combat and Speed), combat is resolved by rolling against the Combat attribute, a limited number of items may be carried, and some characters have access to spellcasting powers.  Although the books are illustrated, their artists are not credited.',1),(200,'Hero\'s Challenge: Sagard the Barbarian','These books, co-authored by <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> co-creator Gary Gygax, are set in the world of Greyhawk and cast the reader as a young barbarian who must first prove his manhood and then set out on various adventures.  The game system is very simple, and the books are designed to allow the reader to dive right in to the story, being directed to instructions only as they are needed.  Whenever an enemy is encountered, a brief table is presented to facilitate combat; combatants take turns generating random numbers and doing damage to one another based on the appropriate tables.  When a character runs out of hit points, it is dead.  During his adventures, Sagard gains experience marks (which can be used to raise his level and thus improve his combat tables) along with various powers and types of equipment.  All of these things carry from book to book.  Randomization is achieved either with four-sided dice or by flipping through the book and using numbers printed on the page corners.  The books were first released in the United States by Archway, then reprinted in England by Corgi with new (but uncredited) cover art.',1),(201,'High Fantasy','This role-playing game spawned a few solo adventures, one in its second edition boxed set and two as stand-alone volumes.  The stand-alone solo adventures contain condensed rules so that they can be played without owning any other products. Many years after the release of the RPG, the same author released a stand-alone gamebook using the same setting.',1),(202,'Homeschool Super Sleuths','These gamebooks were written by a pair of homeschool students and published (in saddle-stitched booklet format) by their parents.  Both books are illustrated with computer clip art.',1),(203,'Horror Classics','These books, based on classic horror novels, allow the reader an interesting choice: to play either the monster or its nemesis.  In the first book, the choice is between Dracula or Jonathan Harker, and in the second, it is between Frankenstein and his creation.  Regardless of which character is chosen, players start with one hundred life points and roll dice to determine scores for five fairly typical attributes.  Two of these attributes (Speed and Courage) are used in combat to determine whether a hit is scored while two others (Strength and Skill) determine how much damage is done.  The final attribute, Psi, determines how many times special mental powers may be used during an adventure.  Players must also keep track of money and inventory items during play.  Because each book essentially contains two different adventures, the paragraphs of each book are split into two distinct sections: a \"Location\" section and an \"Action\" section.  All of the choices and events of the stories are found in the Action section, but some of these paragraphs refer back to the Location section, which contains room descriptions shared between the two stories.',1),(204,'Imagine','One issue of this magazine contained a solo adventure.',1),(205,'Indiana Jones Role-Playing Game','This role-playing game includes a solitaire adventure in the rulebook. The adventure is divided into episodes, each intended to teach the reader a specific part of the game mechanics. The episodes are spread through the book, with each episode being included at the end of the chapter describing the mechanic it is intended to illustrate. In turn, each episode is divided into a small number of sections in standard branching-path adventure fashion. Only the first few episodes of the adventure can be played by a single person without a referee; later episodes, while still using a branching-path narrative, require a player and a referee for play. \n\n<p>In addition to the introductory adventure, this game spawned two standalone solitaire adventures.',1),(206,'Interactive Business Games','These interactive novels allow the reader an opportunity to experiment with different challenges involved in running a business.  They were first released in hardback by McGraw-Hill, with the first two titles also being reprinted in paperback by Prentice Hall.',1),(207,'Interactive Connect-the-Dots Adventures','Each page of these books features several large, intricate, full-color connect-the-dots puzzles.  At each given location in a book, the reader picks one of these puzzles to connect.  Depending on what is revealed, an item may be gained (to be checked off in the reader’s inventory), a monster may be fought (using a simple roll of the dice), or a new location may be advanced to (allowing a new puzzle to be attempted).  The reader\'s goal in each book is to collect six special items without being defeated by monsters.',1),(208,'Interactive Mystery Novels','This pair of mystery novels follows a fairly unique format: each starts with an introduction discussing a crime, and this is followed by a telephone directory.  The phone numbers in the directory are actually page numbers, and the reader must make calls in order to deduce the solution to the mystery.  The answers are explained at the ends of the books, and the fewer moves the reader uses to solve each mystery, the better.',1),(209,'Intergalactic Quest','These gamebooks, which feature a time travel theme, are heavily illustrated 32-page booklets resembling comic books.  They were created by prolific comic author/illustrator Peter Milligan.',1),(210,'Interplay','This is a magazine which featured solo adventures for <a href=\"/Series/154\">The Fantasy Trip</a>.',1),(211,'Sexuality Decision-Making Series for Teens','This three-book set was released by Network Publications two years after their earlier <a href=\"/Series/126\">It\'s Your Choice</a> series of anti-drug books. These books, featuring the same characters and settings, are smaller and less colorful than their predecessors and deal with sexual issues, as the series title suggests.',1),(212,'James Bond 007','This role-playing game spawned a couple of solo adventures.',1),(213,'Knightmare','These books tie in with a British TV game show of the same name.  As in the show, players race through the dungeon of Knightmare Castle, attempting to complete quests before their Life Force runs out.  In addition to its brief gamebook segment, each book contains a non-interactive fantasy novella featuring characters from the series.  The ties between the novella and the gamebook are very loose, but sometimes reading the story will give the reader hints for the later adventure.  The rules vary from book to book, but the essentials are fairly simple: the reader must keep track of life force, spells and inventory; some volumes also feature skills to choose from, additional attributes (Armour, Dexterity and Chivalry) or codewords for keeping track of past events.  The first four books were published by Corgi\'s main imprint and had a somewhat gritty and historically-grounded tone; the last two adventures were published by Corgi\'s Yearling imprint and clearly aimed at a younger audience, featuring more fantastic stories involving modern-day children traveling back through time.  A seventh <a href=\"/Series/213\">Knightmare</a> book by Dave Morris, entitled <i>Lord Fear\'s Domain</i> and illustrated by <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> regular Russ Nicholson, was published in 1994, but it was a straightforward puzzle book without a gamebook element and thus is not listed here.',1),(214,'Storytrails','This series of slim books features a format somewhat different from the usual basic gamebook; the writing is in the first person, and each section of the story lasts exactly two pages – only every other page of each book is numbered.  A variety of genres are covered by the series, and despite their brief length, storylines are written in a fairly mature fashion (though still aimed at children).  The books were first published by Cambridge University Press in the United Kingdom; selected volumes were reprinted in North America by Regensteiner Publishing Enterprises.  The American editions are hardbound and include information on British vocabulary (i.e. \"sweet means dessert\"); because of their hardcover format, they are more commonly found in public libraries than in private collections, though they are becoming increasingly rare.',1),(215,'Knuckleduster Interactive Western Adventures','These interactive Westerns were published as large-format paperbacks.  Both adventures share a basic game system in which the reader keeps track of money and points and uses dice to determine the success or failure of risky actions.  During the course of each adventure, \"bonus dice\" can be accumulated which allow rolls to be strategically influenced at critical moments.  The second book also includes optional rules to make the system more complex by introducing eight character aptitudes (Intelligence, Detection, Agility, Strength, Toughness, Luck, Persuasion and Speed) and seven skills (Gunfighting, Quick Draw, Sneaking, Brawling, Survival, Tracking and Horsemanship).  Both books were carefully researched and include numerous historical footnotes to supplement the action.',1),(216,'Kobolds Ate My Baby!','This pair of brief and silly solitaire adventures, the first of which is an in-name-only parody of the first <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> book, are designed for use with first edition of the <a href=\"/Series/216\">Kobolds Ate My Baby!</a> role-playing game.',1),(217,'Warhammer Fantasy Fiction','This series of non-interactive novels and short story collections based on the popular miniatures game features a great deal of work by <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> veterans and <a href=\"/Series/2\">Life\'s Lottery</a> author Kim Newman, among others.',1),(218,'inebooks','These gamebooks are presented in an electronic format as executable programs for computers and handhelds.  They are stand-alone games, not files for use with eBook readers.  A few of them began life as paper gamebooks, but most are only available electronically.',1),(219,'Lands of Delorian','This series features a plot clearly inspired by <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a>: the reader is a warrior in training who must set off on a mission after a destructive attack by the forces of evil.  The game system also has echoes of <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a>, with the reader\'s character gaining skills over time and being forced to perform careful inventory management.  The most unusual part of the rules is the fact that randomization is achieved by flipping through the book and looking at numbers on the corners of the pages; these numbers are not equivalent to any sort of die rolls, instead being multiples of 25 from 0 through 100.  The series was initially released in book form by a print-on-demand service.  Before the third book was published, the series was withdrawn from the market and reworked, eventually reappearing as part of the electronic <a href=\"/Series/218\">inebooks</a> line.',1),(220,'Last Book, The','This self-described \"allegorical fantasy\" is among the few gamebooks aimed directly at an adult audience.  In it, the reader\'s goal is to reach the Mountain\'s Peak in order to cure an Epidemic; this sounds like a standard fantasy quest, but since it\'s allegory, the whole thing is considerably more abstract and surreal than it sounds.  The book is just over two hundred pages long, and each page contains a single section of text ending in a decision.',1),(221,'Lazer Tag Adventures','This series began publishing immediately after the first <a href=\"/Series/79\">Endless Quest</a> series ended on its thirty-sixth volume, and it is essentially an extension of that series in terms of length, writing style and layout.  The difference, of course, is setting; rather than using the world of one of TSR\'s role-playing games, these books instead take place in a distant future in which Lazer Tag is an intergalactically popular sport.  All of the adventures feature the same characters, but each title allows the reader to control a different athlete.  The decision-making doesn’t generally have anything to do with the game of Lazer Tag, though, since the intrigue-oriented plots instead revolve around events taking place outside of the sport.  As with many later TSR gamebook series, more titles were announced for release than actually ended up hitting store shelves.',1),(222,'Legends of Skyfall, The','This series of interconnected gamebooks, inspired by the author\'s <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> campaign, is set in a fantasy world inhabited by the occupants of a crashed spaceship, thus blending the two most popular gamebook genres.  Its system contains the usual components: three attributes (Expertise, Vitality and Fortune), simple combat resolution and inventory collection.  What sets it apart is that instead of using dice, players must flip coins to determine random numbers.  Whenever coins must be tossed, a formula is presented showing how many coins to flip and what to do with the results; this usually involves addition or subtraction of the number of heads or tails, sometimes also bringing attributes into the picture.  It\'s not the most mathematically sound of systems, but it is novel.',1),(223,'Lemmings Adventure Gamebooks','These books are based on the popular computer puzzle games of the same name, and the basic gameplay is similar – Lemmings are incredibly stupid but can be assigned different tasks which they will single-mindedly perform.  Victory can only be achieved by using the right kinds of Lemmings at the right moments.  There are close to fifty different Lemming jobs listed in the rules of each book.  Both books use six-sided dice for randomization and allow players to keep track of items collected.  There are some differences, though – the first book involves collecting eight medallion pieces while the second involves amassing Victory Points and Mystery Points.',1),(224,'Lifegames','These gamebooks are unusual in that they are aimed at adults and allow choices to be made about various career paths and other real-life situations.  Some offer male protagonists while others offer females.  The books have an interesting format, with each section being given a letter and a number in addition to the regular page numbers.  The letter indicates how far into the story the reader is, while the number indicates which possibility has been chosen; since the stories keep branching outward, each letter has more numbers than the one before it; there\'s only one A section, but there are two B sections, four C sections, and so on.  The books are illustrated, but the artists are not credited.',1),(225,'Lightsaber Dueling Pack','This <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds Fantasy Combat Books</a>-style set simulates a lightsaber duel between Darth Vader and Luke Skywalker.  It was eventually recalled from the market due to an error in game design, but it was available long enough for copies to still exist in collectors\' hands.',1),(226,'Living Adventure Books','Aimed at ages seven through eleven, these religious gamebooks involve the reader in Biblical events and provide \"fun facts\" from the Bible in the back.',1),(227,'Magnamund Companion, The','This book is a companion volume to the <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> and <a href=\"/Series/339\">World of Lone Wolf</a> series, describing the world and inhabitants of the popular gamebooks.  In addition to background information, the book also includes two interactive features: a multiplayer board game set in a tavern and a brief gamebook casting the player as Banedon, a recurring figure in the <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> series, and allowing the reader to experience a different perspective on events from the first <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> adventure.  In the gamebook, the basic rules from <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> are used, but Banedon has magic spells instead of Kai Disciplines.  There is no Willpower score for spellcasting as in <a href=\"/Series/339\">World of Lone Wolf</a>; instead, certain spells drain Endurance points.',1),(228,'Lone Wolf (1984-1998)','The books in this series are among the most influential gamebooks ever published.  The second great British success (after <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>), this series offers a distinctly different style of writing and gameplay.  The first twelve books form a continuing storyline in which the reader controls Lone Wolf, the last survivor of an order of warrior-monks known as the Kai, as he grows in power and wisdom and overcomes his nemeses, the Darklords of Helgedad.  Books thirteen through twenty follow the character\'s further development as a Kai Grand Master, and the final eight titles allow the reader to create a new Kai character, a member of the New Order established as the result of Lone Wolf\'s endeavors.  There were originally supposed to be twelve books in the New Order series, but at the urgings of the publisher, the series came to a somewhat rushed close at book number twenty-eight.  Before this happened, though, the series managed to spawn several spin-off books: <a href=\"/Series/339\">The World of Lone Wolf</a>, a four-gamebook cycle set in the southern part of Magnamund, the fantasy land in which Lone Wolf lives; <a href=\"/Series/227\">The Magnamund Companion</a>, a guidebook to all things <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> which includes a brief gamebook adventure; and <a href=\"/Series/149\">Legends of Lone Wolf</a>, a series of novels retelling the plotline of the first eight books of the main gamebook series.\n\n<p>The Lone Wolf books feature a particularly elegant and enjoyable game system, the basics of which are quite straightforward.  There are two attributes to keep track of (Combat Skill and Endurance), there\'s a limit to how much equipment can be carried around (eight backpack items, two weapons, fifty coins and a variety of \"special items\" carried outside of the backpack), and there\'s a simple combat system in which combatants compare Combat Skill scores, generate random numbers, and refer to a simple table until one or the other runs out of Endurance points.  The system is made most interesting by the fact that the reader must choose the main character\'s special abilities from a list of options such as Animal Kinship, Tracking or Sixth Sense.  Completing books successfully allows new skills to be added, and all of the skills are used frequently during gameplay, either by allowing special actions to be performed or by modifying the rules in various ways (Healing, for example, recovers lost Endurance as time passes, while Hunting prevents the reader from being forced to carry food around).  Later books in the series offer more advanced skills and add a few minor additional details to the rules.\n\n<p>The first twelve Lone Wolf books were published in England by Sparrow Books, which became Beaver Books during the run of the series.  Fairly early on, Hutchinson released <a href=\"/Item/1398\">The Lone Wolf Adventures</a>, a hardback volume containing the first two gamebooks.  The sixteen books following the first twelve (along with reprints of their predecessors, including an omnibus edition of the first two titles) were put out by Red Fox.  In the United States, the series was picked up by Pacer, which printed faithful reproductions (with different covers) of the first two volumes.  Pacer was absorbed into Berkley shortly thereafter, and the quality of the American editions slowly declined from that point on.  Berkley\'s editions reduced the original books\' color maps to black and white, and when Berkley got around to releasing books thirteen through twenty of the series, it actually printed abridged editions with many sections of text removed.  The final eight books were never released in the United States.  In 1999, after all English-language editions of Lone Wolf had gone out of print, Joe Dever announced that he wished to encourage the creation of free Internet editions of his work.  For this purpose, <a href=\"http://www.projectaon.org/\">Project Aon</a> was formed, and the group slowly but steadily began creating authoritative, corrected online versions of the gamebooks.\n\n<p>The efforts of <a href=\"http://www.projectaon.org/\">Project Aon</a> eventually bore fruit, as they helped fuel the revised and expanded <a href=\"/Series/1106\">Lone Wolf Reissues</a> that appeared starting in 2007.\n\n<p>A completely new 29th book was announced from Holmgard Press in 2016. See the <a href=\"/Series/1106\">reissues</a> listing for this and newer titles.',1),(229,'Lone Wolf: The Roleplaying Game','This multiplayer, d20-system role-playing game is based on the <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> gamebooks but takes place fifty years prior to their storyline.  The original gamebooks are also being adapted for solitaire play with these new rules and released as freely downloadable PDF files.',1),(230,'Lone Wolf Citadel Miniatures','',1),(231,'Interface Success in the Fast Lane Programming Series','This computer programming series featured a book which includes a brief gamebook as an example before delving into technology-driven adventure gaming.',1),(232,'Star Wars Role-Playing Game','This popular role-playing game makes use of the D6 System to allow players to adventure in the world of the popular movies.  Both editions of the game, as well as a later boxed set that included a simplified version of the rules, contained introductory solitaire adventures. There was also a solitaire adventure contained in one of the game\'s supplements, as well as a single standalone solitaire module.',1),(233,'Fighting Fantasy: The Introductory Role-Playing Game','This game adapts the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> system to multi-player role-playing.  It was eventually replaced by the more complex <a href=\"/Series/234\">Advanced Fighting Fantasy</a> line.',1),(234,'Advanced Fighting Fantasy','This role-playing game was Penguin\'s replacement for the earlier <a href=\"/Series/233\">Fighting Fantasy: The Introductory Role-Playing Game</a>.  Around the time of its release, some of the supplements for that previous game were reprinted in a slightly different format.\n\n<p>A second edition of the game was released in 2011 by Arion Games/Cubicle7, prompting more reissues (and some revisions) of earlier supplements (as well as some new products). These editions are available in hardback and soft-cover editions. Some are also available in pdf format.\n\n<p>A Limited Edition hardback single volume numbered and signed edition containing the 2nd edition core AFF Rulebook, Out Of The Pit, and Titan: The Fighting Fantasy World was also printed in December 2016.\n\n<p>As of 2017, Arion Games also started to produce full-colour sets of gaming miniatures (numbered AFMxxx) designed for use with the series. These were supplied as either .pdf or .ppt files for the purchaser to print his- or herself.\n\n<p>In 2017, a sci-fi version of the rules was also produced entitled Stellar Adventures. Several modules and reference aids have been subsequently released to accompany the sci-fi game.',1),(235,'Fighting Fantasy Novels','These novels spun off from the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> gamebooks.',1),(236,'Fighting Fantasy: d20 System','These multiplayer-oriented role-playing adventures are adapted from the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> gamebooks.  The books were first released in paperback by Myriador, then reissued as eBooks through a Greywood/DrivethruRPG collaboration.',1),(237,'Star Wars: The Lost Jedi','This pair of gamebooks, also released in a single-volume omnibus, cast the reader as Havet Storm, a fugitive Jedi fleeing bounty hunter Boba Fett.  The two books have connected plots, and it is possible to carry a character from one to the other.  Characters have six attributes (Strength, Speed, Blaster, Lightsabre and Tech Skills); at the start of play, the reader is given twenty points to distribute among these attributes, and some of these may be saved and used as \"Jedi Power Points,\" which allow special actions to be made during play.  Combat involves combining dice rolls with attributes, though Jedi Power Points can be used to help out.  Equipment is handled in an interesting fashion – all inventory items come with a paragraph number, and at the end of each adventure, there is a \"Data Bank\" of special sections that contain item descriptions which may be consulted at any time.  The Data Bank also includes other kinds of information, and it is sometimes necessary to check off Data Bank paragraph numbers on the character sheet for future reference.  The character sheet also features a \"Time Track\" which is used at one point during the mission.  Strangely, despite the American origin and enormous popularity of <i>Star Wars</i>, these books were never printed in the United States.',1),(238,'Lost World: Jurassic Park Role-Playing Game Book','This large, thin volume contains three short interactive adventures and two sheets of punch-out cards (24 in total).  For each adventure, the player gets to pick a card featuring pregenerated character from the movie.  Different characters have different scores in four attributes: Agility, Strength, Perception and Mind.  These values are added to rolls of two six-sided dice and compared to target values in order to resolve actions during play.  There is also a DNA card which features a random event table.  The remaining cards feature information on dinosaurs and plants which can provide hints during play.',1),(239,'Warhammer 40,000 Fiction','This series of non-interactive novels based on the science fiction miniatures game includes some work by gamebook veterans.',1),(240,'Dark Future Fiction','This series of non-interactive books, based on the Games Workshop post-apocalypse miniatures game, are written primarily by Kim Newman, the author of <a href=\"/Series/2\">Life\'s Lottery</a>.  To make matters confusing, the last novel shares a title with the short story collection; the contents of these two volumes are different, however.',1),(241,'Taran Trilogy, The','Designed by <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> author R. A. Montgomery, this multimedia CD-ROM series never made it past the first volume.',1),(242,'Ral Partha Miniatures','Several miniatures from this company inspired early books in the <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds</a> series.',1),(243,'Runesword Novels','This series of fantasy novels features the work of numerous gamebook authors and inspired a number of <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds Fantasy Combat Books</a>.',1),(244,'Lucky Les','This book is one of the earliest examples of interactive fiction, allowing the reader to determine the fate of a cat named Les by making choices and turning pages.  A broad range of adventures can be had, from facing pirates to farming.  It was first published by Anthony Blond in 1967; a revised edition was released by Brockhampton Press in 1974.',1),(245,'Macho Women with Guns','One supplement for this silly role-playing game included a solitaire adventure.',1),(246,'Maelstrom','This role-playing game features the same size and basic format as a <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> book, but it\'s primarily designed as a multi-player RPG set in sixteenth-century Europe. It\'s obvious that a lot of historical research went into the game, the game system is basic and familiar (point-based character creation, percentile-based attribute checks for performing actions), the setting is effective, and a wide variety of possible character professions is available. The book includes a brief solitaire adventure to get players started.\n\n<p>The book was reissued as an eBook by Arion Games.  Arion also released several new accessories (though no additional solo adventures as of this writing).',1),(247,'Magic Mystery','These thin, large-format mystery gamebooks feature numerous full-color illustrations throughout.',1),(248,'Magic Road, The','These fairy tale inspired gamebooks are printed in a large, full-color format.  Rather than having consecutively-numbered pages, they instead feature multiple color-coded sections, each with five numbered pages.  Thus, the reader is faced with options like \"go to page green 5\" or \"go to page red 1,\" with each textual option being accompanied by an appropriate icon.  This color-coding allows even pre-literate children to explore the books.',1),(249,'Magic Voyage','These large-format, full-color paperbacks feature large illustrations and small boxes of text on each of their less than fifty pages.  They put the reader in command of critical decisions in various futuristic missions.',1),(250,'Make a Simple Wish of Your Very Own','This brief book is based on the film <i>A Simple Wish</i>.  In it, the reader plays a lonely child who has just moved to New York and ends up meeting a girl with a magic wand.  It follows the format of the <a href=\"/Category/56\">Find Your Way</a> product family.',1),(251,'Make Believe It\'s You','This series of easy-to-read gamebooks takes its inspiration from popular fairy tales.',1),(252,'Make It Happen','This pair of books, presumably released through the school market rather than ordinary channels, fairly closely resembles entries from contemporary series like <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a>.',1),(253,'Make Your Dreams Come True','This series, aimed at teen girls, provides a series of interactive romance novels, each around 200 pages long.  The amount of text between choices is often quite long, but the books are more interactive than the nearly trivial <a href=\"/Series/460\">Turning Points</a> series.\n\n<p>Books 1 and 4 in this series have first-person narration while 2, 3, 5 and 6 have third person narration.',1),(254,'Double Diamond Triangle Saga','This nine-part serial novel, set in the Forgotten Realms of <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> fame, was presumably inspired by the success of Stephen King\'s serialized <i>The Green Mile</i>.  It also features a gamebook-like characteristic: although all nine volumes can be read one after the other, it\'s also possible to read different subsets of the series in order to have slightly different narrative experiences.  This fact isn\'t actually documented in any of the books, but it is mentioned on several websites.',1),(255,'Make Your Own Adventure','These slim volumes tell mild, fairy-tale-like fantasy stories.  The books are heavily illustrated in full color, with text generally taking up only the bottom quarter or so of each page.',1),(256,'Making Choices','This is a general gamebook series like <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> except that it was produced by a Christian publisher, meaning that the adventures feature numerous moral and ethical decisions and contain religious overtones of varying degrees of subtlety.  Most of the books stand alone, but Donna Fletcher Crow\'s entries in the series have some continuity, as they all revolve around a professor who has discovered time travel.',1),(257,'Manage Your Own Baseball Team','This simple gamebook allows the reader to make decisions which influence a baseball game.',1),(258,'New Worlds','This science fiction magazine contained a short interactive story in one issue.',1),(259,'Maps: The Uncollected John Sladek','This science fiction short story collection contains two short interactive tales, both written in the late 1960s.  The first, <a href=\"/Item/1711\">The Lost Nose</a>, is a previously unpublished humor piece written for the author’s then-girlfriend.  The second, <a href=\"/Item/1709\">Alien Territory</a>, is reprinted from issue 195 of the <a href=\"/Series/258\">New Worlds</a> science fiction magazine.',1),(260,'Marvel Super Heroes','This comic book role-playing game spawned a single solo adventure.',1),(261,'Marvel Super Heroes Adventure Gamebooks (American)','This series of gamebooks uses a system similar to that of the <a href=\"/Series/21\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons Adventure Gamebooks</a>, which isn\'t surprising, since it\'s also a spin-off of a TSR role-playing game.  In each volume, the reader controls one or more characters from Marvel comics.  Each character comes with a set of pre-defined attributes which are combined with dice rolls and compared with target numbers in order to perform actions.  Characters also have Health Points (which are self-explanatory) and Karma Points (which change as a result of good or bad deeds and which may be spent to improve dice rolls).  The books were first published in the United States by TSR and then several were reprinted in a different order by Puffin in the UK.',1),(262,'Silsilat arwa\' al-mughamarat [سلسلة أروع المغامرات]','This Arabic series, published in Iraq, combines titles from multiple English series.',10),(263,'Choose Your Own Clark Adventures','These humorous <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> parodies were printed by students of Clark University.  Although print copies sold out almost instantly (only a thousand exist for each title), the books were available on the web for quite some time.  The first four titles were full-length books, but the fifth \"adventure\" was an insert included in the fourth book.',1),(264,'Attention Span Stories','This series of thin booklets was a fairly early experiment in interactive fiction.  It was designed to help reluctant young readers to gain an appreciation of books by appealing to their short attention spans.  Each two-page spread contains a full-color picture and a page of text.  At the end of each page of text, three choices offer different possible cliffhanger conclusions.  The reader turns to the page of the cliffhanger that he or she finds most interesting, and then the tale proceeds.  Since the reader chooses what happens rather than what the protagonist does next, the feel is different from that of most gamebooks.  Each booklet\'s story is accompanied by some extra material such as teaching guidelines, reading comprehension exercises and even stickers of the illustrations which allow readers to record their paths through the story for future reference.',1),(265,'Masters of the Universe Adventure Game Book','This small hardcover gamebook is based on the Masters of the Universe cartoon and action figures.  It features an extremely simplistic game system; the reader has only one attribute (Life Points), and a six-sided die is used to generate random numbers from time to time.  The combat system is entirely random but more interesting than one might expect; whenever He-Man gets into a fight, a table is presented.  The reader rolls the die, and either the battle is won, Life Points are lost, or the next roll is modified in some way.  Victory is not difficult to achieve, but there is considerable replay value thanks to multiple successful endings.  Although several storybooks were released using this volume\'s format, it is the only interactive offering published.',1),(266,'Maxi-Solo','These fantasy adventures, a follow-up to the <a href=\"/Series/272\">Mini-Series</a>, were designed to be adaptable to any role-playing system and include only vague rules guidelines.  Each is printed on single-sided, hole-punched, unbound sheets of paper; clearly these were amateur publications.  Sections are laid out as in early <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> modules, with each page containing sections marked with the page number followed by a letter.',1),(267,'Meet Me at the Fair','This amateurish-looking book, bound in copy-shop style, describes itself as \"a <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> story,\" though it is most definitely not officially associated with that series. The story was also released in computer game format.',1),(268,'Mega-Mag','This magazine contains scenarios for superhero role-playing games, and it offered a couple of solo adventures during its run.',1),(269,'Mercenaries, Spies, and Private Eyes','This adventure/espionage role-playing game, a relative of <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a>, inspired a few solitaire adventures.  Some material is now available in electronic format.',1),(270,'Middle-earth Quest','These interactive adventures based on J. R. R. Tolkien\'s <i>Lord of the Rings</i> are among the most complex gamebooks ever published, many using hex maps to allow great freedom of movement and all featuring about thirty pages of rules in each volume.  Fortunately for those who don\'t like complexity, the rules are divided into levels.  The basic rules use a pre-created character and offer simple rules for fighting, moving and gathering items.  The advanced rules add the need to keep track of the passage of time and flesh out other elements of the system.  Additional optional rules are provided for creating characters (which have three basic attributes, a variety of skills and, in some adventures, the option to cast spells), gaining experience, and calculating combat results by formula rather than by table.  Finally, guidelines are provided for using the books as solitaire adventures for the full-fledged <a href=\"/Series/271\">Middle-earth Role Playing</a> system.\n\n<p>The publication of the series was plagued by legal problems.  Iron Crown Enterprises, the publisher of Middle-earth Role Playing, had a license with Tolkien Enterprises to produce games based on Tolkien\'s work.  They then forged a partnership with Berkley to publish these gamebooks.  Two volumes came out in 1985 under the <b>Tolkien Quest</b> label, and an additional two were planned for 1986 under the new series title of <b>Middle-earth Quest</b>.  Unfortunately, around the time that the fourth book was to be released, Tolkien Enterprises deemed the books in violation of the game license, which didn\'t include permission to print books.  The first four books were recalled and destroyed, with the fourth book never even making it to market (though it was pictured in an advertisement published in <a href=\"/Series/118\">Dragon</a> #103).  Some copies of the first three books do still exist, but they are quite rare.  Several years after this incident, legal issues were resolved and publication began anew.  Books five through eight were actually numbered one through four in denial of the existence of the earlier volumes.  The series came to an end, however, before all of the volumes announced in the original 1985 and 1986 releases could see the light of day.\n\n<p>Because of all of the publication confusion, the numbers on this site do not necessarily reflect the numbers printed on the books themselves -- they instead reflect the original intended order of the series.\n\n<p>Some books in the series were printed in two different versions: a Berkley-oriented version with the Berkley logo on the spine and a sequential series number on the front cover, and an Iron Crown Enterprises version with the ICE logo on the spine and the ICE product code on the front cover.',1),(271,'Middle-earth Role Playing','This Tolkien-inspired role-playing game includes a booklet which contains a brief solitaire adventure using the QuestGame rules found in the <a href=\"/Series/270\">Middle-earth Quest</a> gamebooks.  The author and illustrator of this booklet are uncredited.  The booklet was provided as a pull-out section in some later (1985) printings of the first edition rulebook and as a separate item in the second edition boxed set.',1),(272,'Mini-Series','This series contained amateur, generic solitaire adventures for use with fantasy role-playing rules.  It was followed by the similar <a href=\"/Series/266\">Maxi-Solo</a> series.',1),(273,'Quicksilver Fantasies Solo-Adventures','This (apparently unauthorized) piece of software contains simplified versions of all of the <a href=\"/Series/272\">Mini-Series</a> and <a href=\"/Series/266\">Maxi-Solo</a> solitaire adventures.  Role-playing elements have been removed, leaving only the basic interactive text.',1),(274,'Miss Adventure','These fairly thick books apply the gamebook formula to more adult-oriented material, with the reader taking the role of a young female who finds herself having a variety of adventures and misadventures, sometimes of a somewhat risque nature.',1),(275,'Mr. Buckethead Adventure Game Series','This series of interactive comic books allows the reader to control the actions of Mr. Buckethead, a superhero with a bucket for a head who also happens to be the star of some low-budget video productions.  In the books, Mr. Buckethead collects Fun Points, Action Points, Major Victory Points, money, equipment and friends, and the reader must keep track of these things.  Fun Points serve as hit points, requiring Mr. Buckethead to go on vacation (at the cost of some money) whenever they run out; Major Victory Points indicate the completion of missions (and only one may be earned in each volume); Action Points may be spent to perform special actions (which vary from book to book); and the rest are self-explanatory.  Action Points are handled by an unusual mechanic in which the reader flips ahead a given number of sections and looks for an icon. If an icon matching the power that he or she is trying to use is there, the action is successful; if not, it fails; either way, a point is spent.  The books actually have no endings, so gameplay lasts indefinitely, and it is possible to move from book to book from the first section of each volume.  Although not as complex as the multi-book format of the <a href=\"/Series/145\">Fabled Lands</a> series, this does offer greater-than-usual flexibility.  Further diversity comes from the inclusion of numerous puzzles and mini-games in each book, including the Monster Fight Game, a multi-player battle game which resembles a greatly simplified version of the <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds Fantasy Combat Books</a>.',1),(276,'Police Files / Spy Files','',1),(277,'John F. Antal\'s Tactical Books','This series of books, written by a former military officer, allow the reader to participate in military operations, learning about the realities of combat in the process.  The books have no game system, but they do make use of dice rolls from time to time.  The interactive portion of each book is supplemented by background information, and blank flowcharts are provided so readers can record their paths through the book for future reference.  The books were originally released as large trade paperbacks, but a special smaller version of the first book was included as a bonus with a collection of computer war games released by Strategic Simulations, Inc.  The books have no official series title; the title used here is purely descriptive.',1),(278,'Monkey God\'s Curse, The','This early solitaire adventure was designed to be used with an unspecified role-playing game (though <a href=\"/Series/20\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> seems the most likely candidate).  No rules are included, though a pregenerated character and wandering monsters are provided, and there are guidelines for which types of characters may be brought in (levels four through seven, and only possessing one magic item).  The book is only twenty-four pages long, and it follows the early <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> format of designating its sections with a page number and a letter.',1),(279,'Monster Horrorshow, The','This light horror role-playing game was sold as a gamebook-style paperback.  Before running the game, the game master (called the \"WereWizard\") has to play through a brief solitaire adventure included with the rules.',1),(280,'Multiple-Ending Bible Adventures','',1),(281,'Mystic Knights of Tir na Nog','This series, based on the Saban television show of the same name, gives the reader control over the decisions of a group of fantasy warriors.  Although there aren\'t any rules and the choices are few and far between, there is a greater degree of continuity between the books than there usually is in this sort of series.  Each book includes a glossary to help inexperienced readers.',1),(282,'Mythworld','',1),(283,'Nancy Drew/Hardy Boys Be a Detective Mystery Series','This series applies Wanderer\'s <a href=\"/Category/58\">Plot-Your-Own / Plot-It-Yourself</a> style to the popular Nancy Drew and Hardy Boys mystery licenses.  Despite the popularity of the subject matter, the final two volumes of the series went unpublished.',1),(284,'Narnia Solo Games','These books, set in the world of C. S. Lewis\' famous fantasy novels, allow the reader to visit the land of Narnia.  They feature a game system in which the reader may either choose a pregenerated character or create one by assigning points to six skills: Fighting, Trickery, Action, Talking, Perception and Inner Strength.  These skills are then combined with rolls of two-sided dice to determine success or failure at various actions.  It is also necessary to keep track of treasures and equipment and to occasionally check off \"keys\" which serve as codewords to track clues found and actions taken.  The series ended before all of the announced titles could be released leaving only the first five books in print.',1),(285,'Nightmares!','These simple horror gamebooks were released as part of the glut of juvenile horror fiction released in the mid-nineties as the result of the phenomenal success of the <b>Goosebumps</b> books by R. L. Stine.  The only feature which makes them slightly unusual is that each book starts by describing its cast of characters and providing some background information on the plot, similar to the <a href=\"/Series/305\">Pick Your Own Dream Date</a> series.  Unfortunately, this introductory material is generally redundant and adds little or nothing to the adventure that follows.',1),(286,'Nintendo Adventure Books','These books, based on the <i>Super Mario Brothers</i> and <i>Legend of Zelda</i> Nintendo games, feature many visual puzzles like mazes and word searches and also require their readers to keep track of inventory items and coins or points.  Difficulty and complexity vary somewhat from book to book, with some titles requiring considerably more effort to complete than others.  The books were published nearly simultaneously in the United States and United Kingdom.  It appears likely that at least some of the author names used here are actually pseudonyms for teams of writers; the books all include a \"series development\" credit for Dan Oehlsen, Lary Rosenblatt and Barbara Stewart, and some titles also include \"text by\" credits for Richard Chevat (books nine and twelve) or Roger Peckinpaugh (book ten).',1),(287,'Sonic the Hedgehog Adventure Gamebooks','This series is inspired by the Sega video game line of the same name, in which Sonic the Hedgehog and his friend Tails battles the ever-scheming Dr. Robotnik and his evil robot creations.  The rules are very much inspired by their electronic predecessor.  In each book, the reader has six attributes to deal with: Speed, Strength, Agility, Coolness, Quick Wits and Good Looks.  The values of these attributes are determined by the reader, who must assign five points to one attribute, four points to another, three points to a third and two points to the remaining three.  To complete various tasks (including combat), the reader must roll a single die, add the roll to the relevant attribute and compare the result to a target number.  Failing at an action frequently leads to Sonic losing all of his accumulated rings (which are found throughout the books) or one of his three lives (if he has no rings left to lose).  Collecting one hundred rings gives Sonic an extra life, but this is generally not an easy task.  In addition to collecting rings, various inventory items can be found and used in the books.  In most books, the reader has control only of Sonic, with Tails showing up from time to time to help out.  In the second book, however, Tails has a more active role (and his own character sheet complete with attributes and inventory), and the narrative frequently switches between the two characters.\n\n<p>A <a href=\"/Series/1376\">different series with the same name</a> was also released by Ladybird, aimed at younger readers.',1),(288,'Nintendo Power','One issue of this official Nintendo magazine included a solitaire adventure based on one of the video game system\'s first role-playing games.  The adventure features similar gameplay to the cartridge, with the reader keeping track of Hit Points, Magic Points and inventory items while fighting monsters, but the story ends in the middle since it is designed to encourage readers to purchase the video game.',1),(289,'NorthCoast RolePlaying','This magazine included a solitaire adventure for the <b>Cyberpunk</b> role-playing game.',1),(290,'Oink','This silly British comic book (allegedly edited by pigs with an anti-butcher agenda) featured gamebook parodies in a couple of issues.',1),(291,'1 on 1 Adventure Gamebooks','Each story in this series was released as a boxed set containing two books.  The books are designed for two players, with each person taking a different book and controlling different characters.  Most of the stories are set in the worlds of TSR role-playing games (mainly <a href=\"/Series/20\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons</a> and <a href=\"/Series/260\">Marvel Super Heroes</a>), though the second book is a generic space opera.  The rules vary from book to book, but in general, players take turns moving from place to place and collecting items by reading passages in the books, occasionally fighting when they happen to end up in the same location.  Some books are extremely tactical in feel, with players sending different characters into different locations to set traps for enemies; others are more story-oriented.  Either way, there is plenty of player interaction, because in addition to fighting the other player, each participant must also frequently act like a game master in a role-playing session, controlling monsters encountered by his or her opponent or, in the case of the particularly role-playing-oriented seventh set, acting out the parts of various personalities encountered in the text.  None of the books require the use of dice; most require the players to simultaneously shout out random numbers and then cross-reference them on a chart, while one book instead uses paper-rock-scissors.  Some of the books can be adapted to be played by a single person, though the adventures that benefit the most from player interaction really do require two people.',1),(292,'Miscellaneous Works by the Oulipo','In 1960, a French literary group known as the Oulipo was formed. The group\'s mission was to explore the role of form in literature and to develop new forms in the process. Before the decade was up, their experimentation led them to invent the gamebook form (under the name \"tree literature\"). The Oulipo\'s interactive writings were not the first of their kind, but they came so early that it is likely they were one of several simultaneous inventors. Within the Oulipo, the idea was proposed by François Le Lionnais and first implemented by Raymond Queneau in his short story <a href=\"/Item/1846\">Un conte à votre façon</a>. The idea of applying this form to theater was later used by Paul Fournel and Jean-Pierre Énard in <a href=\"/Item/1847\">The Theater Tree: A Combinatory Play</a>.',2),(293,'Miscellaneous Works by the Oulipo','In 1960, a French literary group known as the Oulipo was formed. The group\'s mission was to explore the role of form in literature and to develop new forms in the process. Before the decade was up, their experimentation led them to invent the gamebook form (under the name \"tree literature\"). The Oulipo\'s interactive writings were not the first of their kind, but they came so early that it is likely they were one of several simultaneous inventors. Within the Oulipo, the idea was proposed by François Le Lionnais and first implemented by Raymond Queneau in his short story <a href=\"/Item/1846\">Un conte à votre façon</a>. The idea of applying this form to theater was later used by Paul Fournel and Jean-Pierre Énard in <a href=\"/Item/1847\">The Theater Tree: A Combinatory Play</a>. Both of these works were translated into English and published in the anthology <a href=\"/Item/1848\">Oulipo: A Primer of Potential Literature</a>, first released by University of Nebraska Press and later reprinted in paperback by the Dalkey Archive Press with updated biobibliographies.',1),(294,'PageQuest','This gamebook system is designed so that each adventure can be fit on a single sheet of paper, printed as a three-fold, two-sided pamphlet.  Players create their characters by distributing ten points between two attributes (Stamina and Magic).  Spellcasting is achieved by scratching off runes corresponding with the desired spell from a \"Tome of Magic\" (a grid of runes).  While exploring, players copy drawings of rooms onto a map grid, gradually building up a complete map of the area they are in; some of these room drawings include numbers which direct the reader to text encounters.  When combat is engaged, a strategic diceless system is employed.  The first (and so far only) adventure was released at GenCon 97, and it is freely redistributable, so copies can be downloaded from the Internet, photocopied for friends or received by sending a self-addressed, stamped envelope to the author.',1),(295,'Space Gamer / Fantasy Gamer','This magazine included a solitaire adventure for <a href=\"/Series/296\">Paranoia</a> in one issue.',1),(296,'Paranoia','This humorous role-playing game, in which the players are cloned \"troubleshooters\" attempting to survive in a conspiracy-laden world overseen by a paranoid and vindictive computer, spawned several solitaire adventures, one in the basic rules, one as part of a group adventure module, and one in the pages of <a href=\"/Series/295\">Space Gamer / Fantasy Gamer</a> magazine.  The solitaire adventure in the rulebook is the same in both the first and second editions, but the second edition version adds an additional paragraph for clarification.',1),(297,'Participatory Mystery','This carefully-researched gamebook allows the reader to try to solve a real-life crime in the aftermath of the Great Fire of London.  The book is in trade paperback format and contains more than 160 text sections in 230 pages. The second book was mentioned as being in production but has never been released, if it was ever even written.',1),(298,'Party of One','This series of fantasy gamebooks uses the <a href=\"/Category/87\">d20 System</a> introduced in the <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> Third Edition role-playing game. Each book is designed around a pre-created character, and the adventures are rules-light enough that external rulebooks really aren\'t necessary for play. The books are sold in an electronic format; they can be played on a computer or printed out and read in the traditional fashion.',1),(299,'Rangers of Taradoin, The','These three large, thick trade paperback books were published by an amateur author through a print-on-demand service.  Clearly inspired by <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a>, the books include a complex game system with multiple character options, lots of skills to choose from, character advancement, combat, inventory management and the ability to use the information within as a multiplayer role-playing game.  The books feature a fairly unique structure, being broken into many chapters, each of which is essentially a mini-gamebook of approximately thirty to forty sections.  In spite of their interesting characteristics, the books are marred by editorial errors, and their amateur origins are hard to miss.',1),(300,'Pegasus','This game magazine included a <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> adventure in one issue.  This issue was eventually made available in electronic format through RPGnow.',1),(301,'Peter Pan Adventure Game, The','This gamebook, inspired by the popular children\'s novel, follows the format introduced by <a href=\"/Series/39\">Enid Blyton\'s Famous 5 Adventure Games</a>.  It includes a custom die, a folded map and eight cards representing special items.',1),(302,'Phase VII','This role-playing game includes an introductory solo adventure.',1),(303,'Beginning to Read','This series of easy-to-read books aimed at young children includes a few titles which allow their readers to choose the ending.',1),(304,'Pick-a-Path','Just as <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> had <a href=\"/Series/74\">Choose Your Own Adventure for Younger Readers</a> and the <a href=\"/Series/29\">Which Way Books</a> had the <a href=\"/Series/491\">Which Way Secret Door Books</a>, so <a href=\"/Series/319\">Twistaplot</a> had <a href=\"/Series/304\">Pick-a-Path</a>.  Although the connection isn\'t explicitly mentioned on the books, the shared publisher, release dates and styles suggest a fairly clear relationship.  As with <a href=\"/Series/319\">Twistaplot</a>, many genres are covered, and some, but not all, of the books feature puzzles like mazes and rebuses to augment the decision-making.  One book even includes a top that can be cut out and spun to generate random results in the adventure!',1),(305,'Pick Your Own Dream Date','These romance gamebooks are aimed at a fairly young audience and have only two unusual features: they begin with character lists, much like the <a href=\"/Series/285\">Nightmares!</a> horror gamebooks, and at certain points in the story they offer \"But Wait!\" boxes which give the reader an opportunity to go back and change their mind about a decision before the story has come to an end.',1),(306,'Play-It-Your-Way Sports Books','These simple gamebooks are a spin-off of the <a href=\"/Series/29\">Which Way Books</a>, and they follow its basic format while being somewhat more verbose.  The odd-numbered titles let the reader control a baseball team known as the Bombers, while the even-numbered titles revolve around the American football-playing Warriors.  Each of these subsets of the series form a trilogy in which (if optimal endings are reached) a team first qualifies for, then wins, a major sporting event, ending with the team defending their title.',1),(307,'Plot-It-Yourself - A-Team','This pair of interactive books based on the A-Team television series is somewhat unusual in that it is written in the third person and choices are generally presented in a vague, abstract format.',1),(308,'Editorial Andrés Bello: Science Fiction Gamebooks','Editorial Andrés Bello is a Chilean publisher of Spanish-language books for children. Only those books in their children\'s series which are interactive are listed below. These are science fiction books set in a distant future and aimed at young children. The books have no game-system, only choices, and every text section has a title that briefly describes the action in that section, a fairly unique idea. These books have not been translated into English.',6),(309,'Pokémon','This full-color, large-format gamebook, a small part of the output resulting from the massive Pokémon marketing phenomenon, allows the reader to control the actions of Ash the Pokémon trainer as he searches for a new addition to his collection of miniature monsters.  The book is written at a very basic level, lacking any game mechanics and avoiding the development of a very complex plot.  The book appeared a little too late to catch the full force of Pokémon mania, and it thus failed to inspire any follow-up volumes.',1),(310,'Super Powers Which Way Books','This <a href=\"/Series/29\">Which Way Books</a> spin-off features the same basic format as its parent series but feature stories revolving around superheroes from DC comics.  The name of the series underwent a bit of tweaking at the very beginning; the first book was originally published as a <b>DC Super Heroes Which Way Book</b>, but later books and subsequent reprints of the first title made <b>Super Powers Which Way Books</b> the official series title.',1),(311,'Zork','These books, based on the popular Infocom computer game series of the same name, are part of the <a href=\"/Category/76\">What-Do-I-Do-Now</a> collection of gamebook series.  The books are written in the third person and follow the adventures of Bill and June, two schoolchildren who are magically transported to the land of Zork, where they become young adventurers Bivotar and Juranda.  There are no game mechanics here beyond the requisite choice-making, though there are a few interesting features.  Each ending has a score associated with it, and most offer the opportunity to go back and try the other option; only one ending in each book is victorious.  Additionally, some books in the series feature sections designed solely to punish readers who might be tempted to cheat.\n\n<p>The <b>Zork</b> adventures were first published in the United States and Canada by Tor, but Puffin issued three slightly larger-format British editions in 1984.\n\n<p>Several years after its original release, the third book in this series was made freely available over the Internet (though this version can only be read in a web browser).',1),(312,'Défis et sortilèges','These books consist of two distinct sets, each containing four interlinked books.  Each book gives its reader control of a different character, and the books may be played alone or with up to four people simultaneously exploring the same territory.  Characters come precreated with five attributes: Initiative (which determines the order of play), Combat (which determines the effectiveness of attacks), Magic (which determines the strength, not the quantity, of magical power), Aura (a measure of charisma) and Vitality (which serves as hit points).  At certain points in the adventures, players are asked to choose from the \"book of heroes.\"  This allows them to pick an attitude (friendly, aggressive, etc.) to take in a particular situation, adding a role-playing element to the proceedings.  Choosing from the book of heroes also allows players to earn power points which may be spent to increase their powers or access new areas of the game.  \n\n<p>The books are quite non-linear, with readers visiting locations one by one and checking them off on the map when all possibilities are exhausted.  In multiplayer games, when two players simultaneously enter the same location, they may either fight or team up.  Although this series is not the first to allow multiple players to participate, it is one of the more intricate examples of the idea.\n\n<p>The series was originally published by Gallimard and was reissued by Scriptarium starting in 2023, though in a different order.',2),(313,'Légendes et maléfices','This was originally envisioned as a four-book series.  In each of the first three books, the reader would play a different character (a disgraced warrior-prince, a thief and a wizard) and in the final volume, it would have been possible to pick any of these characters as the protagonist.  Unfortunately, the publisher aborted the series after the first entry, so only one volume exists.  The book’s game system features several interesting ideas.  Players have four attributes: Constitution, Strength, Dexterity and Intelligence.  Constitution serves as hit points and is equal to the sum of the other three attributes (whose values are determined by point distribution).  As Constitution lowers due to injury, the other attributes decrease as well (with their points lowered according to the reader\'s preferences).  There are no random numbers used in the book; when a character must perform an action, his or her relevant attribute is compared to a difficulty table, and this determines success or failure.  The reader must carefully manage the character\'s attributes in order to be able to succeed at critical points.  Players must also keep track of gold and inventory, and in a final interesting touch, certain rooms may be searched, leading to the acquisition of items from a \"search and discoveries table.\"',2),(314,'Leyenda ëlfica','This Spanish-language gamebook series, the title of which means \"Elfish Legend,\" was originally available online in HTML and Microsoft Word format.  Although it started as an amateur effort, expanded print editions of some of the titles eventually became available.  The series was developed using the <a href=\"\"\"http://www.sandman.connectfree.co.uk\">Game Book Player</a> freeware program by Paul Jordan, and it features a fairly standard-issue game system reminiscent of that found in the <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> series.  The author tries to give his work a larger context, so his webpage provided a map of Valsorth, the game\'s world, and a novel.  The numbered titles below tell a continuing story; the unnumbered title is a stand-alone gamebook set in the same world as the saga.',6),(315,'Saga du Prêtre Jean, La','This series casts the reader as Prester John the Crusader as he searches for the secret to immortality in Shangri-La, traveling around the world and through time in the process.  The game system is fairly familiar, with two randomly-generated attributes (Strength Points and Life Points), a limited inventory, the need to keep track of gold and food, and a straightforward \"roll the dice, add your Strength and compare the results\" combat system made more dangerous by the possibility of instant death on certain rolls.  The reader can carry Prester John\'s attributes and possessions from book to book, but because publication ceased before the last three books were finished, it is impossible to complete his mission.  Despite being incomplete, this is one of the most successful gamebook series to be written in a language other than English; translations into many languages were made, though English editions never appeared.',2),(316,'Almanaque Disney','This comic included some translations from the Italian <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a> magazine.  One story was later reprinted in <a href=\"/Series/431\">Disney Especial</a>. Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',7),(317,'Plot-Your-Own Horror Stories','This series of interactive horror stories anticipated the mid-nineties horror boom by over a decade.  It follows the same basic format as the other <a href=\"/Category/58\">Plot-Your-Own / Plot-It-Yourself</a> series by Wanderer: slightly oversized paperbacks containing straightforward branching-plot stories complete with backwards references at the top of each entry leading back to all of the pages that could have led there.  Probably the most unusual feature of the series is that quite a few choices determine not what the reader\'s character does, but rather what will happen next; for example, rather than asking whether or not to open a door, a choice may ask what will be behind it!  Obviously, this doesn’t make for a great deal of plot consistency.\r\n\r\n<p>Starting with book five of the American release published by Wanderer, the series changed its name from <b>Plot-Your-Own Horror Stories</b> to <b>Plot-It-Yourself Horror Stories</b>.  However, when six titles (all but books six and eight) were reprinted in the United Kingdom by Magnet, they kept the <b>Plot-Your-Own Horror Stories</b> label even though half of them had their main titles altered for some reason.  Collectors will also be interested to know that Julian Messner library editions of several titles were issued.',1),(318,'Prince of Shadows','The year after he ended his collaboration with Joe Dever on the <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> books, Gary Chalk started this series.  In it, the reader controls the destiny of Edrix, a street-smart thief who is actually rightful heir to the throne of a fantasy kingdom under the rule of a tyrant.  Mechanically, the books are quite similar to the <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> series.  You carry your character from book to book, gaining skills and items along the way. There are two different kinds of skills (Princely Skills and Street Skills), and it is randomly determined at the start of the first book which type you have more of. Also randomly determined is your Strength, which serves as both your combat skill and your hit points. Combat is straightforward, involving adding your Strength to the value of your weapon and any applicable skill bonuses, then subtracting your enemy\'s armor value (if any) and attempting to roll under the resulting number on percentile dice (or, if dice are unavailable, using a <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> style random number table) in order to hit. There are some problems with vagueness in the rules (fighting multiple enemies is unclear, and it\'s not explained whether or not you can exceed your initial Strength value), but it\'s a pretty workable system overall.  Where the series diverges from <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> (and indeed from the vast majority of published gamebooks) is in its format – rather than being a standard paperback, each <a href=\"/Series/318\">Prince of Shadows</a> adventure is a large-format, saddle-stitched, 64-page booklet with a fairly elaborate layout.  This is an attractive change of pace, but it seems likely to have increased the expense of producing the books, which may help to explain why the series was cut short so prematurely.',1),(319,'Twistaplot','These books were Scholastic\'s answer to <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a>.  Like that series, the books cover a wide variety of genres and are aimed at a fairly young audience.  However, several entries in the series distinguish themselves from the more straightforward <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> books by including some simple puzzles and elements of random chance (thinking of a random number, for example).  The <b>Twistaplot</b> name was also used for a series of simple computer games included on Scholastic\'s <b>Microzine</b> electronic magazine, and the series spawned a spin-off for younger readers called <a href=\"/Series/304\">Pick-a-Path</a>.  While the series ended in 1985, three of R. L. Stine\'s books (titles 1, 4 and 9) were reissued with new covers (by uncredited artists) in 1994 and 1995, probably due to the success of his <b>Goosebumps</b> series.  Indeed, when Stine created an interactive <b>Goosebumps</b> spin-off (<a href=\"/Series/82\">Give Yourself Goosebumps</a>) it had something of a <b>Twistaplot</b> tone to it.',1),(320,'QAGS: Quick Ass Game System','QAGS is a quick and silly RPG designed partly as a joke and partly as a fast, cinematic game system. Although no stand-alone solitaire adventures have been released for the system, the main rulebook does include a couple of (entirely unplayable and joke-oriented) solo quests.',1),(321,'Risus: The Anything RPG','Risus: The Anything RPG is a free role-playing game which can be found online. Characters in the game are literally defined by clichés, and these clichés are used the way attributes and skill are in other games. The game is distributed freely on the Internet, and it has inspired three different solo adventures, all of which are also free (although the first is also available in an expanded print edition).  To grab a copy of the rules, you can visit the game\'s <a href=\"http://www222.pair.com/sjohn/risus.htm\">official site</a>. The first of these adventures is a reworking of a solo originally written for the <a href=\"/Series/191\">GURPS</a> system.',1),(322,'Miscellaneous Works by Jason Shiga','Jason Shiga is a self-publishing comic author with a love for unusual structural forms. Fortunately for gamebook fans, this means that he has produced a number of interesting interactive comics. Several of these can be acquired at reasonable prices from <a href=\"http://www.shigabooks.com/\">the author\'s web site</a>.',1),(323,'Sindbad le Magnifique','This single volume, the start of an aborted series, casts the reader as the famous sailor.  It features a fairly standard game system with two randomly-generated attributes (Dexterity and Constitution) and the need to keep track of both money and a limited inventory (ten items, including meals).  The book contains several references to the better-known <a href=\"/Series/315\">La Saga du Prêtre Jean</a> series, with which it shares some authors.',2),(324,'Slave Traders of Ganox, The','This simple gamebook pits the reader and his or her scientist uncle against an evil race of alien slave traders known as the Ganoxians.  It takes the form of a nearly square saddle-stitched booklet, and it contains entirely straightforward gameplay.  A separate activity book (not an interactive gamebook) containing crosswords and other puzzles tied in with the story was also released.',1),(325,'Sniper! Adventure Gamebooks','These books started life as tie-ins to TSR\'s espionage role-playing game, <b>Top Secret</b>, which also inspired titles in the <a href=\"/Series/68\">Catacombs Solo Quest</a> and <a href=\"/Series/79\">Endless Quest</a>  series.  However, not long before being released, the <b>Top Secret</b> connection was broken and the series was re-themed as a tie-in to the <b>Sniper!</b> board game.  The books have an action/espionage flavor, and most feature stories contemporary to their release dates, though the fifth title is set during World War II.  All but the third adventure include gratuitous non-fiction essays.  The books use a variant of the standard format and game system found in most advanced TSR gamebooks of the eighties, though there are no tear-out bookmark character sheets to be found here.',1),(326,'Solve It Yourself','These continuity-free mystery gamebooks were released as one of Troll\'s four <a href=\"/Category/57\">The Choice is Yours</a> sets.  Each of these four-book sets offers the usual basic \"make a choice and turn the page\" gameplay and multiple endings.  The most unusual feature of the series is the fact that each section begins with a list of page numbers from which the reader could possibly have arrived.  Such backwards references are not unique to this collection of series (they\'re also found in the <a href=\"/Category/58\">Plot-It-Yourself and Plot-Your-Own</a> books published by Wanderer), but they are fairly uncommon.',1),(327,'You Can Be the Stainless Steel Rat','This book was written by Harry Harrison as an interactive tie-in to his popular science fiction novels about a futuristic thief known as the Stainless Steel Rat.  The title of the book isn’t very accurate, though, as the book doesn\'t actually allow the reader to take on the role of the Stainless Steel Rat; instead, the player works under his command while tracking down an evil scientist.  The book features no game system, though coin-flipping comes into play from time to time, and the challenge level is decidedly low, since it is actually impossible to fail the mission (though it requires some skill to avoid getting stuck going in circles, as in the <a href=\"/Series/78\">Time Machine</a> series).',1),(328,'Star Wars Episode I Adventures','This series followed the <a href=\"/Series/411\">Star Wars Missions</a> books, and it shares their mail-order distribution and their unusual linear format.  Like those books, the first delivery of this series consisted of a boxed kit containing dice (ten- and twenty-sided rather than the six- and twelve-sided of the original series), a rulebook, score sheets, a blank notebook, a set of cards and the first adventure.  This time, though, rather than featuring linear chapters and an interactive portion in a single volume, each adventure is split into two books: a linear novel that can be read completely by itself, and a separate gamebook which can optionally be used instead of the middle portion of the novel.  When using the gamebook, the reader reads the novel up to a point where he or she is referred to the gamebook, then runs through the interactive portion of the adventure.  As in the <a href=\"/Series/411\">Star Wars Missions</a>, this is a linear sequence of encounters that requires an initial selection of cards (in the same categories as in the <a href=\"/Series/411\">Star Wars Missions</a> but with a broader category of \"devices\" in place of weapons) and features a running score to keep things interesting.  After the interactive portion is done, the reader is referred back to the novel, where the story is concluded.\r\n\r\n<p>The fifteen books of the series make up four distinct storylines, each focusing on different characters and written by a different author (although Ryder Windham wrote two storylines).  The first three plots take place before the <i>Star Wars Episode I</i> film, and the final storyline is actually an adaptation of the movie itself.  With those final books, the series concluded, and nothing more was released even when <i>Star Wars Episode II</i> arrived in theaters.',1),(329,'Star Wars Gamer','This magazine was developed to support the <a href=\"/Category/87\">d20 System</a> <i>Star Wars</i> role-playing game.  It included one solitaire adventure during its run.',1),(330,'Star Wars Solitaire Adventures','This pair of books was released by the company responsible for the original <a href=\"/Series/232\">Star Wars Role-Playing Game</a>, and they resemble other items in that product line. Both of the books are large-sized paperbacks, but their contents vary somewhat from the average role-playing product. They are simple, <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a>-style books which have no rules or dice-rolling (though there are times when the book directs you to take notes, since early events can sometimes affect later choices; perhaps this is why the hype on the books\' back covers claims that they have \"[a] hidden game system that does what no other solitaire adventure can\" and goes on to mention that you can practically hear the blaster bolts, etc., etc.).',1),(331,'Starlight Adventures','This series was designed to be a <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> for girls; the books resemble <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> adventures and feature some of the same artists, but they have a blue color scheme instead of a green one.  Instead of fantasy adventures, they cover a variety of genres which all have two elements in common: female protagonists and mild hints of romance.  Also, while the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series features a common system shared by all of the books, these vary wildly from volume to volume.  The first and last books have no special rules and don\'t even use dice rolls, most of the others feature some mild score-keeping and/or dice-rolling, and the fifth book has a somewhat more complex system that uses three character attributes: Luck, Energy and Confidence.  Although the series wasn\'t a tremendous success, it does fall into a niche that no other series has exactly filled.',1),(332,'Starting Up','This book allows the reader to attempt to start up a new business, providing a learning experience in the process. There are no rules, so readers simply read through the book, flipping from chapter to chapter as the result of decisions. Because the book is about business, a financial summary listing net worth, revenues and expenses is given at the end of each chapter. Whenever the reader fails, he or she is given the opportunity to take the better path offered, and at the end of the book, the successful endings are ranked from best to worst. An index is included for readers who wish to find where in the book a particular concept was discussed.',1),(333,'Swordquest','On their covers, these books claim to be \"[s]uitable for use with <a href=\"/Series/20\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons</a> and other role-playing games.\"  Indeed, they use a detailed game system that resembles <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> right down to the six core attributes, and they feature high-fantasy plots in keeping with the tone of TSR\'s product.  The only difference is that only six-sided dice are used for randomization.  Each story stands alone and features a different hero, but they all revolve around battles with the minions of the Darklord, an evil being who rules a dangerous land behind an enchanted wall of mist.  Despite their apparent potential for use with <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a>, the books are peculiarly ill-suited for their complex game system.  The books feature only minimal interactivity during their almost entirely linear storylines, offering very little opportunity to make use of any rules.  Even though there are detailed character creation and advancement rules, the stories are so tailored to their protagonists that using a different character makes little sense.  The book which gives the reader the most to do is the third title, which adds clerical spell-casting to the rules; in the rest, interactivity is largely limited to protracted battles (which consist simply of rolling three six-sided dice again and again and checking them against target numbers).  The series is not without entertainment value, but it seems uncomfortable in its efforts to mix gameplay with storytelling.',1),(334,'Warhammer Warriors','These multiplayer combat gamebooks are nearly identical to the <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds Fantasy Combat Books</a>, though they are not officially based on them.  The main differences are in the subject matter (science fiction, based on the <i>Warhammer 40,000</i> game, rather than fantasy), the order in which each turn is carried out (only a subtle change) and the means of character advancement (winning battles can unlock special moves in this series, which is a little more interesting than the increasing experience points of <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds</a>).',1),(335,'Time Traveler','This series is a spin-off of the <a href=\"/Series/78\">Time Machine</a> books aimed at a younger audience.  It features the same essential structure and layout, but the historical figures and events covered are less exotic and the adventures themselves are shorter and lack some of their predecessors\' more advanced features like inventory selection and hints.',1),(336,'Transformers, The','These simple gamebooks are aimed at a very young audience and lack any sort of formal game system or rules.  The adventures in the series all stand alone (they were released as unnumbered volumes), and each casts the reader as a child who becomes involved with the Transformers in some way.  Interestingly, an American Transformers series featuring almost exactly the same format was released at the same time as these books as part of the <a href=\"/Category/86\">Find Your Fate</a> line.  The only real differences between the series are that the British books are first-person while the American ones are third-person and that the American books portray the Transformers in a manner more consistent with the animated films and television shows based on the toys.  Even though the British <a href=\"/Series/160\">Make Your Own Adventure with Doctor Who</a> books were reprinted as part of the <a href=\"/Category/86\">Find Your Fate</a> line, the British Transformers adventures were not similarly integrated and thus never made it to America, nor did the American <a href=\"/Category/86\">Find Your Fate</a> books see British publication.',1),(337,'What If? Post-war Choices in Singapore','This stand-alone gamebook allows the reader to make decisions about the future of Singapore following World War II, exploring various alternate histories in the process.  The book is illustrated with photographs and includes a bibliography.',1),(338,'What If Books','These straightforward interactive stories, aimed at ages eight through thirteen and similar in tone and content to the average adventure-oriented <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> book, were released by a Christian publisher.  Despite their origins, the religious content is fairly minimal and some of the humor is slightly twisted.  According to the author, two additional volumes were written and edited but never published.',1),(339,'World of Lone Wolf, The','This spin-off of the <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> series takes place in the southern part of the land of Magnamund and allows the reader to take the role of Grey Star, a mysterious young wizard who must eventually battle the evil Wytch-king Shasarak.  The books feature a game system nearly identical to that of their parent series, except that in place of Lone Wolf\'s Kai Disciplines, Grey Star uses Magical Powers, and these powers drain points from a new attribute, Willpower.  Combat, the need for food, and inventory management remain unchanged, though one of the Magical Powers, Alchemy, gives the reader access to an herb pouch that can store certain small items in addition to those kept in the backpack.  There is some character advancement during the four-book story arc, though new skills are not gained between books two and three.\n\n<p>The series was reprinted in a hardcover <a href=\"/Series/1937\">Collector\'s Edition</a> starting in 2017.',1),(340,'Du är en rymdspion','',11),(341,'Champions','One edition of this game\'s rules includes a solo adventure.',1),(342,'Fantasy Hero','The first edition of this game includes a solo adventure in the rulebook.',1),(343,'Justice, Incorporated','This role-playing game includes a solo adventure in the rulebook.',1),(344,'Men in Black Roleplaying Game','This game includes a solo adventure in the rules.',1),(345,'Metabarons','This role-playing game includes a solo adventure with the rules.',1),(346,'Plot-It-Yourself Adventure Stories','This series of movie tie-ins replaced the <a href=\"/Series/349\">Plot-Your-Own Adventure Stories</a> series.  Apart from the name change, it is identical to its predecessor.  The books were first published in the United States by Wanderer, then both were reissued in the United Kingdom by different publishers.',1),(347,'Plot-It-Yourself - Love Boat','',1),(348,'Plot-It-Yourself - Magnum, P.I.','This television spin-off gives the reader control over the actions of the hero.  It follows the same basic format as Wanderer\'s various other <a href=\"/Category/58\">Plot-It-Yourself / Plot-Your-Own</a> series.',1),(349,'Plot-Your-Own Adventure Stories','This series of movie tie-ins was eventually replaced by the <a href=\"/Series/346\">Plot-It-Yourself Adventure Stories</a> series.  Only the first book in the series featured illustrations.',1),(350,'Powerpuff Girls Plus You Club, The','These slim gamebooks were only released in the United States as part of a mail-order subscription club, much like the <a href=\"/Series/411\">Star Wars Missions</a> and <a href=\"/Series/328\">Star Wars Episode I Adventures</a>.  Unlike those series, though, they do not feature rules or a continuing storyline.  Each book focuses on a particular character, and colored (green, blue, magenta, etc.) rather than black ink is used for the internal text and illustrations.\n\n<p>In some parts of Asia, the books were released as flip-books combining two adventures into a single volume.',1),(351,'Polyhedron','This newsletter included a solo adventure for the <a href=\"/Series/205\">Indiana Jones Role-Playing Game</a> in one issue.',1),(352,'Prime Directive','This <i>Star Trek</i>-inspired RPG included a solitaire adventure in one of its supplements.',1),(353,'Proteus','This series of gamebooks was actually published as a monthly magazine; however, apart from the unusual format and the inclusion of advertisements, it resembles any other line of British fantasy gamebooks.  The rules, which make use of six-sided dice (sets of which were included attached to some issues),  are very much in the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> vein, though their exact details vary from issue to issue.  They generally include a few main attributes, the need to manage inventory items, and spells or other abilities.  A miniature reprint of the first issue was attached to the front cover of the ninth issue, while the fifteenth issue included a reprint of the sixth issue along with a bonus adventure called \"In Search of Christmas.\"  Regular publication stopped at issue nineteen, but a twentieth volume was released as a \"special\" containing an original story plus a reprint of issue eight.',1),(354,'Psycho-Paths','',1),(355,'PuzzleQuest','',1),(356,'Aukeratu zeure abentura','',12),(357,'Whatever You Want','This novel for adults allows the reader to choose either a male or female role and search for romance (or alcohol... or something else) in London.  The book was first published in the United Kingdom by Pan, then reissued in paperback and eBook formats in the United States by HarperCollins.',1),(358,'keuze is aan jou, De','',13),(359,'Kies je eigen avontuur','Due to the way in which <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> was first published, with the two Lippincott hardbacks followed by the Bantam paperbacks, the series ended up also getting two separate releases in the Netherlands.  Van Holkema & Warendorf published translations of the two hardbacks, which are the two unnumbered items listed below.  At roughly the same time, M & P Boeken began printing a series of six numbered paperbacks, which are also listed below.',13),(360,'Kies je eigen avontuur - Avonturen van de jonge Indiana Jones','',13),(361,'Journal de Mickey, Le','This Disney-themed comic book contained two interactive stories based on <i>The Black Cauldron</i>.  Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',2),(362,'Donald Duck','This Dutch Disney comic reproduced one of the <i>Black Cauldron</i> stories from the French <a href=\"/Series/361\">Journal de Mickey</a>.  Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',13),(363,'Dungeons & Dragons (Dutch)','',13),(364,'Fantasy Avonturenboeken','The first four of these books featured original cover art by uncredited artists; the last two recycled the original British covers.',13),(365,'Nintendo kies je eigen avontuur','',13),(366,'Topolino','This Italian Mickey Mouse magazine contained some interactive Disney comics, called \"Storie a Bivi\" (Crossroads Stories).  Some of these adventures were reprinted many times both in translation and in other Italian publications.  Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',5),(367,'Paperinik e altri supereroi','This Italian Disney comic book about a superpowered Donald Duck featured an original interactive strip along with some reprints from <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a>.  One story was actually printed twice during the magazine\'s run.  Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',5),(368,'Pockets 2e reeks','This comic included a translation from the Italian <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a> magazine. Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',13),(369,'Pockets 3e reeks','This comic included a translation from the Italian <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a> magazine. Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',13),(370,'Quark','This quarterly science fiction anthology, published in standard paperback format, contained a satirical interactive comic strip in its fourth issue.',1),(371,'Quasits & Quasars','This magazine included solo adventures for <a href=\"/Series/20\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons</a> and <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a>. It published at least one joint issue with <a href=\"/Series/1844\">DragonLords</a>.',1),(372,'Quest Game Books','This series actually consists of two \"school packs,\" each containing six short booklets.  The first set follows the adventures of Kern the Strong while the second involves Kern\'s friend Oss.  There\'s not really a game system to speak of in the adventures, though the reader is encouraged to take notes along the way.\r\n\r\n<p>The first set was reissued by Scribos Ltd. as interactive CD-ROM software (still aimed at the school market) several years after the release of the books.',1),(373,'Miscellaneous Works by Edward Gorey','Just one of popular artist Edward Gorey\'s surreal, humorous picture books featured an interactive element.',1),(374,'Rally','',1),(375,'Dark One, The','This amateur <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> spin-off, published online in Microsoft Word and HTML format, casts the reader as an evil Drakkar, offering a different perspective on adventuring in the world of Magnamund.',1),(376,'Reading in Action - Choose Your Own Story','Although these books are from Denmark, they are written in English.  They were designed as teaching aids.',1),(377,'Real Life Gamebooks','These gamebooks allow the reader to experience the events of various historical periods, from the eleventh century through the nineteen-eighties, first-hand.  In each book, the reader creates a character by distributing fifty points between seven different attributes and skills (which vary from book to book, since some involve the use of equipment specific to the time).  These skills are then tested against a roll of two six-sided dice whenever they must be used.  In most of the books, the reader must also keep track of Wounds, which are equal to half of his or her Strength attribute; in the adventures with modern weaponry, however, a single hit is enough to kill the main character, so tracking Wounds is unnecessary.  The books were first published in England, where they were simultaneously released in hardback (by André Deutsch) and paperback (by Dragon); a couple of years later, three titles were released in the United States by Berkley Pacer under new, shorter titles.',1),(378,'Reboot Adventure Games','This short-lived gamebook series was based on an animated television show.  They feature a simple game system which sometimes relies on six-sided die rolls and requires the user to keep track of Access Codes (indicating that a useful item or clue has been found) and Game stars (a general measure of success).',1),(379,'Ren and Stimpy Special (American), The','The third issue of this comic book based on the Nickelodeon cartoon series was interactive, allowing the reader to make choices about the flow of the plot and occasionally requiring him or her to write down codewords that influence later parts of the story.  Like a surprisingly large number of interactive comic books, it claims to be the first of its type even though it is not.',1),(380,'Robo Force','This short-lived gamebook series, based on a line of toy robots with moving limbs and suction bases, details the battles between the heroic Robo Force and the villainous Nazgar, a commander of evil robots.  Each full-color paperback volume is thirty-two pages long, with simple text, relatively few choices and an emphasis on visual puzzles.',1),(381,'Role Aids','This series was a line of unauthorized <a href=\"/Series/13\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons</a> supplements produced by Mayfair Games; some of the <a href=\"/Series/13\">AD&D</a> terminology has been changed, presumably for legal reasons.  Just one entry in the series was a solitaire adventure in which the reader must defeat an evil wizard.  The adventure very nearly contains enough rules to be played on its own without additional books, though under combat it does make a vague reference to \"consult[ing] the appropriate charts in the standard rule books to determine Hit and damage results.\"  The adventure itself is fairly non-linear, requires the player to use a pregenerated character included in the book, and includes both wilderness and dungeon portions complete with random encounters.',1),(382,'RollerCoaster Tycoon','These books, written in the third person, are based on the computer game of the same name.  The first sticks with the game\'s theme, allowing the reader to affect the design of an amusement park, while the others are simply set around roller coasters.',1),(383,'RuneQuest: Slayers','Originally intended for publication by Avalon Hill, this role-playing system, a completely new spin on the classic <a href=\"/Series/112\">RuneQuest</a> game, was cancelled when that company was bought out.  The complete rules were eventually released for free online by the designers.  The main rulebook includes a solitaire adventure designed to introduce the game system.',1),(384,'Sagas of the Demonspawn','These four fantasy gamebooks cast the reader as Fire*Wolf, a barbarian with both fighting and (after a little while) magical talents.  The books feature a continuing storyline in which character stats are carried from book to book, and the game system they use is fairly typical.  Characters have seven attributes (Strength, Speed, Stamina, Courage, Luck, Charm and Attraction) which are rolled randomly; the sum of these attributes determines Life Points.  There are also Power points for casting spells and Skill points for measuring experience.  Combat is resolved, as usual, by lots of dice-rolling.  Somewhat unusually, inventory gathering is not a large part of gameplay.  Also interesting is the inclusion of hints in the backs of the later books, printed backwards to prevent accidental reading.',1),(385,'Scream Shop Pick Your Path','This series of horror gamebooks is written on about the same level as the earlier <a href=\"/Series/82\">Give Yourself Goosebumps</a> books, though they take themselves a bit more seriously.  The stories all revolve around objects purchased from Sebastian Cream\'s Junk Shop, a mysterious store located in the town of Bleakville.  Each adventure stands alone, but some characters reappear from book to book, so there is a certain sense of continuity to the series.  The books lack interior illustrations, but the cover illustrator is given prominent credit in each book, a somewhat uncommon occurrence.',1),(386,'Aventuras em Patópolis','One issue of this publication included a translation from the Italian <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a> comic.  Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',7),(387,'Car Wars aventura no solo','',7),(388,'Shadis Magazine','One issue of this magazine contained two <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds</a> characters printed in its margins and featured on its cover.',1),(389,'Sherlock Holmes Solo Mysteries','These mystery gamebooks allow the reader to enter the world of Sherlock Holmes, though they don\'t allow control of Holmes himself – he is merely a character who sometimes shows up during play.  Two six-sided dice are used for randomization, and players have the choice of either using a pre-created character or building their own by distributing bonus points between six attributes: Athletics, Artifice, Observation, Intuition, Communication and Scholarship.  During play, the reader is frequently prompted to note down codes for clues, deductions and decisions.  These codes can then affect the outcomes of subsequent sections.  At least nine books were planned for the series, but only seven were released in English; the eighth book, though unreleased in its native tongue, did see publication in translated editions in several countries.  The ninth book never saw the light of day in any form.\r\n\r\n<p>Some books in the series were printed in two different versions: a Berkley-oriented version with the Berkley logo on the spine and a sequential series number on the front cover, and an Iron Crown Enterprises version with the ICE logo on the spine and the ICE product code on the front cover.',1),(390,'Défis fantastiques (1983-1997)','All but one of the original <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> books were translated into French, released in a slightly different order from the original British editions; an unofficial translation of the one missing title has been made available on the Internet.  Most of the French editions feature the same artwork as the original British editions, though some variant art was used.  The first title has been released in at least four editions, two featuring Peter Andrew Jones\' original painting (but one covering most of it with a solid gold color, being a special edition), another featuring his second painting, and a third (published by Time-Life rather than Gallimard) with an entirely different piece not seen in English  releases but still credited to Jones.  Many other titles were also reprinted multiple times with variations to their cover layouts, some with completely new artwork by French artists.\n\n<p>When the <a href=\"/Series/12\">Fighting Fantasy Wizard Books Series 1</a> reissues were released in English, the French series was reordered to match -- see <a href=\"/Series/1202\">Défis fantastiques (2004-)</a> for details.',2),(391,'Lobo Solitario','These books were first published by Altea starting in 1985, then reissued by Timun Mas starting in 2003.  Each release features its own unique cover artwork, and the Timun Mas reissues also replace all of the interior artwork.',6),(392,'Solo Dungeon, The','This early solitaire adventure was unofficially intended for use with the <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> role-playing game.',1),(393,'Solve It Yourself... The Mystery Squad','These books, which have no relation to the American <a href=\"/Series/326\">Solve It Yourself</a> series, allow the reader to control the actions of the Mystery Squad, a group of children who solve crimes.  The books are written in the third person and feature numerous puzzles and brain-teasers; solving these and making correct deductions score points for the reader, and at the end of each adventure, the reader is given a rating based on his or her score.',1),(394,'Sorcerer\'s Apprentice','This is the official magazine of <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a>, and many issues included solitaire adventures for that game.',1),(395,'Lone Wolf Merchandise','The <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> gamebooks spawned a variety of merchandise....',1),(396,'Lone Wolf Club Newsletter','This brief newsletter was offered to members of the official <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> club.',1),(397,'Lone Wolf Software','Three computer games were released using the <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> license.  The first two, published by Hutchinson, were adapted from gamebooks, while the third, from Audiogenic, featured an original storyline. An additional adaptation (Lone Wolf and the Ice Halls of Terror) was advertised in 1985 but was never released. \"Software Pack\" editions of the first two games were also produced featuring the game cassette and the Sparrow edition of the gamebook in a slipcase. The Mirror of Death came in a boxed set with a free copy of The Plague Lords of Ruel gamebook.',1),(398,'Sorcerer\'s Scrolls, The','This <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> fanzine printed several solitaire adventures.',1),(399,'Lord of the Rings Adventure Game','This role-playing line was designed for a game master and multiple players, but in spite of this, it was written in a completely pre-programmed, gamebook-like fashion highly suitable for solitaire play.',1),(400,'Star Wars Adventure Journal','This magazine supported the <a href=\"/Series/232\">Star Wars Role-Playing Game</a>.',1),(401,'World of Indiana Jones, The','This is the second role-playing game adaptatation of Indiana Jones.  It can be used with two of West End Games\' rule systems: D6 or MasterBook.',1),(402,'Sports Illustrated for Kids: Play Book!','This pair of simple gamebooks allows the reader to make critical decisions during the course of a sports game.  It was followed four years later by another series, <a href=\"/Series/403\">Sports Illustrated for Kids: You Call the Play</a>.',1),(403,'Sports Illustrated for Kids: You Call the Play','These books were the second set of <i>Sports Illustrated for Kids</i> gamebooks, following the <a href=\"/Series/402\">Sports Illustrated for Kids: Play Book!</a> Series.  Like their predecessors, they allow the reader to control decisions during sports games.  There are no special rules to supplement the decision-making, and the books are written in the format of running dialog by two commentators.  Illustrations show the positions of the players in the game at any given time.',1),(404,'Star Challenge','In this series, the reader takes on the role of a space adventurer living in the year 2525.  The books are fairly straightforward, having only one gimmick: each ending has a score associated with it, and only a few allow the reader to rank as a \"Space Ace.\"  The first six books were reprinted in England with new cover art.',1),(405,'Donjons & dragons, niveau avance','',2),(406,'1 Contre 1','The first book in the series featured new cover artwork, but the rest reproduced the original American covers.',2),(407,'1789','This extremely long adventure allows the reader to take on the role of one of ten different characters and experience the drama of the French Revolution.',2),(408,'aventure a partager: Duel, Une','',2),(409,'Confidentially Yours','This series casts the reader as an advice columnist.  Each book contains several letters describing romantic dilemmas; at the end of each letter, the reader picks one solution and reads what happens as a result.  These books are no more interactive than the <a href=\"/Series/460\">Turning Points</a> series, but they offer somewhat more decision-making opportunities by including multiple stories in each volume.',1),(410,'Book of Wizardry, The','This book claims to teach wizardry in twenty-two lessons.  It also includes \"The Wizard\'s Adventure,\" a gamebook adventure which makes use of the information provided in the lessons.  The tone and game system are highly reminiscent of <a href=\"/Series/188\">Grailquest</a>.',1),(411,'Star Wars Missions','This unusual series was released as a mail-order book club promoted through schools.  Members first received a boxed kit containing two dice (one six-sided, one twelve-sided), a rulebook, a pad of score sheets, a blank notebook, some cards, a small poster and the first adventure in the series.  Subsequent packages contained additional books, cards and posters.  Club members also received a magazine called <i>Star Wars Kids</i>.\n\n<p>Each book contains several linear chapters followed by the actual gamebook segment.  Upon reaching the interactive portion of an adventure, the reader must first select some cards (representing characters, vehicles, weapons and powers) based on criteria specified in the book at hand.  Unlike most gamebooks, these adventures don\'t feature numbered sections, nor do they instruct the reader to turn to particular pages; instead, they are composed of a linear sequence of events.  Each event is described, then several choices are listed.  The reader must select one of these choices and then skip ahead to find the matching paragraph describing the results of the choice.  Readers are sometimes forced to revise their decisions before proceeding, but no choice ever leads to a premature ending.  Since victory is inevitable, gameplay revolves around a score kept as a running total throughout the adventure.  Rolls of the dice and values printed on the cards are frequently used to raise or lower this score, and while it has no impact on gameplay, it can be considered a measure of success.\n\n<p>After twenty adventures based on the original <i>Star Wars</i> trilogy, the series came to a close and was replaced by the similar <a href=\"/Series/328\">Star Wars Episode I Adventures</a>.',1),(412,'Star Wars Starfighter Battle Book','This pair of combat books uses a system reminiscent of <a href=\"/Series/18\">Ace of Aces</a> to simulate starship combat.',1),(413,'Starblazer','This comic book featured a couple of interactive issues.',1),(414,'Fantasy Warlord','This fantasy miniatures wargame was co-designed by Gary Chalk and features lots of his artwork, including many pieces reproduced from <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> and <a href=\"/Series/318\">Prince of Shadows</a> gamebooks.',1),(415,'Last Battledroid, The','This stand-alone gamebook allows the reader to equip and control an ancient and powerful giant robot using a fairly complex game system.',1),(416,'Elige tu propia superaventura','These translations feature original cover art by uncredited artists.',6),(417,'Encuentra tu propio misterio (Hermes)','',6),(418,'Your Party Needs You','This hardback gamebook, published in the same format as the <a href=\"/Series/377\">Real Life Gamebooks</a>, casts the reader as a voter interested in British politics.  No dice are used, but the player must fill out a character sheet before playing which determines background information like employment status, residence and gender.',1),(419,'Neither Either nor Or','This surreal book closely follows the <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> format right down to the graphic design, but it is aimed at adults.',1),(420,'Search for the Golden Acorn','Released to tie in with a contest celebrating the centenary of the British National Trust, this book challenges the reader to solve fifty different puzzles (most of them visual) during the course of an interactive time travel story.  The answers to these puzzles could then be used to fill out an entry form that could be mailed in for a chance to win a prize.  The contest closed on December 31, 1995.',1),(421,'Escolha sua aventura','Publication of the series passed from Tecnoprint to Ediouro somewhere in the early nineties.',7),(422,'Steeleye and the Lost Magic','This tiny, full-color hardcover book, published as part of a \"Win a Disney Holiday\" competition, deals with an ancient raven named Steeleye in search of a Hero of Destiny to restore life and magic to a valley left barren by the death of an old wizard.  The reader must prove his or her worth as this Hero by exploring and solving puzzles; an entry form in the back of the book urges the true Hero to finish the sentence \"The message I shouted to Steeleye is...\" in order to win the vacation.',1),(423,'Stephen Thraves Adventure Gamebooks','This series picked up where the <a href=\"/Series/424\">Stephen Thraves Compact Gamebooks</a> left off; the books are similar in style but have a different physical format than their predecessors.  The books are standard paperback size rather than compact, the paper quality is lower, and there are no fold-out flaps (though score cards and \"adventure kit\" items are printed in full color on the inside covers).',1),(424,'Stephen Thraves Compact Adventure Gamebooks','These gamebooks were designed to be portable so that they could be played in the car or in other situations where a pair of dice and a photocopied character sheet might be inconvenient.  Thus, they are designed to be playable without randomization, and they contain all of the necessary accessories on special fold-out flaps attached to the front and back covers.  Although most accessories vary from book to book, every adventure includes two full flap pages printed with thirty to forty small score cards for tracking character attributes and inventory.  The cards are printed many times so that the book can be played again and again even without an eraser, and in one adventure (<a href=\"/Item/2461\">Murder in the Dark</a>), these cards actually determine the paragraph number that provides a solution to the book\'s mystery, allowing a different outcome on each readthrough.  In addition to the cards, the fold-out flaps feature maps, codebooks and other inventory items that the reader may come across during play, thus serving the same purpose as the \"gamekits\" included with Stephen Thraves\' larger-format works.  The adventures all stand alone in terms of plot.  Towards the end of its life, the series featured a format and name change and became the <a href=\"/Series/423\">Stephen Thraves Adventure Gamebooks</a>.',1),(425,'Stepping into the Bible','This relatively thin book allows the reader to participate in a variety of stories taken from the Old and New Testaments of the Bible.  Notes are included in the back to show where in the Bible the stories were drawn from.',1),(426,'Resident Evil Novels','Many entries in this series of non-interactive novels based on the popular video game series were written by the daughter of occasional <a href=\"/Series/78\">Time Machine</a> author </Person/1295>Steve Perry</a>.  The remaining two titles are actually adapted from the movies based on the games rather than being based directly on the games.',1),(427,'Stormbringer','This role-playing game, derived from the <a href=\"/Series/112\">RuneQuest</a> game system, is based on Michael Moorcock\'s Elric writings.  Although only two solitaire adventures were written for the system, they saw print many times.  One first appeared in <a href=\"/Series/110\">Different Worlds</a> magazine, then was reprinted in a supplement where it was joined by a second adventure.  Both of these eventually made their way into the main rulebook after a couple of editions.',1),(428,'Escolhe a tua própria aventura','',7),(429,'Escolha sua aventura - Infantil','',7),(430,'Aventuras mitológicas','',7),(431,'Disney Especial','This comic included a translation from the Italian <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a> magazine.  The same story was previously published in <a href=\"/Series/316\">Almanaque Disney</a>.',7),(432,'Strive to Survive','These religious gamebooks cast the reader as a child living in Biblical times, with each adventure highlighting a different \"Spiritual Building Block.\"  Notes on these religious concepts are included at the end of each volume.',1),(433,'Sum-Way Books','These gamebooks were designed to reinforce math education, so arithmetic problems are frequently integrated into the stories, sometimes even determining which page is turned to next.',1),(434,'Super Eye Adventure','This is a fairly standard series of basic gamebooks with one twist: at several points in each book, the reader is asked to examine a three-dimensional stereogram image (of the variety popularized by the <i>Magic Eye</i> brand) in order to inform his or her decision-making.',1),(435,'Super Sherlock','In these mystery gamebooks, the reader must keep track of the progress of his or her investigation in a journal using a simple game system.  A solution to the entire mystery is presented at the end of the text.  Despite being originally written in English, these titles never saw publication in that language, instead being eventually released in French translation.',1),(436,'Survival','These extremely brief large-format gamebooks allow the reader to take on the roles of various wild animals.  Although aimed at young children, the books do include a mechanism for keeping score – certain sections award points, and readers are encouraged to compare scores with one another to see who did the best; in the case of a tie, the player who used the fewest moves to achieve his or her score is the winner.',1),(437,'Taletwisters','These simple gamebooks closely resemble the <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventures</a> in size and format.  They are fairly rare, however, coming from a small publisher in Iowa.',1),(438,'Teenagers from Outer Space','This role-playing game included an introductory solitaire adventure in its second edition rulebook.',1),(439,'Thrilling Adventures in Space','This series, a science fiction follow-up to the <a href=\"/Series/66\">Castle of Grom Trilogy</a>, is designed as an educational tool to aid reading.  As such, it consists of short sections written with simple language and features a basic game system that requires the reader to track equipment and Life Points and which uses an extremely simplistic combat system in which a single roll of two dice determines victory or defeat.',1),(440,'Time Lord','This simple role-playing game, the second to be based on the <i>Doctor Who</i> television series, contains a brief solitaire adventure.  Unfortunately, the adventure is just a brief fragment of a longer (and presumably unwritten) story.',1),(441,'TORG','The original version of this role-playing game inspired a single solitaire adventure and a handful of non-interactive novels. Many of the products in this line have been reissued electronically in PDF format. When the product line was revived as TORG: Eternity, another solitaire adventure was produced.',1),(442,'Total!','This British Nintendo game magazine once included a solo adventure.',1),(443,'Tower of Elbrith','This solitaire module was designed for use with the role-playing game of the reader\'s choice.',1),(444,'Tower of Indomitable Circumstance','This role-playing adventure, designed to be easily adaptable to multiple game systems, was mistakenly listed as a solitaire adventure, but it is actually a standard group role-playing adventure that can optionally be played by a game master and a single player.',1),(445,'Planescape Novels','These non-interactive novels were based on the Planescape <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> setting and the computer game that it inspired.',1),(446,'Infocom Novels','This series of non-interactive novels consists of adaptations of classic text adventure computer games.',1),(447,'Birthright Novels','These novels are based on a relatively short-lived <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> setting.  The final book was never published in print form but eventually showed up as a free download courtesy of Wizards of the Coast.',1),(448,'DOOM Novels','This series of four non-interactive novels based on the popular computer game was published by Simon & Schuster.  A later novelization of the movie of the game is also listed here, though it was published by Pocket Star and is not directly related to the earlier stories except in its original inspiration.',1),(449,'Magic: The Gathering Novels','These non-interactive novels were inspired by the popular collectible card game.  Several early volumes featured mail-in offers for special cards.  HarperCollins published ten novels and two anthologies before turning things over to Wizards of the Coast.',1),(450,'Creating Adventure Games on Your Computer','This computer programming book includes a brief gamebook as a demonstration of adventure gaming.  It requires two coins for randomization and gives the player three values to keep track of: STRENGTH, WEALTH and MONSTER TALLY.',1),(451,'Spelljammer Novels: The Cloakmaster Cycle','These non-interactive novels are based on the science-fantasy space travel <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> setting.',1),(452,'Dungeons & Dragons Novels: The Penhaligon Trilogy','These non-interactive novels are based on the basic <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> game.',1),(453,'Dragon Dice Novels','Only one non-interactive novel was released in this original series based on the collectible dice game.  It was shrinkwrapped and actually contained a special die in a cavity drilled through the pages. The book was reissued as part of a longer series decades later; see <a href=\"/Series/2334\">Dragon Dice: The Esfah Sagas Novels</a>.',1),(454,'Advanced Dungeons & Dragons Novels','These non-interactive novels were based on adventures for the <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> role-playing game.  Dozens of additional novels set in the game\'s various optional worlds have also been released over the years.',1),(455,'First Quest Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on an introductory product designed to introduce new players to <a href=\"/Series/20\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons</a>.',1),(456,'Tracker Books','These books may well have been the first series of gamebooks (though not the first gamebooks) to be published.  The adventures cover a range of different genres and have a strange layout: the books are in landscape rather than portrait format.  The books are also extremely visual; every page of text has an illustration opposite it, and choices may appear either as page numbers in the text or as numbered arrows in the pictures.  Puzzles are also included in some of the books.',1),(457,'TutorText: Doubleday Series','This pioneering series of interactive books used a gamebook-style format to teach a wide variety of subjects to a mainstream audience.  All of the titles listed here were published by Doubleday, and selected titles were reissued by various British publishers.  More obscure and specialized subjects were covered by the <a href=\"/Series/458\">TutorText: Educational Science Division Series</a>.',1),(458,'TutorText: Educational Science Division Series','This series of interactive tutorial books covers topics more obscure than those found in the mainstream <a href=\"/Series/457\">TutorText: Doubleday Series</a>.',1),(459,'Tunnels and Trolls: Judges Guild','Several companies other than Flying Buffalo released products for the <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> role-playing game.  Judges Guild was the first.',1),(460,'Turning Points','These romance gamebooks really aren\'t very interactive; each features only one choice, a \"turning point\" from which two different endings can be reached.',1),(461,'Virgin Adventure Gamebooks','These relatively brief gamebooks put the reader in dangerous historical situations.',1),(462,'Forgotten Realms Novels','This series of non-interactive novels, based on the popular <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> campaign setting, has been extremely long-lasting and successful.  Books that are listed below without numbers were originally released as hardbacks and don\'t fit into the same release pattern as the paperback-only releases.',1),(463,'Virtual Reality','These books, published by Mammoth toward the end of the popularity of gamebooks in the United Kingdom, were designed as a sort of diceless answer to <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>.  Many of the books have more experimental, unconventional plots than the average gamebook, and the entire series contains no random elements at all.  At the start of each book, the reader picks a character from a list of pregenerated options.  Characters consist of a list of skills, a number of Life Points and a list of equipment and money.  During play, success or failure in various actions is determined by whether or not the chosen character has the appropriate skills for the job.  \n\n<p>In 2000, a few years after the end of the series, author Paul Mason started the <a href=\"/Series/464\">Panurgic Adventures</a> line, reprinting one of the books from this series; further titles have been announced, but it is yet to be seen whether or not they will be produced. Four of the series were then re-printed in the early-2010s under the <a href=\"/Series/1610\">Critical IF</a> series title through the Fabled Lands imprint. Additionally, Book 5 (Heart of Ice) was also later re-issued again in a deluxe hardcover edition by Megara Entertainment with new colour artwork alongside the original black-and-white illustrations.',1),(464,'Panurgic Adventures','This series was created to reprint titles from the <a href=\"/Series/463\">Virtual Reality</a> line; however, it appears that only one title was released, though others were announced.',1),(465,'E agora você decide','Some of these <a href=\"/Series/319\">Twistaplot</a> translations series featured new cover art, but most used the original illustrations.  After all of the English titles were translated, a spin-off featuring works by authors from Brazil was created: <a href=\"/Series/522\">E agora você decide: Série Brasileira</a>.',7),(466,'Aventuras fantásticas (Brazil)','This was the first translation of the series published in Brazil. The books were later reissued, with some different titles and translations, under the <a href=\"/Series/1831\">Fighting Fantasy</a> label beginning in 2009.',7),(467,'Double Agent Novels','These non-interactive books are based on the <b>Top Secret/S.I.</b> role-playing game, which also inspired some <a href=\"/Series/68\">Catacombs Solo Quest</a> gamebooks.  Each book contains two adventures.',1),(468,'Dragonlance Novels','These popular novels, based on an <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> campaign setting, are non-interactive, though several <a href=\"/Series/21\">AD&D Adventure Gamebooks</a> tie in with them.',1),(469,'Way of the Tiger, The','These books, set in the world of Orb first introduced in the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series, cast the reader as a Ninja warrior.  During the course of the series, this character grows in strength and power while battling numerous foes.  Players have several Ninja skills to choose from and must keep track of combat ability in a variety of specializations.  Combat is more strategic than usual, with the reader actually having to make choices about which attacks to make rather than simply rolling dice endlessly.  As usual for this sort of gamebook, there are inventory items to collect, and these, along with the various character stats, are carried from book to book.  All six volumes were first released in England and then reprinted in the United States; in keeping with the general trend, the American editions are inferior to the British ones, omitting the color maps found in the originals and generally using cheaper materials. \n\n<p>All six of the original books were re-released through Amazon CreateSpace in the 2010s, along with a completely new prequel (Book 0) and final part of the cycle (Book 7). All eight books were then further re-issued in deluxe hardcover editions by Megara Entertainment with full-colour artwork.',1),(470,'Tékumel Novels','These non-interactive books take place in the world of the <i>Empire of the Petal Throne</i> role-playing game, a setting also used in the <a href=\"/Series/31\">Adventures on Tékumel</a> solo adventures.',1),(471,'Proteus Books Novels','These non-interactive novels adapt various video games.',1),(472,'World of Aden: Thunderscape Novels','These non-interactive novels were based on a science fantasy setting shared by a computer role-playing game and a collectible card game.',1),(473,'Miscellaneous Works by Alan Dean Foster','Alan Dean Foster has written a lot of movie tie-ins in addition to his original work like the <a href=\"/Series/474\">Adventures of Flinx of the Commonwealth</a> series.  He has also written a couple of novels based on computer games.',1),(474,'Adventures of Flinx of the Commonwealth, The','This series of non-interactive science fiction novels was later adapted into a <a href=\"/Series/191\">GURPS</a> sourcebook and solo adventure.',1),(475,'Cyborg Commando','These non-interactive novels tie in with <a href=\"/Series/13\">D&D</a> co-creator Gary Gygax\'s notoriously unsuccessful <b>Cyborg Commando</b> role-playing game.',1),(476,'Ravenloft Novels','These non-interactive books are based on the horror setting for the <a href=\"/Series/20\">AD&D</a> role-playing game.  Most form a numbered series of paperback novels, but there were a couple of <i>I, Strahd</i> novels (the first of which was a hardback) and one anthology released to supplement the main line.',1),(477,'Star of the Guardians Novels','These non-interactive science fiction novels were written by Margaret Weis of <a href=\"/Series/468\">Dragonlance</a> fame.  They eventually spawned a collectible card game.',1),(478,'Rose of the Prophet Novels','These non-interactive novels were Margaret Weis and Tracy Hickman\'s second non-game-inspired fantasy series following their <a href=\"/Series/479\">Darksword</a> adventures.',1),(479,'Darksword Novels','These non-interactive fantasy novels were Margaret Weis and Tracy Hickman\'s follow-up to their <a href=\"/Series/468\">Dragonlance</a> success.  Although not based on a game, the books quickly spawned a complete multiplayer role-playing game printed in a paperback format closely resembling the novels themselves.',1),(480,'Death Gate Cycle Novels, The','This was Margaret Weis and Tracy Hickman\'s third fantasy series for Bantam Spectra.  Although not interactive or directly inspired by a game, some familiar names can be found in the credits for these adventures....',1),(481,'Agent 13 Novels','These pulp-style adventure novels tie in with the <b>Top Secret/S.I.</b> role-playing line, which included a source book based on the character of Agent 13.  When this series ended, the hero\'s adventures continued in the first of the <a href=\"/Series/467\">Double Agent Novels</a>.',1),(482,'Artes mágicas!','',7),(483,'Aventuras fantásticas RPG','',7),(484,'Aventuras fantásticas RPG avançado','',7),(485,'Aventuras fantásticas romance','',7),(486,'Aventuras fantásticas duplo: Fúria de príncipes','',7),(487,'Webs of Intrigue','These mystery gamebooks cast the reader as a detective known as T.S. (short for \"troubleshooter\").  No dice are used during play, but each book includes a \"web\" on which numbers are marked when certain clues turn up.  These markings (or their absence) can affect later events.  If the right clues are found, things progress to a successful conclusion; otherwise, the player is taken off the case and the criminal escapes.',1),(488,'What If...','This series of comic books looks at alternate outcomes of various events in the Marvel universe. At least one of the issues includes multiple endings and allows the reader to have some say in what happens....',1),(489,'What Would You Do?','This series of books, aimed at a Christian teenage audience, allows the reader to make decisions about various issues like sex and drugs.  The books are presented in a larger-than-average paperback format.',1),(490,'Which Way?','These simple gamebooks are printed in a large, full-color format.  They are aimed at young children and often take advantage of their attractive layout to include visual puzzles.  The books have a fairly confusing publishing history, since they have been published under a number of different series titles, with some of the books not even having a clearly designated series.  In the United States, they can be found as part of the <b>Choose-Your-Challenge</b> Gamebooks or <b>Pick Your Peril</b> series, with at least one book having an alternate American title.  In the United Kingdom, in addition to being known as the <b>Which Way?</b> books, they are sometimes called the <b>Walker Gamebooks</b> or the <b>Choose-Your-Way</b> series, and several were reissued in 2008 as the <b>Puzzle Master</b> series.  To make matters even more confusing, some titles were published in translated editions prior to being published in English.  Other books featuring picture puzzles but no interactive story elements were also printed using the same basic format, but they are not listed here.',1),(491,'Which Way Secret Door Books','This <a href=\"/Series/29\">Which Way Books</a> spin-off is aimed at a younger audience, much like the <a href=\"/Series/74\">Choose Your Own Adventure for Younger Readers</a> series.',1),(492,'Survivor','',1),(493,'Starcraft Novels','This popular real-time strategy game spawned one eBook and several paperback novels.  None are interactive.',1),(494,'Diablo Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on the popular computer role-playing game by Blizzard.',1),(495,'Warcraft Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on Blizzard\'s popular line of real-time strategy computer games.  One title was released exclusively as an eBook.',1),(496,'Xbox Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on various video games released for Microsoft\'s console.',1),(497,'Lara Croft: Tomb Raider Novels','The popular video game series spawned these non-interactive novels.',1),(498,'Myst Novels','The genre-redefining computer adventure game inspired a series of non-interactive novels.',1),(499,'Wing Commander Novels','The popular series of space combat video games spawned these non-interactive novels, which feature some fairly well-known authors for some titles.  A separate series, <a href=\"/Series/500\">Wing Commander: Movie Universe Novels</a>, was spun off from the game-inspired film.',1),(500,'Wing Commander: Movie Universe Novels','This series of novels is based on a movie that in turn was based on a video game.  A separate series of books, the <a href=\"/Series/499\">Wing Commander Novels</a>, was released that was based directly on the game.',1),(501,'Starfire Novels','This series of non-interactive novels is based on a strategy board game.  The four original paperbacks were later reissued as omnibus hardbacks, with the second collection containing some expanded material.',1),(502,'Bard\'s Tale Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on the classic series of computer role-playing games.',1),(503,'Greyhawk Novels','These novels are set in one of the earliest <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> campaign settings.  The unnumbered title is the very first novel based on a <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> setting.  The first six numbered titles are actually numbered on the books themselves -- the rest are listed in chronological release order.  The characters introduced in the first two numbered adventures were also featured in the separately-published series of <a href=\"/Series/504\">Gord the Rogue Novels</a>.  Finally, <a href=\"/Item/3406\">Nightwatch</a> from the <a href=\"/Series/510\">TSR Books</a> line is set in Greyhawk but doesn\'t go out of its way to draw attention to this fact.',1),(504,'Gord the Rogue Novels','This non-interactive fantasy adventures take place in the same setting as the <a href=\"/Series/503\">Greyhawk Novels</a> and the <a href=\"/Series/200\">Hero\'s Challenge: Sagard the Barbarian</a> gamebooks.',1),(505,'Tunnels and Trolls / Golden d20 Dual System','This series of solo adventures, released in electronic format, claims compatibility with both the <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> and <a href=\"/Category/87\">d20 System</a> role-playing rules.  Enough rules are included to make each volume playable as a stand-alone gamebook, however.',1),(506,'Shadowrun Novels','This long-running series of non-interactive novels is based on a fantasy/cyberpunk role-playing game.',1),(507,'Kage Magazine','This magazine included a short story that was adapted into one of the <a href=\"/Series/506\">Shadowrun Novels</a>.',1),(508,'Dark Sun Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on an <a href=\"/Series/13\">AD&D</a> campaign setting.',1),(509,'Mystara Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on the default setting of the original <a href=\"/Series/20\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> game.',1),(510,'TSR Books','Outside of their gamebook and game-inspired novel publishing, the creators of <a href=\"/Series/20\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> also published a variety of stand-alone novels and other items.',1),(511,'Legend of the Five Rings Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on a whole family of popular games.',1),(512,'Dark Matter Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on the dark modern-day setting for the <a href=\"/Category/89\">Alternity</a> science fiction role-playing game.',1),(513,'Saga of the Lost Lands Novels','These non-interactive fantasy novels were written by <a href=\"/Person/584\">Rose Estes</a> of <a href=\"/Series/79\">Endless Quest</a> fame.',1),(514,'Star Drive Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on the space adventure setting for the <a href=\"/Category/89\">Alternity</a> science fiction role-playing game.',1),(515,'Battletech Novels','The popular giant robot combat board game spawned a large number of non-interactive novels.  The first of the books were released by FASA as unnumbered volumes; when Penguin\'s Roc imprint picked up the series a few years later, it started numbering and eventually integrated reprints of some earlier titles into its sequence.  The books are listed on the site using Roc\'s numbering, not their original chronological release order.  The unnumbered title below is a FASA release that was never picked up by Penguin.  There were also two series (<a href=\"/Series/516\">Mechwarrior Novels</a> and <a href=\"/Series/521\">Mechwarrior: Dark Age Novels</a>) inspired by the <b>Battletech</b>-based computer and collectible miniatures games.',1),(516,'Mechwarrior Novels','This series of non-interactive novels, based on a computer game, is a spin-off from the <a href=\"/Series/515\">Battletech Novels</a>.  It was followed by the <a href=\"/Series/521\">Mechwarrior: Dark Age Novels</a>, based on a collectible miniatures game.',1),(517,'Aventuras fantásticas (Portugal)','',7),(518,'Lobo Solitário','These translations were first published in Portugal, with the first two titles eventually being reprinted in Brazil.  A few years after these reprints, new, retranslated editions were released in Brazil as a separate series, also called <a href=\"/Series/519\">Lobo Solitário</a>.',7),(519,'Lobo Solitário','This release from Brazil is the second Portuguese-language <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> translation, following a different series from Portugal also called <a href=\"/Series/518\">Lobo Solitário</a>.',7),(520,'Livro-jogo oracular','This Portuguese-only book, written by a psychoanalyst, uses the gamebook format (and some ancient methods) to supposedly help the reader find the best path for his or her future.',7),(521,'Mechwarrior: Dark Age Novels','This series, based on a collectible miniatures game, follows the <a href=\"/Series/516\">Mechwarrior Novels</a>, which were based on a computer game.',1),(522,'E agora você decide: Série Brasileira','These original gamebooks were released in Brazil as a follow-up to the <a href=\"/Series/319\">Twistaplot</a> translations in the <a href=\"/Series/465\">E agora você decide</a> series.',7),(523,'Você Manda','',7),(524,'Aventuras na terra-media','',7),(525,'Margarida','This comic included a translated adventure from the Italian <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a> magazine.  Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',7),(526,'Pato Donald, O','This comic printed a translation of a story from the Italian <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a> magazine.  Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',7),(527,'E agora você decide: Infantil','',7),(528,'Zé Carioca','This Disney-themed comic book from Brazil featured one interactive story.  Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',7),(529,'Tio Patinhas','This comic included a translated story from the Italian <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a> magazine.  Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',7),(530,'Seleção Disney','This Disney comic included a translation from the Italian <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a> magazine.  Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',7),(531,'Misterios de Sherlock Holmes','',7),(532,'Máquina do tempo','',7),(533,'Deltas Adventure Games','These translations have been reformatted so that section numbers and page numbers don\'t generally correspond, and some pages even contain multiple sections; it\'s a bit more space-efficient than the American layout.',13),(534,'King\'s Quest Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on the computer adventure game series from Sierra.  The numbered volumes below are novels from Berkley which expand upon the games; the unnumbered volumes are strategy guides that provide hints by novelizing the games.',1),(535,'Ultima Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on the popular computer role-playing series.  The first two titles were published by Warner Books, the latter three by Pocket Books.  The unnumbered volumes are actually strategy guides for the games written in a fictionalized format that reads like a novel.',1),(536,'Doctor Who: Past Doctor Adventures Novels','This series picked up where the <a href=\"/Series/972\">Doctor Who: Missing Adventures Novels</a> left off.  While most novels are non-interactive, the series includes one adventure (<a href=\"/Item/3600\">Ten Little Aliens</a>) with a brief interactive segment.',1),(537,'Worlds of Power Novels','This series of non-interactive novels is loosely based on third-party Nintendo games yet not authorized by Nintendo itself.  It is easily mistaken for a gamebook line, especially since one volume features text on the back cover written in the second person.  Each book includes tips for the game it covers along with a list of recommended reading containing classic novels along the same lines as the adventure at hand.  The American editions of the first six novels also include full-color punch-out trading cards on a cardboard sheet in the middle.  A <a href=\"/Series/540\">Junior Worlds of Power Novels</a> series was released for younger readers.',1),(538,'White Dwarf','This fantasy role-playing magazine published a number of gamebook-style adventures before eventually evolving into a miniatures-centered publication.  It first published a solitaire adventure for <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a>, then a four-part serialized stand-alone gamebook which was later revised into the final entry in the <a href=\"/Series/14\">Golden Dragon Fantasy Gamebooks</a> series, then a three-part adventure using the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> rules, and finally a solitaire adventure for the <b>Warhammer Fantasy Roleplay</b> game.',1),(539,'Star Wars: X-Wing Novels','This series of novels based on the ever-popular sci-fi movies was initiated by Michael A. Stackpole of <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> and <a href=\"/Series/515\">Battletech</a> fame.',1),(540,'Junior Worlds of Power Novels','This series follows the same basic format as the <a href=\"/Series/537\">Worlds of Power Novels</a> but is aimed at a younger audience.',1),(541,'World of Darkness Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on the various role-playing games in White Wolf\'s <b>World of Darkness</b> line.',1),(542,'Exalted Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on White Wolf\'s fantasy role-playing game.',1),(543,'Mage Knight Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on the groundbreaking collectible miniatures game.',1),(544,'Défis de l\'histoire','',2),(545,'Aventures à construire','These French gamebooks are about the adventures of children from different parts of the world.  The books feature full-color illustrations, and rather than using page numbering to direct the reader from section to section, they instead use arrows pointing to illustrated tabs cut into the sides of the pages.',2),(546,'Aventures d\'Arthur, Les','These short, colorful gamebooks allow the reader to participate in the misadventures of a child named Arthur.',2),(547,'Bande dessinée dont tu es le héros ou l\'héroïne, Une','This is an original French interactive comic book.',2),(548,'Bob Morane Magazine','Despite the inclusion of the word \"magazine\" in the series title, these volumes are in the format of standard paperback books.  Each issue contains a novella, a full-color comic and a mini-gamebook.  In the gamebook segment of each book, the reader must keep track of two attributes (force and dexterity) along with life points.  All of these come into play during combat, which occurs from time to time.  A series of computer games was released which tied in with the four magazine issues.',2),(549,'God Allows U-Turns for Youth: You Decide What Happens','These basic gamebooks, written from a Christian perspective, allow readers to make choices about everyday middle school situations.  Each book ends with a few pages of \"spiritual building blocks\" offering religious advice on various life issues.',1),(550,'Trio: Rebels in the New World','This series of non-interactive novels takes place in the same war-torn future as two <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> books, <a href=\"/Item/500\">Escape</a> and <a href=\"/Item/502\">Beyond Escape</a>, although the date of the stories appears to have been moved back a couple of decades to 2015.',1),(551,'XO Play iPod Games','These simple gamebooks were designed to be played on early Apple iPods.  Each is broken up into extremely short text files(three sentences or less) in order to fit on the iPod\'s screen.  These text files are then linked together in HTML style.  The games were distributed either via download (no longer available) or on CD-ROM (although they take up only a miniscule portion of the available space on each disc).  Although they were meant to be loaded into iPods from a PC or Mac, the stories are in plain text format and can be read or printed on a computer with a little bit of work.',1),(552,'White Wolf','This magazine contained a brief gamebook-style adventure in one issue.  It uses its own self-contained game system.',1),(553,'Who-Done-It Adventure Game','This murder mystery gamebook follows the component-heavy model initiated by <a href=\"/Series/39\">Enid Blyton\'s Famous 5 Adventure Games</a>.  The book comes in a plastic wallet with a special die, three two-sided detective\'s notebook cards (each side showing a jumble of letters), four different colored clue overlays, a solution overlay, a photograph of the suspects and a murder attempts counter.  At the start of play, the reader randomly selects one of the detective notebooks (there are six in all); this determines who the murderer is, meaning that replay value is quite high.  During play, the clue overlays are sometimes used to reveal information hidden in the detective notebook card.  The object of the game is to solve the mystery before the murder attempts counter reaches three, thus ending the main character\'s life.',1),(554,'Wizards','This role-playing game, based on the bizarre animated film of the same name, includes an untitled solitaire adventure in the main rulebook.',1),(555,'Space Quest Novel','This strategy guide provides hints by novelizing several of Sierra\'s <b>Space Quest</b> computer adventure games.',1),(556,'You Are Spider-Man','The name just about says it all; this series of gamebooks is largely straightforward apart from one thing: each section of text is given a chapter number, but the chapters aren\'t arranged in order, forcing the reader to rely on page numbers when making decisions.',1),(557,'You Are the Star of a Muppet Adventure ','This gamebook puts the reader in control of a story involving muppets and escaped chickens.',1),(558,'You Are There','These fairly thick gamebooks allow the reader to participate in historical events.  They also include historical background material and recommendations for further reading.',1),(559,'You Are There Bible Adventures','This thick hardback contains eight interactive adventures featuring various figures from the Bible: Jonah, Joseph, Mary and Joseph, David and Goliath, Paul, Baby Moses, Jesus and the Disciples, and Jesus and the Paralyzed Man.  Two thirds of each page are taken up by a large, full-color, cartoonish illustration, and the text below each illustration is very brief, reflecting the fact that the book is aimed at ages four through nine.',1),(560,'Your Decision','',1),(561,'Your First Adventure','This spin-off of the <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> line is aimed at a surprisingly young audience: toddlers.  Each thin paperback book consists of a series of two-page spreads printed in full color.  Many also feature, in the center of each of these spreads, a half-sized page which, when flipped from the right side to the left side, causes a change in the picture and reveals additional text.  Each story deals with anthropomorphic animals in situations children might encounter, and each plot contains just one choice for the child to make.  The books were first published in the UK by Transworld before being picked up by Bantam.',1),(562,'Playmark Book Games','This is quite an unusual gamebook format; it uses bookmarks to represent armies while a programmed book represents geographical regions and the links between them.  By placing bookmarks in different parts of the book and then moving them from page to page over time, players can simulate a conflict.  A number of titles were announced using this game system, but only one (plus a free downloadable demo) was actually released.',1),(563,'Texture of the Sky, The','This surreal gamebook has the reader sent through various bizarre dreams by a force known as the Sky.  While reading, the player must keep track of two scores, Negative Time and Positive Time, which can be affected by different paths.  The book is available as a stand-alone eBook download or a paperback volume in which it is combined with the non-interactive <i>The Gospel According to the Christ Brothers</i>.  It has also inspired an audio CD featuring experimental music.',1),(564,'Camp Cheer','This interactive young adult mystery was published in electronic format only.  Both HTML and PDF versions were produced.  It was first published by Wordbeams, then by Hard Shell Word Factory.  It does not currently appear to be available from either publisher.',1),(565,'Storymaze','These bizarre, humorous children\'s books are truly a multimedia experience, combining regular text with comic book sections and (in some volumes) a mild degree of gamebook-style interactivity.',1),(566,'Demi the Demoness','This pornographic comic book included an interactive special issue.',1),(567,'Aukeratu zeure lehen abentura','',12),(568,'appel de Cthulhu, L\'','',2),(569,'Car Wars','The French translations exclude the useless character bookmarks found in the English editions.',2),(570,'Asterix - Alea Jacta Est!','',3),(571,'Auf Cthulhus Spur','',3),(572,'Du und dein Abenteuer','',3),(573,'Schwarze Auge, Das','This original German fantasy role-playing game spawned an impressive number of solitaire adventures.  Different editions of the basic rules boxed set contained different introductory solo adventures; the remaining solo adventures were sold separately.  Solitaire and group adventures were numbered together, so the numbers on the list below are not the same numbers that actually appear on the products; they are simply there to indicate release order.',3),(574,'œil noir, L\'','Two different publishers produced these books; some titles were issued by both publishers while others were released by only one of the two. In some cases, numbering differs between publishers. Only solitaire adventures are listed below.  An additional solitaire scenario was printed in <a href=\"/Series/575\">Casus Belli</a> magazine.',2),(575,'Casus Belli','This gaming magazine contained a solo adventure for <a href=\"/Series/574\">L\'œil noir</a> in its thirty-first issue and two stand-alone adventures in later issues.',2),(576,'C\'est toi... l\'aventurier','This series allows the reader (and his or her dog) to have adventures in various countries around the world.  Mysteries along the same lines can be found in the <a href=\"/Series/577\">C\'est toi... le détective</a> series.',2),(577,'C\'est toi... le détective','These mystery gamebooks allow the reader to solve crimes around the world (with the help of a faithful dog).  Adventures featuring the same characters can be found in the <a href=\"/Series/576\">C\'est toi... l\'aventurier</a> series.',2),(578,'Choisis ton aventure avec...','This original French series of illustrated gamebooks for children is based on Jean de la Fontaine\'s fables.  Each volume is just under fifty pages long and features full-color illustrations on every page.',2),(579,'Choisissez d\'être un héros','These original French gamebooks feature interactive science fiction stories free from formal rules apart from the obvious decision-making and page-turning.',2),(580,'Choisis ta propre aventure','',2),(581,'Terror T.R.A.X.','This series of audio CDs was released by TSR in 1994, presumably as an attempt to create something gamebook-like but more marketable. The series was based on a concept by Flint Dille, co-creator of the <a href=\"/Series/200\">Hero\'s Challenge: Sagard the Barbarian</a> gamebooks. The basic premise is that 911 operators forward calls of a supernatural nature to an agency known as T.R.A.X. (Trace, Research, Analyze and Exterminate). Each disc allows the listener to control a team of T.R.A.X. officers by skipping around the tracks of the CD. The gameplay is no more complex than the average <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a>.  Although only four audio adventures were designed, the T.R.A.X. concept lived on in the form of a 1996 computer game and a 2000 TV movie which appears to have been announced but never released.  A few years after being released on CD, the first two of these adventures appeared as downloadable RealAudio games on the <a href=\"http://www.wizards.com\">Wizards of the Coast</a> website.',1),(582,'Miscellaneous Works by Kim Newman','In addition to writing <a href=\"/Series/2\">Life\'s Lottery</a> and a variety of game-inspired fiction, Kim Newman is the author of several volumes of film criticism and a handful of genre-defying novels and short stories.  His fiction tends to be interconnected in interesting ways, so fans of his game-related work could benefit by reading everything else as well.',1),(583,'F.I.S.T. (Fantasy Interactive Scenarios by Telephone)','Featuring some of the same creative team as <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>, these gamebook-style adventures were played by calling up a toll phone number and pressing buttons on a touch-tone phone.  Later adventures in the series introduced player vs. player combat.',1),(584,'Joe Dever\'s Phone Quest','Like <a href=\"/Series/583\">F.I.S.T.</a>, this was a gamebook-like adventure that could be played using a touch-tone telephone.',1),(585,'Fighting Fantasy Poster Book','This volume collects sixteen <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> cover illustrations as tear-out posters.',1),(586,'Lone Wolf Graphic Novel','A non-interactive adventure in comic book was published in 1994, based on the <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> gamebooks. Thirty years later it was repackaged along with an interactive adventure as part of the franchise\'s 40-year celebrations.',1),(587,'Choisis ton aventure','There is another series called <a href=\"/Series/588\">Choisis ton aventure</a> which contains <a href=\"/Series/319\">Twistaplot</a> translations; this is a coincidence, and the two series are not related.',2),(588,'Choisis ton aventure','There is another series called <a href=\"/Series/587\">Choisis ton aventure</a> which contains <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> translations; this is a coincidence, and the two series are not related.',2),(589,'Choisis ta propre aventure','Some of these titles was released in Braille format by Manitoba Education and Training.',2),(590,'Choisis ton aventure - Walt Disney','',2),(591,'Chroniques Crétoises','',2),(592,'Double jeu','These translations combine two unrelated English-language series.',2),(593,'Sortilèges','',2),(594,'Donjons & Dragons','',2),(595,'Quêtes sans fin, Les','',2),(596,'Epervier, L\'','',2),(597,'Maître du destin, Le','',2),(598,'Donald Duck','This comic included an adventure translated from the Italian <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a> magazine; this translation was later reprinted under a different title in <a href=\"/Series/599\">Donald Duck Comics & Mehr</a>.  Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',3),(599,'Donald Duck Comics & Mehr','This comic printed a retitled version of the <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a> translation first published in <a href=\"/Series/598\">Donald Duck</a>.  Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',3),(600,'D&D Fantasy-Rollenspiele','The German editions of the solo modules replace the magic ink pen with a red plastic decoder sheet.',3),(601,'Abenteuer ohne Ende','',3),(602,'Sagaland','',3),(603,'Fünf Freunde und Du','This series began as translations of English gamebooks and was then extended with original German titles.',3),(604,'Fantasy-Spiel, Ein','These fantasy gamebooks, written with a light sense of humor, feature a typical game system with three attributes (strength, skill and intelligence) and the need to keep track of inventory.',3),(605,'FantasyAbenteuerSpielbücher','These books were first published in a large format by Thienemann, then reissued in a more standard paperback size by Goldmann, and finally reissued again by Thienemann as a series of three-in-one omnibus volumes available in paperback and hardback (and later reissued by Bertelsmann).  The Goldmann reissues changed the order a little from the Thienemann releases, reversing the order of books nine and ten, then delaying book thirteen until after book sixteen.  The final two volumes do not appear to have been released by Goldmann.  Most of the German editions feature new cover art, often by uncredited illustrators.',3),(606,'Gänsehaut','',3),(607,'Grosse Enwor Rollenspielbuch, Das','This book, coauthored by a well-known German fantasy writer, has a reputation for being exceedingly difficult (if not impossible) to complete successfully.',3),(608,'Grosse Spielbücher, Das','These thick (400-page, approximately 750-section) German gamebooks take their inspiration from history and mythology.  They feature a typical game system with attributes, abilities and inventory (with exact characteristics varying from book to book).\r\n\r\n<p>A smaller volume satirizing the series (<a href=\"/Item/7517\">Das kleine Emanzipations-Spielbuch</a>) was also released by the same publisher.',3),(609,'Space Gamer','This magazine included an article on solo adventure design in one issue.',1),(610,'Grüsel & Co. - Der Club','This series of books contains various horror and fantasy titles for children.  Some are regular novels, others are gamebooks; only the interactive volumes are listed here.',3),(611,'Micky Maus','Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',3),(612,'Knaurs Buch der Rollenspiele','This is a non-fiction book on role-playing which, like the English-language <a href=\"/Series/109\">Dicing with Dragons</a>, contains a short solitaire adventure.',3),(613,'Konfliktsimulations- und Rollenspiele: die neuen Spiele','This is a non-fiction book on role-playing which, like the English-language <a href=\"/Series/109\">Dicing with Dragons</a>, contains a short solitaire adventure.',3),(614,'Einsamer Wolf (1984-1990)','Some of these books use colorized and edited versions of Gary Chalk\'s interior illustrations on their covers while others feature original art by uncredited artists.  The remainder use the original British covers.',3),(615,'Kurvav mech [Кървав меч]','',8),(616,'Xhoromag 1: Le Secret de l\'Univers','The following description is provided by the author:\r\n\r\n<p>In this original gamebook series, a young boy named Jamie discovers the existence of an alien universe named Xhoromag, a vast realm besieged by evil entities from a dark, distant dimension. There is only one way to repel them forever : finding and uniting the Seven Crystals of Light, ancient artefacts whose power, it is said, originated with the Universe itself. As the chosen champion of the Timeless One, Jamie will have to recover the 7 Crystals, all the while battling the minions of the Evil Forces. Can one young boy triumph? It will depend on you!',2),(617,'Xhoromag 2: Le Flot Infini du Temps','The following description is provided by the author:\r\n\r\n<p>There is a new threat hanging over the vast Xhoromag universe, a threat which originates in the distant past, when five powerful entities came over from their own dying universe, T\'Qixia. When one of the five turned evil, the other four sealed him away forever. Forever, however, has long since passed, and once again, Jamie must embark on an epic quest. This time, he will have to reunite the four ancient saviors of Xhoromag, the four Forgotten Ones, in order to defeat and destroy the demented Oaxu... one final time.',2),(618,'Xhoromag 3: La Somme de Toutes les Vérités','The following description is courtesy of the author:\r\n\r\n<p>The evil overlord Borex Qrahi seeks to conquer the Xyâna, the One Truth and the Sum of All Truths. For that purpose, he has unleashed his otherworldy servants, the inhuman Disciples of the Second Death, and their endless foul armies. Faced with Qrahi\'s threat, a strange being calling himself Q\'zen, Eternity, comes to Jamie for help. The young adventurer must now venture in the worlds beneath the Fifth Plane and pass through the mysterious Doors of Truth, all to reach Borex Qrahi, Lord of the Second Death, and stop him from becoming the new god of Xhoromag.',2),(619,'Xhoromag 4: Les Passés les Plus Lointains','This description was provided by the author:\r\n\r\n<p>A message from the past sends Jamie on a new quest, to the lost city of Xanova. There, after saving the last descendants of the legendary Xe\'Roxuul Empire, the young adventurer learns of the existence of a powerful artefact called Time\'s Triad. Since it may be needed to save Xhoromag from an extradimensionnal invasion, Jamie will seek out its two missing elements, which were sent a long time ago, along with Xe\'Roxuul\'s generational starships, to the other Planes of Xhoromag. Although this series is incomplete, you can read the finished adventures without fear of cliffhangers, as the stories they tell are complete.',2),(620,'Xhoromag 5: Les Enfants d\'un Autre Ciel','This description is courtesy of the author:\r\n\r\n<p>An unknown enemy force invades and pillages the city of Nieslev, making off with four of the most treasured Relics of the First Era. The evil of Sn\'hh has returned to cast its shadow upon the land, and soon, two new heroes will rise: a twelve-year-old two-bladed-weapon prodigy named Miyalrel and his phantom \"li\'l sis\" Siyanlis. In this gamebook chapterplay, you take on the role of Miyalrel -- Miya to his friends -- and do battle with the dark minions of an overdimensional being called the Supreme One. Every small adventure is technically playable as a stand-alone mini-gamebook, but the full story is told over 21 episodes grouped in 12 volumes.',2),(621,'Xhoromag 6: Paix aux Anges','Here is a description courtesy of the author:\r\n<p>The first Xhoromag sequel-series, this one is set 500 years after the cataclysmic ending to \"Les Enfants d\'un Autre Ciel\". A new threat looms over the Xiellnis Empire of 100 Kingdoms, as sinister foes gather in darkness to fulfill an ancient prophecy. Tanilya is a fifteen-year-old girl with a unique trait: a white \"xishâzen nâ\" gem in her throat. This marks her as the Chosen Child, destined to gather the five Angel\'s Children and oppose the Dark Sovereign\'s five Apostles. The epic battle will soon begin, and to be a part of it, all you need is this series\' special Adventure Sheet, where you can keep track of the six heroes\' stats and equipment. Note that this is another chapterplay series. Although it\'s made up of 21 volumes (42 individual episodes), it\'s better read as a single, great adventure (somewhat like an animé series). You don\'t even have to play the \"game\" aspect of the \"gamebook\" to enjoy it.',2),(622,'Xhoromag 7: Le Cercle sans Commencement','The author has provided a description of this series:\r\n\r\n<p>This is the third gamebook chapterplay written in the Xhoromag universe. You play the role of Zayas ix Oshaqimarendahl (Ziô to his friends!), sixth prince by lineage, ruin explorer by passion. The story begins as Ziô discovers the lost Saraveth Sanctuary and awakens a sleeping child of the vanished ancestral race: Fleur Aimée (lit. Flower Love) of the Sihyls. Suddenly transported to another world by the strange artefact known as the Ring of Vanxeros, Ziô is thrown headlong into a terrible war against the invading forces of the Twilight. Ziô soon learns that he has taken on the identity of the Champion of Vanxeros, but how can he defeat the dark armies of the Twilight by himself, all the while protecting a five-year-old child with beautiful pink hair and a remarkable healing touch? There are complex schemes to unravel in the War of the 999th Circle!\r\n<p>Please note that this series is incomplete as of this writing but should reach its conclusion in 2005.',2),(623,'Xhoromag 8: Le Retour des Xhâ Niâs','The author has provided a description of this series:\r\n<p>A brand-new series of long gamebooks featuring young Shania, the last survivor of the Xhâ Niâs, the most powerful living beings in the entire universe. Sixteen in human years, Shania is an awakening Xhâ Niâ with a deeply personal mission: to discover why her universally feared and despised people were ever born at all. Absolute power, they say, corrupts absolutely, but never has there been a more innocent girl alive than sweet, naïve Shania. Wielding powers of insane potential, she will enter the overrealms to discover why she was given life... without knowing she already has two powerful enemies in their own right, one of whom has sworn to make the Xhâ Niâs disappear forever. YOU are Shania, and as you will learn, there is nothing the Universe fears more... than the return of the Xhâ Niâs.\r\n<p>Please note that this is the most recent series, and only consists of 1 of the planned 8 volumes.',2),(624,'Original Gamebooks [オリジナルゲームブック]','These gamebooks were published by the same company that first put out the Japanese translations of <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>, and they seem to use the same basic game system, though they tell stories with a more Japanese flavor and feature manga-style artwork.  Some were reissued with different covers about a decade after their initial release.',4),(625,'Advanced Dungeons & Dragons Spelböcker','Instead of having the cardboard bookmarks included in the original editions, the translations feature multiple copies of the character sheet on regular pages in the backs of the books.',11),(626,'Boken med…olika slut','',11),(627,'Drakar och demoner','This original Swedish role-playing system contained a brief introductory solitaire adventure with its rules; the adventure featured lettered, rather than numbered, paragraphs.',11),(628,'Dungeons & Dragons (Swedish)','',11),(629,'Dungeons & Dragons Äventyrsböcker','',11),(630,'Enid Blyton\'s Famous 5 Adventure Games','',11),(631,'Falken','',11),(632,'Fighting Fantasy','',11),(633,'Äventyr i det Okända','In their original British release, these books stood alone; in their Swedish re-release, however, they were given new interior illustrations and tied in with the <b>Chock</b> role-playing game (though the game was not required for play).',11),(634,'Kalle Anka och hans vänner önskar God Jul!','Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',11),(635,'Kalle Ankas Disneytajm','Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',11),(636,'Kalle Ankas Pocket','Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',11),(637,'Ensamma Vargen (1985-1990)','',11),(638,'Boken om Magnamund','',11),(639,'Äventyr i Midgård','',11),(640,'mystiska påsen, Den','This early gamebook involves a bag of stolen gems.  It has never been published in English, though Danish and German translations exist.',11),(641,'Nintendo äventyrsböcker','',11),(642,'Historiska soloäventyr','',11),(643,'Twistaplot','',11),(644,'Tigerns väg','',11),(645,'Ensamma Vargens värld','',11),(646,'Abenteuer des Katers Lucky Les in fünf Geschichten, Die','',3),(647,'Lustiges Taschenbuch','Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',3),(648,'Mittelerde-Questbücher','The first two of these books were issued by Citadel in 1990; both original titles were reissued in a larger format by Queen in 1996 and joined by a third title in 1997.',3),(649,'Miscellaneous Works by Thea Lust','This author writes humorous gamebooks dealing with such issues as relationships and politics.',3),(650,'Saga von Bruder John, Die','',3),(651,'Vous êtes le héros','',2),(652,'Sherlock Holmes KrimiAbenteuerSpielBücher','',3),(653,'Analand Saga, Die','This is the first German translation of the series; it was later given a different translation as <a href=\"/Series/1921\">Steve Jacksons Sorcery!</a>.',3),(654,'Erlebnis Tierwelt','',3),(655,'Merlins Zeitmaschine','',3),(656,'dòng wù qiú shēng shù [動物求生術]','',14),(657,'Portes interdites, Les','',2),(658,'Chair de poule extra','',2),(659,'Frouss\'land: Timothy MacLean','These books cast the reader as Timothy MacLean, a young Scottish orphan who solves mysteries in Victorian London.',2),(660,'Highland Mysteries','',3),(661,'Tom Traveller','These books cast the reader as an adventurous time traveler and require the reader to keep track of some basic attributes.',3),(662,'Torte schlägt zurück, Die','This book is a chaotic parody of the gamebook form and is intended more as a source of mirth than as an actual playable game.  There aren\'t really any rules, though there are amusing but meaningless creature statistics scattered through the book.',3),(663,'Schwerter & Dämonen','The German editions feature different cover artwork from the English versions.',3),(664,'Ullstein Abenteuer Spiele','These stand-alone gamebooks all use similar game systems but feature entirely different plots.  The system features three attributes (strength, luck and intelligence) and inventory management.  The plots range from H. P. Lovecraft inspired horror to post-apocalyptic survival.',3),(665,'Walt Disneys Lustiges Taschenbuch Sonderband','Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',3),(666,'Silberstern der Magier','',3),(667,'Zauber-Zeit','This German gaming magazine occasionally featured a solitaire adventure scattered among its pages.  Sections were marked with a page number and a letter, much like the early <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> modules.',3),(669,'Angus Dewer','Either these books are translations of English titles that were never published in their native tongue, or else they feature fictional English-sounding pseudonyms and original titles in an effort to disguise their Hungarian origins.',15),(670,'Dragon d\'or','',2),(671,'Quête du Graal, La','These editions featured different cover art than the British originals, and in at least one case, multiple versions of the covers appeared over the years.',2),(672,'Histoires à jouer - Les Livres à remonter le temps','These gamebooks cover a broad range of historical (and in one case pre-historical) periods, with each book covering a different time and offering rules customized to fit that era.  In spite of the ever-changing rules and the presence of occasional special features like wargame-style battle resolution, there is some consistency between the books, with each featuring similar methods of testing attributes, tracking hit points and managing weapons and inventory.',2),(673,'Þitt eigið ævintýri','',16),(674,'Leikjabók','',16),(675,'Vyber si vlastní dobrodružství','The first eight titles listed here were published by Pocket Book as the <a href=\"/Series/675\">Vyber si vlastní dobrodružství</a> series; the remaining title was published by Baronet & Littera Bohemica without a series title.',17),(676,'Vesmírní Sokoli','',17),(677,'Is kahani ke hiro ap hain','',18),(678,'Histoires à jouer - Missions spéciales','These books allow the reader to join a secret agency and go on various espionage missions.  The complexity level of the books is high, with eight attributes and eight skills to keep track of, heroism points which may be spent to modify dice rolls, a complex combat system with multiple wound levels, codewords for keeping track of player actions, inventory management, experience points which carry from book to book, and additional special features specific to certain stories.',2),(679,'Histoires à jouer - Quatrième dimension','These books tell <i>Twilight-Zone</i>ish tales with a French flavor.  Different books feature different character attributes, and it is also necessary to keep track of hit points and occasionally resolve combat.',2),(680,'Epouvante!','',2),(681,'Tunnels & Trolls','',2),(682,'Ta première aventure','',2),(683,'Võitlus-fantaasiad','',19),(684,'Livres-jeux historiques','These thick volumes allow the reader to take on the roles of famous historical figures.',2),(685,'Métamorphoses','These books, which form a continuing storyline in the fashion of <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a>, allow the reader to take on the role of Jack Madrigal, a man gifted with the ability to change his form into that of such creatures as a yeti or a basilisk.  In addition to transformation, the series\' rules cover combat, inventory management and use of a few basic characteristics (Initiative, Combat, Vitality and Wounds).',2),(686,'Je cherche, je trouve','',2),(687,'Loup*Ardent','The books were all originally printed with their British covers, but the last two were later reissued with new cover illustrations by French artists.',2),(688,'Barcelona màxima discreció','This series was simultaneously published in Catalan and Spanish, with some titles written in Spanish then translated into Catalan and others written in Catalan then translated into Spanish.',20),(689,'Barcelona máxima discreción','This series was simultaneously published in Catalan and Spanish, with some titles written in Spanish then translated into Catalan and others written in Catalan then translated into Spanish.',6),(690,'Časovni stroj','',21),(691,'Loi du sabre, La','These books allow the reader to take on the role of a samurai warrior in a fantasy version of seventeenth-century Japan.  Players must keep track of Endurance and Honor points, an inventory of items, and a variety of combat techniques.',2),(692,'Sorcellerie!','',2),(693,'Scegli la tua avventura - Le avventure del giovane Indiana Jones','',5),(694,'Advanced Dungeons & Dragons Solo-eventyr','',22),(695,'Zigzag','',2),(696,'Histoire','',2),(697,'Super Sherlock','',2),(698,'Survie','',2),(699,'Destins','French translations of <a href=\"/Series/463\">Virtual Reality</a>. The first four books were printed in France by Gallimard in 1994/5. The fifth and sixth books were finally published in French by Megara Entertainment in hardback Collector\'s Editions 20 years later, although #5 (Heart Of Ice) was not explicitly given the Destins series title, unlike the other five in the series. The fifth volume was reissued under a different title in 2022. In 2023, the first four volumes were reissued by Alkonost.',2),(700,'Enchanteurs et chevaliers','',2),(701,'Funfax INTER Active Secret Agent File','This gamebook series, unusual in that the contents of the books could be torn out and stored in a special binder, allows the reader to take on the role of a member of INTER (International Elite Reconnaissance), an espionage organization. The unnumbered adventure was included with the binder and was meant to be played before the subsequent books were added. Audio tapes were also included to provide extra information and clues.',1),(702,'Quasar - Solo','These adventures take a very visual approach to gamebook design in telling their tales of a hero (the reader) bound to protect the universe from the forces of chaos.  Many full-color illustrations are included, and players make many decisions by examining these pictures and turning to text sections linked to numbers in the illustrations.  Rules are also fairly complex, with a variety of items and skills to obtain and use.',2),(703,'Quasar - Saga','These books use the same basic format as the <a href=\"/Series/702\">Quasar - Solo</a> series but feature more color artwork, add comic book segments to the introductions, and encourage multiple players to team up.  The computer game flavor of the earlier series is further enhanced by complex inventory management (where different kinds of items are equipped in different specific places) and conversation (where players pick what to say from a list of options).',2),(704,'Quasar - Méta Solo','These books are a more advanced spin-off of the <a href=\"/Series/702\">Quasar - Solo</a> series.',2),(705,'Super Héros Marvel, Les','',2),(706,'Bureau 13 Novels','These novels are based on the role-playing game of the same name, involving a secret government agency that investigates the paranormal.  The books were first released by Ace, then reissued in expanded editions several years later.',1),(707,'Fantom Empires','This series follows the adventures of Nicholas Fantom, a reporter living in an alternate-history Victorian England where \"steampunk\" technology is rampant.  In addition to the day-to-day adventures of his newspaper work, Fantom is on a long-term quest to find out what happened to his family, having lost both parents and a sister over the course of his young life.  Each volume can stand alone, but there are continuing subplots, and it\'s possible to carry inventory items, money and skills forward from book to book.\n\n<p>The game system is built around three core attributes: Body, Intellect and Nerve.  The reader determines the values of these attributes by assigning points between them.  Constitution (a measure of health) and Combat Skill are derived from some of the main attributes.  Each attributes also has four key skills associated with it, and these skills are only available when the attribute reaches a minimum number of points.  The reader can start the game with no more than six of the available skills.\n\n<p>Combat is table-driven; in every battle, a to-hit difficulty level is assigned to each side of the fight (easy, normal or hard).  Difficulty is cross-referenced with the weapon being used to determine the random number range needed to hit and the damage dealt by a successful hit.  Random numbers fall between 1 and 10 and are generated by pointing at a table in the back of the book (though a 10-sided die works just as well).\n\n<p>Only the first book was released. Wizard ran out of funding before any more titles could be completed.',1),(708,'Histoires à jouer - Sherlock Holmes','These original French gamebooks allow the reader to help Sherlock Holmes solve a variety of mysteries.  Rules vary slightly from book to book, but they are always fairly complex, with several attributes, a list of skills to pick from, a combat resolution system, and the need to keep track of clues discovered.  The first five volumes of the series were published by Presses pocket, with the final three presented in a slightly smaller format by Librairie générale française.',2),(709,'Choose','This single stand-alone book allows the reader to spend a single night searching for lost keys in New York City.',1),(710,'Interactive Football Gamebook','This volume allows the reader to manage a game of British Football.  Activities such as team-building are covered, and the proceedings are spiced up by a mystery involving a kidnapped player.',1),(711,'Piranha','This French magazine included a solitaire adventure in the debut issue.',2),(712,'Superpouvoirs','These adventures allow the reader to take on the role of a superhero, either by taking a pregenerated character or by building one by selecting superpowers that tie in with the game\'s four main attributes (Attack, Defense, Ego and Charisma).  It\'s also necessary to keep track of hit points and resolve combat from time to time.',2),(713,'llamada de Cthulhu, La','',6),(714,'Loup solitaire','In the French version of the series, the twenty-third and twenty-fourth books were accidentally published in inverted order.  The first seven books were published first with Peter Andrew Jones’ Red Fox cover artwork and then later reprinted with new covers by French artists.  The first edition of the second book features an illustration by Peter Andrew Jones that is very close to, but still different from, his piece on the English-language edition.  Past book seven, only books twenty-three, twenty-four and twenty-seven used the original British cover artwork.\n\n<p>Starting in 2018, some titles were reprinted to include bonus material curated by partner publisher Scriptarium.',2),(715,'Histoires à jouer - A travers les siècles','This series picked up where <a href=\"/Series/672\">Histoires à jouer - Les Livres à remonter le temps</a> left off, featuring similarly complex gameplay and varied historical settings in a slightly smaller paperback format.',2),(716,'Grecia antica','',5),(717,'Petits Retz pédagogiques, Les','This series teaches the reader about various topics using a puzzle-filled fantasy gamebook format.',2),(718,'Livre qui fera de vous le voyageur du temps, Le','',2),(719,'Voie du tigre, La','The translations of the original <a href=\"/Series/469\">The Way of the Tiger</a> series were published in France by Gallimard. The later prequel and final seventh book were published in French translations by Megara Entertainment in Collector\'s Editions.',2),(720,'Sherlock Holmes','',2),(721,'Enquêtes policières','Each of these books has been published with two different covers, and none of the French covers feature the same illustrations as the British originals.',2),(722,'Astre d\'Or','',2),(723,'Vivez l\'aventure','While the the English titles from the <a href=\"/Series/490\">Which Way?</a> series were being translated, a number of original French entries plus a translation of an unreleased English title were also produced.',2),(724,'Challenge des étoiles, Le','',2),(725,'messagers du temps, Les','These science fiction gamebooks pretend to be French translations of English adventures that were never published in their native tongue. The reader is cast as a Prince or Princess of Time, a being from the depths of the Earth who must occasionally rise to complete missions in different periods of history.  Players must keep track of attributes, talents, money, inventory and, in some cases, the passage of time.',2),(726,'Votre passepeur pour un horrible cauchemar','This original French-language series is inspired by the American <a href=\"/Series/82\">Give Yourself Goosebumps</a> series. Similar to R.L. Stine\'s Goosebumps franchise, both interactive and non-interactive Passepeur novels were published (though unlike Goosebumps, the non-interactive Passepeur novels came out years after the interactive ones). Non-interactive Passepeur novels are not listed on gamebooks.org.</p>\n\n<p>Several interactive Passepeur spin-off series (yet to be listed on this site) exist.</p>\n\n<p>The word \"passepeur\" is wordplay combining the French words for \"passport\" and \"fear\". The series title roughly translates as: \"Your scary passport to a horrible nightmare\".',2),(727,'Frouss\'land: Week-end d\'enfer','These books allow the reader to control either Eric Brian or Mimi Merlin, both agents of Phoenix, an organization that battles the forces of evil.',2),(728,'Aku Ankan Taskukirja','Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',23),(729,'Valitse oma seikkailusi','',23),(730,'Dungeons & Dragons (Finnish)','',23),(731,'Sinun suuri seikkailusi','',23),(732,'Taistelupeli','',23),(733,'Tria la teva aventura','',20),(734,'hemmelige vej, Den','This original Danish gamebook is meant to impart the teachings of Jesus.',22),(735,'Drengekøn - pigekøn','This original Danish series contains only one interactive volume.',22),(736,'Fantasi & terninger','This basic fantasy role-playing game includes an introductory solitaire adventure.',22),(737,'Myrekryb','',22),(738,'scelta finale, La','',5),(739,'libri di facile lettura Erickson, I','These books were published as part of an educational series which also integrated several non-gamebook titles.',5),(740,'Scegli la tua avventura con...','',5),(741,'Unicorno','',5),(742,'Pandora junior','',5),(743,'Oltre l\'incubo','',5),(744,'Horror classic','',5),(745,'Leggende e malefici','',5),(746,'Lupo Alberto','This gamebook features a popular Italian comic book character.',5),(747,'Tria la teva aventura, Globus blau','',20),(748,'Animorphs','',5),(749,'Walt Disney tria la teva aventura','',20),(750,'Vesmírní inspektoři','',17),(751,'Krysa z nerez oceli','',17),(752,'Mørke magter','',22),(753,'Ridder & troldmand','',22),(754,'Intrepida game','',5),(755,'Scegli la tua avventura con Walt Disney','',5),(756,'Partita a quattro','',5),(757,'Faccia a faccia','',5),(758,'Dungeons & Dragons (Italian)','',5),(759,'Guerrieri della strada','Starting in 2015, these books were reissued by Vincent Books using their original English titles, under a series title of \"Freeway Warrior, Il guerriero della strada.\"',5),(760,'Golden dragon','',5),(761,'Real life gamebooks','',5),(762,'Simbad il marinaio','',5),(763,'Detectives club','Unlike the British originals, the Italian editions of these books all feature the same cover art: a gloved hand pointing itself like a gun.  Each book has a different background color behind this image.',5),(764,'Superpoteri','',5),(765,'reino de Zork, El','',6),(766,'Izberi svoeto priklyuchenie! [Избери своето приключение!]','',8),(767,'Poreditsa Bezbroy priklyucheniya [Поредица Безброй приключения]','',8),(768,'Bitki Bezbroy [Битки безброй]','',8),(769,'Trimata detektivi [Тримата детективи]','',8),(770,'Six Actions','',8),(771,'Buffy the Vampire Slayer: Stake Your Destiny','These interactive adventures are based on the popular television series, allowing the reader to take on the role of the lead character and battle vampires and other supernatural dangers.  Although they do not feature any sort of game system, some of the books are quite thick and are aimed at a slightly older audience than the average simple gamebook.',1),(772,'Chroniques d\'Outre Monde','This gaming magazine included some interactive comics translated from <a href=\"/Series/108\">Diceman</a>.',2),(773,'Muppets','',3),(774,'Puzzle dein Abenteuer','',3),(775,'Tú eres quien defiende a los animales','These simple gamebooks challenge the reader to help protect animals.  Each volume features several full-color interior illustrations with hidden images that can be revealed using a transparent red filter (similar to the <a href=\"\"\"/Series/65\">Crimson Crystal Adventures</a>).',6),(776,'Turlogh der Streuner','',3),(777,'engendros del demonio, Los','',6),(778,'saga del cruzado, La','The Spanish editions feature different cover art from the French originals.',6),(779,'Senda del tigre, La','',6),(780,'Decide tu mejor aventura','',6),(781,'Aventuras en la Tierra Media','',6),(782,'máquina del tiempo – Globo azul, La','These editions featured new cover art by uncredited illustrators.',6),(783,'Galaxia','',6),(784,'búsqueda del Grial, La','',6),(785,'Forums eventyrspil','',22),(786,'Julefesten i nissebjerget','This Danish gamebook allows the reader to participate in a Christmas festival.',22),(787,'Skyggernes prins','Unlike the original English editions, which were staple-bound, these translations are perfect-bound with lettering on their spines.',22),(788,'Escull la teva aventura','',20),(789,'Tu ets l\'autor','',20),(790,'Tu ets l\'autor','',20),(791,'Tria la primera aventura','',20),(792,'sárkányvár titka, A','',15),(793,'Magányos farkas','',15),(794,'Brujos y guerreros','This series was also translated into Spanish as <a href=\"/Series/795\">¡Brujeria!</a>.',6),(795,'Brujeria!, ¡','This series was also translated into Spanish as <a href=\"/Series/794\">Brujos y guerreros</a>.',6),(796,'Kraj po vaša želba','',24),(797,'Pilih sendiri petualanganmu','',26),(798,'Pilih sendiri pengembaraan anda','',25),(799,'Lone Wolf','',25),(800,'Száguldó harcos','',15),(801,'Blood sword','',5),(802,'Megaigra [Мегаигра]','This magazine contained some original Bulgarian solo adventures.',8),(803,'Samotniya valk [Самотния вълк]','',8),(804,'Avventure infinite','',5),(805,'Skyfall','',5),(806,'Leggere per divertirsi alla scuola media','This is a series of books used by Italian intermediate school students for weekly assigned fiction reading.  Several volumes include alternate gamebook-style versions of their stories at the end, and all volumes have been released with two different covers.',5),(807,'Letture, Le','This series of books for small children includes a few volumes where the reader may make choices to create alternate versions of famous stories.',5),(808,'Tú eres el protagonista: Las Leyendas de Skyfall','',6),(809,'Miscellaneous Works by Timm Daniels [Тим Дениълс]','',8),(811,'Monster Horror Show [モンスター・ホラーショウ]','',4),(812,'Magnamund Companion [マグナマンド・コンパニオン]','',4),(813,'Dragon Quest [ドラゴンクエスト]','This series is a fairly faithful adaptation of the video game of the same name, which is better known to English-speaking audiences as <i>Dragon Warrior</i>.  As in the electronic version, the player moves around a world map (which, in the paper version, takes up quite a bit of space), randomly fighting monsters and gaining experience.  There are also dungeons to explore and map and towns where items can be purchased and rest can be found.  Players must keep track of hit points, attack strength, defensive ability, magic points and gold in addition to their equipment and experience.',4),(814,'Dragon Quest II [ドラゴンクエストII]','This series is based on the video game of the same name, which is better known to English-speaking audiences as part of the <i>Dragon Warrior</i> series.',4),(815,'Dragon Quest III [ドラゴンクエストIII]','This series is based on the video game of the same name, which is better known to English-speaking audiences as part of the <i>Dragon Warrior</i> series.',4),(816,'Dragon Quest IV [ドラゴンクエストIV]','This series is based on the video game of the same name, which is better known to English-speaking audiences as part of the <i>Dragon Warrior</i> series.  This is more story-oriented and less mathematically complex than the adaptations of the previous three games in the series.',4),(817,'Dragon Quest V [ドラゴンクエストV]','This series is based on the video game of the same name, which is better known to English-speaking audiences as part of the <i>Dragon Warrior</i> series.',4),(818,'Dragon Quest VI [ドラゴンクエストVI]','This series is based on the video game of the same name, which is better known to English-speaking audiences as part of the <i>Dragon Warrior</i> series.',4),(819,'Earthdawn Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on the role-playing game of the same name.',1),(820,'Asterix - Alea Jacta Est!','',13),(821,'Lupo solitario','',5),(822,'Dungeons & Dragons Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on the third edition of the popular role-playing game and feature that release\'s \"iconic\" characters.',1),(823,'Lightbringer Trilogy, The','These non-interactive fantasy novels were written by gamebook author and frequent <a href=\"/Series/538\">White Dwarf</a> contributor Oliver Johnson.',1),(824,'VOR: The Maelstrom Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on a sci-fi miniatures game.',1),(825,'Paranoia Novels','These humorous, non-interactive novels are based on the <a href=\"/Series/296\">Paranoia</a> role-playing game.  The first three titles listed here were published in print; the remaining titles were released years later as e-books.',1),(826,'Sid Meier\'s Alpha Centauri Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on a science fiction computer game.',1),(827,'Miscellaneous Works by Garth Nix','',1),(828,'Schwarze Auge Novels, Das','These non-interactive novels were inspired by the role-playing game of the same name.',3),(829,'Anno Dracula Novels','These non-interactive, genre-bending historical horror novels by <a href=\"/Series/2\">Life\'s Lottery</a> author Kim Newman also tie in with his <a href=\"/Series/217\">Warhammer Fantasy</a> novels through the character of Genevieve, an ancient vampire.',1),(830,'Sortilegio','',5),(831,'Boi Kniga [Бой Книга]','These translations, published between 1999 and 2000, credit the fictional Michael Frost as their author and list no translator.',27),(832,'Advanced Dungeons & Dragons','',5),(833,'Aventura juego de Dungeons & Dragons','Like the original American releases, each of these books included a bound-in cardstock bookmark/character sheet.',6),(834,'Du er hovedpersonen','',22),(835,'Elige tu propia aventura (1983-1998, Argentina)','',6),(836,'Elige tu propia aventura (1981)','This Spanish translation was released by the original American publisher of <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> and is identical in format to the English original.',6),(837,'Elige tu propia aventura (1983-1998, Spain)','',6),(838,'En busca de tus pesadillas','',6),(839,'Elige tu primera aventura','',6),(840,'Walt Disney elige tu propia aventura','',6),(841,'Alla corte di re Artu','',5),(842,'Falcon','',5),(843,'Scegli la tua avventura','',5),(844,'Elige tu propia aventura - Globo azul','',6),(845,'Elige tu propia aventura - Halcones del espacio','',6),(846,'Elige tu propia aventura - Las aventuras del joven Indiana Jones','',6),(847,'Crónicas cretenses','',6),(848,'dos caras del diablo, Las','This was described as an interactive comic book by Andreu Martin and Jaume Ribera. It was probably never published.',6),(849,'Dungeons & Dragons (Spanish)','Spanish translations of some <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> modules were advertised but never actually published.',6),(850,'Jóvenes dragones de Dungeons & Dragons, Los','',6),(851,'Aventura sin fin','The Spanish translations were printed on better-quality paper than their American equivalents and had black, plain-looking spines instead of colorful, gemstone-decorated ones.  Many also feature new cover art, though it tends to be similar to the original illustrations; this new art may have been commissioned so that translations of later American titles could have covers in the style of the earlier American releases, with illustrations in an ornate box rather than filling the whole cover.  Only one of the non-fantasy adventures was translated (<a href=\"/Item/5872\">El misterio de los antiguos</a>), but its <b>Gamma World</b> origins are not apparent in the translation, which tries to pass itself off as yet another <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a> adventure.',6),(852,'Lucha ficción','',6),(853,'Fighting Fantasy Reissues','',6),(854,'Sherlock Holmes libro-juego','',6),(855,'Compact libro-juego','',6),(856,'Tú resuelves el caso','',6),(857,'Misteri d\'Oriente','',5),(858,'Marvel superhéroes','These translations include removable character bookmarks, just like the American originals.',6),(859,'Tú eres el protagonista','This series combines translations from various English series with a single original title. The original title is not a branching-path gamebook, but a work of linear fiction interspersed with puzzles for the reader to solve.',6),(860,'Planea tu fuga de Frome','A teaching guide was released to support this series; it is listed under the companion series, <a href=\"/Series/926\">Planea tu fuga de Tenopia</a>',6),(861,'Choose Your Own Nightmare (Multipath Adventures)','These computer-animated adaptations of the book series were released in several different forms -- on regular movie DVDs, as computer games and as downloadable Internet adventures.  Not every episode was released in every format.',1),(862,'Dungeons & Dragons Interactive Movies','',1),(863,'Make Your Own Adventure','',1),(864,'terra di mezzo, La','The full-color maps from the original English editions are reproduced in black and white in the Italian translations.',5),(865,'Famosos 5, Los','These translations have the gamekit components slipped inside the back cover, but they do not come with dice.  Choices that were random in the original English editions are now decisions for the reader to make!',6),(866,'Avventure stellari','',5),(867,'Reto de las galaxias, El','',6),(868,'torre y la flor - Tu aventura, La','These books are printed on thick paper in an unusual two-color format: the text is black, and the illustrations are all a pale green color.',6),(869,'torre y la flor - Tu aventura, La','These books are printed on thick paper in an unusual two-color format: the text is black, and the illustrations are all a pale green color.',6),(870,'Choose Your Own Adventure (2005-)','This series of reissues from the classic <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> series is aimed at the educational market as well as consumers.  As with the <a href=\"/Series/853\">Fighting Fantasy Reissues</a>, the release order is switched around significantly and some original titles are mixed in with the reprints.  Australian editions were issued with different covers and with some volumes skipped (see <a href=\"/Series/1335\">this page</a> for details). Starting in 2021, some longer and higher-profile original adventures were released in the separate <a href=\"/Series/2921\">Choose Your Own Adventure: New Classics</a> line.',1),(871,'Choose Your Own Adventure Interactive Movies','This series ties in with the <a href=\"/Series/870\">Choose Your Own Adventure Reissues</a>.',1),(872,'Wehikuł Czasu','',28),(873,'Star Wars Adventures','This series is a follow-up to the <a href=\"/Series/328\">Star Wars Episode I Adventures</a>.  It features a similar game system and its original volumes take place during the period between the Episode I and Episode II films.  The series also included reprints of some adventures from the previous series.',1),(874,'FEAR Adventures','',1),(875,'Fantasolo','This fantasy dungeon adventure features a simple game system.',28),(876,'Wojownik autostrady','The Polish editions of these books feature original cover art.',28),(877,'Adventure Gamebook','This booklet is published on a not-for-profit basis by its translator.',28),(878,'Stare Istoty','This horror series was inspired by the work of H. P. Lovecraft.',28),(879,'Seria Intryga','The Polish editions of these books feature original cover art.',28),(880,'I Ty Możesz Zostać Stalowym Szczurem','This features different cover art than either English-language edition.',28),(881,'Miscellaneous Works Published by Budus','These original Polish gamebooks have no actual series title, but they are numbered on the front covers. At least one (the first) was later reprinted by another publisher.',28),(882,'Fantastiske farer','',29),(883,'Parløb','This relationship-themed gamebook (dealing with marriage, family life, careers, and so forth) is designed to be played by a couple.  As they read through together, some decisions are made by one partner alone and others allow both players to decide collectively.',22),(884,'Liefdesspel','',13),(885,'Sværd og trolddom (Borgen 1984-1991)','In 2003, the Danish translations were reissued using the new Wizard Books ordering and covers; see <a href=\"/Series/2404\">Sværd og trolddom (Borgen 2003)</a>. Only the first eight books were published.<p>Starting in 2020, the books were newly translated, this time using a new ordering and different covers, and also including new Scholastic titles; see <a href=\"/Series/2403\">Sværd og trolddom (Faraos Cigarer 2020-)</a>.<p>The original Danish translation of <i>The Warlock of Firetop Mountain</i> contains a preface by the translator Erik Swiatek, which some might enjoy. Italics, parenthesis and quotations marks are taken from the text:\n\n<blockquote><p>There have been a few, but exciting difficulties regarding the translation of this series. The biggest being that a number of beings (what a clumsy word compared to the original \"creature\") haven\'t appeared previously in Danish in this relation.<p>\'This relation\' is here meant as fantasy-figures in the fantasy (adventure) universe. Using the common translations for <I>ogre, goblin, orc, gremlin, troll</I> and <I>wight</I> one largely gets trolls, leprechauns and spirits. This is because the Nordic folklore isn\'t as rich in subterranean beings as the English-Celtic. In the cases where <i>Lord of the Rings</i> has shown the way that way has been followed, apart from that we\'ve gone new ways, which is why a number of beings appear with their own name where preferred (goblin, ogre) and others have been given a fitting Danish name (ghoul = bodyeater, wight = spectre). A special problem was caused by the arch villain The Wizard, who is really a <I>warlock</I>, a male witch, a sorcerer; for practical reasons the slightly misleading Wizard was chosen.<p>Other difficulties include the staccato-like form in the book, along with the particular language used which contains terms accepted by roleplayers. If this is new country for you, then give yourself a bit of time and suddenly form and content will feel natural for you.</blockquote>\n\n<p><i>Thanks to Morten Gottschalck for the English translation of the translator\'s comments.</i>',22),(886,'Magt og magi','',22),(887,'Ensomme Ulv','',22),(888,'Sommer & Sørensens spillebøger','',22),(889,'Firewolf','',22),(890,'Sværd og trolddom: Sagaen om den magiske krone','',22),(891,'Sporhunden','',22),(892,'Serie di avventure','Only one <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> book was translated under this series title.  Many additional titles were translated a few years later by a different publisher as the <a href=\"/Series/893\">Dimensione avventura</a> series.',5),(893,'Dimensione avventura','This is the second series of Italian <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> translations, following <a href=\"/Series/892\">Serie di avventure</a>.',5),(894,'Supereroi Marvel','',5),(895,'Astérix - Alea jacta est!','',5),(896,'finale sceglilo tu, Il','This series combined a translation from <a href=\"/Series/28\">The Adventures of You Series</a> with an original adventure.',5),(897,'Giocare a dadi col drago','This translation includes a new preface written by Giampaolo Dossena. In light of this added material, it seems odd that the lists of English-language game publishers are included but no appendices for Italian equivalents are added. Also a bit strange is the fact that one of the included game excerpts (the map from the <a href=\"/Item/823\">D&amp;D Basic Set</a>\'s Dungeon Master book) is translated into Italian, but the others aren\'t (the pages from <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels & Trolls</a> and <b>Traveller</b> products remain in English). Also untranslated is the name of the solo adventure\'s game system; even in Italy it\'s called <i>Fantasy Quest</i>.',5),(898,'Survival','',5),(899,'Realtà virtuale','',5),(900,'Patyat na tigara [Пътят на тигъра]','For some reason, the Bulgarian translation of this series began with book three.',8),(901,'Ninja','',5),(902,'Oberon','The first book of this series was also sold in a boxed set with the first <a href=\"/Series/821\">Lupo Solitario</a> book. The box is listed under that series.',5),(903,'mystiske pose, Den','',22),(904,'Legendmaker','This British series of interactive comic books allows the reader to play as one of three characters: a warrior, a wizard or an elf.  Inventory and gold coins are tracked during the course of play, but there is little else in the way of a game system.',1),(905,'Lost Worlds: Queen\'s Blade [クイーンズブレイド]','This Japanese series uses the <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds</a> game system under license.  Each volume is a full-color hardback.  It was followed by <a href=\"/Series/1207\">Queen\'s Gate</a>, <a href=\"/Series/1206\">Queen\'s Blade Rebellion</a> and <a href=\"/Series/1491\">Queen\'s Blade Grimoire</a>.',4),(906,'Spellcaster Gamebooks','The first book in this series was released in 2006 by Wizard, but no subsequent volumes were printed until the books were relaunched in 2013 in app form by Tin Man Games.',1),(907,'Du er rejsende i tid','',22),(908,'Et spændende eventyr, hvor du selv er helten','',22),(909,'Időgép-regény','',15),(910,'Time machine','',5),(911,'Taimu mashin [タイムマシン]','',4),(912,'Tidsmaskinen','All of these translations were reprinted in 1995.',29),(913,'Kaland, játék, paródia','',15),(914,'Vremenski stroj','',30),(915,'Serija Vuk samotnjak','',30),(916,'máquina del tiempo, La','Only the book about the Spanish Inquisition failed to see Spanish translation, probably due either to familiarity or sensitivity of the subject matter.',6),(917,'Myth Makers','This amateur <a href=\"/Category/39\">Doctor Who</a> fanzine included an interactive story in one issue.',1),(918,'Shooting Star Comics Anthology','This series of comic book collections included an interactive story in at least one issue.',1),(919,'Plotbusters','',1),(920,'Rescue the Princess','This gamebook is accompanied by a CD-ROM containing illustrations.  It features an optional character creation system based around four attributes (Strength, Health, Luck and IQ) along with a simple combat system and some inventory management.  Standard dice are used for randomization, though random images are included on the CD-ROM for players without dice.',1),(921,'Multiaventura','This series of original gamebooks is largely derived from classic literature, though some original stories are mixed in. Additionally, most books have no game system, but others include rules, scores, combat, etc.',6),(922,'S.O.S., tu libro juego de aventuras','At least one of these books (the ninth in the series) is not a traditional interactive gamebook, instead featuring a linear story interspersed with puzzles that the reader must solve; it is possible that some of the later books are also designed this way.',6),(923,'Vidaventura','These original Spanish gamebooks resemble the <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> series; there are no rules, and the subject matter varies greatly from book to book.  All of the books are illustrated, but the artists are not given credit. Fourteen titles were announced, but only six seem to have been published.',6),(924,'Macera tüneli dizisi','',31),(925,'Kendi serüvenini kendin','',31),(926,'Planea tu fuga de Tenopia','',6),(927,'Guía básica del juego de rol','This is an introductory-level Spanish-language role-playing magazine which, in its only issue, included a gamebook-style adventure.  A single six-sided die is used for randomization, and the reader must keep track of a few attributes, a set of skills, and an inventory. Many years after the magazine went out of print, the author released the adventure for free as a pdf over the Internet. Even though the author no longer hosts the adventure on his site, it is still relatively easy to find.</p>\n\nThe Guia basica was preceded by another standalone magazine titled Guia del Juego de Rol. The first Guia did not include any solitaire adventures, and thus is not listed here.',6),(928,'Novo pokolenie knigi-igri [Ново поколение книги-игри]','',8),(929,'Storytrails','',8),(930,'Miscellaneous Works by Adrian Wayne [Ейдриън Уейн]','',8),(931,'Miscellaneous Works by Ayvan Fogger [Айвън Фогър]','',8),(932,'Miscellaneous Works by Berthram Uster [Бъртрам Устър]','',8),(933,'Miscellaneous Works by Bob Quinn [Боб Куин]','',8),(934,'Miscellaneous Works by Christopher Macdoell [Кристофър Макдоел]','',8),(935,'Miscellaneous Works by Collin Walumberry [Колин Уолъмбъри]','',8),(936,'Fiaba game','This series of children\'s gamebooks features full-color illustrations throughout and very basic gameplay in which the reader picks which equipment to take along.  Some early volumes in the series were published in both paperback and hardback editions.',5),(937,'Eternauta, L\'','This comic magazine included an interactive story in one issue. The magazine is probably a translation of a legendary Argentinian science fiction comic strip dating back to the fifties, but it is not known whether the interactive adventure is based on the titular stories.',5),(938,'Gioca l\'avventura - I fantastici 5','This series of Italian translations merges the two different English series based on the Famous Five license.',5),(939,'Galactic foundation games','These original Italian gamebooks are set in the universe of Isaac Asimov\'s <i>Foundation</i> novels.',5),(940,'Fighting Fantasy','',17),(941,'Fighting Fantasy Second Age: Negart Group','These <a href=\"/Category/37\">Fighting Fantasy</a> adventures were written in Czech and are unavailable in any other language.  Starting with the eleventh volume, the <i>Fighting Fantasy</i> logo was replaced by <i>P&#345;íb&#283;hy Fantasy</i>.',17),(942,'Grailquest','',17),(943,'Král Artus a jeho druzina','',17),(944,'Lone Wolf','The Czech editions feature new cover art.',17),(945,'Magie','',17),(946,'Vyber si vlastné dobrodružstvo','',32),(947,'Odaberi svoju pustolovinu','All of the 1984 releases were reprinted in 1990; the first book was again reprinted in 1994.',30),(948,'Odaberi svoju pustolovinu','',30),(949,'Sonic the Hedgehog: American Novels','These novels, based on the popular video game series, are aimed at very young children.',1),(950,'Sonic the Hedgehog: British Novels','These novels, based on the popular video game series, share some authors with the <a href=\"/Series/287\">Sonic the Hedgehog Adventure Gamebooks</a>.',1),(951,'Mutant Chronicles Novels','These non-interactive novels were inspired by the Mutant Chronicles role-playing game.',1),(952,'Ice Age Novels','These non-interactive children\'s adventure novels were written by frequent gamebook author <a href=\"/Person/98\">J. H. Brennan</a>.',1),(953,'Knights of the Dinner Table','This comic book included a solo adventure in one issue.',1),(954,'SoloQuest','This series of stand-alone gamebooks takes place in the Kingdoms of Kalamar campaign setting.  Each book is a stand-alone gamebook with full rules, but the series is also compatible with the <i>HackMaster</i> and <a href=\"/Category/87\">d20 System</a> rules.  The included rules are a subset of <i>HackMaster</i> (which itself is derived from <a href=\"/Series/20\">Advanced Dungeons & Dragons</a>), and they come in two levels of complexity: Basic and Advanced.  Basic rules cover inventory management, basic skill checks and combat.  Advanced rules add character creation, increase the complexity of combat, and add time keeping.  Polyhedral dice are required for play, but dice tables are included in the back of the book for players without actual dice to roll. The first book was originally released in paper format, while the second is only available in PDF format.',1),(955,'Ariona: The Bounty Hunter','This self-published adventure casts the reader as a female bounty hunter. It was later revised and reprinted as part of the <a href=\"/Series/1612\">Explore-a-Quest</a> series.',1),(956,'Once Upon... 1001 Stories','This interactive storybook featuring familiar fairy tale characters is aimed at very young children.',1),(957,'Gabriel Knight Novels','These non-interactive novels are adapted from the computer game series by the original designer.',1),(958,'Rupert il selvaggio','The Italian editions eliminate all of the fancy extra equipment included with the original British versions;  character sheets are used in place of cards and wheels, and combat is redesigned to use regular rather than custom dice.',5),(959,'Crom','This role-playing magazine first published five issues on an amateur basis, then went professional for a second series of issues.  During its amateur existence, one issue included a mystery adventure in which the reader had three randomly-generated attributes (Force, Skill and Constitution) and a limited inventory (two items chosen from a list of seven).  The adventure was later reprinted in <a href=\"/Series/189\">Kaos</a> magazine.',5),(960,'Dudley Serious Interactive Comics','Only one issue of this comic book was published by itself, but a second issue was bundled with <a href=\"/Item/6569\">Kaos #21</a>.\n\nThe 2nd volume contains the short novel \"Torna a casa, Zombi\" (Zombie, come home), a story in 55 paragraphs which uses the OD&D system. The hero is a zombie.',5),(961,'Giochi del mistery, I','This small booklet discusses different kinds of mystery games, including gamebooks, and offers a reprint of an interactive mystery story from <a href=\"/Item/6561\">Kaos #13</a>.',5),(962,'Quando protagonista e il lettore','This is a small Italian gamebook catalog written in gamebook style.  It is an expanded version of a shorter work found in <a href=\"/Item/6565\">Kaos #20</a>.',5),(963,'Firewolf','',5),(964,'Uno sguardo nel buio','',5),(965,'Sherlock Holmes','',5),(966,'Scegli tu l\'avventura','This series began with translations and continued with original Italian works.  In 1993, the series was retitled <i>Libriavventura</i>, with some earlier titles being reissued with the new name and all new books being issued for the first time with this title.',5),(967,'Compact','',5),(968,'Realtà & fantasia','',5),(969,'Paranoia','',5),(970,'Modulo vacanze','This is a series of Italian schoolbooks for children to use\r\nduring vacation; one of them includes a gamebook story.  \r\n',5),(971,'DragonCrown War Cycle Novels, The','These non-interactive fantasy novels were written by pioneering solo adventure author Michael A. Stackpole.',1),(972,'Doctor Who: Missing Adventures Novels','Although this series of novels featuring characters from various periods in the history of the television series did not feature any interactive volumes, its follow-up, <a href=\"/Series/536\">Doctor Who: Past Doctor Adventures Novels</a>, included a single gamebook-style volume.',1),(973,'Doctor Who: New Adventures Novels','This series of original novels picked up where the TV series left off in 1989.  While it featured no interactive volumes, the series was edited by <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> author <a href=\"/Person/69\">Peter Darvill-Evans</a>.  When the publisher lost the <i>Doctor Who</i> license after volume 61, the series continued minus the Doctor as simply <i>The New Adventures</i>.  The <i>Doctor Who</i> storyline was then picked up in the <a href=\"/Series/974\">Doctor Who: Eighth Doctor Adventures Novels</a>.',1),(974,'Doctor Who: Eighth Doctor Adventures Novels','This series picked up where the <a href=\"/Series/973\">Doctor Who: New Adventures Novels</a> left off.  While it featured no interactive volumes, its companion series, <a href=\"/Series/536\">Doctor Who: Past Doctor Adventures Novels</a>, included a single adventure with a gamebook-style section.',1),(975,'Doctor Who: Decalog Collections','These short story collections are companions to the <a href=\"/Series/973\">Doctor Who: New Adventures Novels</a> and <a href=\"/Series/972\">Doctor Who: Missing Adventures Novels</a>.  None of the included stories are interactive.  The last two volumes were assembled after the publisher lost its <i>Doctor Who</i> license, so their connections to the series are loose.',1),(976,'Miscellaneous Works by George M. George [Джордж М. Джордж]','',8),(977,'Miscellaneous Works by Jack Blood [Джак Блъд]','',1),(978,'Miscellaneous Works by Kevin Slaighter [Кевин Слейтър]','',8),(979,'Miscellaneous Works by Lyubomir Nikolov [Любомир Николов]','',8),(980,'Miscellaneous Works by Michael Mindcrime [Mайкъл Майндкрайм]','',8),(981,'Kniga, v koiato geroiat si TI! [Книга, в която героят си ТИ!]','',8),(982,'Space Giant','A Bulgarian translation of this title exists, but information on the original is hard to come by -- it is possible that it was never published or that it doesn\'t even exist (some original Bulgarian books include fictional information to make them appear to be of foreign origin).',1),(983,'Galakticheskiyat gigant [Галактическият гигант]','',8),(984,'Miscellaneous Works by Mont Dias [Монт Диас]','',8),(985,'Miscellaneous Works by Robert Blond [Робърт Блонд]','',8),(986,'Miscellaneous Works by Robert Blond [Робърт Блонд] and Adrian Wayne [Ейдриън Уейн]','',8),(987,'Magosnichestva [Магьосничества]','',8),(988,'Miscellaneous Works by Virgil Dreamond [Върджил Дриймънд]','',8),(989,'Miscellaneous Works by Ted Grey [Тед Грей]','',8),(990,'Miscellaneous Works by Robert Blond [Робърт Блонд] and Ted Grey [Тед Грей]','',8),(991,'Miscellaneous Works by Stewart Dark [Стюърт Дарк]','',8),(992,'Miscellaneous Works by Silvester Gold [Силвестър Голд]','',8),(993,'Miscellaneous Works by Sim Nikolov [Сим Николов]','',8),(994,'Miscellaneous Works by Roger Wilko [Роджър Уилко]','',8),(995,'Legends of the Ancient World','This role-playing system, inspired by <a href=\"/Series/154\">The Fantasy Trip</a>, features adventures that can be played solitaire or by a group of players.  Conversion rules are available for other sets of rules such as the <a href=\"/Category/87\">d20 System</a>.',1),(996,'Legends of Time and Space','This is the science fiction companion series to <a href=\"/Series/995\">Legends of the Ancient World</a>.',1),(997,'Legends of the Untamed West','This is the Western companion series to <a href=\"/Series/995\">Legends of the Ancient World</a>.',1),(998,'Siedler von Catan, Die','This non-interactive novel is based on the board game of the same name, better known to English-speakers as <i>The Settlers of Catan</i>.',3),(999,'Nintendo, játék az erővel','',15),(1000,'Bern Børneridder','',22),(1001,'Der var engang - en grøftekant','In this Danish gamebook, the reader makes decisions involving a farm.',22),(1002,'Dingo Duel','This gamebook series contains a variety of different adventures.  In Jørgen Munck Rasmussen\'s books, the reader goes on various vacations with his or her family and a hamster named Bent, while in Bent Silberbrandt\'s adventures, the reader goes on scavenger hunts.',22),(1003,'Find vej i Mytanien','These short, large-format gamebooks are heavily illustrated.',22),(1004,'Kan du overleve','These books challenge the reader to survive in different past eras.',22),(1005,'Kunsten at få en is','',22),(1006,'Læs dansk bøgerne: Rød serie','This series of Danish children’s books contains only one interactive entry, in which the reader navigates a dangerous forest.',22),(1007,'Miscellaneous Works by Freddy Milton','These gamebooks were designed in comic book format by a popular Danish cartoonist.',22),(1008,'Miscellaneous Works by Patrick Leis','These are short, original Danish gamebooks.  Although they have no series title, they share the same format.  More than one player may participate, and there are different levels of complexity available.',22),(1009,'Politiet efterlyser','In this original Danish gamebook, the reader searches for a missing friend who may have fallen victim to crime.',22),(1010,'Sex i skolen','These books, designed for use in sex education, allow their readers to make decisions about relationships.',22),(1011,'Pas på!','',22),(1012,'Kaland, játék, kockázat','The first seventeen of these translations were published by Rakéta; the remaining two were published by Szukits Könyvkiadó.  Several different <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> titles were also published as the <a href=\"/Series/1013\">Fantázia harcos</a> series.',15),(1013,'Fantázia harcos','Several different <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> titles were also published as the <a href=\"/Series/1012\">Kaland, játék, kockázat</a> series.',15),(1014,'Űrkaland játékkönyvek','',15),(1015,'Harcos képzelet : játékkönyvek','These are original Hungarian books, but they all feature authors using English-sounding pseudonyms and provide official English titles.',15),(1016,'Kaland, játék, szerelem','',15),(1017,'Nyomkereső','',15),(1018,'Western Kalandjáték','This book claims to be a translation of an English title, but it appears more likely that the English information is fictionalized, as in the <a href=\"/Series/1015\">Harcos képzelet : játékkönyvek</a> series.',15),(1019,'Csillagpróba','The third volume of this series was issued along with an issue of the magazine <a href=\"/Item/7406\">Robur</a>.',15),(1020,'Fantasy Kalandjáték','',15),(1021,'Kaland játék varázslat','',15),(1022,'Akció és kaland','',15),(1023,'Doctor Who: Target Books','These books consist primarily of adaptations of television episodes.  While none of the volumes are interactive, later series based on the show (some sharing authors with these classic titles) explored gamebook territory from time to time.\r\n\r\n<p>These books were originally released without numbers, and a numbering scheme was imposed later on which ordered early volumes alphabetically and then picked up chronologically from there; as a result, early titles in the series are not necessarily listed in publication order.  Adaptations of a radio episode and several unbroadcast scripts were released without numbering.  Additionally, three tales featuring companions of the Doctor were published, as were a few activity books.\r\n\r\n<p>In the late eighties, Star rebound pairs of old Target novels in dual editions called <i>Doctor Who Classics</i>.',1),(1024,'Doctor Who: Telos Novellas','This series of hardcover novellas based on the popular television series features no interactive stories, but its debut volume is by <a href=\"/Series/2\">Life\'s Lottery</a> author Kim Newman.  The books are available in two forms: a standard edition and a deluxe edition with extra artwork.',1),(1025,'Piccoli brividi','The Italian editions of these books are actually sold as parts of holiday-themed gift packs.  Some of these gift packs include short story collections rather than gamebooks; these sets are not listed here.',5),(1026,'Samurai','',5),(1027,'Digital Detectives Mysteries','This series of interactive mysteries is an attempt to bring gamebooks into the digital age.  In each book, the reader reads up to a certain point, then has to go to an interactive website which provides clues and allows the user to make choices.  After each website session is complete, the user is directed back to a particular page of the book.  The adventures are fairly linear, but they\'re certainly not devoid of interactivity, particularly at the end when it comes time to name the culprit.\n\n<p>Unfortunately, as of this writing, the publisher has taken the online content down, so there is no longer any way to play through these books due to the missing interactive components.',1),(1028,'Choose Your Destiny','These thick gamebooks are aimed at teen girls and deal with various life choices.',1),(1029,'Follow Your Heart (2006)','Not to be confused with the similar but unrelated <a href=\"/Series/170\">Follow Your Heart Romance</a> series, this series allows young readers to make choices and experiment with relationships.  Interestingly, in spite of being aimed at a female audience, the series was initiated by a male author.',1),(1030,'Kim Possible: Pick a Villain','Based on the Disney cartoon series, these books allow the user to first decide which villain superspy Kim Possible will battle and then make further choices to assist in the adventure.',1),(1031,'Disney\'s Juleklassikere - Fra alle os til alle jer','Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',22),(1032,'Jumbobog','This thick Danish comic included some translations from the Italian <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a> magazine. Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',22),(1033,'Onkel Joakim','Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',22),(1034,'Classico Estate, Il','This comic book reprinted a story from <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a>. Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',5),(1035,'Paperino','This comic book reprinted a story from <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a>. Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',5),(1036,'Paper Fantasy','This comic book reprinted a story from <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a>. Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',5),(1037,'Piú Disney','This comic book reprinted some stories from <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a>. Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',5),(1038,'Super Disney','This comic book reprinted some stories from <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a>. Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',5),(1039,'Topomistery','This comic book reprinted some stories from <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a>. Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',5),(1040,'Buraddo soodo [ブラッド・ソード]','',4),(1041,'Pocketbok','Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',29),(1042,'Iron Dragons','This non-interactive novel is set in the world of Mayfair Games\' fantasy rail board game <i>Iron Dragon</i>.',1),(1043,'Ghostwriter','This series, based on the PBS television show, consists primarily of non-interactive novels, but it contains at least one fully interactive branching-path volume and at least one other book that encourages reader participation through puzzle-solving within a linear narrative.',1),(1044,'Descent Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on a 3-D video game.',1),(1045,'Miscellaneous Works by Jorge Luis Borges','Argentinian author Jorge Luis Borges never wrote a gamebook, yet he speculated on the form before it actually existed.  Borges enjoyed writing stories and fictional criticism dealing with books that never actually existed, and in two of his writings, he described interactive narratives.\r\n\r\n<p><a href=\"/Item/7380\">Examen de la obra de Herbert Quain</a> describes fictional author Herbert Quain\'s <i>Abril marzo</i>, a 13-chapter novel which is actually a three-part story containing two branch-points, thus having nine possible readings.\r\n\r\n<p>The better known <a href=\"/Item/7381\">El Jardín de senderos que se bifurcan</a> describes an ancient Chinese writer, Ts\'ui Pên, who went into seclusion to write a book and to construct a maze.  One of the story\'s twists is that the book and the maze are in fact the same item; Pên\'s novel, <i>El Jardín de senderos que se bifurcan</i>, is a branching, maze-like narrative which only makes sense if read in the correct manner.  Indeed, it is only in the correct manner of reading that Borges\' descriptions fail to exactly describe the modern gamebook; while actual gamebooks include instructions on when and how to turn to another section of the text, the works Borges describes seem to rely entirely on the deductive powers of the reader to determine the correct order of reading.',6),(1046,'Miscellaneous Works by Jorge Luis Borges','',1),(1047,'Elige tu propio escalofrío','',6),(1048,'Silhouette Romance','Although none of the volumes in this series were interactive, one story was converted into a simple gamebook upon being translated into Italian.',1),(1049,'Harmony Special','This book is actually an Italian translation of a regular American Harlequin romance novel with a second ending by an Italian author added on; thus, the reader may make one choice: read the passionate ending or read the romantic one.',5),(1050,'Robur','This science fiction magazine bundled the third book of the <a href=\"/Series/1019\">Csillagpróba</a> series with one issue.',15),(1051,'Paths of Doom','This series strives to recapture some of the feel of the classic <a href=\"/Series/79\">Endless Quest</a> series, but it adds a couple of important twists -- each book has only one positive ending, so there\'s more challenge in finding a successful ending to the story, and the writing is third person, past tense for a more novel-like feel.\n\n<p>Some books in the series were also made available in electronic format.',1),(1052,'Doctor Who: Target Books (American Reprints)','Several of the <a href=\"/Series/1023\">Target Books</a> were reprinted in America.  Pinnacle released ten stand-alone paperback reprints, while Doubleday produced two hardback collections (sold through book clubs) which collected three adventures each.  All of the paperbacks plus the first hardback include an essay by science fiction author Harlan Ellison singing the praises of the series.',1),(1053,'Doctor Who Quiz Books','Not to be confused with the trivia volumes included in the <a href=\"/Series/1023\">Target Books</a> series, these volumes feature Peter Davison\'s Doctor talking to his companions about various subjects.  Along the way, the reader gets to participate in various games, quizzes and activities.  Although none of these books are interactive in the \"make a choice and turn the page\" sense, their author did later contribute to the <a href=\"/Series/160\">Find Your Fate - Doctor Who</a> series.',1),(1054,'To the Magical Pond','This self-published offering from the author of the <a href=\"/Series/219\">Lands of Delorian</a> series is aimed at a younger audience.  An electronic edition is available as part of the <a href=\"/Series/218\">inebooks</a> series.',1),(1055,'Choose Your Own Mind-Fuck Fest','Although the books in this series claim to be volumes 3 and 17, there are many gaps in the sequence, which parodies the <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> books.',1),(1056,'Libro Avventura','These original Italian gamebooks resemble Patrick Burston\'s <a href=\"/Series/490\">Which Way?</a> books.',5),(1057,'gioco di ruolo del signore degli anelli, Il','',5),(1058,'Novecentonovanta','Novecentonovanta is a club for gamers which publishes a few small booklets, some of which happen to be gamebooks.',5),(1059,'Sfida alla lettura','This series of books is aimed at elementary school students, and most of its entries are regular linear books.  However, at least two titles were interactive, featuring branching plots with a few educationally-oriented puzzles scattered through them.  The books are large and nearly square in shape with full-color illustrations throughout.',5),(1060,'Straniero, Lo','This is among the first original gamebooks to be written in Italian.  Its game system, described very concisely, features a few basic attributes, inventory management, combat and a means of tracking special events.  Random numbers are printed at the tops of the pages in order to avoid the need for dice.',5),(1061,'Tunnel e troll','The Italian editions were translated from Corgi\'s British versions of the adventures.',5),(1062,'Tattica game','This series consists of translations collected from a variety of different English-language series.  Books that originally came with a collection of fancy components are simplified in their Italian editions -- everything is inside the books, with dice replaced by paper cut-outs and full-color artwork reduced to black and white.',5),(1063,'Novecento Giochi','This series of books includes a revised edition of a gamebook previously published with a magazine and a history of a famous board game.',5),(1064,'E Giochi','This game magazine included short gamebook adventures in a couple of issues, and it also had a full stand-alone gamebook bundled with another issue.',5),(1065,'Libri di Mondo Erre, I','This series of Italian children\'s books includes two interactive entries.  The first casts the reader as a Boy Scout and involves an adventure at camp; the second involves a child of ancient Rome searching for missing neighbors.  Both books are large paperbacks and contain supplemental information (such as Scout activities and historical notes) in sidebars.',5),(1066,'Storia e dossier - Storiagame','The <b>Storia e dossier</b> history magazine included gamebooks (using the <b>Storiagame</b> label) as supplemental booklets released with several issues.',5),(1067,'Fantastica game','',5),(1068,'Giocarea','This magazine featured a number of complete games in each issue, including several brief gamebooks presented as a series of small boxes of text at the bottoms of selected pages.',5),(1069,'Tunnels and Trolls: Infinity Limited','Infinity Limited, a small role-playing company, released a solo adventure for <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a>.',1),(1070,'Tunnels and Trolls: Outlaw Press','This company published a wealth of third-party <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> products.  Most were originals, but the series also included reprints of vintage items like <a href=\"/Item/1863\">The Old Dwarf Mine</a> through a collaboration with Ken St. Andre and his Trollhalla community.  After January of 2010, Ken St. Andre stopped publishing with Outlaw Press and began his own imprint, <a href=\"/Series/1344\">Trollhalla Press</a>.',1),(1071,'Hobbit Hole, The','This <a href=\"/Item/1863\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> magazine includes solo adventures in most issues; it was also the subject of a plagiarism controversy.',1),(1072,'Tunnels and Trolls: Tavernmaster Games','This company has produced several third-party <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> products.  It also served as a British distributor for many <a href=\"/Series/1070\">Outlaw Press</a> items while that company was active.',1),(1073,'Abenteuer-Spielbuch im Geborgenen Land','',3),(1074,'Abenteuer-Spielbuch in Ulldart','',3),(1075,'Hook Up or Break Up','This minimally interactive romance series allows the reader to choose the protagonist\'s partner.',1),(1076,'Lost in Austen: Create Your Own Jane Austen Adventure','This book allows the reader to take on the role of Elizabeth Bennet (protagonist of <i>Pride and Prejudice</i>) and explore alternative paths featuring characters and situations from a variety of Jane Austen books.  The adventure features a simple game system where the reader tracks three key scores (Intelligence, Confidence and Fortune) along with key Accomplishments and Connections.  The objective, of course, is to obtain a comfortable marriage.',1),(1077,'Doctor Who: Decide Your Destiny (Series 1)','These interactive adventures tie in with the revived <i>Doctor Who</i> television series. This first series of twelve books focuses on the David Tenannt era.',1),(1078,'Interactive Mega-Zine','This fanzine, a product of <a href=\"http://www.chooseyourstory.com\">www.chooseyourstory.com</a>, features original interactive adventures and gamebook-themed articles.',1),(1079,'Fighting Fantasy: Amateur Adventures','Countless amateur authors have written unofficial, unauthorized adventures using the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> system.  All of the adventures listed have found free online publication in one form or another, be it downloadable pdf/Word documents, play online versions or, in some cases, both.  Creating a thorough catalog of these gamebooks is nearly impossible, but a sampling are represented here.',1),(1080,'Miscellaneous Works by Mark Wightman','Mark Wightman is a game designer and author who has self-published a gamebook inspired by the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> system.  <a href=\"/Item/7560\">Last Stand at Kirrinbahr</a> features a simple game system in which the reader has three pre-set attributes (Combat, Health and Karma, which are equivalent to <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>\'s Skill, Stamina and Luck).  Expanding upon the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> system are four additional attributes which the reader can assign points to: Agility, Stealth, Engineering and Diplomacy.  Combat and inventory management are extremely <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>-like.  The adventure requires fairly extensive note-taking (you can return to locations and need to keep track of what remains there, and the number of enemies defeated during the course of the story is significant), and it also allows for detailed mapping, with overhead diagrams of rooms accompanying many sections.',1),(1081,'Choices (free versions)','This series of short adventures is published online for free download.  The series was later rebooted as the in-print, full-length <a href=\"\"\"/Series/1350\">Choices</a> books.',1),(1082,'Ulysses','This self-published series allows the reader to control the actions of the entire crew of the starship Ulysses.  There is no game system, though the reader is sometimes required to remember prior events in order to determine where to turn next.',1),(1083,'DestinyQuest','This self-published series applies anarchic humor to the standard interactive format.',1),(1084,'Charlie Choicemaker Chooses...','Self-published by a licensed mental health counselor and registered nurse, this book is designed as a tool to help children make good decisions.  It is available in both print and electronic editions.',1),(1085,'Miscellaneous Works by Christian Kindschy','This author has self-published an interactive romance.',1),(1086,'Divinity','This electronically-published role-playing game of god-like powers has a free solitaire quickplay adventure available.',1),(1087,'Solitaire Tic-Tac-Toe','This unusual book uses the gamebook format to provide the reader with an opponent in a game of Tic-Tac-Toe.  The game is filled with numbered game grids.  You turn to the page corresponding with the space where you wish to put your mark, and then you see your opponent\'s next move.  At some points, you are asked to rotate your grid (which allows the author to economize on space).  It is possible (but obviously not common) to win the game, even though a crueler author could theoretically have designed the book to force no better than a stalemate.',1),(1088,'Pretty Little Mistakes','Starting on the last day of high school, this book allows the reader (in the role of a female character) to explore a wide array of possible lives.',1),(1089,'Choose Your Boyfriend: Date Him or Dump Him','As the title suggests, this is a series of romances for young readers.',1),(1090,'Boy Shopping','This series of teen romances allows the reader to choose the protagonist\'s romantic entanglements.',1),(1091,'Choose Your Own Adventure - Dragonlarks','This series mixes reprints from the earlier <a href=\"/Series/74\">Choose Your Own Adventure for Younger Readers</a> series with original titles.  The adventures are illustrated in full color throughout.  The two unnumbered titles in the list below had ISBNs registered but were never released.',1),(1092,'Choose Your Own Adventure - iPod Downloads','This audio series features the complete text of the original books in a format playable on an iPod.  The first volume is read by the author.',1),(1093,'Choose Your Own Adventure - The Golden Path','This is the first original spin-off series from the <a href=\"/Series/870\">Choose Your Own Adventure Reissues</a>.  The books are set on a dangerous future Earth where the government cannot be trusted and powerful mystical forces are at work.  The series forms a continuing storyline, and each book can lead the reader to different starting points in the following volume depending on which ending is reached. Although initially announced as a six-book series, it was eventually reduced to a trilogy.',1),(1094,'Girls About Town','This series of short story collections included a semi-interactive tale in one volume.',1),(1095,'Miscellaneous Works by Christopher Manson','Christopher Manson, a designer of puzzle books, created at least one volume in gamebook format.',1),(1096,'You Are the Coach','These books describe real sports scenarios from the past and present multiple options for the reader to choose from.  After the reader makes a choice, the actual outcome of the historical game is described.  The reader\'s goal is simply to guess what actually happened; hypothetical outcomes of the other possibilities are not described.  Since the reader\'s choices have no impact on the flow of the books, the series is more closely related to the \"solve it yourself\" mystery genre than to even the most minimally interactive gamebooks.\r\n\r\n<p>The books are only loosely grouped as a series -- while they all have the same format, the series title varies from book to book (\"You Are the Coach,\" \"You Are the Manager,\" \"It\'s Your Team\").\r\n\r\n<p>The series was first published in hardback by Lerner, then reprinted in paperback by Dell.',1),(1097,'Encyclopedia Brown','One of the most popular of the \"guess the ending\" mystery series, these books follow the adventures of a young detective and challenge readers to solve several short mysteries in each volume (solutions are in the back).  The series has been in print for decades, and over the years a couple of books have been renamed, but little else has changed.',1),(1098,'Billy Acres','This full-color, full-size adventure is the latest in a long line to claim to be the first interactive graphic novel.',1),(1099,'Woodland Forest Chronicles','These fantasy gamebooks, aimed at ages 8-12, follow the adventures of anthropomorphic animals living in Woodland Forest.  They feature a game system unique to this setting -- in place of the standard-issue combat system, there is a \"fleeing/chasing\" system that simulates running from predators or (in rare cases) catching other characters.  This distinctive feature is accompanied by some more traditional inventory management plus a single Energy attribute used mainly for measuring the player\'s ability to continue running.  Randomization (used both in fleeing/chasing and to cause random story branches) is accomplished either with a six-sided die or with the dice rolls printed at the bottoms of the book\'s pages.',1),(1100,'Dragonology Pocket Adventures','These tiny gamebooks are part of the <i>Dragonology</i> franchise, which is built around a coffee table book offering a natural history of dragons written by fictional 19th century \"dragonologist\" Dr. Ernest Drake.  In each adventure, the reader undertakes a different dragonological mission on behalf of Dr. Drake.',1),(1101,'Tangled Tales','These gamebooks are presented in an unusual format -- each story is a deck of numbered cards, and each choice is accompanied by a series of symbols.  The reader makes decisions by punching the appropriate series of symbols into a battery-operated electronic device which then emits appropriate sound effects and directs the reader to the next card.  Although a couple of stand-alone adventures were produced, the <i>Gatekeeper Chronicles</i> were the main focus of the product line -- the first adventure in the series comes with the electronic reader, and two additional stories were sold separately.  Successfully completing all three of these adventures then unlocked a fourth story (hidden among the three main decks) concluding the saga.',1),(1102,'Tunnels and Trolls: Dragon Tree Press','This company has published several third-party <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> products.',1),(1103,'Raven','This <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> \'zine was first published in England in the late seventies and was reissued more recently by Outlaw Press.',1),(1104,'Grim Reaper','This <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> \'zine was first published in England in 1987 and was reissued more recently by Outlaw Press.',1),(1105,'Dungeonier Digest','This <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> magazine includes a solo adventure in at least one issue.',1),(1106,'Lone Wolf, Collector\'s Edition (2007-)','Starting in 2007, the 28 classic <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> books began to be reissued.  These reissues differ from the original editions to varying degrees. All incorporate corrections and clarifications drawn from <a href=\"http://www.projectaon.org/\">Project Aon</a>, but the first volume is significantly rewritten, and subsequent volumes, while much closer to original printings, include short bonus adventures.<p>Vols. 29-32 were published here for the first time in the form of \"collector\'s editions\"; vols. 29 and 30 also contain bonus adventures.<p>The first 17 volumes in the reissue series were published by Mongoose, with Mantikore-Verlag publishing volumes 18-22, and Holmgard Press publishing the remaining volumes.',1),(1107,'Lupo solitario (2007-)','',5),(1108,'Fabulous Terrible: The Adventures of You','Although produced by the publisher of the <a href=\"/Series/870\">Choose Your Own Adventure Reissues</a> and written in the second person, this series is not interactive.  It\'s a series of linear novels aimed at teen girls.  It tells of the reader\'s adventures after mysteriously gaining acceptance to a prestigious private school, but it doesn\'t allow any actual decision-making along the way.',1),(1109,'EDGE - I, Hero: Decide Your Own Destiny','These basic gamebooks tell stories in a variety of time periods and genres. Some were reprinted in a collection under the \"Be a Hero\" title.',1),(1110,'Pandora','',5),(1111,'Librogame','',5),(1112,'Kimi nara dou suru? [きみならどうする?]','This is the first series containing Japanese translations of <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure Books</a>.  It was followed by <a href=\"/Series/1113\">Adobenchaa Bukkusu [アドベンチャーブックス]</a>',4),(1113,'Adobenchaa Bukkusu [アドベンチャーブックス]','This is the second series containing Japanese translations of <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure Books</a> following the earlier <a href=\"/Series/1112\">Kimi nara dou suru? [きみならどうする?]</a>',4),(1114,'Deemon kuesto [デーモン・クエスト]','',4),(1115,'Multiple Ending Stories','The branching in this adventure is controlled by random coin tosses rather than reader decisions.',1),(1116,'Diario Game','This diary includes a short gamebook story.',5),(1117,'Girisha shinwa adobenchaa geemu [ギリシャ神話アドベンチャーゲーム]','',4),(1118,'AD&D Geemu bukku [AD&Dゲームブック]','',4),(1119,'Dyueru masutaa [デュエル・マスター]','',4),(1120,'Car wars [カー・ウォーズ]','',4),(1121,'Ace of Aces','',4),(1122,'Doragon uoriaazu [ドラゴン・ウォーリアーズ]','',4),(1123,'Adobansuto Faintingu Fantajii [アドバンスト・ファイティング・ファンタジー]','This translation splits the original rulebook into two separate volumes.',4),(1124,'Danjonzu & doragonzu [ダンジョンズ&ドラゴンズ]','',4),(1125,'Endoresu kuesto geemu bukku [エンドレスクエストゲームブック]','',4),(1126,'Sara bukkusu doragon fantajii [サラ・ブックスドラゴン・ファンタジー]','',4),(1127,'Roon Urufu [ローン・ウルフ]','',4),(1128,'Farukon [ファルコン]','',4),(1129,'Yunikoon Feitomasutaa [ユニコーン・フェイトマスター]','',4),(1130,'Gendai kyouyou bunko: adobenchaa geemu bukku [現代教養文庫―アドベンチャーゲームブック]','',4),(1131,'Lost Worlds','',4),(1132,'Soosarii [ソーサリー]','This series was first published in 1985 and then reprinted with new covers starting in 2003.',4),(1133,'Kishi to mahoutsukai kimi wa dochira wo erabu ka? [騎士と魔法使い 君はどちらを選ぶか?]','',4),(1134,'X fairu [X-ファイル]','This gamebook, only released in Japan despite being based on an American TV series, adapts and expands upon one of the episodes of the show.',4),(1135,'Ouji no taiketsu [王子の対決]','',4),(1136,'Indi Joonzu adobenchaa geemu bukku [インディジョーンズ アドベンチャー・ゲームブック]','',4),(1137,'Jeemuzu Bondo shiriizu [ジェームズ・ボンド・シリーズ]','',4),(1138,'Way of the Tiger, The','',4),(1139,'Mech\' I sud\'ba [Меч и судьба]','This large-format gamebook deals with King Arthur and the Knights of the Round Table.',27),(1140,'Priklyuchencheskaya kniga-igra [Приключенческая книга-игра]','These gamebooks feature a basic game system reminiscent of <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>\'s, though they have an interesting innovation: some items collected have numbers associated with them, and these numbers may be added to or subtracted from certain section numbers in order to use the object.  \n\n<p>The first four books of this series were revised and expanded and included in the <a href=\"/Series/1141\">Put\' geroya [Путь героя]</a> series. The first and fifth books were also released in CD-ROM format by Buka Entertainment.\n\n<p>The last two books listed here were not officially part of this series; book five was first released on CD-ROM and later printed as a stand-alone volume by BookAlive, while book six was only playable online.  They are listed here because they share the same author and game system.',27),(1141,'Put\' geroya [Путь героя]','This multi-genre series started by releasing revised and expanded versions of the four printed <a href=\"/Series/1140\">Priklyuchencheskaya kniga-igra [Приключенческая книга-игра]</a> books, then added a few additional titles with a similar game system.',27),(1142,'Stan\' stalnoy krisoy [Стань стальной крысой]','This translation was first published in <a href=\"/Series/1150\">Esli</a> magazine, then later reprinted as a stand-alone book.',27),(1143,'Superpriklyucheniye [Суперприключение]','',27),(1144,'Koschsheeva cep\' [Кощеева цепь]','',27),(1145,'Strannik, izgonayuschiy mrak [Странник, изгоняющий мрак]','',27),(1146,'Deviens conquérant de l\'impossible','',27),(1147,'Labirint Kolduna [Лабиринт колдуна]','',27),(1148,'Tri dorogi [Три дороги]','This original Russian gamebook contains three unrelated adventures, each dealing with a different fantasy hero.  Conan is the only character likely to be familiar to English-speaking readers, and the adventure featuring him is actually an expansion of a previously published mini-solo from <a href=\"/Item/8118\">Conan i talisman ognya [Конан и талисман огня]</a>.',27),(1149,'Vibery sebe priklyucheniye [Выбери себе приключение]','',27),(1150,'Esli','This magazine included a gamebook in at least one issue.',27),(1151,'Inz Interactive Novels','This series, projected as a four-volume story arc, follows the adventures of Inz, a young warrior in a world where science and magic coexist.  Like <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a>, the books follow a single hero as he grows in power.  However, there is no game system here -- the format is more along the lines of the later <a href=\"/Series/79\">Endless Quest</a> books, with fairly lengthy text sections between choices.  The books are self-published through a print-on-demand service.',1),(1152,'Doctor Who Magazine','This magazine is devoted to coverage of the <i>Doctor Who</i> TV series, very occasionally featuring interactive content.',1),(1153,'Full Flight','This series of books aimed at reluctant readers includes some interactive volumes.',1),(1154,'Epic Adventure Game Books','These fantasy gamebooks were originally designed to take place in the world of the <i>Cursed Empire</i> role-playing game and use a simplified version of the RPG\'s \"Spartan\'s Unleashed Game System.\"  The first book was briefly available in paperback and eBook formats before being pulled off the market due to a licensing issue.  The first book was later reissued in a larger format with a different setting and game system, and the remaining adventures retained this format.\r\n\r\n<p>The game system used in the revised version of the series uses six-sided dice for randomization.  Players have two attributes (Physical and Mental) along with Life Points and a variety of skills.  Character generation is accomplished through a combination of dice-rolling and point allocation.  All action resolution is achieved by cross referencing a skill or attribute against an opposed skill or difficulty level in a special table -- this yields the target number that must be beaten to succeed.  Combat uses this table to determine who hits each round and then uses the difference between an attack score and a defense score to determine damage dealt.',1),(1155,'Witchsnare','Possibly the first original gamebook published in India (released in English by the Indian arm of Penguin), this fantasy adventure involves a battle with an evil witch in the fantasy world of Pushpasthala.',1),(1156,'Choose Your Own Misadventure','',1),(1157,'Submission','This erotic gamebook is available both in a physical version from Lulu and as a Kindle download from Amazon.',1),(1158,'Choose the Right','',1),(1159,'Miscellaneous Works by Jacob Dean and Maggie DiRenzo','These authors have written an interactive story available in paperback and eBook format.',1),(1160,'Star Frontiers','This science fiction role-playing game includes a programmed adventure in its basic rulebook that, while designed to be run by a game master, very closely resembles a gamebook.',1),(1161,'Journey to the Center of the Earth 3D: Choose Your Own Journey','This movie tie-in uses the standard basic \"make a choice and turn the page\" format.  Text between choices tends to be fairly long, and each section of the story begins with its own chapter title.',1),(1162,'Computer Adventures','This series of graphic novels featured linear stories occasionally interrupted by type-and-play computer games written in BASIC.  Only one adventure was widely distributed, but more were planned.  Separate versions of the book were released for different computer platforms.',1),(1163,'Micro Adventure','These linear novels are occasionally interrupted by simple BASIC computer programs designed to be typed in and played.  The idea is to simulate key moments in the story, though the programs are generally of the extremely simplistic \"type in a word or number and hit ENTER\" variety.  The series was followed by the <a href=\"/Series/1164\">Magic Micro Adventure</a> books for younger readers.',1),(1164,'Magic Micro Adventure','These books follow the \"linear novel interspersed with computer program listings\" format of the <a href=\"/Series/1163\">Micro Adventure</a> series, but they are aimed at a younger audience.',1),(1165,'Uzhastiki 2 [Ужастики 2]','',27),(1166,'Solo-Würfel-Abenteuer','This gamebook series (just one volume as of this writing) was distributed for free over the Internet in PDF format.',3),(1167,'sLAng','',6),(1168,'Oro y el Acero, El','',6),(1169,'Revista NoSoloRol','This freely downloadable, PDF format online magazine published several solitaire adventures to tie in with the publisher\'s <a href=\"/Category/94\">Singular</a> gamebook line.',6),(1170,'Reinos olvidados','',6),(1171,'Bytes Brothers, The','Sort of a cross between <a href=\"/Series/1097\">Encyclopedia Brown</a> and <a href=\"/Series/1163\">Micro Adventure</a>, each volume in this series contains several short mysteries.  The user must read carefully and run very simple BASIC computer programs in order to guess the solutions.',1),(1172,'Can You Solve the Mystery?','Each volume in this series contains several short mysteries for the reader to solve.  Illustrations accompanying the stories contain additional clues toward the solutions.  Answers are printed at the back of each book in mirror type.',1),(1173,'Oscar giochi: G&L','These two reality-based gamebooks were published under the same label as the Italian translations of <a href=\"/Series/842\">Falcon</a> and <a href=\"/Series/1061\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> and various volumes about games in general.',5),(1174,'protagonista sei tu, La','',5),(1175,'superchicche, Le','',5),(1176,'IperLibri','This series, marketed as a cross between a book and a video game, consists of three sub-series: the <i>La Leggenda degli Erranti</i> fantasy trilogy, the <i>L.B.</i> fantasy/sci-fi hybrid, and the <i>Gratuiti</i> sample adventure available online as a free download.',5),(1177,'Abenteuer-Spielbuch in Wlachkis','',3),(1178,'Abenteuer-Spielbuch in der Welt des Hexer von Salem','These gamebooks tie in with Pegasus Spiele\'s short-lived <i>Hexer von Salem</i> role-playing game, which itself was an offshoot of <a href=\"/Series/60\">Call of Cthulhu</a>.',3),(1179,'Mutants & Masterminds','This superhero role-playing game includes a solo adventure in one of its supplements.',1),(1180,'Seventh Expert, The','This stand-alone book casts the reader as an advisor helping to rebuild a medieval village destroyed by storms.  The book is not the familiar \"read the section, choose, turn the page\" style of gamebook.  Instead, it is a linear series of chapters.  Each chapter is a fictional narrative laced with historical sidebars.  At the end of each chapter, the reader chooses from a list of items that might help the village.  Some items have prerequisites and must be built in a particular order.  The items chosen, combined with die rolls, determine how several scores (Effort, Supplies and Defense) fluctuate during the course of play.  The story can end prematurely if Supplies run out.',1),(1181,'Resol el misteri','',20),(1182,'Resuelve el misterio','',6),(1183,'Club Penguin Pick Your Path','These gamebooks tie in with the Disney-owned online club.',1),(1184,'Regional Accounts Director of Firetop Mountain, The','The title says it all -- an office-themed parody of <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>.',1),(1185,'Otogirisou orijinaru geemu noberu [弟切草オリジナルゲームノベル]','',4),(1186,'Breadman','This independent comic book, the second series to feature superhero Breadman, went interactive starting with its second issue.  The reader chooses the course of the story, manages an inventory of items and interacts with various characters and scenery.  The overall tone is not unlike a point-and-click computer adventure game.  The reader moves from location to location collecting items.  An \"interaction table\" allows certain items to interact with locations or each other by adding numbers together.  The user records letters at various points to keep track of certain key events; when letters have been recorded, it can change the results of interactions or open up new areas to explore.',1),(1187,'Adventures in Logan','This book created by high school students in Utah as a collaborative project between schools.',1),(1188,'Fabled Worlds','This role-playing system was designed for solitaire play.  One book is currently available using the system containing a pair of zombie-horror-themed solo adventures.',1),(1189,'Dungeons & Dragons Miniatures Game','This miniatures combat game inspired an official free online scenario that, while not exactly a gamebook, is a close relative, having the player read a story, resolve combats along the way, and occasionally read different blocks of text based on decisions made.',1),(1190,'Miscellaneous Works by Mathias Dewald','This author has written a single gamebook, available as a printed book or a free download.',2),(1191,'Gloire Posthume','This series is available in printed and (currently free) eBook formats.',2),(1192,'Just Make a Choice!','This series puts a humorous spin on the gamebook format.',1),(1193,'Aventure dont vous êtes le héros','These books use the gamebook format to explore historical topics.',2),(1194,'You Choose: Interactive History Adventures','These gamebooks are designed to introduce children to various historical topics.  They were followed by several spin-off series, including <a href=\"/Series/1508\">You Choose: Interactive Survival Adventures</a> and <a href=\"/Series/1791\">You Choose: Interactive Mythological Adventures</a>.\n\nThese books appear to be unrelated both to the to the <a href=\"/Series/1\">You Choose the Way</a> Dick Tracy gamebook and to the Australian <a href=\"/Series/1623\">You Choose</a> series.',1),(1195,'Elige tu propia aventura - Original Mexican Releases','This series was published by Aconcagua Ediciones y Publicaciones, who were distributors of the Spanish <a href=\"/Series/837\">Timun Mas Choose Your Own Adventure translations</a> in Mexico. Despite this, it shares that series\' title, and the format is practically identical to the <a href=\"/Series/74\">Choose Your Own Adventure for Younger Readers</a> series, except the covers are blue. Apparently, only one book was released, but it was numbered, and copies are extremely difficult to find nowadays.',6),(1196,'Miscellaneous Works by Shane Garvey','This Australian game designer has released a stand-alone gamebook, available in print and eBook formats.',1),(1197,'Miscellaneous Works by Manuel da Silva Carneiro','',7),(1198,'Kniga volshebstva [Книга волшебства]','',27),(1199,'Conan [Конан]','The Russian Conan series included a mini-gamebook in one volume.  This adventure was later expanded as part of <a href=\"/Series/1148\">Tri dorogi [Три дороги]</a>',27),(1200,'PuzzleQuest','These two hardcover books are not gamebooks in the \"make a choice and turn the page\" sense, but are filled with visual and textual clues that the reader must search for in order to solve a larger puzzle.  They are set in the <a href=\"/Category/37\">Fighting Fantasy</a> universe.  The first book was also printed in paperback as part of the <a href=\"/Series/1201\">Fantasy Questbook</a> series, while the second book\'s paperback edition was a stand-alone release.\n\n<p>These books are notorious for the ultra-complex solutions to the visual puzzles that they consist of. Games Workshop even went as far as to publish a solution pamphlet to aid players in navigating the first book in the series.',1),(1201,'Fantasy Questbook','These books are not gamebooks in the \"make a choice and turn the page\" sense, but are filled with visual and textual clues that the reader must search for in order to solve a larger puzzle.  One volume, reprinted from the <a href=\"/Series/1200\">PuzzleQuest</a> series, is set in the <a href=\"/Category/37\">Fighting Fantasy</a> universe.',1),(1202,'Défis fantastiques (2004-)','These translations feature a new order from the original <a href=\"/Series/390\">Défis fantastiques (1983-1997)</a> releases and include several titles not printed as part of the original sequence. Starting in 2018, some titles were reprinted to include bonus background material and pregenerated characters curated by partner publisher Scriptarium.',2),(1203,'Dragonroar','This role-playing system included a solo adventure in cassette tape format with the base game.  One adventure module was also released which included another tape that could serve as a game master.',1),(1204,'Adventure Gaming','This one-off publication included two board games and a small gamebook. The gamebook contained in this set seems to be the earliest example of a solo adventure which included its own game system (rather than requiring an external rules set to play), predating both the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> gamebooks and the <a href=\"/Series/201\">High Fantasy</a> solitaire adventures.',1),(1205,'Solve-the-Puzzles Adventure Tales','',1),(1206,'Lost Worlds: Queen\'s Blade Rebellion [クイーンズブレイド リベリオン]','This Japanese series uses the <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds</a> game system under license.  Each volume is a full-color hardback.  It follows <a href=\"/Series/905\">Queen\'s Blade</a> and <a href=\"/Series/1207\">Queen\'s Gate</a> and precedes <a href=\"/Series/1491\">Queen\'s Blade Grimoire</a>.',4),(1207,'Lost Worlds: Queen\'s Gate [クイーンズゲイト]','This Japanese series uses the <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds</a> game system under license.  Each volume is a full-color hardback.  Some characters are licensed from anime and video games.  It is a companion to <a href=\"/Series/905\">Queen\'s Blade</a>, <a href=\"/Series/1206\">Queen\'s Blade Rebellion</a> and <a href=\"/Series/1491\">Queen\'s Blade Grimoire</a>.',4),(1208,'Sweet Agatha','This experimental book invites the reader to solve the mystery of a girl\'s disappearance.  Presented in the form of a journal, it contains clues which are intended to be cut out and shared with a friend.  After a thorough study of the book, the reader and friend must improvise an ending to its story.',1),(1209,'Choose Your Path','',1),(1210,'Kingdom of Loathing','The first of these short gamebooks was given out at the 2006 San Diego Comic Con to promote the online game that inspired it.  Additional adventures were released in the following years.  The adventures are also available free online in PDF format.',1),(1211,'Fantasy Hero Gamebooks','',1),(1212,'Lone Wolf: Chronicles of Magnamund Novels','These novels, based on the <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> gamebooks, are a separate series from the earlier <a href=\"/Series/149\">Legends of Lone Wolf</a> novels.',1),(1213,'Miscellaneous Works by Lyess Scarandeti','',2),(1214,'Miscellaneous Works by Dennis Guerrier','Likely inspired by programmed instruction methods, author Dennis Guerrier experimented with interactive books fairly early in their development, using the format for solitaire games and producing some of the earliest interactive fiction through collaborations with other writers.\r\n\r\n<p>Most of the books listed here were published by Panther, except for <a href=\"/Item/8155\">State of Emergency</a>, which was published by Penguin.',1),(1215,'Adventure Quest','',1),(1216,'Hanse der Winde, Die','Unlike most gamebooks of this complexity level, this series does not break down into small numbered sections -- instead, all jumps refer to specific page numbers of the book, and all text segments take up at least one full page (sometimes more).',3),(1217,'Über den Sylinthpass','',3),(1218,'Envoyer Rollenspielmagazin','',3),(1219,'Choose Your Own Adversity','This self-published effort is the result of National Novel Writing Month.  It was made available both in paperback format and as a free download.',1),(1220,'Choose Your Path!','This collection of five interactive stories was written as a charity project by sixth graders at the American International School of Lagos, Nigeria.',1),(1221,'Testigo','This interactive horror movie was released in DVD format in Mexico.',6),(1222,'Miscellaneous Works by Robertson Sondoh Jr.','Robertson Sondoh Jr. occasionally produces online mini-gamebooks.',1),(1223,'Chronicles of the Magi, The','These non-interactive novels take place in the world of Legend, the same setting used by the <a href=\"/Series/55\">Dragon Warriors</a> RPG and the <a href=\"/Series/56\">Blood Sword</a> gamebooks.  The series was reissued in eBook format to coincide with the re-release of <a href=\"/Series/55\">Dragon Warriors</a>.',1),(1224,'Wizardry: Crea la tua avventura da giovane mago','',5),(1225,'Endless Quest Reissues','Following in the footsteps of the <a href=\"/Series/12\">Fighting Fantasy Reissues</a> and <a href=\"/Series/870\">Choose Your Own Adventure Reissues</a>, this series contains updated and reordered reprints drawn from the <a href=\"/Series/79\">Endless Quest</a> and <a href=\"/Series/65\">Crimson Crystal</a> series.',1),(1226,'Twisted Journeys','These adventures combine prose and full-color comic book panels to provide interactive experiences in a variety of genres.',1),(1227,'Star Wars - The Clone Wars: Decide Your Destiny (American)','The first two titles in this series of traditional gamebooks do not have an online component.  The third title is reprinted from the British <a href=\"/Series/1286\">Star Wars - The Clone Wars: Decide Your Destiny</a> series and thus adds online elements to the story.',1),(1228,'Dragontales','',6),(1229,'Armchair General','This military magazine has a focus on gaming and interactivity.  The first few years of issues featured serialized pick-a-path combat scenarios.  Later issues have less gamebook-like content, replacing full-fledged interactive stories with shorter articles that simply encourage the reader to think about solutions to hypothetical problems.',1),(1230,'Switching','This interactive film plays on an infinite loop.  The viewer can interact with the film by hitting a button at any time, causing the narrative to vary as a result.',22),(1231,'Late Fragment','This interactive film is largely inspired by <a href=\"/Series/1230\">Switching</a>, and uses a similar system in which the viewer causes the narrative to change by hitting a button during the unfolding of the story.',1),(1232,'Late Hour, The','This band produced an album in which each song ends with a voice prompt directing the listener to choose which track to listen to next.  A different story in song is told each time based on these branches.',1),(1233,'QUERP: Quick Easy Role Play','This fantasy role-playing game, aimed at inexperienced players, includes a solo adventure with the second edition rules.',1),(1234,'ForeSight','This generic role-playing system inspired a single solo adventure.',1),(1235,'Perfect Dark Novels','These non-interactive science fiction novels are related to a video game series.',1),(1236,'City of Heroes Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on the massively multiplayer online superhero game.',1),(1237,'Defender Novels','The classic video game spawned a non-interactive novel.',1),(1238,'Might and Magic Novels','The popular fantasy role-playing computer games spawned several non-interactive novels, though not all of them actually reached store shelves.',1),(1239,'Command & Conquer Novels','The real-time strategy computer game series eventually spawned a non-interactive novel.',1),(1240,'Starship Titanic Novels','Douglas Adams\' ambitious computer game project was adapted into a non-interactive novel by <i>Monty Python</i> veteran Terry Jones.',1),(1241,'Gravity Angels (Multipath Adventures)','These interactive science fiction videos were built using the same technology as the <a href=\"/Series/861\">Choose Your Own Nightmare Interactive Movies</a>.  Several episodes were released online, but only the first two made it to DVD before the line was discontinued.',1),(1242,'Miscellaneous Works by Leslie Turek','',1),(1243,'Miscellaneous Works by Franz S. Sklenitzka','<i>Die Ruine</i>, <i>Höbarths Zeitmaschine</i> and <i>Die Bibliothek</i> were first published in hardback by NP Buchverlag; they were later reissued by Arena in paperback (some under different titles). <i>Tempo, Tricks und Tolle Tore</i> was an NP hardcover release; no paperback version has been seen as of this writing. All other titles listed here were published by Arena.',3),(1244,'Miscellaneous Works by Leila Johnston','',1),(1245,'Heart of Harkun, The','These non-interactive graphic novels are based on a BBC radio serial that was set in the same world as the <a href=\"/Series/145\">Fabled Lands</a> gamebooks.',1),(1246,'Tales of the Fabled Lands','This non-interactive novel is set in the same world as the <a href=\"/Series/145\">Fabled Lands</a> gamebooks.',1),(1247,'Médiéval Fantastique','These fan-written gamebooks, utilizing the game system from <a href=\"/Series/15\">Sorcery!</a>, began life as free online downloads but were eventually reissued in enhanced printed format through Lulu.',2),(1248,'Outbreak, The','This interactive zombie film was made available online through <a href=\"http://www.survivetheoutbreak.com/\">www.survivetheoutbreak.com</a>.',1),(1249,'Einsamer Wolf (2009-2015)','This series of translations is continued by the <a href=\"/Series/1899\">Einsamer Wolf: Die Neuen Kai Krieger</a> series, which begins at number 1 where the English series continued at 21.',3),(1250,'Fiabe per giocare','This series consists of anthologies containing books previously published in the <a href=\"/Series/936\">Fiaba game</a> series along with a couple of original titles.',5),(1251,'Miscellaneous Works by Davide Tolu','',5),(1252,'Whai Whai','This unusual series of gamebooks provides a sort of interactive tour guide system.  By combining a book full of numbered paragraphs with directions provided interactively via cell phone, this series allows the reader to have an adventure in a real city.  It is necessary to tour actual historic sites while reading in order to find the clues necessary to proceed with the story.  Different books are available for different cities, and all titles were published simultaneously in Italian and English.',5),(1253,'Whai Whai','',1),(1254,'Enchanted Tales, The','These simple gamebooks adapt famous fairy tales.',1),(1255,'826NYC Review, The','This journal collects writings by New York City school students, and it included an interactive story in one issue.',1),(1256,'You Are the Star','',1),(1257,'Miscellaneous Works by Geavonnie Frazier','',1),(1258,'Transformers Animated - Be the Hero','This is the third gamebook series based on the popular franchise (following <a href=\"/Series/336\">The Transformers</a> and <a href=\"/Series/136\">Find Your Fate Junior - The Transformers</a>).',1),(1259,'You Make It Happen','',1),(1260,'Raygun','These books were published by a small press in Oregon; it\'s entirely possible that more titles were announced than were actually published.  As of this writing, only the existence of the first book is confirmed.',1),(1261,'Miscellaneous Works by Rosemary Preece','',1),(1262,'Dudley Serious Interactive Comics','The second issue of this interactive comic book series has been released in a freely-available online version.',1),(1263,'Miscellaneous Works by Kathy I. Turner','',1),(1264,'Oxford Bookworms','This is a long-running series of books aimed at students of English as a second language. Only interactive titles are listed below.',1),(1265,'Adventures in the Rain Forest','',1),(1266,'East African Adventures','',1),(1267,'Miscellaneous Works by P. F. Kozak','',1),(1268,'Choose-Your-Own-Solutions Guidebooks for Parents','This book applies the gamebook format to parenting self-help.',1),(1269,'Jughead and Friends Digest','This comic digest contained an interactive story in at least one issue.',1),(1270,'Fighting Fantasy Video Games','Several <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> books have been adapted into games for a variety of computer and video game platforms over the years.\n\n<p>The 1980s platform games were coded and distributed by several different companies as the series progressed: number 1 was by Crystal Computing (published through Puffin Books\' software range); numbers 2 and 3 were entirely Puffin Books products; number 4 was the only game to bear the Fighting Fantasy Software branding (published by Adventure International); numbers 5 and 6 were coded by Adventuresoft (UK) Ltd and distributed by US Gold Ltd. Three further adaptations were advertised, but would never be released (Sword of the Samurai, Appointment with F.E.A.R. and Demons of the Deep.)\n\n<p>\"Software Pack\" editions of the first three ZX Spectrum/Commodore 64 games were also produced featuring the game cassette and the Puffin gamebook in a slipcase.\n\n<p>The 1984 edition of The Citadel of Chaos was advertised for the ZX Spectrum but the existence of a ZX Spectrum version has never been confirmed.\n\n<p>In 1998 a loose adaptation of Deathtrap Dungeon was produced by EIDOS for PlayStation/Windows PC. A huge marketing campaign featured a young Kelly Brook portraying the female player-character from the game.\n\n<p>The 2009 Nintendo DS version of The Warlock of Firetop Mountain was only released in North America due to licensing/copyright issues in Europe and is quite collectible.',1),(1271,'SpaceView','This science fiction magazine included an interactive feature in at least one issue.',3),(1272,'Whizzy\'s Incredible Journeys','',1),(1273,'Miscellaneous Works by Boris Akunin [Борис Акунин]','',27),(1274,'Gormiti','This series ties in with a popular line of toys.',5),(1275,'Magos y guerreros','',6),(1276,'Mini-GURPS','This is a series of standalone books intended to introduce readers to roleplaying. Each book is set in a specific time and place from Brazil\'s colonial historical period. Each book also uses a simplified version of the rules for the <a href=\"/Series/191\">GURPS</a> role-playing game (with permission from the American publisher). Only those books including solitaire adventures are included below. \nThe books including mini-adventures (at least three in total) also contain historical background information, setting-specific rules, and multiplayer scenarios. One book in the series is a standalone solitaire adventure of much greater length than the mini-adventures, dealing with similar historical topics. The standalone adventure is based on a Brazilian film with the same title, directed by Geraldo Moraes.',7),(1277,'Curumatara','This one-off gamebook uses the context of Brazilian mythology to present an educational story.',7),(1278,'Miscellaneous Works by Renato Silvestrini','This author has written at least two gamebooks, one alone and one with a collaborator.',7),(1279,'Miscellaneous Works by Flavio Andrade and Carlos Klimick','These two game designers are founders of the publisher Akritó; they have written several gamebooks independently and in collaboration with other authors.',7),(1280,'Miscellaneous Works by Péter Kardos and Nyári Gábor','These authors write gamebooks on cultural and historical topics.  Their first book, for example, was designed to raise awareness about the plight of the gypsies in Hungary and to help fight prejudices.',15),(1281,'Geheimnis des Goldenen Jaguar, Das','',3),(1282,'bizonytalanság börtönében, A','A horror gamebook with Lovecraftian overtones, this diceless adventure emphasizes puzzle solving over combat.  ',15),(1283,'Galactic Chronicles','This series is planned to eventually become a trilogy.',1),(1284,'Fighting Fantazine','This free online fanzine provides news, articles and mini-adventures inspired by the <a href=\"/Category/37\">Fighting Fantasy</a> franchise.\n\nInitially published on a roughly quarterly basis, later editions\' publishing dates became increasingly more sporadic, with Issue 15 being originally stated as due November 2015, but not appearing until late-May 2016, followed by Issue 16 in August 2017.\n\nAs of Issue 11, physical print copies could also be purchased.',1),(1285,'Fighting Fantasy (2009-2012, Wizard Books Series 2)','This series marks the second time that Wizard Books relaunched the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series, changing the order of earlier titles and adding new ones.  This time around, the books are slightly larger and have some additional character background material, but little else has changed.  See <a href=\"/Series/12\">Fighting Fantasy Reissues (Series 1)</a> for more information on the original relaunch.\n<p>Blood of the Zombies (released for the series\' 30th Anniversary) was the 17th book to be released in the series but is un-numbered and has a different cover and spine style which emulates and modernises that of the classic \"green spine\" Puffin editions rather than using the silver \"shield\" cover design of Wizard\'s 2nd Series as a whole.',1),(1286,'Star Wars - The Clone Wars: Decide Your Destiny (British)','Although this British series shares its name with the American <a href=\"/Series/1227\">Star Wars - The Clone Wars: Decide Your Destiny</a> books, it is partially different.  The first books in both series have the same title but are different stories.  While the American series title started out as standard pick-a-path gamebooks, these volumes add an online element, with codewords in the books unlocking videos and interactive games on the web.  The second book from this series eventually appeared as the third title in the American one.',1),(1287,'Chooseomatic Books','This series of interactive books (planned to expand by one title every year) parodies the popular <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> format, providing humorous, fast-paced adventures.',1),(1288,'Tom Clancy\'s Splinter Cell Novels','These non-interactive novels are based on a video game that is in turn endorsed by novelist Tom Clancy.',1),(1289,'Tom Clancy\'s EndWar Novels','This non-interactive novel series is based on a video game that is in turn endorsed by novelist Tom Clancy.',1),(1290,'Tom Clancy\'s Ghost Recon Novels','This non-interactive novel series is based on a video game that is in turn endorsed by novelist Tom Clancy.',1),(1291,'Miscellaneous Works by Gaël Navard ','This author has written a gamebook with the aim of teaching music to children and teenagers.',2),(1292,'Lobo\'s Greatest Hits','This stand-alone comic book combines several excerpts from previously-published DC Comics books featuring the character Lobo into a branching-path storyline.',1),(1293,'Silly Basketball Sportsmysteries','This series recycles the same interactive text with different team names pasted in at key points to customize the books for various states.',1),(1294,'Dictate Your Own Destiny','This independent adventure was published online, images manipulated to look like scans of a battered copy of a physical book.',1),(1295,'D6 Adventure','This modern-setting role-playing game, available on paper and pdf formats, includes a short introductory solitaire adventure in the rulebook.',1),(1296,'All-Star Stories','At least one book in this series of short fiction anthologies contains an interactive story.',1),(1297,'Silly Football Sportsmysteries','This series recycles the same interactive text with different team names pasted in at key points to customize the books for various states.',1),(1298,'You Are a Mathematician','One chapter in this guide to mathematics is presented in the form of a gamebook, allowing the reader to explore various aspects of a math problem.  The book also includes a full map of its interactive portion.',1),(1299,'D6 Fantasy','This fantasy role-playing game, available both in pdf and paper formats, includes an introductory solitaire adventure in the rulebook.',1),(1300,'D6 Space','This science-fiction role-playing game, available in paper and pdf formats, includes a short introductory adventure in the rulebook.',1),(1301,'Literactivo','Apparently, only one Literactivo book was released, but this was intended to be a series. This series uses Mayan mythology for its setting and purports to be used as a teaching aid. The book is actually quite simplistic, with only one choice point with four paths leading from it. It is intended to be read aloud to a group of younger children and to involve them in group activities. The book includes a boardgame with RPG elements which is to be played once the gamebook is completed.    ',6),(1302,'Brain of Katherine Mansfield, The','',1),(1303,'Tú decides la aventura','This is a Spanish series of gamebooks with no game system, directed at older children.',6),(1304,'Mini Adventure Comics','These British interactive comics provide adventures in a variety of genres.',1),(1305,'Aquelarre','This medieval horror Spanish-language role-playing game included solo adventures in the first and third editions of the main rulebook, as well as in some supplements for the first edition.  The first edition was published by Joc Internacional, while the third edition was published by Nosolorol Ediciones. In 2010, a gamebook based on the game was published in pdf over the Internet, free of charge, by Nosolorol.',6),(1306,'Oráculo','This Spanish role-playing game with a mythic Greece setting included a solitaire adventure in the rulebook.',6),(1307,'Crash Gamebook','This Argentinian series, based on an anime collectible card game, contains only one unnumbered title.',6),(1308,'Miscellaneous Works by Anshumani Ruddra','',1),(1309,'Espada y Brujeria','This Argentinian live-action fantasy role-playing game has been described as containing a solo adventure in the rulebook, but the information appears to be incorrect.',6),(1310,'Laberintro','This series of gamebooks is set in a variety of genres. Extra chapters for the second book in the series have been made available online.',6),(1311,'Miscellaneous Works by Virginia A. Roper','This book is available both in paper format and in an online version.',1),(1312,'Viaje en el tiempo','This short-lived series is perhaps the first original Spanish-language gamebook series. The books are set in the Age of Discovery. Two decades after their initial release in Spain, they were republished by Andrés Bello in Chile, with no numbering and no mention made of their earlier publication.',6),(1313,'Welt der 1000 Abenteuer, Die','The publisher of this series switched from Egmont Franz Schneider to Mantikore-Verlag beginning with book 9 while also reissuing previous books.',3),(1314,'Sound of Drowning, The','One issue of this comic book series is interactive. The book is out of print but the author has made it available online.',1),(1315,'Miscellaneous Works by Kristopher James','This author has written a single fantasy gamebook which is available in paper and pdf formats. The only game system used is inventory management.',1),(1316,'Miscellaneous Works by Charlie McCarthy','',1),(1317,'Miscellaneous Works by Gianni Rodari','Famous Italian author of children\'s literature Gianni Rodari can be considered a forerunner to interactive fiction. In the early seventies he published a book consisting of short stories for children. The reader does not make choices for a character in the manner of most gamebooks, but each story allows the reader to choose among several endings.',5),(1318,'Team Leadership','This stand-alone book includes five mini-adventures dealing with company management scenarios.',1),(1319,'TimeXplorers','This is a multimedia project that consists of novels, a role-playing game, a wargame, comic books, and gamebooks. The first gamebook is available as a pdf.',6),(1320,'Xanth Novels','Piers Anthony\'s popular humorous fantasies have multiple connections to gaming.  In addition to inspiring interactive volumes in the <a href=\"/Series/97\">Crossroads</a> series, this non-interactive series included a volume designed as a tie-in to the <i>Companions of Xanth</i> computer game.',1),(1321,'James Bond Jr. Adventure Gamebooks','',1),(1322,'Branching Path Books','',1),(1323,'Miscellaneous Works by Quim Bou','This Catalan comic book writer has released an interactive comic book, downloadable free of charge. The book is available in both Catalan and Spanish.',6),(1324,'Los piercings de Hymeko','This erotic, pdf-only gamebook is available for free download.',6),(1325,'Miscellaneous Works by Quim Bou','This author has published a free interactive comic book in pdf format. The book is available in both Catalan and Spanish.',20),(1326,'Miscellaneous Works by Shon Richards','',1),(1327,'Elige tu camino hacia','',6),(1328,'Elige tu propia Argentina','This stand-alone book makes the reader a minister in the Argentinian government.',6),(1329,'Challenges of Zona, The','This series comprises a couple of erotic fantasy interactive comics. They were cheaply produced and are long out of print.',1),(1330,'Trollcrusher','This early British game fanzine published at least one solo adventure.',1),(1331,'Club de Aventuras AD','This Spanish fanzine dedicated to interactive fiction contained a gamebook which was serialized from issue 1 to 10.',6),(1332,'CEO: an Interactive Book, The','As the title suggests, this stand-alone book is a business simulation.',1),(1333,'Save the Fair Maiden','This is a series of fantasy gamebooks in the Endless Quest vein.',1),(1334,'Romance Stories - An Interactive Fiction Collection','This volume contains six short interactive adventures.  It was retitled due to trademark issues.',1),(1335,'Choose Your Own Adventure Reissues (Australian Versions)','The Australian release of the <a href=\"/Series/870\">Choose Your Own Adventure Reissues</a> features a different order (and in many cases cover art) than the original American versions.',1),(1336,'Choose Your Own Adventure Junior','This series is the Australian version of the <a href=\"/Series/1091\">Dragonlarks</a> books (though it doesn\'t include as many titles).',1),(1337,'Phudd Files, The','This series features the exploits of the Phudd Family in a series of mini adventures (3-4 adventures per book) in which the bumbling Phudds solve/prevent crimes. Each book features a different Phudd (one based on a James Bond Parody and another a Miss Marple character) and their animal companion who helps them to solve the crimes.',1),(1338,'Miscellaneous Works by Kevin Brockmeier','Kevin Brockmeier has written at least one interactive short story.',1),(1339,'Miniature Wargames','This wargame-dedicated magazine included the occasional solo adventure. The adventure in issue 143 deals with a British Spy in the Napoleonic era.  The other entries on this page are based on a fairly sketchy article index; confirmation that they are gamebook-like would be appreciated.',1),(1340,'Huntik','This series ties in with an original Italian animation.',5),(1341,'Dietro le porte','This was published first in hardcover by Guanda, then in paperback by Tea.',5),(1342,'Ranger in Danger (Decide Your Destiny)','These books cast the reader as an international wildlife ranger.',1),(1343,'GURPS','Apparently, the only solitaire adventure for <a href=\"/Series/191\">GURPS</a> that was translated from English into Portuguese was the one included in the basic ruleset (the adventure is listed here). In addition to this adventure, however, several original Portuguese-language solo adventures were released for the system (see <a href=\"/Series/1276\">Mini-GURPS</a> for details).',7),(1344,'Tunnels and Trolls: Trollhalla Press','After the end of his relationship with <a href=\"/Series/1070\">Outlaw Press</a>, Ken St. Andre started this imprint to publish <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> material.',1),(1345,'Choisis le chemin de ton aventure: Donjons et Dragons','The French translation of the American animated series was called <i>Le sourire du dragon</i>.  It aired on Antenne 2, hence the \"a2\" visible on the covers of the translated gamebooks.',2),(1346,'Spielbuch-Abenteuer Weltgeschichte','',3),(1347,'Legenden von Harkuna','Only the first four titles in this series were released individually. The series was subsequently re-released under the \"Fabled Lands: Legenden von Harkuna\" title as three omnibus volumes, each containing two titles.',3),(1348,'Miscellaneous Works by Emiliano De Marco','Emiliano De Marco has written at least one interactive book, designed as a simple history lesson for young readers.',5),(1349,'Blackstaff Gamebooks','These fantasy adventures feature a complex game system and follow the continuing adventures of a wizard named Blackstaff.',1),(1350,'Choices (expanded editions)','These full-length adventures are adapted from the earlier, more experimental online <a href=\"\"\"/Series/1081\">Choices</a> series.',1),(1351,'Samotny wilk','',28),(1352,'Miscellaneous Works by Michael A. Stackpole','After getting his start writing <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> solo adventures, Michael A. Stackpole moved on to become a successful novelist.  While he hasn\'t written any interactive material in a while, many of his works are either directly linked to game licenses or at least fall into genres that gamers tend to enjoy.',1),(1353,'Miscellaneous Works by Rose Estes','In addition to writing many entries in the popular <a href=\"/Series/79\">Endless Quest</a> series, Rose Estes also wrote a handful of non-interactive fantasy novels.',1),(1354,'Doctor Who: New Series Novels','These novels feature adventures of the Doctors seen starting with the television series\' 2005 revival.',1),(1355,'American Girl: Innerstar University','This series, part of the \"American Girl\" brand and tied in with an online game, features standard branching-path gameplay with the added bonus of extra online endings.',1),(1356,'Paths of Storytelling','Released online as an April Fool\'s joke, this satirical gamebook takes place in White Wolf\'s World of Darkness.',1),(1357,'DestinyQuest','This gamebook series, which has a bit of a computer RPG flavor, consists of large books which can be expanded through shorter downloadable mini-adventures. Some of the print content has also been adapted into <i>DestinyQuest Infinite</i>, an online browser-based game from Adventure Cow.',1),(1358,'Blades & Wizardry','',1),(1359,'Miscellaneous Works by Dale M. Brethower','Dale M. Brethower wrote at least one book on programmed instruction featuring interactive elements.',1),(1360,'Doctor Who 2 in 1 Adventure Novels','This series of books contains two Doctor Who adventures for younger readers in each volume.  The stories are non-interactive, but they share the target audience and many of the authors of the <a href=\"/Series/1077\">Doctor Who: Decide Your Destiny</a> series.',1),(1361,'Revue Héros','This French magazine contains several solo adventures in each issue.',2),(1362,'Miscellaneous Works by Wolfgang Kramer','This German board game designer has published two interactive books. In these books the reader determines which section to turn to by solving puzzles.',3),(1363,'Seja Um Espião Interplanetário','',7),(1364,'Club Penguin Monte A Sua História','',7),(1365,'Você É o Herói','',7),(1366,'Aventuras em RPG','Several years after its first release, the first book in this series was reissued as the first entry in the Viver ou Morrer series.',7),(1367,'Clássicos Disney - O Filme em Quadrinhos','',7),(1368,'Só Aventuras','',7),(1369,'Coleção RPG','',7),(1370,'Dragão Brasil','',7),(1371,'Worst-Case Scenario Ultimate Adventure','',1),(1372,'zì wǒ lì xiǎn cóng shū [自我歷險叢書]','These appear to have been published in Hong Kong based on the price information on the covers.',14),(1373,'Legendary Journeys','Written by an author familiar with computer-driven interactive fiction, this series brings some of the flavor of classic text adventures to the branching-novel format.',1),(1374,'Gamebook Adventures','Digital gamebooks with a classic RPG gamebook flavour.\n\nThis series follows a sequence of apps (known as GA1-GA12) that constitute the Orlandes-Drymar-Rema cycle, some of the later entries being sequels to earlier ones. A stand-alone Sci-Fi entry (Infinite Universe) was also released, but without a sequential number to differentiate it from the Orlandes cycle.\n\nAll Tin Man\'s apps include artwork, animated dice mechanics, stat-based systems, and a fun feature that allows the player to collect various Achievements (some which involve having to lose or follow the incorrect path) to increase re-playability once the game has been beaten in the traditional gamebook sense. Some also feature full-colour maps, biographical material, and other \"extra features\".\n\nThe system itself is based around four primary stats (although a few of the apps add unique attributes too), these being Offence ranging from 1-6 (the better the weapon the higher this is, up to maximum score of 6), Defence (works the same as Offence but based on stronger armour), Fitness (generated by rolling 1d6 and add 6), and Vitality. Vitality is your life-force (eat or rest and it rises, get wounded and it drops with 0 usually meaning death), Fitness is a mixture of physical skill and personal fortune. Combat involves rolling as many D6s as the attacker has Offence points, countered by rolling as many D6s as the defender has Defence points. Naturally this can make some combats aginst strong (or well-armed/defended) foes very one-sided. Even more damaging is wounding - if a character is wounded they take as much Vitality damage as the total of all the succesful attacker\'s dice in that given roll ie it is possible to do 6 x 6 (36) Vitality points of damage which will kill most opponents instantly! Similarly, instant deaths (or at the very least, negative outcomes) can result from failing Fitness tests which involve rolling 2D6 and getting a number less than your Fitness. Roll equal to or above and you fail the test. Overall, the early stages of these apps can be quite tough, although they tend to get easier as your character gets better weapons and armour.\n\nOfficial web page: www.gamebookadventures.com',1),(1375,'Choice Comix','',1),(1376,'Sonic the Hedgehog Adventure Gamebooks (Ladybird)','Not to be confused with the other <a href=\"/Series/287\">Sonic the Hedgehog Adventure Gamebooks</a>, these small hardcover volumes are aimed at younger readers.  Each book consists of full-color two-page spreads featuring visual puzzles (reminiscent of the <a href=\"/Series/490\">Which Way?</a> series); the reader\'s ability to solve each puzzle determines which page is visited next.  The individual books do not have separate titles -- they are simply numbered.',1),(1377,'U-Ventures','Following his split with the <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> brand, Edward Packard started this series (available first in electronic format and later also in print) to reissue expanded versions of some of his <a href=\"/Series/30\">CYOA</a> titles.',1),(1378,'Tricky Journeys','This series is a companion to <a href=\"/Series/1226\">Twisted Journeys</a>, aimed at a younger audience.',1),(1379,'Psyche: Gamebooks Reborn','This game for two to four players provides rules for turning any gamebook into a cooperative, multi-player game in which different players represent different aspects of the protagonist\'s mind.  The rulebook also includes a brief sample solo adventure.  The game is available in physical and electronic forms.',1),(1380,'Choose Your Own Adventure Graded Reader Series','This series adapts books from the <a href=\"/Series/870\">Choose Your Own Adventure Reissues</a> to make them suitable for use by English-as-a-Second-Language students.  The language is simplified, the protagonists are made more age- and gender-neutral (since many ESL students are adults of both genders), and in some cases plot threads and endings are significantly changed.',1),(1381,'Change the Game','A series of junior fiction books, set in Australia, which bases each story on a single sporting match or game.\nWritten as a running commentary, rich with references to Australian vernacular and culture, each book begins just before the match and ends just after ‘the final siren’.\nFour of the six books are based on the sport of Australian Rules Football (AFL) and follow the Westpark Scorpions Under-12s football team.\nThroughout each story, readers come to various ‘decision points’ where they decide what happens next. This is not always character-based, and can include changes in the weather, the success of certain actions, etc.\nThe series is currently out-of-print.',1),(1382,'Beast Quest Master Your Destiny','Based on the Beast Quest series of children\'s books, the Beast Quest Master Your (Own) Destiny series teams you up with Tom and Elenna in their quest to defend the Kingdom of Avantia from the evil wizard Malvel and his terrifying beasts.\nEach book in the series introduces two new beasts to battle.',1),(1383,'Aventuras Interativas','',7),(1384,'Miscellaneous Works by Ramón Martínez Rodríguez','',6),(1385,'Track It Yourself Adventures','',1),(1386,'Adventure Begins Here','',1),(1387,'Sorcery! (Seb EJ & Bright AI)','A digital gamebook remake of Steve Jackson\'s <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> spin-off book series, <a href=\"/Series/15\">Sorcery</a>',1),(1388,'Fighting Fantasy (Big Blue Bubble)','Based on the renowned Fighting Fantasy series of gamebooks by Steve Jackson and Ian Livingstone, Big Blue Bubble were given the license to produce a number of digital versions. The digital counterparts were very faithful to the content of the original books, and included features such as:\n• Tap the on screen buttons to flip pages\n• Shake to roll the 3D dice using the accelerometer\n• Inventory items selectable via touch-screen\n• Game-saves, allowing you to retrace your steps\n• Beautifully painted illustrations\n• A scrolling image gallery where you can view the in-game artwork you\'ve unlocked.\n• Universal app iPad support\nNOTE -- June 2012: Big Blue Bubble are no longer licensed to sell Fighting Fantasy products and it is anticipated that these digital gamebooks will be removed from the app store in the very near future.',1),(1389,'Pick-a-Plot','This trilogy of light-hearted and witty gamebooks puts you in the role of Holden Catfield, a cat trying to survive a variety of situations.',1),(1390,'Miscellaneous Works by Julio Cortázar','Julio Cortázar, an Argentine writer, wrote the 1963 experimental novel <i>Rayuela</i>, which can be read either in the normal linear fashion or in a different order achieved by following links at the end of each chapter.  It\'s not exactly a gamebook, but it\'s an interesting early example of non-linear reading in fiction.',6),(1391,'Miscellaneous Works by Julio Cortázar','',1),(1392,'Race Against Time Novels (US)','These books are not interactive, but they were marketed to the same audience as the gamebooks of their era.  This marketing was so effective that some of them were even reviewed in a column of <i>School Library Journal</i> devoted specifically to interactive books!  Please do not be confused: this entry is not here to encourage gamebook fans to buy these books; it exists solely to emphasize the fact that they are <i>not</i> gamebooks.\n\nThe order of publication in the US and UK differed slightly.',1),(1393,'voiture dont vous êtes le héros, La','Created by a car manufacturer for commercial purposes, this gamebook mimics the style of the popular <a href=\"\"\"/Category/78\">Un livre dont vous êtes le héros</a> product family while challenging its reader to help bring a new car to market.  The book features a simple game system with three attributes (PERFECTIONNISME, RIGUEUR and RESPONSABILITÉ) and a time-tracking element.',2),(1394,'SFX','This science fiction and fantasy magazine featured an interactive article in at least one issue.',1),(1395,'Laberintro','The Polish editions do not appear to have a series title of their own, so they are listed here under the original Spanish name.',28),(1396,'Zebra Mystery Puzzlers','This long-running (in quantity, if not in years) series of \"guess-the-ending\" mysteries featured a couple of distinguishing characteristics: the cover illustrations included clues that helped the reader solve the mystery, and the final solution was bound together so that it could only be read by cutting part of the book open, preventing accidental peeks.  The basic formula was revived years later (aimed at a younger audience) in the <a href=\"/Series/1397\">You-Solve-It Mysteries</a> series.',1),(1397,'You-Solve-It Mysteries','Like the earlier <a href=\"/Series/1396\">Zebra Mystery Puzzlers</a> series from the same publisher, these books encourage the reader to guess the ending, providing clues in the cover illustration and sealing the solution in bound-together pages that must be cut open to read.',1),(1398,'Choose Your Own ROFLCon','The ROFLCon Internet conference published one of its programs in interactive format -- it looks very much like one of the <a href=\"/Series/870\">Choose Your Own Adventure Reissues</a>.',1),(1399,'Aventures sur mesure','This series started out with French translations of the <a href=\"/Series/1416\">It\'s Your Call - Prince of Persia</a> series and the <a href=\"/Series/1286\">Star Wars - The Clone Wars: Decide Your Destiny (Multimedia)</a> series. From the fifth title onwards, it started including original French gamebooks based on <i>Fort Boyard</i> (a French TV show similar to <a href=\"/Series/98\">The Crystal Maze</a>) and the <a href=\"/Series/492\">Survivor</a> TV show.\n\nThe \"Aventures sur mesure\" label is also used for other series which include translations of English gamebooks into French.',2),(1400,'Je bouquine','This magazine contains a mix of comics, fiction and pop-culture articles.  Occasionally it also includes a mini-gamebook.  Adventures printed in the magazine have varied in terms of genre and complexity.',2),(1401,'Clue','Based on the board game of the same name (known as Cluedo in some parts of the world), these books each include a collection of short mysteries featuring characters from the game.  The reader is challenged to guess the solution of each mystery.  Solutions are printed upside-down at the end of each section.  A similar series for younger readers, featuring characters from the junior edition of the game, was published as <a href=\"/Series/1402\">Clue Jr.</a>.\n\n<p>The eighteen numbered volumes were aimed at a young audience and released by Scholastic and Turtleback.  The two unnumbered volumes were released later by Running Press and are aimed at a more mature audience.',1),(1402,'Clue Jr.','Based on the board game of the same name, these books each include a collection of short mysteries featuring characters from the game.  The reader is challenged to guess the solution of each mystery.  Solutions are printed upside-down at the end of each section.  A similar series for older readers, featuring characters from the standard edition of the game, was published as <a href=\"/Series/1401\">Clue</a>.',1),(1403,'T*A*C*K','These books tell of the adventures of T*A*C*K, a group of children who solve mysteries (the acronym comes from the children\'s names).  The reader is challenged to guess the solution of each story before reading it.',1),(1404,'You Be the Jury','In addition to working on the <a href=\"/Series/1403\">T*A*C*K</a> and <a href=\"/Series/1405\">Scene of the Crime</a> series, author Marvin Miller wrote a variety of books in which the reader is asked to guess the solutions to mysteries and outcomes of court cases.',1),(1405,'Scene of the Crime','These \"guess the ending\" books encourage the reader to solve short mysteries using crime scene illustrations as clues.',1),(1406,'Secret Files of Dakota King, The','Each of these books provides a single, book-length mystery for the reader to solve.  Illustrations (the files of the series title) are included to provide additional clues.  The final page of each story is a photo of the guilty party -- the reader\'s job is to guess the villain\'s identity before revealing it.',1),(1407,'Chip Mitchell','These books follow the same basic format as the <a href=\"/Series/1097\">Encyclopedia Brown</a> series but attempt to update the premise by using a computer expert as the protagonist.',1),(1408,'Two-Minute Mysteries','Before creating <a href=\"/Series/1097\">Encyclopedia Brown</a>, Donald J. Sobol wrote a syndicated column of brief \"guess-the-ending\" mysteries.  These columns were later collected into books.',1),(1409,'Ed Noon','Ed Noon was the protagonist of a long-running series of mystery novels.  One book in the series was a collection of brief \"guess-the-ending\" mysteries, possibly inspired by the better-known <a href=\"/Series/1408\">Two-Minute Mysteries</a>.  This should not be confused with a later, longer-running series of <a href=\"/Series/1410\">Five-Minute Mysteries</a>.',1),(1410,'Five-Minute Mysteries','These collections of short \"guess-the-ending\" mysteries should not be confused with the <a href=\"/Series/1409\">Ed Noon</a> book that shares a similar title and format.',1),(1411,'Armchair Detective, The','These \"guess-the-ending\" mysteries are similar to the author\'s earlier <a href=\"/Series/1410\">Five-Minute Mysteries</a> series.',1),(1412,'Watchers Novels','These non-interactive young adult novels were written by occasional gamebook author Peter Lerangis.',1),(1413,'Miscellaneous Works by Andrea Angiolino','While Andrea Angiolino helped pioneer gamebooks in Italy, not too much of his work has been seen in English.  However, at least one of his collections of game rules and puzzles has seen English publication.',1),(1414,'Pop-Up Whodunit','These pop-up books challenge the reader to search for clues and solve a mystery.  Each book contains a revolving wheel component at the end which allows multiple possible clues and solutions, so each book can be replayed several times.',1),(1415,'Carmen Sandiego Novels','These non-interactive novels were based on the same computer game series as the <a href=\"/Series/63\">Carmen Sandiego</a> and <a href=\"/Series/64\">Carmen Sandiego - Mark & See</a> gamebooks.',1),(1416,'It\'s Your Call','',1),(1417,'Strategicheska kniga-igra [Стратегическа книга-игра]','This elaborate multi-genre series consists of folders containing gamebooks (sometimes more than one volume per title) and various play aids -- boards, cards, maps, stand-up character tokens, etc.\n\n<p>And yes, apparently the series contains two different volume 15\'s.',8),(1418,'SPRI KFWP [ספרי כשוף]','The <a href=\"\"\"/Series/15\">Sorcery!</a>, <a href=\"\"\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> and <a href=\"\"\"/Series/234\">Advanced Fighting Fantasy</a> books were all released under a single series title in Israel.  The first <a href=\"\"\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> book was released as an unnumbered volume by a different publisher than all the other titles.',34),(1419,'MIKISNH QBAM [םיכיסנה קבאמ]','',34),(1420,'Choisis ton parcours','',2),(1421,'Back Brain Recluse','This series of short story collections includes one vaguely gamebook-inspired story in one book.  Although it doesn\'t have actual branching paths, it periodically offers multiple options related to the plot.',1),(1422,'Isotopes, The','This band released an album featuring an interactive audio story following the music.',1),(1423,'Manuali educativi','This series of books for educators included a volume providing a history of gamebooks, several short adventures (mostly reprinted from other sources), and advice on how to use gamebook-writing as a student activity.',5),(1424,'Stop al vandalismo grafico','This set, funded by several Italian banks and published by the City Council of Rome’s Assessorships for the Educational and Juvenile Politics, is a collection containing the winners of a school contest to write short gamebooks with an anti-graffiti theme.  It consists of one large color album containing the first-place winner and a boxed set of twelve small booklets of runners up.',5),(1425,'Choose Your Own Adventure','Some of these translations appear to be derived from German translations rather than the English originals.',35),(1426,'Choose Your Own Adventure Super Adventure','',35),(1427,'Virtual Murder','This series of multimedia computer games (renamed \"Murder Mystery\" starting with the third entry) was written by <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> veteran Shannon Gilligan.',1),(1428,'ChoicePoint Films','These interactive movies were originally released theatrically, with special equipment used to allow the audience to vote at decision points.  At least one title was eventually released as an interactive DVD.',1),(1429,'Miscellaneous Works by Gianni Rodari','',6),(1430,'Aventures à lire - messages secrets','These books were designed to help teach primary school subjects.  They may not exactly qualify as gamebooks -- the story path doesn\'t branch -- but they combine fiction with programmed instruction techniques, so they are the cousins of the less linear <a href=\"/Series/717\">Les Petits Retz pédagogiques</a> series.',2),(1431,'Jeux et Stratégie','This game magazine included gamebook-style adventures in several issues.  Many were classified as \"labyrôles,\" combining maze-like illustrations with text sections.',2),(1432,'Final Destination','One entry in this horror movie series featured gamebook-style interactive features on the DVD release.',1),(1433,'Vigilante, El','This gaming magazine featured gamebook-style adventures in several issues.',6),(1434,'TutorText','',2),(1435,'Warhammer: Path to Victory','These large trade-paperback-format gamebooks take place in the Warhammer Fantasy and Warhammer 40,000 settings and use game systems inspired by the corresponding tabletop miniatures rules (though knowledge of those games is not required for play).',1),(1436,'Children\'s Problem Solving Books','These books are designed to help parents and children explore various emotional topics together using the gamebook format.',1),(1437,'Kids Can Choose','',1),(1438,'Ellery Queen','Several of the early novels in this famous mystery series featured a \"challenge to the reader\" page, noting the point in the story at which all the clues have been revealed and offering an opportunity to guess the solution before it unfolds.  This is one of the earliest examples of the \"guess the ending\" formula that would later become the signature of series like <a href=\"/Series/1097\">Encyclopedia Brown</a>.',1),(1439,'Ellery Queen - Sfida al Lettore','',5),(1440,'Fantasy-Játék-Könyv','',15),(1441,'Mágus - Kalandorkrónikák','These gamebooks take place in the world of Ynev, a popular Hungarian fantasy setting.',15),(1442,'Star Wars - A klónok háborúja','',15),(1443,'GamesMaster','This video game magazine included an interactive feature in at least one issue.',1),(1444,'Escoge tu propia aventura','',6),(1445,'Monachikos Lύkos [Μοναχικός Λύκος]','',36),(1446,'S.O.S.. Fantastikes peripeteies [S.O.S.. Φανταστικές περιπέτειες]','',36),(1447,'Giochi magazine','This was a magazine about all kinds of games.  Many issues have original gamebook stories.',5),(1448,'Lamentations of the Flame Princess','This RPG combines mechanics and settings inspired by vintage <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons &amp; Dragons</a> with a weird fantasy/horror flavor reminiscent of <a href=\"/Series/60\">Call of Cthulhu</a>, decorating it all with rather graphic artwork.  The base game includes introductory solo adventures structurally similar to those in the <a href=\"/Item/823\">original D&amp;D red box</a>',1),(1449,'Usborne Puzzle Adventures','These puzzle books feature a narrative punctuated by a series of visual puzzles for the reader to solve.  There is no gamebook-style branching path -- the stories are completely linear -- but there is still a melding of game and book here.  The first three titles were published under the \"Usborne Solve It Yourself\" label before the series changed to \"Usborne Puzzle Adventures.\"\n\n<p>At least three titles from the series were published in Denmark as the Gjellerup & Gad Solve It Yourself series (in English language, for educational use).',1),(1450,'Wired','This technology magazine featured a gamebook-related interactive article in at least one issue.',1),(1451,'Android Novels','These non-interactive stories are based on the Fantasy Flight board game of the same name.  At least one title included a mail-in offer to order game components to add elements from the novel to the game.',1),(1452,'Astrapi','This children\'s magazine included interactive stories in several issues.',2),(1453,'Ready Brek Game','These tiny gamebooks were given away in breakfast cereal boxes.  They combine to form a fantasy quest pitting readers against an Ice Demon.  Their sections are numbered in random order.  Readers must keep track of GLOW points and track which locations they have visited.',1),(1454,'Reiter der Schwarzen Sonne','',3),(1455,'Gamebook Experience','',1),(1457,'On the Spot','Each page of this somewhat unusual gamebook sets up a hypothetical scenario involving a moral dilemma.  Each scenario includes yes or no questions which either advance or terminate the scenario.  While a lone reader can enjoy these questions, the real purpose of the book is as a component in a party game where players read scenarios aloud and try to anticipate each other\'s responses.\n\n<p>The book includes a call for submissions of additional scenarios for a second book, but it does not appear that the follow-up volume was ever released.',1),(1458,'Bouclier Ardent','This French-Canadian series was designed to contain multiple volumes, but it appears to have been discontinued after the first volume.  The inclusion of vocabulary words suggests that it was intended for educational use.',2),(1459,'Oog des Meesters, Het','',13),(1460,'Mischiastorie, Il','A gamebook without text, this children\'s game contains a series of illustrated tiles that can be linked together by matching up icons in order to tell a variety of stories about the adventures of a rabbit.',5),(1461,'Marvel Super Heroes','',3),(1462,'Usborne Fantasy Adventures','These highly visual, puzzle-filled books follow a similar format to the <a href=\"/Series/1449\">Usborne Puzzle Adventures</a>: completely linear storylines (no gamebook-style branching plots), but lots of puzzles for the reader to solve while reading the adventure.',1),(1463,'Usborne Fantasy Adventures','',22),(1464,'BD Interactive','',2),(1465,'BD Interactive','',22),(1466,'Dwarfstar Games','This division of Heritage Models published mini-board-games, some of which were solo-playable with gamebook elements.',1),(1467,'2000 AD Extreme Edition','This comic reprint magazine included at least one interactive story.',1),(1468,'2000 AD','This famous British comic magazine included at least one serialized interactive story.',1),(1469,'Labyrinthon','These puzzle books tell the story of the reader being trapped in a video game.  The only way to proceed is to complete mazes within a designated time limit.  The narrative is completely linear -- there is no gamebook-style branching path -- but the mazes make this series a blend of game and fiction.',1),(1470,'Sinkadus','This role-playing magazine contained a simple interactive story in one issue.',11),(1471,'Kimi nara dousuru shokuryou mondai [君ならどうする食糧問題]','',4),(1472,'Determine Your Destiny','',1),(1473,'Elige tu propia aventura (Editorial Terracota, 2007-)','',6),(1474,'Miscellaneous Works by Paul Partington','',1),(1475,'Necromunda Novels','These non-interactive novels are set in the world of the miniatures skirmish game of the same name.',1),(1476,'Freelance Traveller','This label is used to release unofficial online support material for the classic Traveller role-playing game.',1),(1477,'Fantomen','This comic magazine (featuring the Swedish version of American superhero \"The Phantom\") included a gamebook-style adventure in at least one issue.',11),(1478,'Choose Your Own Afterlife','This interactive novella was available online for a time but no longer seems to exist.',1),(1479,'Miscellaneous Works by David Whyld','This author of computer-based interactive fiction has written several entries that are straightforward gamebook-style branching-path adventures with a multiple-choice interface.',1),(1480,'Miscellaneous Works by Mike Tolar','',1),(1481,'Anel Elemental','This role-playing game includes a solo adventure in the rulebook.',7),(1482,'A-Team, The','These small booklets were distributed in packets of Smiths crisps in the UK.  There were five titles in the series, all Games Workshop productions.',1),(1483,'Moshi Monsters Pick Your Path','',1),(1484,'Relic Worlds: Pick Your Path','',1),(1485,'Interactive Short Stories','These simple gamebooks were released on the Kindle platform.',1),(1486,'Miscellaneous Works by Jacques Penrod','',1),(1487,'Bin Weevils Choose Your Own Path','',1),(1488,'Cyberswine (Multipath Adventures)','',1),(1489,'Miscellaneous Works by Robert DuValle','This author wrote a minimally-interactive fantasy novel which offers the reader a choice of two possible endings.',1),(1490,'Decide Your Destiny: Aaron Stone','',1),(1491,'Lost Worlds: Queen\'s Blade Grimoire [クイーンズブレイド グリムワール]','This Japanese series uses the <a href=\"/Series/49\">Lost Worlds</a> game system under license.  Each volume is a full-color hardback.  It is a companion to <a href=\"/Series/905\">Queen\'s Blade</a>, <a href=\"/Series/1206\">Queen\'s Blade Rebellion</a> and <a href=\"/Series/1207\">Queen\'s Gate</a>.',4),(1492,'Lost Worlds: Queen\'s Blade','',1),(1493,'Tunnels and Trolls: Lone Delver Games','This series of <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> solo adventures is sold online in electronic format.',1),(1494,'Tales of the Arabian Nights','This board game includes a \"book of tales\" which includes gamebook-style paragraphs that are read as a core part of gameplay.  The first edition was published in 1985 by West End Games; a revised and significantly expanded version was released in 2009 by Z-Man Games.',1),(1495,'Geschichten aus 1001 Nacht','The German edition features additional adventures not found in the original English edition.',3),(1496,'Jeu des Mille et Une Nuits, Le','',2),(1497,'Agents of SMERSH','This cooperative board game features a paragraph book similar to the one found in <a href=\"/Series/1494\">Tales of the Arabian Nights</a>.',1),(1498,'Pathfinder Roleplaying Game','This fantasy role-playing game includes a brief tutorial solo adventure in the basic rules as well as several full-length quests (produced by third-party publishers) sold separately.',1),(1499,'Party of One','This series of solitaire adventures is designed for use with the <a href=\"/Series/1498\">Pathfinder Roleplaying Game</a> (or compatible d20-based systems).',1),(1500,'Avalon Solo Adventures','This series of solitaire adventures is designed for use with the <a href=\"/Series/1498\">Pathfinder Roleplaying Game</a> (or compatible d20-based systems).',1),(1501,'Dungeons & Dragons (4th edition)','The fourth edition of Dungeons & Dragons only had two solitaire adventures made for it. One of them was included in the Dungeons & Dragons Essentials introductory boxed set, while the other appeared in an issue of Dungeon magazine.',1),(1502,'Razem','This youth/students magazine included a gamebook-style story (using the <a href=\"/Series/875\">Fantasolo</a> game system) serialized over the course of four issues.',28),(1503,'Miscellaneous Works by Walton D. Stowell II','',1),(1504,'What Should Gavin Do Next?','',1),(1505,'Alternative Storyline Pathways','These books feature a visual mechanism for navigating from page to page -- markers at the page edges direct the reader to the next section.  In electronic editions, the markers are clickable; in physical editions, the markers feature cut-out notches that enable turning to the correct destination page (similar to, but less complex than, the system in <a href=\"/Item/2195\">Meanwhile...</a>.  The first title was published by Project A in board book format, the second by CreateSpace in multiple formats.',1),(1506,'Pick-Your-Own-Plot Adventure','',1),(1507,'Wollarp Trilogy, The','This series parodies the British style of fantasy gamebooks.',1),(1508,'You Choose: Interactive Survival Adventures','This series shares its format with the earlier <a href=\"/Series/1194\">You Choose: Interactive History Adventures</a> series. The reader participates in a variety of survival scenarios, most of them involving wilderness exploration or ocean travel, though other types of adventure are also explored. Later books in the series tend to deal with the reader becoming trapped or imprisoned in a famous historical location (such as the Paris Catacombs or Alcatraz) and trying to find a way out. These books are unrelated both to the <a href=\"/Series/1\">You Choose the Way</a> Dick Tracy gamebook and to the Australian <a href=\"/Series/1623\">You Choose</a> series.',1),(1509,'Miscellaneous Works Published by Perfect Day Publishing','',1),(1510,'Gamebook Experience','These electronic gamebooks provide a digitally-enhanced experience but remain faithful to the conventions of the paper format.',20),(1511,'Gamebook Experience','',6),(1512,'TrollsZine!','This free online journal (distributed in PDF format) covers <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> and frequently includes solo adventures.',1),(1513,'Intruder II: The Next Dimension','This six-volume miniseries was one of TSR\'s \"comic module\" hybrid comic book/game series, a follow-up to an earlier four-volume set called <i>Intruder</i>.  The idea of the \"comic module\" was that each volume included a conventional linear comic book and some associated gaming material (usually role-playing rules and/or small board games with cut-out pieces).  One volume in this set included a gamebook-style solo adventure.  Note that the solo adventure is text-based even though it was printed as part of a comic book.  It makes use of dice rolling in a few places but is otherwise a simple branching-path story.',1),(1514,'Elder Tunnels','This RPG magazine covers <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> and <a href=\"/Series/1515\">BEAN!</a> and includes solo adventures in several issues.',1),(1515,'BEAN! The D2 RPG','This rules-light role-playing game has spawned several solo adventures (including one in the rulebook).',1),(1516,'Tunnels and Trolls: Eposic','',1),(1517,'Witherwind Games Solo Adventures','These d20 solo adventures are designed to be <a href=\"/Series/1498\">Pathfinder</a>-compatible.',1),(1518,'Tunnels and Trolls: Peryton Publishing','',1),(1519,'Rising Phoenix Games Solo Adventures','This d20 solo adventure is designed to be <a href=\"/Series/1498\">Pathfinder</a>-compatible.',1),(1520,'Visual Baker Gamebook Apps','Visual Baker augments classic gamebooks with additional endings, new graphics, animation, sound effects and interactive maps.',1),(1521,'Adventure Paths','',1),(1522,'Unbelievably Simple Roleplaying (USR)','',1),(1523,'Tunnels and Trolls: Trollish Delver Games','',1),(1524,'Tunnels and Trolls: David A. Ullery','This author/artist has self-published at least one adventure.',1),(1525,'Tunnels and Trolls: Stuart Lloyd','This author has self-published several solo adventures.',1),(1526,'Tunnels and Trolls: Alligator','',1),(1527,'Tunnel e troll: Chimerae Hobby Group','This publisher produces third-party, Italian-language <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> products.',5),(1528,'Tunnels and Trolls: Chimerae Hobby Group','',1),(1529,'Miscellaneous Works by Jennifer and John Robinson','',1),(1530,'Miscellaneous Works by Jo Cadwallader','',1),(1531,'Survive Your Own Adventure','',1),(1532,'Battleground General','These gamebooks allow the reader to experience historic battles, giving options to play from the perspective of either the Allied or the Axis side.',1),(1533,'Saturday Morning Breakfast Cereal','This webcomic features interactive adventures in its printed compilation volumes.',1),(1534,'Trial of the Clone','',1),(1535,'QADD: Quick and Dirty Dungeons','This simple, dungeon-oriented role-playing game is published in electronic format and offers several solo adventures.',1),(1536,'Barbarians, Booze, & Battle Axes!','This RPG includes a solo adventure in the rulebook.',1),(1537,'Zombie Death Town','',1),(1538,'Choose-Your-Fate Adventure Books','',1),(1539,'Miscellaneous Works by Colin Webster','',1),(1540,'Choose Your Own Fate','',1),(1541,'Interactive Mysteries','These \"guess-the-ending\" books include a linear narrative peppered with clue props that can be removed from the book and studied.  The final solution to each mystery is held within a sealed section at the end of the book.',1),(1542,'Choose Your Own Vague Adventure','',1),(1543,'Miscellaneous Works by Rudolf Kerkhoven and Daniel Pitts','',1),(1544,'Miscellaneous Works by Will Entrekin','',1),(1545,'Miscellaneous Works by mobipoki','',1),(1546,'Ares','This gaming magazine included a complete fantasy or science fiction game in each issue, some featuring gamebook-like elements.  Several <i>Ares</i> offerings were later republished as stand-alone boxed games.  The series included two adventures of the spaceship B.S.M. Pandora; the first of these, <i>The Wreck of the B.S.M. Pandora</i>, contained no gamebook elements (and thus is not listed here), but the second included text paragraphs providing a narrative for the game.\n\n<p><i>Ares</i> was eventually absorbed into <a href=\"/Series/118\">Dragon Magazine</a> after SPI was purchased by TSR.',1),(1547,'Tunnels and Trolls: Michael Haensel','This author has produced at least one self-published solo adventure.',1),(1548,'Abandon All Hope','This survival horror game has spawned at least one solitaire adventure.',1),(1549,'Muttkins\' Stories','',1),(1550,'Opowieści Kundelkowicza','',28),(1551,'Mass Movement','This free online magazine included gamebook-related interviews in at least one issue.',1),(1552,'Miscellaneous Works by Craig Earl','',1),(1553,'King Arthur','Neocore, the publishers of the King Arthur series of role-playing/real-time strategy computer games, released a free electronic gamebook to promote their second game.',1),(1554,'Pesni ot kosmicheskite drumi [Песни от космическите друми]','',8),(1555,'Miscellaneous Works by Anthony Chris [Антъни Крис]','',8),(1556,'Miscellaneous Works by George Blekbak [Джордж Блекбък]','',8),(1557,'Sutvori svoia Megasviat [Сътвори своя Мегасвят]','',8),(1558,'Wings Keeton Role Playing Game, The','This 1920s adventure role-playing game, written for a game design competition and released free online, includes a solo adventure with the rules.',1),(1559,'How Big is Your Brain?','This book contains a series of brain-training puzzles which are navigated through gamebook-style branching.  The reader attempts to solve each puzzle as quickly as possible (faster solutions yield higher scores) and then makes a choice which determines the next puzzle that needs to be solved.  There is no plot here -- it is simply a navigable collection of puzzles.  The central 16 pages of the book are printed in full color.  An abridged version of the book was given away with the <i>Independent</i> newspaper to promote the release.',1),(1560,'Águila Roja','',6),(1561,'EDGE - Crime Team: You Crack the Case','These books involve a team of investigators who try to solve mysteries in a modern-day setting. The only game mechanic is a scoring system - when prompted by the text, the reader has to write down whether they have made a bad decision or received a warning. The reader is assigned a score at the end based on how many bad decisions and warnings they received during the adventure.',1),(1562,'EDGE - You Choose If You Live or Die','',1),(1563,'iHorror','',1),(1564,'Miscellaneous Works by Mihály Bene','',15),(1565,'Miscellaneous Works by Thorin N. Tatge','',1),(1566,'Crunch','This children\'s magazine included a mini-gamebook in one issue, designed to promote the <a href=\"/Series/14\">Golden Dragon Fantasy Gamebooks</a> (and featuring the same basic game system).',1),(1567,'inklebooks','',1),(1568,'Scenarios: Interactive Fiction for Girls','',1),(1569,'Click Your Poison','In spite of the title, this series is available in both print and electronic formats.',1),(1570,'Maths Quest','This series challenges the reader to solve math problems in order to progress through each story.',1),(1571,'Twisted Tales','These stories include branching plots where the reader makes choices, but all decisions eventually lead to the same ending.',1),(1572,'Cunningham Kids Adventures, The','',1),(1573,'Traveller: Twilight Sector','This third-party product line for the <i>Traveller</i> RPG included a free downloadable solo adventure.',1),(1574,'Miscellaneous Works by Luca de Perux','',2),(1575,'Fighting Fantasy Board Games','Two board games have been produced based directly on books from the original Puffin <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series: numbers 1 and 54. There is no direct link between the two games and they are stand-alone creations, as opposed to one following the other in any way. The first was produced by Games Workshop, the second by Parker. The plastic miniatures included with The Warlock of Firetop Mountain board game were also available to buy separately in a blister pack.',1),(1576,'Reader as Detective, The','These books use the \"guess the ending\" format to teach reading comprehension.',1),(1577,'Labyrinth Lord','',3),(1578,'Pocket Troll','This online magazine (sold in PDF format) covers <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> and sometimes includes solo adventures.',1),(1579,'Dark Game','',6),(1580,'Miscellaneous Works by Daniel McCoy','',1),(1581,'große Buch der Fantasy-Rollenspiele, Das','This book about role-playing includes a sample gamebook-style adventure.',3),(1582,'Miscellaneous Works by A. J. Lauer and Daniel P. Keidl','',1),(1583,'Choose a Path','These interactive books are designed for use in reading instruction, and different titles are aimed at different reading levels.  Activity sheets and audio materials are available in support of the books themselves.',1),(1584,'Choose Your Doom!','',1),(1585,'Miscellaneous Works by Gaetano Abbondanza','',1),(1586,'Prince Groffy','',1),(1587,'Choose Your Own Epic','',1),(1588,'Take Control','These electronic-format gamebooks offer erotica in a variety of settings.',1),(1589,'Fantasy','This fiction magazine included an interactive story in at least one issue.',1),(1590,'Find Yourself Inside','In these books, each chapter ends with a personality quiz which determines how the reader would react to the situation described.',1),(1591,'Choose the Ending','',1),(1592,'Mythweaver Solitaire','This series contains solo adventures for the <i>Mythweaver</i> role-playing game.  The books can be played without access to the main RPG rules.',1),(1593,'Miscellaneous Works by Giorgio Enrico Luca Garrapa','',5),(1594,'Bac Philo Dont Vous Etes le Heros, Le','This gamebook is designed to help teach philosophy.',2),(1595,'Miscellaneous Works by Felbrigg Herriot and K. A. Cartlidge','These two authors collaborated on an Edgar Rice Burroughs-inspired gamebook (based on his Mars series) but first revised/retitled and subsequently withdrew it from the market over licensing concerns.',1),(1596,'One Way Out Novel','',1),(1597,'Centre social de Pessac Saige','',2),(1598,'Smart Choices Kids: Interactive Fairy Tale Adventures','',1),(1599,'Quest: Zeit der Helden','This board game features gamebook-style scenarios.',3),(1600,'Quest: A Time of Heroes','',1),(1601,'Lose Your Own Adventure','This parody series imitated the design style of <a href=\"/Series/30\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> and adds the twist that every ending is some sort of failure. There was a delay between its initial announcement and publication, but it is now available in both standard (<a href=\"/Series/30\">CYOA</a>-sized) and deluxe (oversized) editions. Three further titles were advertised but have yet to appear.',1),(1602,'Miscellaneous Works by Zoot Sax','',1),(1603,'Psychosis','This role-playing game features multi-player adventures that are structured like gamebooks.  Only the first title was released in print, but a second adventure was released when the first was reissued in electronic format.',1),(1604,'Make-Your-Fate Books','',1),(1605,'Ambush!','The <a href=\"/Series/1605\">Ambush!</a> game plus expansions is\na solitaire board game about a squad of American soldiers in late world war 2 on missions across Europe. Played on a typical wargame hex grid game board with cardboard tokens, the game is driven by a combination of cards and paragraphs.',1),(1606,'Miscellaneous Works by Ricardo Pinheiro','',7),(1607,'Prikazka-igra [Приказка-игра]','This gamebook is aimed at toddlers, the first of its type to be published in Bulgaria.',8),(1608,'Laberinto','',6),(1609,'Usborne Advanced Puzzle Adventures','',1),(1610,'Critical IF','This series reprints titles from the <a href=\"/Series/463\">Virtual Reality</a> series in revised/expanded editions. Heart Of Ice was also further re-issued in a collector\'s hardback edition by Megara Entertainment with new colour artwork alongside the original black-and-white illustrations, and subsequently as a deluxe hardback edition by Other Worlds in a box with further material. Once Upon a Time in Arabia was also re-issued in a collector\'s harback edition.',1),(1611,'Atama iiBooks','This is a gamebook series designed for teaching English but featuring diverse subject matter designed to engage adult readers.',1),(1612,'Explore-A-Quest','',1),(1613,'Dragon Roads','This e-book series reissues <a href=\"/Series/121\">Dragontales</a> books with minor textual and title changes.',1),(1614,'Choose Your Own Magic','These interactive books are a spin-off from the <i>Rainbow Magic</i> series, in which each volume discusses a different fairy.',1),(1615,'Penguins of Madagascar Pick Your Path, The','',1),(1616,'WWE Pick Your Path','',1),(1617,'Słuchaj swego serca','',28),(1618,'Choose Your Own Adventure - Promotional','On at least one occasion, an officially licensed <a href=\"/Category/38\">Choose Your Own Adventure</a> was released for a corporate event.',1),(1619,'Cubus Games (Spanish)','Cubus Games produces gamebook-styled mobile apps.',6),(1620,'Cubus Games (English)','Cubus Games produces gamebook-styled mobile apps. Some of their English titles are English originals while others are translated from Spanish.',1),(1621,'Heavy Metal Thunder','',1),(1622,'Choose Your Own Nightmare (2014-)','This newer series from Chooseco contains titles not previously published in the original <a href=\"/Series/89\">Choose Your Own Nightmare</a> series.',1),(1623,'You Choose','This series of Australian gamebooks seems to be completely unrelated to other series which use the same title.',1),(1624,'Dangerous Worlds','',1),(1625,'Complete Role-Playing Game, A','This is an open-ended, exploration-oriented, diceless series.',1),(1626,'Maelorum','',1),(1627,'Argor','This fantasy role-playing game includes a solo adventure in the rulebook.',1),(1628,'Hanako Games','This company produces digital games, some of which are adapted from paper-based gamebooks.',1),(1629,'CKS Studio','This game development studio has produced a digital gamebook (along with many other non-gamebook titles).',1),(1630,'Pocket RPG','This fantasy game, set in the skeleton-ruled land of Lanthir Lamath, puts the computer RPG experience into book form, allowing the reader to navigate a visual environment, collecting items and battling monsters. The adventure is available in both digital and purely print-based formats.',1),(1631,'Gary Chalk\'s Gun Dogs','',1),(1632,'Find Adventure','',1),(1633,'Miscellaneous Works by Beniamin Tytus Muszyński','',28),(1634,'Choose Your Own Horrible History','',1),(1635,'Compass Raiders','',1),(1636,'Miscellaneous Works by Christian and Florian Sussner','',3),(1637,'Narborion Saga','',1),(1638,'RoleBook','',1),(1639,'Gamebook Adventures','Digital gamebooks with a classic RPG gamebook flavour.\n\nThese two apps were originally only available in French but one (Les fils d\'Uruzime) has subsequently been translated into an English-language version. However, the Vietnam War-based La bataille de la Drang remains only available in French.\n\nAll Tin Man\'s apps include artwork, animated dice mechanics, stat-based systems, and a fun feature that allows the player to collect various Achievements to increase re-playability once the game has been beaten in the traditional gamebook sense.\n\nOfficial web page: www.gamebookadventures.com',2),(1640,'Road Less Traveled, A','',1),(1641,'Rider of the Black Sun','Translated from the German-language original, this English version of the book was initially limited to an exclusive short print run of just 50 copies, before a very successful Kickstarter campaign in 2017 resulted in a standard production print run.',1),(1642,'Gamebook Adventures: Fighting Fantasy','This series of Android/iPhone apps based on the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series, which are textually identical to the original books, use the same gamebook engine as Tin Man\'s own in-house <a href=\"/Series/1374\">Gamebook Adventures</a> series.\n\nSome feature newly-created digital cover art. All give options to use colourised or black-and-white internal art as you play. Starship Traveller, Appointment with F.E.A.R. and Caverns of the Snow Witch all feature brand new digital internal art - Caverns of the Snow Witch also has the option of playing with either the new art in colour or the original book\'s art in black and white. Appointment with F.E.A.R. adds a collectable trading card feature where cards can be traded amongst other users of the app. A ninth conversion (Sword of the Samurai) was announced in mid-2014 but was never released.',1),(1643,'Legend of the Wayfarer','This series of free pdf downloads merges RPG elements with more standard solitaire gamebook concepts. The rules system can be modified as the player sees fit and characters can be carried from one book to another to create continuity via experience earning and acquiring additional talents. Books 1-5 were intially available from Lulu.com before slightly revised versions were offered on RPGNow along with the later entries in the series.',1),(1644,'Minecraft Self Adventure','Thse gamebooks, available in both print-on-demand hard copy and Kindle soft copy, are based on the hugely successful Minecraft video game. Billy Miner, the author of some of the books, has also written several strategy guides for Minecraft. It is not clear if the Jeff Kid titles share the series with the Billy Miner titles, or if two separate authors happened to choose the same series title.',1),(1645,'Fighting Fantasy: Amateur Short Stories','A small number of fan-written amateur short stories based on the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series\' world have appeared online for free downloading.',1),(1646,'Fighting Fantasy: Amateur Adventures','A small number of amateur adventures based on the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> system and series have appeared on the internet for free download written in French. These unique fan fictions written as collaborative efforts by several contributors have yet to appear in English as far as is known.',2),(1647,'Fighting Fantasy: The Newsletter','This short-lived amateur monthly fanzine contained a short Fighting Fantasy fan-tale in each issue. Presentation was very basic, being a Word document that could be downloaded for free. Characters could be carried over from Issues 2 to 3 to 5. The adventure in Issue 4 was a stand-alone offering. The Newsletter ceased to be produced after Issue 5.',1),(1648,'Kaland Játék Kockázat','This web-based Hungarian fanzine primarily covering the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series takes a similar format to <a href=\"/Series/1284\">Fighting Fantazine</a> and generally re-prints material from that publication. However, Issue 5 contained three unique amateur adventures that have only ever been printed in Hungarian.',15),(1649,'Falcon Software','Based on the popular <a href=\"/Series/146\">Falcon</a> gamebook series, this single arcade adventure adapted from the first book in the series was critically panned and no further adaptations would be produced. Gameplay was rather limited and the graphics were disappointing, the book being reduced to little more than a map-based shoot-\'em-up.',1),(1650,'Way of the Tiger Software, The','Two nominal adaptations of the first book in <a href=\"/Series/469\">The Way of the Tiger</a> series were produced for several popular 1980s home computer systems by Gremlin Graphics. These would prove successful enough for the first game to be re-issued twice in budget editions (one being exclusive to the UK chain store Boots produced by Kixx) and the second to receive a budget re-isuue (from Kixx) once also.',1),(1651,'Interactive Fiction Gamebooks','',1),(1652,'Wyldemere Chronicles, The','',1),(1653,'Sextrap Dungeon','This series of electronic gamebooks mixes eroticism with comedy.',1),(1654,'Choose Your Erotic Destiny','',1),(1655,'Interactive Pick-Your-Path Erotica','This series of digital gamebooks produced several titles over a relatively short span of months.',1),(1656,'Aurora Sparks Interactive Erotica','This series of erotic Kindle gamebooks has a primarily transgender theme.',1),(1657,'Interactive Adventure Story','This digital gamebook allows you to assume the role of a choice of one of two social media cats (the Wyatt and Marlow of the title) as you guide it through New Year\'s Eve.',1),(1658,'Choose Your Own Adventure Dystopian SciFi Interactive Erotica','This erotic Kindle gamebook series has no connection with the more famous series that shares the first four words of its title!',1),(1659,'Choose-Your-Own-Kink BDSM Adventure','This electronic gamebook for Kindle takes BDSM as its central theme.',1),(1660,'Interactive Erotic Parody','This single Kindle gamebook is an unofficial parody of two highly succesful franchises (Star Wars and Dragon Age) merging characters from both in primarily lesbian situations.',1),(1661,'Choose Your Erotic Adventure','An erotic gamebook for Kindle. A second title exists, but is in fact a standard prose sequel and has no interactive elements.',1),(1662,'Interactive Erotica','This series of electronic erotic gamebooks focus on domination and humiliation. They are noteworthy for their extreme brevity and are possibly the shortest Kindle gamebooks ever released.',1),(1663,'Silkwords LLC','Interactive Kindle erotica aimed at a female audience. Written by various authors, this series of generally fairly short gamebooks uses three different approaches: branched fiction (\"pick your path\"), linear stories with reader participation, and a third type in which readers choose which couples to follow in a shared world.',1),(1664,'Choose Your Own Erotic Story','A Roman-themed erotic gamebook for Kindle devices.',1),(1665,'Pick Your Erotic Adventure','A rather short erotic Kindle gamebook.',1),(1666,'Miscellaneous Works by Liriel Saarinen','These erotic gamebooks for Kindle devices included a feature to unlock free bonus material and even get more free Kindle products on completion.',1),(1667,'You Say Which Way','This series of books, aimed at 8-12 year olds, involves reading a chapter and then choosing which direction the story will take, rather than the usual selecting an option within a paragraph and navigating to that numbered section to move forward through the story. Various genres are covered including Fantasy and Sci-Fi. These publications are available both in paperback form (through Createspace) and for Kindle devices.\n\n<p>The numbering of this series is a best guess; online listings contain contradictory information about the official ordering.</p>\n\n<p>The series spawned a spin-off <a href=\"/Series/2178\">You Say Which Way Adventure Quiz</a> series.',1),(1668,'Marvel Super Heroes Adventure Gamebooks (British)','Published initially in the USA by TSR, seven of the eight TSR books were later re-published in the UK by Puffin Books, albeit in a slightly different number order. The eighth TSR book (Daredevil: Guilt by Association) was never reissued in the UK series. Puffin also produced a very scarce slipcase boxed set containing the first three books.\n\n<p>In each volume, the reader controls one or more characters from Marvel comics. Each character comes with a set of pre-defined attributes which are combined with dice rolls and compared with target numbers in order to perform actions. Characters also have Health Points (which are self-explanatory) and Karma Points (which change as a result of good or bad deeds and which may be spent to improve dice rolls).',1),(1669,'You Are The Hero: A History of Fighting Fantasy Gamebooks','Funded by Kickstarter campaigns, these large format coffee table books chart the entire history of the popular <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series from the early days of Games Workshop\'s creation through to more recent projects, many of which never came to fruition. The books are written in a journalistic style with many interviews with those directly involved as well as fans of the series. The third book, which reworks and updates both previous volumes, is partially written in the style of a gamebook comprising 400 sections.',1),(1670,'Games Workshop Jigsaws','In 1984 Games Workshop produced these six fantasy-themed jigsaw puzzles featuring art by well-known fantasy artists, some with gamebook associations. Two of the jigsaws featured cover art from <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> books - these two jigsaws having only 500 pieces. The remaining four jigsaws were more complex (with 750 pieces); three were fantasy-themed with the fourth taking a Sci-Fi theme. Interestingly, the Sci-Fi image was also used for the cover of the Blue Öyster Cult album Cultösaurus Erectus. \n\n<p>The 500 piece jigsaws measure 49cm x 36cm, the 750 piece examples are slightly larger at 42cm x 60cm.',1),(1671,'Fighting Fantasy Heroes','This system was designed to expand upon <a href=\"/Series/233\">Fighting Fantasy: The Introductory Role-Playing Game</a> by offering a range of plastic figures, paints, and also a stand-alone \"Battlegame\". The plastic figures were definitely sold (in individual blister packs), but the Paint Set and Rules/Dice set appears to be very rare. Images for the figures of Elves and Zombies were advertised but these were never available commercially. Trolls were mentioned in a magazine article only, but these were never actually advertised or produced. The plastic figues had inter-changeable heads to add variety. The figures were also intended to be used for \"mass battles\" by following the Battlegame Rules themselves.',1),(1672,'Fighting Fantasy Licensed Merchandise','A number of items of official merchandise associated with the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series has been produced over the years, some of which has become very collectible due to it being released in relatively small numbers and/or the amount of it that has survived over the years (especially in perfect or complete condition.) \n\n<p>The Ornament series was produced by Clarecraft in 1992 and all were sculpted by Leigh Pamment, except items JL07 and JL11 which were sculpted by John Goadby. The Hydra figure (JL12) was never actually produced due to the complexity of its shape. In line with Wizard Books\' 2004 relaunch of the series, ten of the Clarecraft figures were again produced - JL2 was not re-issued due to its sheer size and pricetag and JL13 could not be re-issued as it included the Puffin Books logo. \n\n<p>Wizard also produced a 2004 Calendar featuring the revised cover artwork from that series.  \n\n<p>The Quest Pack is very difficult to find complete as many were put into use meaning the pencils/dice/Adventure Sheets were used or lost, plus the badges were prone to rusting and the iron-on transfers often decayed over time. \n\n<p>A <a href=\"/Series/585\">Fighting Fantasy Poster Book</a> of cover art and two <a href=\"/Series/1670\">Games Workshop Jigsaws</a> of FF cover art were also produced. \n\n<p>A further series of figurines cast in resin (including the Bloodbeast, Shapechanger, and Devourer) were advertised in September 2014 and a Kickstarter campaign was succesful, but no product has yet seen the light of day. \n\n<p>To coincide with the Fighting Fantasy Fest event on 7th September 2014 in London, Otherworld Miniatures produced a metal mini-figure of Zagor in a Limited Edition of 1,000. In a similar vein, in the mid-1980s, Citadel Miniatures produced a Limited Edition \"Warlock\" figure based on John Blanche\'s cover image from issue 10 of <a href=\"/Series/156\">Warlock</a> magazine, itself a re-working of a reader\'s competition-winning entry to draw the titular Warlock that gave the magazine its name. In early-2017 French publisher Scriptarium produced a limited run of 200 resin miniatures of Yaztromo.',1),(1673,'Fabled Lands Quests','This series develops the world of <a href=\"/Series/145\">Fabled Lands</a>, opening it out into a more quest-based system. The first entry is an adaptation of a gamebook originally published in Puffin\'s <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series. The first book was also issued in a deluxe hardcover edition by Megara Entertainment with full-colour artwork alongside additional illustrations. The second entry is an adaptation of a gamebook originally published in Berkley\'s <a href=\"/Series/14\">Golden Dragon Fantasy Gamebooks</a> series.',1),(1674,'Sorcery! (Inkle)','These digital app conversions of Steve Jackson\'s <a href=\"/Series/15\">Sorcery</a> series are faithful to the books whilst also adding additional features, in particular a rather elaborate combat system allowing you to take either a defensive or offensive stance and giving you options of which bodyparts to strike. As digital gamebook apps go these are very large being over 200MB each so are better-suited to tablets than phone devices due to the amount of memory space they require.',1),(1675,'Pick Your Path Interactive Erotica Games','Another example of the many erotic Kindle gamebook series that appeared in the mid-2010s this one covers a far broader spectrum of erotic interests than most. At some point in time, the series title was changed from \"Choose Your Own Adventure Interactive Erotica,\" presumably due to trademark issues.',1),(1676,'Choose Her Peril','This series of erotic Kindle gamebooks expands on ideas created in various erotic short stories written by the same author.',1),(1677,'Von L-Interactives','Another of the large number of erotic Kindle gamebooks available from Amazon in the mid-2010s.',1),(1678,'EroticGameBooks','This Kindle gamebook series explores various sexual fantasies.',1),(1679,'Wicked Way Interactive','This erotic Kindle gamebook series has a better pedigree than most, being written by a USA Today and New York Times bestselling erotica author. Three of the series are spin-offs from the same author\'s hit erotic novel cycle called Savage Tales.',1),(1680,'Miscellaneous Works by Amelia Moore','Several relatively short erotic Kindle gamebooks.',1),(1681,'Choose Your Own Fantasies','This series of erotic gamebooks for Kindle devices explores various fetishes.',1),(1682,'Exploring The Warm Castle','A relatively short erotic Kindle gamebook.',1),(1683,'Stray Cat','This series of Kindle erotica includes one choose-your-path interactive book, this being number 2 in the overall series.',1),(1684,'Gamebook Adventures: Ryan North\'s To Be Or Not To Be','This series of Android/iPhone apps uses the same gamebook engine as Tin Man\'s own in-house <a href=\"/Series/1374\">Gamebook Adventures</a> series.',1),(1685,'Gamebook Adventures: Judge Dredd','This series of Android/iPhone apps based on the 2000AD\'s hugely popular comic book charater, uses the same gamebook engine as Tin Man\'s own in-house <a href=\"/Series/1374\">Gamebook Adventures</a> series.\n<p>A second app was announced in 2012 with a planned 2013 release but this never appeared and, as of 2017, Tin Man Games no longer have the rights to the Judge Dredd property so the second app is unlikely to ever appear. Similarly, the first app can no longer be purchased due to this rights issue.',1),(1686,'Gamebook Adventures: Trial of the Clone','This series of Android/iPhone apps uses the same gamebook engine as Tin Man\'s own in-house <a href=\"/Series/1374\">Gamebook Adventures</a> series.',1),(1687,'Gamebook Adventures: Chooseomatic','This series of light-hearted Android/iPhone apps uses the same gamebook engine as Tin Man\'s own in-house <a href=\"/Series/1374\">Gamebook Adventures</a> series.',1),(1688,'Gamebook Adventures Masters','This series of Android/iPhone apps based on other gamebook properties, which are textually identical to the original books, use the same gamebook engine as Tin Man\'s own in-house <a href=\"/Series/1374\">Gamebook Adventures</a> series.',1),(1689,'Gamebook Adventures Choices','This series of Android/iPhone apps uses the same gamebook engine as Tin Man\'s own in-house <a href=\"/Series/1374\">Gamebook Adventures</a> series. This particular app was originally only available in French, but was later released in an English-language version.',1),(1690,'Gamebook Adventures: The Spellcaster Trilogy','This series of Android/iPhone apps based on the <a href=\"/Series/906\">Spellcaster Gamebooks</a> series, which are textually identical to the original books, use the same gamebook engine as Tin Man\'s own in-house <a href=\"/Series/1374\">Gamebook Adventures</a> series. Only number 1 was originally released in hard copy format, but this Tin Man series of electronic gamebooks saw the first and previously unreleased second book get released, both will all-new artwork by Tony Hough. Number three was advertised, but has yet to be released.',1),(1691,'Gamebook Adventures: Strange Loves','This series of Android/iPhone apps, aimed at a teenaged female market, use the same gamebook engine as Tin Man\'s own in-house <a href=\"/Series/1374\">Gamebook Adventures</a> series.',1),(1692,'Lone Wolf (BulkyPix)','This Android app from BulkyPix features a completely new <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> story written by Joe Dever. Graphics are presented from a 3D perspective. The initial app includes Episodes I and II of the story with further instalments periodically available as add-ons, although the price may be off-putting as this is an expensive package by usual Android app standards. Options are available to play in English, French, Italian, German or Spanish.',1),(1693,'Miscellaneous Works by Susan Alexander','These Kindle gamebooks takes a less conventional approach to the interactive erotica genre by presenting sexual combat situations.',1),(1694,'Your Naughty Playmate','A series of erotic Kindle gamebooks that deal with various fantasy situations.',1),(1695,'Choose Your Own Erotic Odyssey','This series of unusually long (for the erotic genre) Kindle gamebooks presents erotic situations but in a traditional fantasy world setting with orcs, goblins, elves, etc.',1),(1696,'Princess to Pleasure Slave Adventure','In a similar style to another of this author\'s series (<a href=\"/Series/1695\">Choose Your Own Erotic Odyssey</a>), this Kindle gamebook presents erotica in a traditional fantasy adventure setting with elves, trolls, orcs, goblins, etc. This book is a spin-off from a lengthy (20+ books) series of erotic novels by the same author set in the same fantasy world milieu.',1),(1697,'Pick Your Pleasure','A series of erotic Kindle gamebooks by a New York Times and USA Today bestselling author.',1),(1698,'Long Shots','This series of erotic Kindle novellas produced one interactive entry where readers\' choices can affect the final outcome of the story.',1),(1699,'Doctor Who: Decide Your Destiny (Series 2)','These interactive adventures tie in with the revived <i>Doctor Who</i> television series. This second series of four books focuses on the Matt Smith era.',1),(1700,'Basic Role-Playing','Early editions of this set of role-playing game rules included a solitaire adventure that is completely linear (it includes no numbered sections or \'turn-to\' instructions). The adventure, however, uses game mechanics to resolve combat and other actions.</p>\n\nReṕortedly, only the reissued third edition of these rules (from 2002) was available as a standalone product. The previous editions were all included as part of boxed sets of other Chaosium roleplaying games (such as <a href=\"/Series/112\">RuneQuest</a>). While there are several newer editions of the ruleset than those mentioned here, apparently none of them contain the solitaire adventure.',1),(1701,'The Legend of Zelda','This interactive comic book, published by Nintendo of America, was intended as a promotion for two Zelda videogames: <i>The Legend of Zelda: A Link to the Past</i> (for the Super NES console) and <i>The Legend of Zelda: Link\'s Awakening</i> (for the Nintendo Game Boy).',1),(1702,'Heroes and Other Worlds','This fantasy role-playing game draws inspiration from Metagaming\'s <a href=\"/Series/154\">The Fantasy Trip</a>, as well as the Moldvay version of <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons & Dragons</a>. The rulebook includes a reprint of one of the solo adventures in the <a href=\"/Series/995\">Legends of the Ancient World</a> series (note: information about the book\'s content comes from the RPGGeek database).',1),(1703,'Estandarte Sangrento','This one-off gamebook is based on Tagmar, a Brazilian fantasy role-playing game.',7),(1704,'Herói da Copa, O','This Brazilian gamebook has a soccer theme.',7),(1705,'Juego de rol del capitán Alatriste','This Spanish role-playing game is based on a series of novels written by Arturo Pérez-Reverte, about a fictional Seventeenth-Century Spanish swordsman and soldier. The rulebook contains a solitaire adventure.',6),(1706,'The Temple on Terilek Prime','This adventure was originally released as part of a supplement for GURPS Third Edition. A standalone version of the adventure was later released as part of Free RPG Day in 2007, with rules reworked to make it compatible with both GURPS Fourth Edition and the <a href=\"/Category/87\">d20 System</a>.',1),(1707,'Last RPG Fantasy','This is a fantasy interactive comic book produced in Brazil by a gaming design group known as Lobo Limão, and published by Jambô Editora. A sequel has been announced as being in the works.',7),(1708,'Tormenta','Tormenta is a Brazilian fantasy role-playing game. The books listed here, while not formally branded as part of a series, are all set in the Tormenta universe. The books have been numbered below for convenience. All of them are available in paper and pdf formats.',7),(1709,'Libri Gioco','This series includes a reprint of a gamebook by Andrea Angiolino, as well as an original title by a different author.',5),(1710,'Choose Your Own Misery','This fun satirical gamebook is aimed at an adult readership and is written by two contributors to The Onion. The concept follows the (mis-)adventures of you, an office worker trying to get through a day at work in a job you hate and with a hangover to boot!',1),(1711,'Choose Your Own Celebrity Adventure','Satirising the cult of celebrity, the back-cover blurb says it all about this humorous gamebook where you are determined to achieve fame and fortune at all costs.',1),(1712,'Dungeons & Dragons: The Complete Animated Series','This 5-disc DVD boxed set contained an electronic version of the adventure from the 1985 <a href=\"/Item/841\">Dungeons & Dragons Sticker Book</a>.\n\n<p>(Note: information about this item is taken from the Dungeons & Dragoons Cartoon Encyclopedia blog: http://www.dungeonsdragonscartoon.com).',1),(1713,'What\'s Your Story?','Broadcast on BBC1 in the UK, these two childrens\' TV series were an experiment in live televised interactive fiction. The premise was that Episode 1 of each story was pre-written, the remaining episodes being assembled based on elements of suggestions made by viewers who phoned in their ideas. Series 1 (10 episodes) was shown over two weeks (Monday-Friday) from 31st October 1988 to 11th November 1988. The phone in feature of the show was highly successful with 500,000 calls in the first week. The volume of calls was so great that, at one point, BT couldn\'t cope and the line collapsed. The second week of the first series changed the format slightly, in that four ideas were chosen from the viewers and then these were voted on by the public.\nThe second series (of only 5 episodes) ran over one week from 19th March 1990 to 23rd March 1990. Whilst the series got decent viewing figures, the practicalities of shooting to a very tight schedule made for a hectic atmosphere in the studio with scripts having to be written over night, lines learnt, sets built, and the next episode filmed, all in time for 4pm broadcasting on the following day. Sylvester McCoy (the Seventh Doctor Who) presented the show on each day\'s live broadcast whilst a bank of telephonists sat taking calls from viewers giving their suggestions for the next episode. Peter Corey would then assemble the ideas and write the next instalment that night. Very little footage has subsequently surfaced from the two series since their original transmissions, with only one full episode from Series 1 plus a few very brief teasers and a clip from a BBC documentary about 1980s childrens\' television being available on YouTube.',1),(1714,'Heroes Against Darkness','This freely-available fantasy RPG has had one solitaire adventure made for it.',1),(1715,'Knightmare: Amateur Adventures','Amateur pdf adventures based on the Knightmare TV series. All are available to download for free from www.knightmare.com',1),(1716,'Sobrevive','',6),(1717,'Grim Choices','Darkly comedic gamebooks targeted at adult readers and written by an Australian stand-up comedian.',1),(1718,'Gruesome Gamebooks','Aimed at 7+ early readers, these large print gamebooks are based on famous fairy tales and are designed to encourage young children in learning to read. The books in this series are not numbered as such.',1),(1719,'Opt for the Path','',1),(1720,'Tú descubres el enigma','',6),(1721,'Doctor Who: Choose the Future','Published by Penguin\'s Puffin imprint and based on the 12th (Peter Capaldi) Doctor, these relatively short gamebooks have no system as such, being more in the \"Choose Your Own Adventure\" vein.',1),(1722,'Gamebook Adventures','Snowbooks published these paperback versions of Tin Man Games\' <a href=\"/Series/1374\">Gamebook Adventures</a> series of apps. They are exactly the same as the digital versions in every way.\n\n<p>In spite of Amazon listing the second and third books and them initially being advertised to pre-order, neither has seen print, leaving the first book as the only one actually available.',1),(1723,'Temple Run: Run for Your Life','This series is based on a video game franchise for Android, iOS, and Windows Phone devices, in which you play an Indiana Jones-type character. The first book includes only a simple inventory mechanic. The books are available in paperback and Amazon Kindle formats.',1),(1724,'Fabled Lands','Megara Entertainment published this French translation in full colour softback edition. However, to date, they have only issued the core rule book.',2),(1725,'Autumn Snow','In 2015 Megara Entertainment began to publish this all-new <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> spin-off series in hard cover editions with black-and-white internal artwork. After a very limited printing of the second book, the series went on hold until being picked up again by Holmgard Press in 2024.',1),(1726,'Neige d\'Automne','The French-language editions of Megara Entertainment\'s all-new <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> spin-off series which began to be published in 2015.',2),(1727,'Nils Jacket','Large-format detective gamebook series published in French by Megara Entertainment.',2),(1728,'Arcana Agency','English-language detective gamebooks published by Megara Entertainment in full-colour deluxe hardback editions.',1),(1729,'Megara Entertainment produit en français','Not strictly-speaking a series as such, but (with the exception of the <a href=\"/Series/463\">Virtual Reality</a> re-print Coeur de Glace) these otherwise original French-language gamebooks are part of an ongoing series published by Megara Entertainment (based in Cannes) showcasing home-grown French gamebook talents. Some feature colour illustrations, others black-and-white, and all are printed in hardback deluxe Collector\'s Editions.',2),(1730,'Miscellaneous Works by Faringdon Junior School','Produced as part of an annual project run by Duncan Saunders at Faringdon Junior School in England, these CreateSpace gamebooks were written as collaborative efforts by schoolchildren aged 7-11.',1),(1731,'Fighting Fantasy Colouring Books','This series of official colouring books produced as a spin-off from the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series was published by Snowbooks.',1),(1732,'Art of Lone Wolf, The','The Art of Lone Wolf features all of Gary Chalk\'s artwork from the original <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> books numbers 1 to 8. Artwork from the spin-off series <a href=\"/Series/1725\">Autumn Snow</a> is also included. Almost 200 pieces of black and white line work have been restored to look like new in this coffee table book available in both paperback and hardback format.',1),(1733,'Miscellaneous Works by Stuart and Donna Paltrowitz','These authors created collections of stories augmented with BASIC computer programs, similar to the later and better-known <a href=\"/Series/1163\">Micro Adventure</a> series.',1),(1734,'ACE Gamebooks','This project was funded by several Kickstarter campaigns. Based on various public domain properties these gamebooks put a horror-fairy tale twist on childrens\' classics. \n\n<p>The first book allows for standard-style play with dice or an innovative randomisation method that employs a specially-created deck of Alice playing cards. The second and third books employ character stat bookmarks to allow play as any one of several characters. Book 4 is only playable with a single character, the titular Beowulf, though there is an Easter egg, where you can play as a different character.\n<p>Colouring Books featuring Kev Crossley\'s illustrations were also released to accompany the first two gamebooks.',1),(1735,'Choose Your Own Career Adventure','Aimed at kids, this gamebook series (available in paper and on Kindle) encourages readers to consider possible (admittedly, very stereotypically \"American Dream\"-esque) career paths. Readers pick from eight different scenarios and experience \"next best thing to being there yourself\" opportunities for interactive career exploration. Sidebars promote additional learning activities and independent reaserch.',1),(1736,'Timmi Tobbson Solve-Them-Yourself Mysteries Adventures','This linear children\'s novel features a \"solve-it-yourself\" mystery puzzle (featuring visual clues in an accompanying illustration) at the end of each chapter.',1),(1737,'Miscellaneous works by Paul Guimard','French author Paul Guimard may have been the earliest writer of multiple-path fiction. At least one of his novels branches out into two alternate storylines at one point, allowing the reader to experience two possible outcomes.',2),(1738,'Holdfast','',1),(1739,'Two-Fisted Fantasy','Funded by a Kickstarter campaign, this tongue-in-cheek gamebook series is available from DriveThruRPG.com. The concept is that this is a rediscovered \"lost\" series from the early-80s (this is a joke, of course)! Play is possible with either the book on its own or with the use of a specially-designed deck of cards.\n\n<p>The completed first and second books are available in pdf and hardcopy formats. The third book is currently only available as a shorter preview edition in free downloadable pdf-format.',1),(1740,'Midnight Legion, The','Funded by a Kickstarter campaign, this Sci-Fi series allows for single- or two-player play. The first book is presented in a boxed set along with the Memory And Rulebook and a pair of custom dice.',1),(1741,'Bibliothèque rose','',2),(1742,'Chronicles of Magnamund','This series consists of stand-alone gamebooks set in the world of Magnamund.',1),(1743,'Lone Wolf: The Boardgame','This boardgame based on the <a href=\"/Series/228\">Lone Wolf</a> franchise was funded by a Kickstarter campaign and, after several production/distribution delays, was eventually retailed by Megara Entertainment at 90 Euros including a blister pack of 80 playing character tokens.',1),(1744,'Fighting Fantasy / Dark Game','This series contains Spanish translations of English-language <a href=\"/Category/37\">Fighting Fantasy</a> gamebooks with the <a href=\"/Series/1579\">Dark Game</a> logo included alongside the traditional FF logo.',6),(1745,'DestinyQuest','',6),(1746,'Tú decides la leyenda','',6),(1747,'Golden Dragon Fantasy Gamebooks: Augmented Collector\'s Edition','These deluxe editions of the <a href=\"/Series/14\">Golden Dragon Fantasy Gamebooks</a> series were funded by Kickstarter campaigns and published by Megara Entertainment, but are rather more than just straight reissues. Printed on high quality paper in oversized hardback format each book has had 100 extra sections added by new gamebook writers (working in conjunction with the original authors) and all internal art has been colourised.',1),(1748,'Doctor Who and the Fescan Threat','This very large gamebook printed on A4 format in an unofficial limited run through an independent publisher was sold to raise money for the mental health charity MIND in Summer 2016. Taking over 10 years to write, this is one of the longest single-volume gamebooks ever written. The book allows for play as any of the first Ten Doctors, each with different stats and a slightly different adventure, which explains its considerable length.',1),(1749,'Gamebook Adventures: The Warlock of Firetop Mountain','Funded by a Kickstarter campaign, this <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> adaptation for Windows pcs expands hugely on the source material, adding new art (and colourising the already existing art), many additional areas, new encounters and puzzles, many playable characters with various strengths and weaknesses, and different game missions dependent on the chosen characters. \n<p>Tin Man took their <a href=\"/Series/1642\">Gamebook Adventures: Fighting Fantasy</a> series of apps to a whole new level with this outing as the approach mixes traditional gamebook styles (text with choices, illustrations, dice-rolling, etc) with a visual tabletop RPG design where each character is a digital gaming miniature moving around an ever-expanding on-screen gameboard. Battles mix <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>\'s dual-stat combat system with RPG-style attacking and retreating movement around a gridded board. Extra player-characters can be \"bought\" as you collect Souls by killing enemies and Tin Man\'s fun Achievements mechanic is also included.',1),(1750,'The Smoke Dream','This gamebook requires a deck of playing cards to play.\n\n(Note: information about this book was obtained from https://thesmokedream.wordpress.com/about/).',1),(1751,'Choose-Your-Own-Path Shakespeare','<p>This series of spoof adventures based on classic Shakespeare plays is written by the author of the very popular Adventure Time childrens\' story books based on the Cartoon Network animated series.\n<p>The first book in the series set a record for the highest-earning paperback book Kickstarter campaign ever at the time, raising more than $500,000 and gaining over 15,000 backers! As part of the campaign a short prequel adventure was also made available entitled Poor Yorick which was exclusive to campaign backers.',1),(1752,'Mistério na Vila da Neblina','This is a modern-day gamebook with some elements taken from Egyptian mythology.',7),(1753,'Seu Turno','This is a fantasy interactive comic book.',7),(1754,'Viver ou Morrer','This series includes two fantasy gamebooks. The first title in this series is a reissue of a book originally published in 1997.',7),(1755,'The Maze of Games','This book contains many puzzles that the reader must solve. Solving each puzzle allows the reader to determine which page to turn to next, in a manner reminiscent of choose-your-own-adventure books.',1),(1756,'jué zé cóng shū [抉擇叢書]','These translations contain many illustrations that have been re-drawn rather than reprinted from the original.',14),(1757,'Sværd og trolddom roman','',22),(1758,'Sværd og trolddom fantastiske rollespil','',22),(1759,'Ashkar the Magnificent','This standalone non-interactive novel is set in the same region as the author\'s <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> gamebook Seas of Blood.',1),(1760,'Aquelarre Siglo XIV','This series, which contains only one title, is based on the <a href=\"/Series/1305\">Aquelarre</a>  role-playing game. Even though the author has written an official gamebook and a introductory solo for the game, this book does not appear to be sanctioned by any of the game\'s publishers. The book is only available for free over the Internet in pdf format.',6),(1761,'Miscellaneous Works by Juan Pablo Fernández','This author has self-published a paperback version of an electronic gamebook based on the <a href=\"/Series/1305\">Aquelarre</a> roleplaying game. The paperback version uses a modified version of the rules included in the electronic version. The paperback does not make any reference to the RPG, though it retains the same basic story and a similar setting.',6),(1762,'Sección 14','Section 14 was created with the idea of offering gamebooks with a classic-style game system but featuring innovative mechanics and carefully balanced gameplay.',6),(1763,'Follow Your Fates','This series is based on Greek mythology.',1),(1764,'Chroniques d\'Hamalron, Les','',2),(1765,'Asterix - Alea Jacta Est!','Apparently, only the first two titles in this French series were translated into Spanish.',6),(1766,'Detective\'s Choice','These electronic books (available for Android, iOS, and Windows Phone devices) combine noir and horror elements.',1),(1767,'ReaderActive','This series is based on the Pokémon media franchise.',1),(1768,'Un livre dont vous êtes l\'aventurier - Aventures','',2),(1769,'Un livre dont vous êtes l\'aventurier - SF','',2),(1770,'Vijf Speurboeken, De','These translations replace the dice from the English version with a pencil-mounted spinner.',13),(1771,'Red Giant','This short-lived gaming magazine included a sci-fi detective solo adventure in the second (and last) issue. The adventure involves finding clues to solve a mystery. Players were asked to mail in their solution to the mystery as part of a competition. Both the correct solution and the competition results would be revealed in the following issue. The third issue was never published, and apparently neither was the official solution.',1),(1772,'Gravity Falls \"Select Your Own Choose-Venture\"','',1),(1773,'Weg der Wachtel, Der','This gamebook takes place in a setting inspired by feudal Japan and features illustrations of anthropomorphic quail. It was distributed freely online and sold in a print-on-demand edition.',3),(1774,'Ring Quest','This computer game included a brief interactive story in the manual as an introduction to the digital portion of the adventure.',1),(1775,'Advanced Dungeons & Dragons Aventura Sem Fim Série Avançada','',7),(1776,'Dungeons & Dragons Aventura Sem Fim','',7),(1777,'Classici di Walt Disney seconda serie, I','This comic book reprinted some stories from <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a>.  Some issues of the comic were also made available in the <i>Raccolta I Classici di Walt Disney seconda serie</i> anthology series. Some of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',5),(1778,'Gooruden doragon [ゴールデン・ドラゴン]','',4),(1779,'Star Challenge Series [スター チャレンジ シリーズ]','',4),(1780,'GameTale','',1),(1781,'Fiaba-Gioco','',5),(1782,'Skazka-Igra [Сказка-Игра]','',27),(1783,'Spēlējamā pasaka','',37),(1784,'Konton no uzu [混沌の渦]','',4),(1785,'Shinté Warrior Series','This fantasy gamebook series follows the ongoing adventures of Tsai, a martial arts practitioner.',1),(1786,'Miscellaneous Works by \"Alan George\"','',1),(1787,'Lost: Can You Survive?','',1),(1788,'Enigmas y aventuras que tú decides','Five titles from this series were translated into Spanish. The translations spawned an original Spanish-language spinoff series titled Enigmas y aventuras que tú debes resolver.',6),(1789,'Grandi Classici Disney','This comic book reprinted some stories from <a href=\"/Series/366\">Topolino</a>.  Some issues of the comic were also made available in the <i>Raccolta Grandi Classici Disney</i> anthology series. \nSome of the information on this page is derived from the <a href=\"http://inducks.org\">Inducks</a> database.',5),(1790,'American Girl Beforever: My Journey Books','These books tie in with the popular toy line, telling historical stories about some of the characters represented by the dolls. Abridged editions of some of these books were distributed with the matching dolls. Some books have additional endings available online.',1),(1791,'You Choose: Interactive Mythological Adventures','These books allow the reader to take part in a series of ancient Greco-Roman myths. The series appears to be unrelated both to the to the <a href=\"/Series/1\">You Choose the Way</a> Dick Tracy gamebook and to the Australian <a href=\"/Series/1623\">You Choose</a> series.',1),(1792,'Car Warriors','This 4-part non-interactive comic book series is set in the same world as the Car Wars gamebook series.',1),(1793,'Car Warriors Novels','This series of three non-interactive novels is set in the same world as the Car Wars series of gamebooks.',1),(1794,'Dungeon Saga','Based on the same publisher\'s RPG/board game series of the same name, these gamebooks based in the same world are available in both paperback and as multi-format compatible digital editions.',1),(1795,'Dark Game','Translated from the Spanish series of the same name, these gamebooks are aimed at an adult audience as, whilst they are traditional fantasy fare, they include mature themes which are not suited for younger readers.',1),(1796,'Dragon d\'or: édition Collector','French translations of the extended Collector\'s Editions of the Golden Dragon series.',2),(1797,'Choose Your Own Fate','',1),(1798,'Can You Survive the Zombie Apocalypse?','',1),(1799,'2000 AD Rebellion Reprints','After Rebellion acquired this long-running British Sci-Fi anthology comic they started reprinting many of the more popular series in A4-sized trade paperback format. One title in the Slaine series contained a 15-part story with a separate interactive portion.',1),(1800,'Pick Your Path','Funded by a Kickstarter, these short adventures come in both pdf and physical formats as folded-down mini pocket pamphlets. The three adventures (one fantasy, one horror, and one Sci-Fi) are supplied as a set and are designed for play \"on the move\" (for example on public tarnsport), hence the pocket-sized format.',1),(1801,'Fighting Fantasy Comics: Freeway Fighter','This 4-part non-interactive comic book series is based on Fighting Fantasy book number 13 and expands on the general setting and concept.',1),(1802,'Duel Master Software','Software for \'80s home computers adapted from the Duel Master gamebook of the same name. As with the book, the option is given for One or Two Player play.',1),(1803,'Fighting Fantasy Comics: Steve Jackson\'s The Trolltooth Wars','Based on the non-interactive novel of the same title set in the Fighting Fantasy world, this graphic novel was funded by a Kickstarter which became notorious in the fan community for being delivered overdue by 18 months.',1),(1804,'You Choose: Interactive Doomsday Adventures','This series enmeshes the reader in a variety of disaster and end-of-the-world scenarios. While other series in the <a href=\"/Category/98\">You Choose</a> line feature three short adventures per book, each of the books in this series consists of a single adventure. Like the other series in the line, this one appears to be unrelated both to the to the <a href=\"/Series/1\">You Choose the Way</a> Dick Tracy gamebook and to the Australian <a href=\"/Series/1623\">You Choose</a> series.',1),(1805,'You Choose: Chilling Interactive Adventures','',1),(1806,'You Choose: Interactive Engineering Adventures','',1),(1807,'How Would You Survive?','Each book in this series deals with a specific historical period. The books are written much like regular history books for children, but at the end of each two-page spread the reader is offered a choice of where to go next (and must turn to a different text section accordingly). There are no multiple endings, but at the end of each book the reader must answer a quiz based on the content in the book. The reader is then evaluated on his/her knowledge and assigned a score.',1),(1808,'You Choose: Interactive Space Exploration Adventures','',1),(1809,'You Choose: Interactive Espionage Adventures','',1),(1810,'Rytíř temného slunce','',17),(1811,'You Choose: Interactive Modern History Adventures','',1),(1812,'Miscellaneous Works by Siobhán Gallagher','This author creates interactive fiction from a blend of concise text and cartoon-like illustrations.',1),(1813,'Disney/Pixar Inside Out: You Decide the Ending!','This series of gamebooks is based on Pixar\'s hit animated film.',1),(1814,'Woven Paths','',1),(1815,'Knightmare TeleText Adventures','<p>These adventures were made available via Channel 4\'s now defunct TeleText interactive service in the UK. FastText buttons in four colours on your TV remote control corresponded to choices on screen allowing you to navigate through the adventures. Each adventure was only available to play for a 24 hour period, after which the TeleText pages they used were rewritten for other uses, normally an on-screen daily quiz called Bamboozle.\n<p>In 2017 a VHS cassette was found containing a programme that was recorded from UK TV on the day that one of the adventures was originally available (22nd December 1993). The screen images were lifted from the recorded material and converted to html stored in a web archive. From the full day\'s archived material, the adventure pages were retrieved, any on-screen character corruptions corrected, and the full reconstructed adventure was made available for online play here: http://www.castleteletext.com/pages/Page%20458.htm',1),(1816,'Fighting Fantasy Audio Dramas','Non-interactive audio dramas based on books from the Fighting Fantasy series. Available in CD and digital download format.',1),(1817,'Ultimate Ending','',1),(1818,'Fighting Fantasy (2017-, Scholastic)','This new series of reissues sees classic titles reprinted alongside brand new books. These new editions of the books are in a different order than the originals were and are oversized format that matches Wizard Book\'s 2nd reissued series\' format; for more information on the original series, visit the <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> entry.\n<p>For the first time ever, Scholastic\'s reissues also feature newly-commissioned internal art (as well as new updated cover art) which met with a mixed reaction from fans.',1),(1819,'You Choose: Scooby-Doo','',1),(1820,'Dawn of the Donald','A political satire aimed squarely at the global political scene as of the 2016 Presidential Elections. Intended to be funded via Kickstarter in October 2017, the project was cancelled due to low take-up rates. A short mocked-up preview made in MS Word and containing the introduction, section 1, and a few art samples was available to download on pdf from the project page and is currently all that exists of this gamebook.',1),(1821,'Bionic Agent','',1),(1822,'Miscellaneous Works by Cloud Buchholz','',1),(1823,'Decide Your Fate','',1),(1824,'Chooseomatic Games','',1),(1825,'Miscellaneous Works by Kristan J. Wheaton and Melonie K. Richey','',1),(1826,'Skyrim','The video game <i>Skyrim</i> contains a brief gamebook that can be discovered in-game as an Easter egg.',1),(1827,'Castles of Imagination','This ongoing gamebook series, in which decisions in early books can affect later ones, follows a pair of high-schoolers who create a fantasy world to help cope with their real life.\n\n<p>The unnumbered title is a sampler collection which contains a brief prequel to the series.\n\n<p>There is no third book; the third story is featured as an incomplete bonus as part of the fourth book.',1),(1828,'You Choose: Interactive Battlefield Adventures','',1),(1829,'Countdown to Danger: Choose Your Own Ending','This series was originally published in Australia by Scholastic. It was later reissued by Sterling Children\'s Books in the United States, with less explicit series branding (the \"Countdown to Danger: Choose Your Own Ending\" name is not used on the books, and while online listings use the \"Choose Your Destiny!\" name, it does not appear to be printed on the books themselves).',1),(1830,'Miscellaneous Works by Simone Campos','',7),(1831,'Fighting Fantasy (2009-, Jambô)','This is the second translation of the series published in Brazil, following <a href=\"/Series/466\">Aventuras fantásticas</a>.',7),(1832,'Story Thieves','This series of standard linear novels includes one interactive volume.',1),(1833,'Fenix','This magazine included a mini-gamebook adventure in at least one issue.',11),(1834,'Entre Linhas Aventura','',7),(1835,'Dark Eye, The','',1),(1836,'Adventure is Yours!, The','',1),(1837,'BD dont vous êtes le héros, La','',2),(1838,'Dungeons & Dragons (Norwegian)','',29),(1839,'Black Power: The Superhero Gamebook','',1),(1840,'Fighting Fantasy: Make Something Unreal Live','In 2012, a competition was held challenging student developers to use the Unreal engine to adapt <a href=\"/Category/37\">Fighting Fantasy</a> gamebooks into 3-D phone apps. These games are the result.',1),(1841,'Tunnels and Trolls: Khaghbboommm Press','',1),(1842,'Miscellaneous Works by Dan Abnett','',1),(1843,'Ace of Aces','',7),(1844,'DragonLords','This UK RPG fanzine, edited by Ian Marsh and others, included a <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> solo adventure in one issue, which is listed under <a href=\"/Series/371\">Quasits &amp; Quasars</a> due to joint publication.',1),(1845,'Miser\'s Hoard','This UK fanzine included a generic solo adventure in one issue, suitable for <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons &amp; Dragons</a> or <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a>.',1),(1846,'Trollish Tribune, The','This <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> fanzine included a solo adventure in one issue.',1),(1847,'Lego Batman Movie: Build Your Own Story, The','',1),(1848,'You Choose: Batman','These systemless choose-your-path books allow the reader to play the role of DC Comics superhero Batman. Unlike many other series in the <a href=\"/Category/98\">You Choose</a> line, each book in this series consists of a single long adventure, instead of three short ones. This is one of several series by the same publisher dealing with DC Comics characters, the others being You Choose: Superman, You Choose: Justice League, and You Choose: Wonder Woman.\n\n<p>There have been several interactive series and games which have featured Batman as a player character, though this series appears to be unrelated (except for the subject matter) to any of them. These books also appear to be unrelated both to the <a href=\"/Series/1\">You Choose the Way</a> Dick Tracy gamebook and to the Australian <a href=\"/Series/1623\">You Choose</a> series.',1),(1849,'Follow Your Heart (2014-)','This is the third unrelated romance series to use this title, following <a href=\"/Series/170\">Follow Your Heart Romance</a> and the 2006 <a href=\"/Series/1029\">Follow Your Heart</a> series.',1),(1850,'Miscellaneous Works by Pippa Goodhart','This author does not write gamebooks in the traditional sense, but her picture books encourage parent/child interaction in the form of making decisions about what characters in the story should do.',1),(1851,'Way of the Quail, The','',1),(1852,'Défis Fantastiques - Le jeu de rôle','These translations are in some cases significantly expanded from the English-language editions and include new artwork, mainly commissioned from original <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a> series artists, including Malcolm Barter, Leo Hartas, Tony Hough, Bill Houston, Alan Langford, Russ Nicholson, John Sibbick and Duncan Smith.',2),(1853,'Rêve de Dragon','',2),(1854,'Alarums and Excursions','This long-running amateur press association publication discusses role-playing games and occasionally covers gamebook-related topics.',1),(1855,'Miscellaneous Works by Jorge Luis Borges','',7),(1856,'DezAventuras','',7),(1857,'Miscellaneous Works by Julio Cortázar','',7),(1858,'Ensamma Vargen (2014-)','',11),(1859,'Freeway Warrior','',11),(1860,'Dungeons to Dominions','This series can be played gamebook-style or used for solitaire adventuring with various fantasy RPGs.',1),(1861,'History Quest','These educational gamebooks are very visual, with full-color layouts and navigation involving icons.',1),(1862,'Science Quest','These educational gamebooks are very visual, with full-color layouts and navigation involving icons.',1),(1863,'Quest Adventure Set','These boxed set combine books from the <a href=\"/Series/1861\">History Quest</a>, <a href=\"/Series/1570\">Math Quest</a>, <a href=\"/Series/1942\">Geography Quest</a> and <a href=\"/Series/1862\">Science Quest</a> series.',1),(1864,'Misión Ciencias','',6),(1865,'Jornada Científica','',7),(1866,'Grøssernes mysterier','',29),(1867,'Miscellaneous Works by Jens Schumacher','',3),(1868,'Knights of Torbalia','',1),(1869,'Miscellaneous Works by Adam Carter','',1),(1870,'Miscellaneous Works by A. K. Forest','',1),(1871,'EDGE - I, Hero: Legends','',1),(1872,'Saga of the Ariax, The','',1),(1873,'Miscellaneous Works by Charles A. F. King','',1),(1874,'Miscellaneous Works by Claudia Wülfrath','',3),(1875,'Random Solo Adventure','',1),(1876,'Avalon Quests','These solo adventures use something called the SPAGS system, a party-based game system for a solitaire player.',1),(1877,'Vengeance Game Books','',1),(1878,'Metal Heroes and the Fate of Rock','',3),(1879,'Plants vs. Zombies','',1),(1880,'Solv-a-Crime','These books are collections of brief (usually around two-page) guess-the-ending mysteries.',1),(1881,'Miscellaneous Works by Doris Webster and Mary Alden Hopkins','These two authors were pioneers in interactive books in the 1920\'s and 1930\'s. They wrote several books where readers answer a series of questions in order to come up with a key number that leads to a paragraph containing a fortune or bit of personal analysis. Subsequent to these early experiments, they wrote <a href=\"/Item/11406\">Consider the Consequences</a>, a collection of three related, gamebook-style, branching-plot stories. All of the books were marketed as \"party books,\" fun to read and play aloud with a group of friends.',1),(1882,'Miscellaneous Works by Demian Katz','',1),(1883,'Miscellaneous Works by Nicholas Bourbaki','',1),(1884,'Adventure Time: Which Way, Dude?','',1),(1885,'Make Your Own Mistakes','',1),(1886,'Usborne Young Puzzle Adventures','',1),(1887,'Usborne Young Puzzles','This series started out as \"Usborne Young Puzzle Books\" but eventually changed to \"Usborne Young Puzzles.\" At least some titles first printed under the original title have been reprinted under the newer, shorter banner.',1),(1888,'Usborne First Steps to Reading','',1),(1889,'Lego Puzzle Storybooks','',1),(1890,'Lego Action Maze Books','',1),(1891,'Lego Libro-Quiz Interattivo','',5),(1892,'Puzzle Storybooks','',1),(1893,'Miscellaneous Works by Paul Adshead','',1),(1894,'Enid Blyton Solve-It-Yourself Mysteries','While these do not have branching plots like many of Stephen Thraves\' other books, they do come in wallets with gimmicky extra components -- in this case, \"suspect line-ups\" designed to help the reader solve the mystery cases included in each volume.',1),(1895,'Usborne Science Puzzle Adventures','',1),(1896,'Usborne Solve It Yourself','\"Usborne Solve It Yourself\" was the original series title for the <a href=\"/Series/1449\">Usborne Puzzle Adventures</a>, used on the first three titles of that sequence before being changed. The title was later revived for this series of original puzzle books, separate from the main <a href=\"/Series/1449\">Usborne Puzzle Adventures</a> line.',1),(1897,'Usborne Spinechillers','',1),(1898,'Usborne Whodunnits','',1),(1899,'Einsamer Wolf: Die Neuen Kai Krieger','Where the English <a href=\"/Series/1106\">Lone Wolf</a> series is continuously numbered, the German translations restarted the numbering on a new series title beginning with the twenty-first book. The translations pick up where the 2009-2015 series of <a href=\"/Series/1249\">Einsamer Wolf</a> translations left off.',3),(1900,'Choose Your Own Ever After','Choose Your Own Ever After is a pick-a-path/choose your own adventure series targeted at tween girls (aged 10-12 years). The series focuses on the idea that a girl\'s destiny is in her own hands and covers topics such as camping with besties, parties, cute boys, mega crushes and netball.',1),(1901,'Segue o Teu Coração','',7),(1902,'Choose Your Fairy Tale','Interactive versions of a classic tales for readers of all ages.',1),(1903,'Computer Gamer','<p>Computer Gamer was a video game magazine published in the UK covering home gaming from April 1985 to June 1987.</p>\n\n<p>It contained sections of news, game reviews, previews, tips, help guides, columnists, reader\'s letters, and occasionally cover-mounted game demos.</p>\n\n<p>Issues #7 through #19 contained <a href=\"/Series/11\">Fighting Fantasy</a>-esqe mini-adventures titled <i>Heroic Warrior Adventures</i>.</p>',1),(1905,'Miscellaneous Works by Matt Barton','Matt Barton is a scholar who has referenced some gamebook-related materials in his work.',1),(1906,'Live Your Own Adventures','The launch of the Saab 9-3 SportCombi in 2005 involved a number of promotional items being produced. One item was a book titled \"The Race Against Time\", published in-house by Saab Automobile (United Kingdom). The 100-page adventure game book featuring the Saab 9-3 SportWagon was sent to 50,000 prospects and existing customers. In addition, at the time of the book\'s release, there was a corresponding online version of the book (game) which unfortunately is no longer available.',1),(1907,'Steam Highwayman','Funded by a Kickstarter, this free-roaming series, set in Victorian England, has a distinctly steampunk flavour.',1),(1908,'You Choose: Fractured Fairy Tales','',1),(1909,'Sprints Flips','Humorous and interactive adventure books where students are the main characters making all the choices.',1),(1910,'Tales of Quahnarren','This series explores the island continent of Quahnarren; a detailed fantasy environment where a wide variety of friendly people, unwelcoming opponents and fearsomely wild creatures will be encountered. Tales of Quahnarren features a structure and game system similar to Fighting Fantasy, with the addition of an enhanced battle process and the inclusion of armour. Fantasy adventuring that tests awareness, decision-making skills and resource management.',1),(1911,'Coleção Adrenalina','',7),(1912,'Miscellaneous Works by Kitty Curran and Larissa Zageris','',1),(1913,'Agile Adventures','This series uses the gamebook format to teach about Agile project management through an interactive business novel.',1),(1914,'Sigue la pista','',6),(1915,'Minecraft: Aventura Interativa','',7),(1916,'Miscellaneous Works by Othniel Poole','',1),(1917,'Miscellaneous Works by Thomas Finn','',3),(1918,'Miscellaneous Works by Michael Schultz-Koernig','',3),(1919,'sieben Schwerter Lerons, Die','',3),(1920,'Aschkalon: An Interactive Adventure for German Learners','This series is designed to help English speakers learn German.',3),(1921,'Steve Jacksons Sorcery!','This is the second German translation of the series, following <a href=\"/Series/653\">Die Analand Saga</a>.',3),(1922,'Simon Kest','',3),(1923,'Miscellaneous Works by Denis Herold','',3),(1924,'Miscellaneous Works by Falko Kötter','',3),(1925,'DestinyQuest','',3),(1926,'Tunnels and Trolls: The Crown of Klade','This series of Android/iPhone apps is based on nine books in the <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> series, four extended mini-adventures from <a href=\"/Series/394\">Sorcerer\'s Apprentice</a>, and it also contains two original titles. These are ordered into a campaign named \"The Crown of Klade\".',1),(1928,'Gamebook Where You Are the Hero, A','Only the second volume of the French series <a href=\"/Series/1769\">Un livre dont vous êtes l\'aventurier - SF</a> has been translated. Unfortunately, this seems to have been the work of an automatic translation device as it is riddled with errors.',1),(1929,'Be Your Own Duck Commander','This series ties in with the <i>Duck Dynasty</i> reality show.',1),(1930,'Tunnels and Trolls','These Android/iPhone apps are mostly conversions of items in the <a href=\"/Series/111\">Tunnels and Trolls</a> series, but unlike <a href=\"/Series/1926\">Tunnels and Trolls: The Crown of Klade</a> these conversions do not form part of any particular campaign. There is also one original title.',1),(1931,'Tunnels and Trolls: Mini Solo','These Android/iPhone apps are based on some shorter Tunnels and Trolls adventures in <a href=\"/Series/394\">Sorcerer\'s Apprentice</a> and <a href=\"/Series/1344\">Tunnels and Trolls: Trollhalla Press</a>.',1),(1932,'Gamebook Adventures: Legacy of Dorn','',1),(1933,'Road Less Traveled, A','So far only the first two books of <a href=\"/Series/1640\">A Road Less Traveled</a> have been converted into digital gamebook format.',1),(1934,'One Hundred Series','',1),(1935,'Select Your Own Continuum','',1),(1936,'Tunnels and Trolls: Supers Solo Series','',1),(1937,'World of Lone Wolf: Collector\'s Edition, The','This series reprints <a href=\"/Series/339\">The World of Lone Wolf</a> with new illustrations and added mini-adventures. Only the first volume was published in English, though all four volumes were translated into German and Italian.',1),(1938,'Play-Your-Way Midnight Arcade','',1),(1939,'Protodimension','This magazine included a solitaire adventure for <a href=\"/Series/60\">Call of Cthulhu</a> in one issue.',1),(1940,'Destiny\'s Role','This series by a well-known gamebook blogger and fandom member has a very classic old school 1980s gamebook feel and system.',1),(1941,'Four Against Darkness','This series provides random adventures for a single player who can control up to four characters. The first book is needed in order to play the other volumes. The books are not numbered but are listed here in order of appearance.',1),(1942,'Geography Quest','These educational gamebooks are very visual, with full-color layouts and navigation involving icons.',1),(1943,'Anduin','This magazine included a solitaire adventure in one issue.',3),(1944,'Miscellaneous Works by Daniel Bissot','',1),(1945,'Ancient Odysseys: Treasure Awaits!','',1),(1946,'Jupiter\'s Glory Gamebooks','',1),(1947,'Operation WetFish, Vampire Detective Gamebooks','',1),(1948,'Sheriff Grizzly Gamebooks','',1),(1949,'World of Lone Wolf: Greystar','',3),(1950,'Tunnels and Trolls: Necropolis of Vaarh','This series of Android/IPhone apps was created by using the MetaArcade Creators Pilot Program.',1),(1951,'T&T Adventures Japan','',1),(1952,'Star Wars Galaxy','At least one issue of this Star Wars collecting magazine included a solo adventure. The adventure is based on Star Wars: The Roleplaying Game by West End Games, but includes all rules needed to play.',1),(1953,'Miscellaneous Works by J. S. Quast','',3),(1954,'Aventuras dos Trapalhões - Especial RPG','This original Brazilian series consists of two pulp-genre interactive comic books.',7),(1955,'Miscellaneous Works by John E. Kirk','',1),(1956,'Tunnels and Trolls: Bolt Thrower Press','',1),(1957,'Gaturro: El protagonista sos vos','This series of interactive books is based on a Spanish-language comic strip aimed at children, which was created by Cristian Dzwonik (using the pen name Nik).',6),(1958,'Miscellaneous Works by Ashton MacSaylor and Jamie Thomson','',1),(1959,'Miscellaneous Works by Dan Shamir','A maze consisting solely of illustrations from a first-person perspective.',1),(1960,'Kunoichi','',5),(1961,'Kunoichi','',1),(1962,'Miscellaneous Works by Miguel Ángel García and Adrian Benatar','',1),(1963,'Adventure Time: Crea tu propia aventura','This is an original Spanish-language gamebook series based on the Cartoon Network animated TV show. Except for the subject matter, these books are unrelated to the <a href=\"/Series/1884\">American Adventure Time interactive book series</a>.',6),(1964,'Miscellaneous Works by Nicholas Gauthier','',1),(1965,'Tiny Toon Adventures','This Spanish-language series consists of a single book which apparently can only be found as an electronic document on the Internet. The book uses the <i>Tiny Toons</i> animated TV series property, but it is very unlikely it was licensed by the owners. It was apparently intended as a fanmade spoof more than a tie-in. The book uses the same cover and a similar visual design as a published 1992 \"Little Golden Books\" title based on the show, but it includes sexual content and is definitely intended for adult readers. The book is credited to Linda Williams Aber (who has written an interactive book based on the <i>Apollo 13</i> film), but it is improbable she was involved in its creation.',6),(1966,'Interactive Dating Sim Erotica, An','',1),(1967,'Fantasy-Spielbuch','',3),(1968,'Survival-Spielbuch','',3),(1969,'Spiele-Comic Abenteuer','',3),(1970,'Chevaliers: La BD dont vous êtes le héros','',2),(1971,'Spiele-Comic Krimi','',3),(1972,'Graphic Novel Adventures','',1),(1973,'Sherlock Holmes: La BD dont vous êtes le héros','',2),(1974,'Spiele-Comic Noir','',3),(1975,'Fabled Lands','',2),(1976,'Hocus & Pocus','These interactive fantasy comic books are aimed at older children. The reader can choose to control a male (Hocus) or female (Pocus) character, and controls Pokemon-like creatures with special powers. The book requires that you keep track of inventory, food for the creatures, and stars (which are the scorekeeping device for the game). Dice are required but only used for a limited number of purposes.',2),(1977,'Hocus & Pocus','',1),(1978,'Knights Club','',1),(1979,'Dinosaur World Gamebooks','',1),(1980,'Livro-Jogo de RPG','This is an original Brazilian series of fantasy gamebooks.',7),(1981,'Miscellaneous Works by Felipe Ruwer','',7),(1982,'Cthulhu Chronicles: Investigations in Lovecraft Country','',1),(1983,'Miscellaneous Works by Anthony Lampe','At least one book by this author was previously published as part of the  <a href=\"/Series/1612\">Explore-A-Quest</a> series.',1),(1984,'Kind der dunklen Sonne, Das','',3),(1985,'Miscellaneous Works by Mae McKinnon','',1),(1986,'Tunnels and Trolls: Fabled Worlds','',1),(1987,'Dragon Pathways','This self-published paperback series reissues <a href=\"/Series/121\">Dragontales</a> books with minor changes.',1),(1988,'Oregon Trail: Choose Your Own Trail, The','This series, based on the classic computer game, tells a serialized story across its first four volumes.',1),(1989,'Choose-Your-Own Erotic Fantasy','',1),(1990,'Choose Your Own Adventure Board Games','This series of board games uses decks of cards containing story and choices (and sometimes representing inventory items) to expand classic books into more game-like experiences. Unlike the source material that inspired them, these games involve dice-rolling for skill checks and tracking of various scores that affect game state. The exact details of the system vary from game to game, but the basic mechanics are similar.',1),(1991,'Nemezis','<i>Nemezis</i> is the sci-fi setting of the successful <i>Savage Worlds</i> RPG from GG Studio. <a href=\"/Item/11878\">Golgota Blues</a> is the first single-player gamebook written using this setting. The gameplay uses the Venture System, albeit simplified, which is a system which doesn\'t use any randomized method. Nemezis, in particular, utilises only two scores, Toughness and Reason, to which points are assigned by the reader.',5),(1992,'Dungeonesque','This old-school, Dungeons and Dragons-compatible game includes a solitaire adventure in its \"Red Box\" set.',1),(1993,'Savage Worlds','One fantasy setting book for this generic roleplaying system contains a solo adventure.',1),(1994,'Ballkaya Adventures','',1),(1995,'Ballkaya Adventures','',31),(1996,'Miscellaneous Works by Pavel Tomcsányi','',17),(1997,'Miscellaneous Works by Christopher McLean-Wheeler','This author successfully submitted a gamebook as an undergraduate honors thesis.',1),(1998,'Guardian Maia','This digital gamebook series is based on the mythology of the Maori culture, with a modern spin. It is planned as a duology, with a free-to-play first episode. The apps are illustrated, with soundtracks featuring birdsong and Maori sounds. Gameplay involves managing Maia\'s mana score, which can impact which ending is reached.',1),(1999,'E-scrita (Revista do Curso de Letras da Uniabeu)','This scholarly journal included a gamebook-related review in at least one issue.',7),(2000,'Revista Re-Unir','This scholarly journal included a gamebook-related review in at least one issue.',7),(2001,'Þín eigin...','These original Icelandic gamebooks cover a variety of themes. A companion series, <a href=\"/Series/2805\">Þín eigin saga</a>, contains stories aimed at younger readers.',16),(2002,'Miscellaneous Works by Mariusz Pisarski and Marcin Bylak','',28),(2003,'Miscellaneous Works by Khara Khang','',1),(2004,'Miscellaneous Works by Tsunami Media','This company produced a single interactive gamebook-style adventure for the Sony Data Discman, a very early experiment in electronic reading which used small CD-ROMs to carry content.',1),(2005,'Miscellaneous Works by R. Wayne Schmittberger','',1),(2006,'Eastgate Hypertext Fiction','Eastgate Systems was an early publisher of hypertext systems (and remains active as of this writing), developing the Storyspace system used to build some of the earliest significant examples of digital gamebook-like narrative. This hypertext fiction movement can be viewed as a sibling of the traditional gamebook, but generally with higher literary aspirations and with a greater emphasis on exploring a narrative in a non-linear fashion rather than shaping the narrative through decision-making.',1),(2007,'Viva Essa Aventura!','',7),(2008,'Miscellaneous Works by Pavel Tomcsányi (English)','',1),(2009,'Miscellaneous Works by Peter Andrew Jones','',1),(2010,'Steve Jackson\'s Swordmaster','This computer game series only appears to have lasted for a single volume (and evidence suggests that even the first title may never have been released). From an advertisement in <i>Sinclair Programs</i> magazine issue 29 (March, 1985): \"Swordmaster is the result of a collaboration between Adventure International and Steve Jackson of Games Workshop. The result is a unique combination of book and computer game which Adventure International consider to be the best computerised version of Dungeons and Dragons ever produced.\"',1),(2011,'Kata Kumbas','Kata Kumbas is a fantasy setting of the successful <i>Savage Worlds</i> RPG from GG Studio. Kata Kumbas itself was first published as an independent Italian RPG in the \'90s; its setting is Laitia, a medieval and fantastic version of Italy. This trilogy of gamebooks follows the adventures of the reader, who travels through the Level Gate to find  himself in the role of Ugger, the Knight of the Gate. The gameplay uses the Venture System, albeit simplified, a system which doesn\'t use any randomized method. Kata Kumbas, in particular, utilises three scores, Strength, Cunning and Wisdom, to which points are assigned by the reader. Their sum will be Ugger’s Energy. Another resource, Fate Points, will be useful to overcome the numerous checks of the game.',5),(2012,'Micro Adventurer','This magazine included gamebook-themed reviews and articles in several issues.',1),(2013,'Choose Your Own Minecraft Story','',1),(2014,'Choose Your Own Story: Sneezy Steve','',1),(2015,'Choose Your Own Story: Sol Wars','',1),(2016,'Choose Your Destiny: Star Wars','',1),(2017,'Black Mirror','This British science-fiction television series inspired a stand-alone interactive episode, released between its fourth and fifth seasons. The episode is only available through the Netflix streaming service.',1),(2018,'Choose Your Own Story: Wendigo\'s Wizarding Academy','',1),(2019,'Lego Star Wars','This systemless stand-alone book is illustrated with photographs of <i>Star Wars</i>-based scenes created using Lego bricks and figurines. While there have been several video games, short films, and television shows which combine the Lego and <i>Star Wars</i> properties, this book does not seem to be directly based on any of them. Instead, the book allows the reader to experience adventures based on more canonical <i>Star Wars</i> materials such as the first eight episodic films, <i>The Clone Wars</i> and <i>Rogue One</i> films, and the <i>Star Wars Rebels</i> television show.\n\n<p>This is only one of several gamebooks which use the Lego license, most of which have also been released by DK. DK-published Lego gamebooks include the <a href=\"/Series/1890\">Lego Action Maze Books</a> and the <a href=\"/Series/1889\">Lego Puzzle Storybooks</a>.\n \n<p>This is also only one out of many gamebooks and solitaire adventures based on <i>Star Wars</i>, but it is unrelated (except for the subject matter) to any others.',1),(2020,'Comics Squad','This series of children\'s comics anthologies included an interactive adventure in at least one volume.',1),(2021,'Playmobil Interactive Movies','',1),(2022,'Cardventures','This unusual series presents gamebooks as decks of cards, allowing some new mechanics like encountering sections in random order.',1),(2023,'Cards of Fate','This card game challenges a team of players to survive a series of random encounters in a dangerous forest. The map is constructed of randomly laid out cards. Each card has paragraph numbers that cross reference with character skills. Players decide which character will face each encounter and which skill will be used, and the book (available in electronic and printed versions) reveals the outcome.',1),(2024,'Oracle System','This boxed, multi-player gamebook was marketed as a board game.',1),(2025,'Pick-a-Path Apocalypse','This is a series of systemless gamebooks set in post-apocalyptic scenarios.',1),(2026,'Ultima Forsan Solo Adventures','These books are part of the <i>Ultima Forsan</i> RPG series, a bestselling official Savage Setting for <i>Savage Worlds</i>, published by GG Studio (now Space Orange). The trilogy is completed and takes place in the Macabre Renaissance of Ultima Forsan, with lots of characters also depicted in the RPG and in the novels of this brand. The books are connected, but each of them can also be played as a stand-alone. You also can select two different ways to play them: as an official solo adventure for <i>Ultima Forsan</i>, with the complete rules in the Appendix, or with a very simple ruleset, that turns them into an introductory gamebook.',5),(2027,'Path of Legends: Lost City of the Dwarves','',1),(2028,'You Are the Hero','',1),(2029,'Fantasy-Universal-Abenteuer Abenteuer-Set','This series of role-playing books has some solitaire and some group adventures; only the solo adventures are listed here.',3),(2030,'Miscellaneous Works by Jack Craig','',1),(2031,'Miscellaneous Works by Kris Schnee','',1),(2032,'Miscellaneous Works by Peter Lichtenberger','',3),(2033,'Miscellaneous Works by S. P. M.','',3),(2034,'Aborea','',3),(2035,'Miscellaneous Works by S. C. Cunningham','',1),(2036,'Miscellaneous Works by Michael David Burgess','',1),(2037,'Dungeon Crawlers','',1),(2038,'Miscellaneous Works by David Maroto','',1),(2039,'Time Crashers','',1),(2040,'Thunderbirds Are Go','',1),(2041,'Pocket Dungeon','',1),(2042,'Dungeon Crawl Classics','This retro role-playing system inspired at least one third-party solitaire adventure.',1),(2043,'RPG Review','At least one issue of this Australian online fanzine includes a solo adventure.',1),(2044,'Legends of Lone Wolf (Berkley, 1990-1991)','This short-lived series of reprints from <a href=\"/Series/1494\">Legends of Lone Wolf</a> changed the numbering slightly by splitting what was originally the third book into two parts (numbers 3 and 4 here).',1),(2045,'Try Not to Die','',1),(2046,'Trollhammer','Trollhammer is a pocket module written expressly for play with the Tunnels and Trolls 7.5™ Roleplaying game system.',1),(2047,'Políklona Paramíthia [Τα Πολύκλωνα Παραμύθια], Ta','',36),(2048,'Miscellaneous Works by Vicente Leñero','This author wrote a non-interactive novel that includes several alternate paths the protagonist\'s life can take, therefore resembling a gamebook.',6),(2049,'Scarthey, University of the Arcane: Choose Your Destiny','',1),(2050,'Adobenchā noberusu [アドベンチャーノベルス]','A series of gamebooks that appeared in Japan during the later half of the gamebook boom in the 1980s. Each novel is based on an computer game including titles published by Infocom (e.g. Zork series) and Origin (e.g. Ultima series). Each book included a bookmark that served as the adventure sheet.',4),(2051,'Miscellaneous Works by Janina Scarlet','',1),(2052,'Shadowrun','A beginner boxed set for the fifth edition of this cyberpunk role-playing game contains an extremely simple introductory solo adventure.',1),(2053,'Miscellaneous Works by D. Mann','',1),(2054,'Pyramid','This gaming magazine included a solo adventure in one issue. The adventure is also included in the basic set of the <a href=\"/Series/2645\">Dungeon Fantasy Roleplaying Game</a>, whose rules are based on the <a href=\"/Series/191\">GURPS</a> system. The magazine version includes a simplified version of the rules, meaning neither the GURPS ruleset nor the Dungeon Fantasy Roleplaying Game are needed to play it.',1),(2055,'Miscellaneous Works by Mehdi Fakrahmad','',1),(2056,'Miscellaneous Works by Cory Tucholski','',1),(2057,'The New Road You Uncover So','',1),(2058,'Choose Your Own Adventure Spies','',1),(2059,'Can You Survive?','',1),(2060,'Choose Your Way Through','',1),(2061,'Unbeatable Squirrel Girl (2015), The','This comic book series included an interactive issue.',1),(2062,'Adventure Time','This comic book series included an interactive issue. It was followed by the <a href=\"/Series/1884\">Adventure Time: Which Way Dude?</a> series.',1),(2063,'Unwritten, The','This comic book series included an interactive issue.',1),(2064,'You Choose: Superman','',1),(2065,'You Choose: Justice League','',1),(2066,'My Little Pony: Equestria Girls: Make Your Own Magic','',1),(2067,'What Lies Beneath the Clock Tower','Descend into the depths of the undercity and embroil yourself in the political struggles of colonialist gnomes and indigenous goblins. Fly in air balloons, drink mysterious and pleasant cocktails, smoke opium with the dregs of gnomish society. Or dream and speak of liberation for all the races. Fall in love and abscond into the caverns. It\'s up to you, because this is an adventure of your own choosing.',1),(2068,'Elige tu cuento','This Spanish-language series is based on fairy tales.',6),(2069,'Case Closed','These gamebooks allow the reader to play the part of a Latino child who, together with his best friend and her annoying little brother, takes up cases for his mother\'s detective agency. The books\' format is unusual for interactive fiction, being hardbacks with a long page count. The only game elements present involve visual and logic puzzles which the reader must solve in order to make progress.',1),(2070,'Labyrinth Lord','This \"old school\" rule set is compatible with classic <a href=\"/Series/13\">Dungeons &amp; Dragons</a>.\n\n<p><a href=\"/Item/12150\">Crypt of the Shadow Lord</a>, a third-party solitaire dungeon crawl adventure for the game, is written for one, first-level, lawful cleric. With little to no adjustment, the Basic Rules from other, similar FRPG’s may also be employed successfully. Although designed as a solitaire, this adventure also plays well as a duet (one player and one referee), and is intended as something of an introduction for both novice players and game referees. The idea was to create an introductory, origin adventure for a 1st level character that, when successfully completed, would allow for advancement for the 2nd level. This character could then later be included in a more traditional, group game.',1),(2071,'Interactive Story Series','',1),(2072,'Code Crackers','',1),(2073,'Rolled & Told','At least one issue of this RPG magazine contains a solo adventure.',1),(2074,'Demon\'s Bane, The','',1),(2075,'Piccolo Mysterysolvers','',1),(2076,'Mission Pornstar','This adult choose your own adventure book was included as a supplement with an issue of Newlook Magazine (April 2005).',2),(2077,'Can You Brexit? Without Breaking Britain','',1),(2078,'Ghostly, Time Travelling Game Books','',1),(2079,'Choose-Your-Own-Path Books','',1),(2080,'Savage Realms: The Legorian Kings Saga','The three volumes of this series, the third of which was not published, were combined and reworked into volume \"0\" of the series <a href=\"/Series/2493\">Savage Realms Gamebooks</a>.',1),(2081,'400 Series','',1),(2082,'Forgotten Realms Endless Quest','La serie è l\'edizione italiana della serie \"Endless Quest\" di Wizard of the Coast.',5),(2083,'Tempel der bösen Träume','',3),(2084,'Fortezza Europa','“Fortezza Europa – Londra” is a stand-alone investigative gamebook set in a dystopian future, designed to be playable both on paper and in ebook format. Sometimes the reader will have to decide where to go and what to say, without the text presenting the relative options - like in a text-based adventure for computers.',5),(2085,'Dossier Anunnaki','An investigative series in the darkest mysteries of the modern age (rated 18+)',5),(2086,'Write Your Own Adventure','Write Your Own Adventure is a literacy programme that uses choice-based fiction to engage reluctant writers, challenge gifted students and bring about an enthusiastic culture of writing for an audience. It draws on years of classroom practice and the powerfully engaging qualities of a story style that puts the reader – and the writer – fully in control.',1),(2087,'Thomerion Trilogy','',1),(2088,'Interactive Adventure, An','This series was reissued under the name \"Gone South Interactive Tales (Big Book)\" and renumbered.',1),(2089,'Necronomicon Gamebook, The','un\'avventura letteraria a bivi, scritta usando brani originali di Lovecraft',5),(2090,'Child Wood','Le avventure di Ron Stephenson, guardiacaccia del paese di Child Wood.',5),(2091,'Choose Cthulhu (ITA)','Con la serie Choose Cthulhu puoi rivivere in prima persona i più celebri racconti di H. P. Lovecraft: seguirai la trama originale oppure scoprirai nuovi inaspettati finali?',5),(2092,'EXIT','Una stanza. Nessuna via d\'uscita. Dieci enigmi. Riesci a risolvere il caso e a scappare?',5),(2093,'Escape Book - Salani','Riuscirai a uscire da questo libro?',5),(2094,'Escape Book - Piemme','Libri da leggere e da esplorare muovendo